positive impact of GAI on their academic performance. Personality traits also play a significant role in shaping perceptions and usage of GAI. After controlling demographic factors, we found that personality still significantly predicts GAI use and attitudes: (a) Students with higher conscientiousnes s use GAI less . (b) Students who are higher in agreeableness perceive a less positive impact of GAI on academic performance and express more ethical concerns about using it for academic work. (c) Students with higher emotional stability report a more positive impact of GAI on learning and fewer concerns about its academic use. (d) Students with higher extraversion show a stronger preference for GAI over traditional resources. (e) Students with higher intellect/imagination tend to prefer traditional resources.\", \"research_topics\": [\"University students\' use of generative AI (GAI)\"], \"assessment_tools\": [\"50-item International Personality Item Pool (IPIP)\"], \"research_subjects\": [\"undergraduate and graduate students in the United States\"], \"teaching_strategies\": [\"探究式学习\"], \"intervention_duration\": \"30分钟\", \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": [\"AI_COGNITION\"]}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, NULL, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (14, 9, 1012, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"MOCK\", \"intervention\": \"MOCK\", \"mainFindings\": \"MOCK\", \"participants\": \"MOCK\", \"researchTopic\": \"MOCK\", \"assessmentTools\": \"MOCK\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"MOCK\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"MOCK\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 1, '2026-09-08 19:44:28', '2026-09-08 19:44:28', '{\"limitations\": [\"Experiments primarily focus on non-reasoning LLMs, and evaluation on reasoning-oriented models (e.g., ChatGPT-o1) is needed.\", \"AutoSCORE has not yet been deployed in a real classroom or large-scale student assessment setting.\", \"The work is limited to text-based assessments and does not handle multimodal inputs like images and audio.\", \"Rubric component templates are currently manually derived.\"], \"main_findings\": [\"AutoSCORE predominantly improves scoring accuracy, human-machine agreement (QWK, correlations), and reduces error metrics (MAE, RMSE) compared to single-agent baselines.\", \"The framework shows particularly strong benefits on complex, multi-dimensional rubrics.\", \"The framework yields especially large relative gains on smaller LLMs.\", \"The two-stage design constrains the LLM\'s reasoning path to pass through human-aligned intermediate states, improving interpretability and robustness.\"], \"evidence_ready\": false, \"source_excerpt\": null, \"research_topics\": [\"Automated scoring of constructed responses\", \"Large language models in education\", \"Multi-agent framework design\"], \"assessment_tools\": [\"ASAP-SAS\", \"ASAP-AES\", \"Accuracy\", \"Quadratic Weighted Kappa (QWK)\", \"Mean Absolute Error (MAE)\", \"Root Mean Square Error (RMSE)\", \"Pearson correlation\", \"Spearman correlation\"], \"research_subjects\": [\"Grade 10 students\", \"Grade 8 students\"], \"teaching_strategies\": [], \"intervention_duration\": null, \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": []}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, NULL, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (15, NULL, 1018, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"MOCK\", \"intervention\": \"MOCK\", \"mainFindings\": \"MOCK\", \"participants\": \"MOCK\", \"researchTopic\": \"MOCK\", \"assessmentTools\": \"MOCK\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"MOCK\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"MOCK\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 1, '2026-09-08 19:52:45', '2026-09-08 19:52:45', '{\"limitations\": [], \"main_findings\": [], \"evidence_ready\": false, \"source_excerpt\": null, \"research_topics\": [], \"assessment_tools\": [], \"research_subjects\": [], \"teaching_strategies\": [], \"intervention_duration\": null, \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": []}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, 12, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (16, 10, 1018, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"MOCK\", \"intervention\": \"MOCK\", \"mainFindings\": \"MOCK\", \"participants\": \"MOCK\", \"researchTopic\": \"MOCK\", \"assessmentTools\": \"MOCK\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"MOCK\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"MOCK\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 1, '2026-09-08 19:53:44', '2026-09-08 19:53:44', '{\"limitations\": [\"The controlled lab environment may not fully mirror the complexities of real-world educational settings where multiple visualisations are viewed simultaneously.\", \"The one-hour intervention period limits the ability to assess long-term learning gains.\", \"The participant pool was predominantly from North America and Europe, limiting the generalisability of the findings.\", \"The study did not thoroughly investigate the specific design features of the proactive GenAI agents contributing to their effectiveness.\", \"The evaluation focused on lower levels of Bloom\'s taxonomy (knowledge and comprehension) and did not assess higher-order thinking skills.\"], \"main_findings\": [\"Passive GenAI agents yielded comparable improvements to standalone scaffolding (data storytelling) both during and after the intervention.\", \"Proactive GenAI agents significantly enhanced students\' VLA comprehension compared to both passive agents and standalone scaffolding, with these benefits persisting beyond the intervention.\", \"All three interventions (data storytelling, passive GenAI, proactive GenAI) significantly improved the effectiveness and efficiency of extracting insights from VLA.\", \"The proactive GenAI agent condition showed a significant improvement from the intervention phase to the post-intervention phase, unlike the other conditions.\", \"No significant interaction effects were found between visualisation literacy and intervention conditions, suggesting the interventions were beneficial across literacy levels.\"], \"evidence_ready\": true, \"source_excerpt\": \"The results show that passive agents yield comparable improvements to standalone scaffolding both during and after the intervention. Notably, proactive GenAI agents significantly enhance students’ VLA comprehension compared to both passive agents and standalone scaffolding, with these benefits persisting beyond the intervention.\", \"research_topics\": [\"Generative AI agents\", \"Scaffolding\", \"Visual learning analytics (VLA) comprehension\", \"Data storytelling\"], \"assessment_tools\": [\"mini-Visualisation Literacy Assessment Test (mini-VLAT)\", \"Analytical writing tasks\", \"Multiple-choice evaluation questions (Knowledge and Comprehension levels of Bloom\'s taxonomy)\"], \"research_subjects\": [\"higher education students\"], \"teaching_strategies\": [\"Data storytelling\", \"Passive GenAI agents (reactive support)\", \"Proactive GenAI agents (scaffolding questions and feedback)\"], \"intervention_duration\": null, \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": [\"Data visualisation literacy\"]}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, NULL, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (17, 10, 1018, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"TEACHER\", \"intervention\": \"TEACHER\", \"mainFindings\": \"TEACHER\", \"participants\": \"TEACHER\", \"researchTopic\": \"TEACHER\", \"assessmentTools\": \"TEACHER\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"TEACHER\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"TEACHER\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 2, '2026-09-08 21:01:51', '2026-09-08 21:01:51', '{\"limitations\": [\"The controlled lab environment may not fully mirror the complexities of real-world educational settings where multiple visualisations are viewed simultaneously.\", \"The one-hour intervention period limits the ability to assess long-term learning gains.\", \"The participant pool was predominantly from North America and Europe, limiting the generalisability of the findings.\", \"The study did not thoroughly investigate the specific design features of the proactive GenAI agents contributing to their effectiveness.\", \"The evaluation focused on lower levels of Bloom\'s taxonomy (knowledge and comprehension) and did not assess higher-order thinking skills.\"], \"main_findings\": [\"Passive GenAI agents yielded comparable improvements to standalone scaffolding (data storytelling) both during and after the intervention.\", \"Proactive GenAI agents significantly enhanced students\' VLA comprehension compared to both passive agents and standalone scaffolding, with these benefits persisting beyond the intervention.\", \"All three interventions (data storytelling, passive GenAI, proactive GenAI) significantly improved the effectiveness and efficiency of extracting insights from VLA.\", \"The proactive GenAI agent condition showed a significant improvement from the intervention phase to the post-intervention phase, unlike the other conditions.\", \"No significant interaction effects were found between visualisation literacy and intervention conditions, suggesting the interventions were beneficial across literacy levels.\"], \"evidence_ready\": true, \"source_excerpt\": \"The results show that passive agents yield comparable improvements to standalone scaffolding both during and after the intervention. Notably, proactive GenAI agents significantly enhance students’ VLA comprehension compared to both passive agents and standalone scaffolding, with these benefits persisting beyond the intervention.\", \"research_topics\": [\"Generative AI agents\"], \"assessment_tools\": [\"mini-Visualisation Literacy Assessment Test (mini-VLAT)\", \"Analytical writing tasks\", \"Multiple-choice evaluation questions (Knowledge and Comprehension levels of Bloom\'s taxonomy)\"], \"research_subjects\": [\"higher education students\"], \"teaching_strategies\": [\"Data storytelling\", \"Passive GenAI agents (reactive support)\", \"Proactive GenAI agents (scaffolding questions and feedback)\"], \"intervention_duration\": null, \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": [\"Data visualisation literacy\"]}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, NULL, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (18, 10, 1018, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"TEACHER\", \"intervention\": \"TEACHER\", \"mainFindings\": \"TEACHER\", \"participants\": \"TEACHER\", \"researchTopic\": \"TEACHER\", \"assessmentTools\": \"TEACHER\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"TEACHER\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"TEACHER\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 3, '2026-09-08 21:02:21', '2026-09-08 21:02:21', '{\"limitations\": [\"The controlled lab environment may not fully mirror the complexities of real-world educational settings where multiple visualisations are viewed simultaneously.\", \"The one-hour intervention period limits the ability to assess long-term learning gains.\", \"The participant pool was predominantly from North America and Europe, limiting the generalisability of the findings.\", \"The study did not thoroughly investigate the specific design features of the proactive GenAI agents contributing to their effectiveness.\", \"The evaluation focused on lower levels of Bloom\'s taxonomy (knowledge and comprehension) and did not assess higher-order thinking skills.\"], \"main_findings\": [\"Passive GenAI agents yielded comparable improvements to standalone scaffolding (data storytelling) both during and after the intervention.\", \"Proactive GenAI agents significantly enhanced students\' VLA comprehension compared to both passive agents and standalone scaffolding, with these benefits persisting beyond the intervention.\", \"All three interventions (data storytelling, passive GenAI, proactive GenAI) significantly improved the effectiveness and efficiency of extracting insights from VLA.\", \"The proactive GenAI agent condition showed a significant improvement from the intervention phase to the post-intervention phase, unlike the other conditions.\", \"No significant interaction effects were found between visualisation literacy and intervention conditions, suggesting the interventions were beneficial across literacy levels.\"], \"evidence_ready\": true, \"source_excerpt\": \"The results show that passive agents yield comparable improvements to standalone scaffolding both during and after the intervention. Notably, proactive GenAI agents significantly enhance students’ VLA comprehension compared to both passive agents and standalone scaffolding, with these benefits persisting beyond the intervention.\", \"research_topics\": [\"Generative AI agents\"], \"assessment_tools\": [\"mini-Visualisation Literacy Assessment Test (mini-VLAT)\", \"Analytical writing tasks\", \"Multiple-choice evaluation questions (Knowledge and Comprehension levels of Bloom\'s taxonomy)\"], \"research_subjects\": [\"higher education students\"], \"teaching_strategies\": [\"Data storytelling\", \"Passive GenAI agents (reactive support)\", \"Proactive GenAI agents (scaffolding questions and feedback)\"], \"intervention_duration\": \"45min\", \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": [\"Data visualisation literacy\"]}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, NULL, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (19, NULL, 1002, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"MOCK\", \"intervention\": \"MOCK\", \"mainFindings\": \"MOCK\", \"participants\": \"MOCK\", \"researchTopic\": \"MOCK\", \"assessmentTools\": \"MOCK\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"MOCK\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"MOCK\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 1, '2026-09-08 21:03:17', '2026-09-08 21:03:17', '{\"limitations\": [], \"main_findings\": [], \"evidence_ready\": false, \"source_excerpt\": null, \"research_topics\": [], \"assessment_tools\": [], \"research_subjects\": [], \"teaching_strategies\": [], \"intervention_duration\": null, \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": []}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, 14, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (20, 11, 1018, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"MOCK\", \"intervention\": \"MOCK\", \"mainFindings\": \"MOCK\", \"participants\": \"MOCK\", \"researchTopic\": \"MOCK\", \"assessmentTools\": \"MOCK\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"MOCK\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"MOCK\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 1, '2026-09-08 21:18:00', '2026-09-08 21:18:00', '{\"limitations\": [\"The sample was primarily composed of educational technology majors with uneven gender distribution, limiting generalizability to other disciplines and populations\", \"The instructional tasks focused on research design activities, leaving uncertainty about effectiveness in other learning domains\", \"The six-week intervention duration was relatively short, limiting the ability to examine long-term effects on reflective practices and sustained behavioral change\", \"The number of group-level submissions was small (eight proposals per condition), constraining statistical power and precision of effect estimates\", \"Ethical considerations regarding LLM-based learning processes, including data privacy, content originality, and intellectual property rights, were not comprehensively addressed\"], \"main_findings\": [\"The S-ICA group showed marginal improvement in conceptual knowledge gains compared to the nS-ICA group (Time × Condition interaction ̂𝛽 = 0.821, 𝑝 = 0.056)\", \"The S-ICA group produced significantly higher-quality experimental research proposals (Hedges’ 𝑔 = 1.69, 95% CI [0.57, 2.80]; CLES = 0.88)\", \"S-ICA students reported significantly higher overall reflective thinking, with particularly pronounced differences in Reflection (𝐹 = 11.438, 𝑝 = .001) and Critical Reflection (𝐹 = 13.712, 𝑝 < 0.001)\", \"Epistemic Network Analysis revealed that S-ICA students activated more advanced reflective pathways, showing stronger connections between Understanding–Reflection (U–R, 0.53), Reflection–Critical Reflection (R–CR, 0.43), and Understanding–Critical Reflection (U–CR, 0.32)\", \"No significant difference was found in learning motivation between the groups (𝐹 = 1.749, 𝑝 = 0.190)\", \"Interviews indicated that S-ICA facilitated deep thinking, effective learning, questioning and reflection, knowledge acquisition, and prevention of cognitive dependence, while nS-ICA users reported superficial learning and reliance on ready-made answers\"], \"evidence_ready\": true, \"source_excerpt\": null, \"research_topics\": [\"Effects of an LLM-based Socratic Intelligent Conversational Agent (S-ICA) on students\' academic performance and reflective thinking in higher education\", \"Integration of Socratic questioning strategies with GenAI/LLM technologies\", \"Comparative study of Socratic vs. non-Socratic conversational agents in inquiry-based learning for research design\"], \"assessment_tools\": [\"Conceptual knowledge pretest and posttest on experimental research methods\", \"Research proposal quality rubric (six dimensions: Research topic, Research question, Theoretical rationale, Research content, Research methodology, Research procedures)\", \"Reflective thinking scale adapted from Kember et al. (1999) measuring four dimensions: Habitual Action, Understanding, Reflection, and Critical Reflection\", \"Learning motivation questionnaire adapted from Chen et al. (2015) measuring Intrinsic Motivation and Extrinsic Motivation\", \"Epistemic Network Analysis (ENA) of dialogue transcripts to model co-occurrence of reflective categories\", \"Semi-structured interviews with selected students to capture learning perceptions and experiences\"], \"research_subjects\": [\"94 second-year undergraduates majoring in Educational Technology from a teacher training university in central China (mean age 19)\"], \"teaching_strategies\": [\"Inquiry-based learning (IBL) framework with Orientation, Conceptualization, Investigation, and Discussion phases\", \"Socratic questioning integrated into LLM-based conversational agents (S-ICA) to promote reflective thinking\", \"Collaborative group work with structured worksheets and turn-taking scaffolds\", \"Scaffolded feedback through adaptive prompt engineering and dynamic dialogue adjustment\", \"Iterative revision cycles guided by AI agents for experimental research proposal development\"], \"intervention_duration\": \"6 weeks\", \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": [\"Use of large language models (LLM) for learning support\", \"Socratic questioning strategies implemented via AI agents\", \"Inquiry-based learning with AI scaffolding\", \"Reflective thinking supported by conversational agents\", \"Collaborative human–AI learning systems\"]}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, NULL, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (21, NULL, 1019, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"MOCK\", \"intervention\": \"MOCK\", \"mainFindings\": \"MOCK\", \"participants\": \"MOCK\", \"researchTopic\": \"MOCK\", \"assessmentTools\": \"MOCK\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"MOCK\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"MOCK\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 1, '2026-09-08 21:20:58', '2026-09-08 21:20:58', '{\"limitations\": [], \"main_findings\": [], \"evidence_ready\": false, \"source_excerpt\": null, \"research_topics\": [], \"assessment_tools\": [], \"research_subjects\": [], \"teaching_strategies\": [], \"intervention_duration\": null, \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": []}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, 16, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (22, 12, 1019, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"MOCK\", \"intervention\": \"MOCK\", \"mainFindings\": \"MOCK\", \"participants\": \"MOCK\", \"researchTopic\": \"MOCK\", \"assessmentTools\": \"MOCK\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"MOCK\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"MOCK\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 1, '2026-09-08 21:22:16', '2026-09-08 21:22:16', '{\"limitations\": [\"Sample drawn primarily from Educational Technology undergraduates; limited generalizability to other disciplines and populations.\", \"Task focused on research design; it is unclear whether benefits would transfer to other learning domains.\", \"Short six-week intervention; long-term effects on reflective practice and sustained behavior change were not examined.\", \"Small number of group-level proposals (8 per condition) reduced statistical power and precision for team-level outcomes.\", \"Ethical issues of LLM use (privacy, originality, intellectual property) were not comprehensively addressed in the study design.\"], \"main_findings\": [\"S-ICA (Socratic agent) produced substantially higher quality experimental research proposals than nS-ICA (non-Socratic agent), with a large effect size (Hedges’ g = 1.69) and significantly better team-level scores.\", \"S-ICA significantly improved self-reported reflective thinking overall, with the strongest effects in the higher-order dimensions of Reflection and Critical Reflection.\", \"ENA showed S-ICA strengthened mid- to higher-order reflective pathways (e.g., Understanding–Reflection, Reflection–Critical Reflection, Understanding–Critical Reflection), while the control condition showed stronger connections in lower-order pathways (e.g., Habitual Action–Understanding).\", \"Conceptual knowledge gains were substantial in both groups, but the incremental advantage of S-ICA over nS-ICA was small to moderate and at most marginally significant.\", \"No statistically significant difference was found between S-ICA and nS-ICA on learning motivation.\"], \"evidence_ready\": true, \"source_excerpt\": null, \"research_topics\": [\"Effects of an LLM-based Socratic conversational agent (S-ICA) on students’ academic performance and reflective thinking in higher education\"], \"assessment_tools\": [\"Conceptual knowledge test\", \"Experimental research proposal quality rubric\", \"Reflective thinking scale (Kember et al., 1999; dimensions: Habitual Action, Understanding, Reflection, Critical Reflection)\", \"Learning motivation questionnaire (Chen et al., 2015; subscales: Intrinsic Motivation, Extrinsic Motivation)\", \"Epistemic Network Analysis (ENA) of dialogue transcripts\", \"Semi-structured interviews\"], \"research_subjects\": [\"second-year undergraduates majoring in Educational Technology\"], \"teaching_strategies\": [\"Socratic questioning strategy embedded in an LLM-driven conversational agent (S-ICA)\"], \"intervention_duration\": \"six-week intervention period\", \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": [\"Academic performance\", \"Reflective thinking\", \"Learning motivation\"]}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, NULL, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (23, NULL, 1011, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"MOCK\", \"intervention\": \"MOCK\", \"mainFindings\": \"MOCK\", \"participants\": \"MOCK\", \"researchTopic\": \"MOCK\", \"assessmentTools\": \"MOCK\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"MOCK\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"MOCK\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 1, '2026-09-09 10:17:08', '2026-09-09 10:17:08', '{\"limitations\": [], \"main_findings\": [], \"evidence_ready\": false, \"source_excerpt\": null, \"research_topics\": [], \"assessment_tools\": [], \"research_subjects\": [], \"teaching_strategies\": [], \"intervention_duration\": null, \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": []}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, 18, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (24, 13, 1011, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"MOCK\", \"intervention\": \"MOCK\", \"mainFindings\": \"MOCK\", \"participants\": \"MOCK\", \"researchTopic\": \"MOCK\", \"assessmentTools\": \"MOCK\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"MOCK\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"MOCK\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 1, '2026-09-09 10:19:26', '2026-09-09 10:19:26', '{\"limitations\": [\"样本量较小且集中于社科背景的研究生群体，可能影响结果的代表性与普适性\", \"实验干预时长有限，系统支持的长期影响仍有待探索\", \"ABCKC-AI虽对低结构观点的支持有效，但对高阶论证结构（如多源证据整合、复杂观点的综合）的促进作用有限\", \"当前学习者—智能体的互动机制相对单一，主要局限于单向提示和对话窗口互动\"], \"main_findings\": [\"ABCKC-AI支持能够显著提高个人论证水平，实验组前后测得分提升显著高于对照组\", \"ABCKC-AI支持的小组协作文档得分显著高于对照组，整体论证质量更佳\", \"实验组在与论证相关的实质性互动上产出更多，包括提出更多主张、更倾向于通过逻辑推理和事实证据增强观点的说服力、更积极主动地回应他人观点；对照组在社会情感互动上更为频繁\", \"实验组的平均论证层级高于对照组，具体层级分布上，实验组1级结构最多（46.13%），对照组0级结构最多（48.60%），实验组在1~3级结构上的比例均高于对照组，说明实验组出现了更多相对复杂的论证结构\", \"实验组的知识建构深度显著高于对照组，实验组更多进入阐释和修正阶段，对照组主要停留在分享和回应观点阶段\", \"学习者对ABCKC-AI支持的论证式协作知识建构整体持满意态度，认同其提示能帮助专注讨论并补充解释和证据，认同其主动插入的观点能启发思路、拓展讨论方向并生成有用的总结，认同其对话支持能帮助完善观点及整合讨论内容\"], \"evidence_ready\": true, \"source_excerpt\": null, \"research_topics\": [\"多智能体支持论证式协作知识建构\", \"基于大语言模型的多智能体系统（ABCKC-AI）设计与准实验评估\", \"论证式协作知识建构（ABCKC）\"], \"assessment_tools\": [\"GRE写作任务改编的论证水平测试（前测与后测）\", \"协作文档评分（基于已有评分框架，由两位教育技术学专家独立评分）\", \"《ABCKC-AI技术接受度问卷》（涵盖对AI提示论点相关度与论证结构的评价、对AI提供观点刺激的评价、对AI支持对话的评价、对ABCKC-AI的整体评价四个维度）\"], \"research_subjects\": [\"62名大学生（涵盖本科、硕士和博士不同学历层次，学科背景包括自然科学、人文学科、工学、社会科学）\"], \"teaching_strategies\": [\"通过开放性提示进行干预，避免过度脚本化\", \"对学习者在不同维度的实时表现进行个性化评估\", \"在小组的论证水平达到一定程度后，逐渐淡化并撤出干预\", \"提供外部观点刺激，促进讨论的深度和广度\"], \"intervention_duration\": \"70分钟\", \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": [\"参与维度\", \"论证维度\", \"认知维度\", \"社会共建维度\"]}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, NULL, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (25, 14, 1002, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"MOCK\", \"intervention\": \"MOCK\", \"mainFindings\": \"MOCK\", \"participants\": \"MOCK\", \"researchTopic\": \"MOCK\", \"assessmentTools\": \"MOCK\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"MOCK\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"MOCK\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 1, '2026-09-09 11:38:37', '2026-09-09 11:38:37', '{\"limitations\": [\"当前研究资源中尚未找到足够证据支持这一判断\"], \"main_findings\": [\"基于多模态大模型的协作论证支架引发了丰富的协作论证话语转移行为和高阶知识建构观点\", \"总结升华话语转移行为与关键观点密切相关，关键观点是促进知识建构由低阶至高阶跃迁的主导因素\", \"基于多模态大模型的协作论证支架有助于提升知识建构水平，尤其是在意义协商、检验修改与创新应用方面\"], \"evidence_ready\": true, \"source_excerpt\": null, \"research_topics\": [\"基于多模态大模型的协作论证支架对知识建构的影响\"], \"assessment_tools\": [\"AI素养量表\", \"话语转移行为编码框架\", \"IAM模型（交互分析模型）\"], \"research_subjects\": [\"68名大学二年级学生（男生30人，女生38人）\"], \"teaching_strategies\": [\"多模态开放系统\", \"差异化支持策略\", \"协同共生机理\"], \"intervention_duration\": \"5周\", \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": [\"AI技术理解\", \"AI评估\", \"AI应用\"]}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, NULL, 'READY');
INSERT INTO `research_analysis` VALUES (26, 15, 1002, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '{\"limitations\": \"MOCK\", \"intervention\": \"MOCK\", \"mainFindings\": \"MOCK\", \"participants\": \"MOCK\", \"researchTopic\": \"MOCK\", \"assessmentTools\": \"MOCK\", \"teachingStrategies\": \"MOCK\", \"aiLiteracyDimensions\": \"MOCK\"}', 0, 'INCOMPLETE', 0, 1, '2026-09-09 12:24:00', '2026-09-09 12:24:00', '{\"limitations\": [\"本文尚未回应在具体教学智能体或其他人工智能教育产品中内嵌智能化学习支架的具体做法\"], \"main_findings\": [\"本研究提出了学习支架的三种演化形态及典型特征：传统学习支架、技术支持型学习支架以及智能化学习支架\", \"本研究将智能化学习支架划分为动机性、概念性、策略性和元认知四种表征类型\", \"本研究提出了智能技术支持学习支架创变的“辅助—优化—重塑”三层次内涵\", \"本研究从支架表征、支架设计、支架内嵌和支架优化四方面提出智能化学习支架设计的基本原则\", \"本研究构建了包含“需求分析层—AI赋能层—框架设计层—学习环节层”四个层级的智能化学习支架设计流程\"], \"evidence_ready\": true, \"source_excerpt\": null, \"research_topics\": [\"人机协同教育场景中的智能化学习支架\", \"学习支架的演进历程\", \"智能化学习支架的内涵与表征\", \"智能化学习支架的设计方略\", \"智能化学习支架的发展路向\"], \"assessment_tools\": [], \"research_subjects\": [\"学生\"], \"teaching_strategies\": [\"支架表征流程化、可视化\", \"支架设计个性化、定制化\", \"支架内嵌常态化、循证化\", \"支架优化证据化、智适应化\"], \"intervention_duration\": null, \"teaching_implications\": null, \"ai_literacy_dimensions\": [\"动机性\", \"概念性\", \"策略性\", \"元认知\"]}', 'VALIDATED', NULL, NULL, 'READY');
