nerated misinformation and evaluating\nalgorithmic and human solutions,” in Proc. CHI Conf. Hum. Factors\nComput. Syst., 2023, pp. 1–20.\n[172] Heaven, “Why meta’s latest large language model only survived three\ndays online,” 2022. [Online]. Available: https://www.technologyreview.\ncom/2022/11/18/1063487/meta-large-language-model-ai-only-\nsurvived-three-days-gpt-3-science/\n[173] K. Wach et al., “The dark side of generative artiﬁcial intelligence: A\ncritical analysis of controversies and risks of ChatGPT,”Entrepreneurial\nBus. Econ. Rev., vol. 11, no. 2, pp. 7–24, 2023.\n[174] V . Kumar and D. Boulanger, “Explainable automated essay scoring:\nDeep learning really has pedagogical value,” Front. Educ., vol. 5, 2020,\nArt. no. 572367.\n[175] T. Susnjak, G. S. Ramaswami, and A. Mathrani, “Learning analytics\ndashboard: A tool for providing actionable insights to learners,” Int. J.\nEduc. Technol. Higher Educ., vol. 19, no. 1, 2022, Art. no. 12.\n[176] S. Polak, G. Schiavo, and M. Zancanaro, “Teachers’ perspective on\nartiﬁcial intelligence education: An initial investigation,” in Proc. CHI\nConf. Hum. Factors Comput. Syst. Extended Abstr ., 2022, pp. 1–7.\n[177] X. Wu et al., “Usable XAI: 10 strategies towards exploiting explainability\nin the LLM era,” 2024, arXiv:2403.08946.\n[178] E. Latif, X. Zhai, and L. Liu, “AI gender bias, disparities, and fairness:\nDoes training data matter?,” 2023, arXiv:2312.10833.\n[179] R. S. Baker and A. Hawn, “Algorithmic bias in education,” Int. J. Artif.\nIntell. Educ., vol. 32, pp. 1052–1092, 2021.\n[180] V . Dignum, Responsible Artiﬁcial Intelligence: How to Develop and Use\nAI in a Responsible Way , vol. 2156. Berlin, Germany: Springer, 2019.\n[181] C. Prunkl, “Human autonomy in the age of artiﬁcial intelligence,” Nature\nMach. Intell., vol. 4, no. 2, pp. 99–101, 2022.\n[182] E. M. Levesque, “The role of AI in education and the changing US\nworkforce,” Brookings Institution , Oct. 2018. [Online]. Available:\nhttps://www.brooking