 for the wilcoxon rank sum test and the wilcoxon signed rank test.\nSelected Tables in Mathematical Statistics, 1, 171–259.\nWu, Q., Bansal, G., Zhang, J., Wu, Y., Zhang, S., Zhu, E., et al. (2023). Autogen: Enabling next-gen llm applications via multi-agent conversation framework.\nArXiv preprint arXiv:2308.08155.\nXun, G., & Land, S. M. (2004). A conceptual framework for scaffolding III-structured problem-solving processes using question prompts and peer interactions.\nEducational Technology Research and Development, 52(2), 5–22.\nYan, L., Echeverria, V., Jin, Y., Fernandez-Nieto, G., Zhao, L., Li, X., et al. (2024). Evidence-based multimodal learning analytics for feedback and reflection in\ncollaborative learning. British Journal of Educational Technology, 55(5), 1900–1925.\nYan, L., Greiff, S., Teuber, Z., & Gašević, D. (2024). Promises and challenges of generative artificial intelligence for human learning. Nature Human Behaviour,\n8(10), 1839–1850.\nYan, L., Martinez-Maldonado, R., & Gasevic, D. (2024). Generative artificial intelligence in learning analytics: Contextualising opportunities and challenges through\nthe learning analytics cycle. In LAK ’24 (pp. 101–111).\nYan, L., Sha, L., Zhao, L., Li, Y., Martinez-Maldonado, R., Chen, G., et al. (2024). Practical and ethical challenges of large language models in education: A\nsystematic scoping review. British Journal of Educational Technology, 55(1), 90–112.\nComputers & Education 234 (2025) 105322\n23\n\nL. Yan et al.\nYan, L., Zhao, L., Echeverria, V., Jin, Y., Alfredo, R., Li, X., et al. (2024). VizChat: Enhancing learning analytics dashboards with contextualised explanations\nusing multimodal generative AI chatbots. In International conference on artificial intelligence in education (pp. 180–193). Springer.\nYe, Y., Hao, J., Hou, Y., Wang, Z., Xiao, S., Luo, Y., et al. (2024). Generative ai for visualization: State of the art and future directions. Visual Informatics.\nZdanovic, D., Lembcke, T. J., & Bogers, T. (2022). The influence of data storytelling on the ability to recall information. In Proceedings of the 2022 conference\non human information interaction and retrieval (pp. 67–77). New York, NY, USA: Association for Computing Machinery.\nZhang, Y., Reynolds, M., Lugmayr, A., Damjanov, K., & Hassan, G. M. (2022). A visual data storytelling framework. Informatics, 9, http://dx.doi.org/10.3390/\ninformatics9040073.\nZimmerman, B. J., & Schunk, D. H. (2011). Self-regulated learning and performance: An introduction and an overview. Handbook of Self-Regulation of Learning\nand Performance, 15–26.\nZohny, H., McMillan, J., & King, M. (2023). Ethics of generative AI. Journal of Medical Ethics, 49(2), 79–80.\nComputers & Education 234 (2025) 105322\n24', NULL, '2026-09-08 19:53:02', '2026-09-08 21:01:51', '2025-Yan-C&E-The effects of generative AI agents and scaffolding on enhancing.pdf', 'project_1018/raw/2025-Yan-C&E-The effects of generative AI agents and scaffolding on enhancing.pdf', 'application/pdf', 4677684, 'fc78e9ad69c979f45396a071ea6d3e92b7d4d1efcedbc847bb3a33b754c7ae54', 'fc78e9ad69c979f45396a071ea6d3e92b7d4d1efcedbc847bb3a33b754c7ae54', NULL, 'pending', NULL);
INSERT INTO `research_resource` VALUES (11, 1, 1018, 'UPLOAD', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'CARD_READY', 'Contents lists available at ScienceDirect\nComputers & Education\njournal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/compedu\nInvestigating the effects of an LLM-based Socratic conversational\nagent on students’ academic performance and reflective thinking in\nhigher educationI\nLinjin Xi a\n,∗, Yi Zhang a\n,∗, Qiyun Wang b\na Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education, Central China Normal University, 152 Luoyu Avenue, Wuhan, China\nb National Institute of Education, Nanyang Technological University, 50 Nanyang Avenue, Singapore, Singapore\nA R T I C L E I N F O\nKeywords:\nEducational agent\nInteractive learning environments\nPostsecondary education\nTeaching/learning strategies\n21st century skills\nA B S T R A C T\nThe rapid advancement of GenAI has created unprecedented opportunities to enhance student\nlearning through large language models (LLMs). While LLM-based conversational agents have\ndemonstrated potential in improving accessibility and instructional support, evidence of their\nimpact on higher-order thinking remains limited, especially within the context of Chinese higher\neducation. This study introduces the Socratic Intelligent Conversational Agent (S-ICA), which\nintegrates Socratic questioning strategies with semantic generation mechanisms to promote\nstudent learning and reflective thinking in disciplinary contexts. A total of 94 university students\nfrom China participated in the study, with random assignment to an experimental group (EG)\nusing the S-ICA and a control group (CG) using a non-Socratic conversational agent (nS-\nICA). The results showed that the EG outperformed the CG in both academic achievement\nand reflective thinking, particularly in the dimensions of ‘‘reflection’’ and ‘‘critical reflection’’.\nCognitive network analysis revealed that the EG students activated more advanced reflective\npathways, linking understanding, reflection, and critical reflection processes. Although no\nsignificant differences were found in learning motivation between the groups, interviews with\nstudents indicated that the S-ICA facilitated more effective learning and deeper reflective\nengagement. These findings contribute to research on integrating classical pedagogical strategies\ninto GenAI-based systems, offering insights into how such technologies can foster higher-order\nthinking and guide the design of future collaborative human–AI learning systems.\n1. Introduction\nReflective thinking, as a critical higher-order thinking ability, has been widely recognized as fundamental for solving complex\nproblems, fostering deep learning, and promoting innovation (Bauer, 1991; Dewey, 1922). It involves an active and sustained process\nthrough which individuals critically examine and evaluate their own experiences and beliefs, allowing cognitive restructuring and\nknowledge reconstruction (Schön, 1995). An increasing emphasis within higher education highlights the importance of cultivating\nstudents’ ability to reflect to better prepare them for increasingly dynamic and uncertain environments (Harvey et al., 2016).\nEmpirical evidence has shown that learners with well-developed reflective thinking skills achieve deeper conceptual understanding,\ngreater transfer, and improved problem solving in novel contexts (Askell-Williams et al., 2007). To this end, diverse pedagogical\nI This article is part of a Special issue entitled: ‘GenAI enhanced learning’ published in Computers & Education.\n∗ Corresponding authors.\nE-mail addresses: xilinjin@mails.ccnu.edu.cn (L. Xi), zhangyi@mails.ccnu.edu.cn (Y. Zhang), qiyun.wang@nie.edu.sg (Q. Wang).\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.compedu.2025.105494\nReceived 2 July 2025; Received in revised form 27 October 2025; Accepted 31 October 2025\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\nAvailable online 3 November 2025\n0360-1315/© 2025 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.\n\nL. Xi et al.\nstrategies, such as dialogic classroom discourse (Cui & Teo, 2023), reflective journals or blogs (Chu et al., 2012), and collaborative\ngroup work (Lee et al., 2016)—have been employed to cultivate students’ reflective awareness and critical dispositions (Cloude\net al., 2021). Nevertheless, while digital technologies—and more recently advanced GenAI systems—offer promising opportunities\nto scale and scaffold reflective practices, their systematic integration into authentic classroom settings remains limited.\nRecent advances in GenAI, particularly large language models (LLMs), offer new opportunities to address educational challenges\n(Yan et al., 2024; Zirar, 2023). Intelligent conversational agents (ICAs powered by LLM) can produce fluent and contextually\nrelevant dialogue, provide immediate feedback, and deliver personalized and adaptive learning support (Kasneci et al., 2023).\nEmpirical studies have reported encouraging results, showing that such agents can improve conceptual understanding, problem\nsolving abilities, and knowledge transfer (Wang et al., 2025). (Liu et al., 2024). However, concerns have also emerged that excessive\nreliance on AI-generated answers can reduce students’ critical reflection, self-regulation, and long-term engagement (Darvishi et al.,\n2024; Zhai et al., 2024). These concerns underscore the importance of examining not only cognitive outcomes, but also affective\ndimensions such as motivation and perceptions, areas that remain not adequately explored in authentic higher education contexts\n(Yu, 2023). Therefore, the integration of GenAI into education must address both cognitive and affective dimensions to fully\nunderstand its impact on student learning (Liu et al., 2024).\nThe integration of advanced AI technologies into the pedagogy requires careful alignment with robust instructional principles\n(Barrot, 2023; Hu et al., 2025). Researchers are increasingly focusing on the fact that learners should be guided through exploratory\nquestioning and scaffolded instructions, rather than provided with ready-made solutions (Borchers et al., 2025). Among such\napproaches, the Socratic method, rooted in probing questions that encourage learners to articulate reasoning, justify claims, and\nconfront inconsistencies, has long been recognized as effective for cultivating critical and reflective thinking (Hsu et al., 2022).\nRecent work has begun to explore how Socratic strategies can be implemented through agents powered by LLM. For example, Kuo\net al. (2026) documented the application of the TALPer system in fifth-grade mathematics education, where it employed the Socratic\ndialogue method and carefully designed questioning strategies to foster reflective thinking and enhance mathematical understanding\namong students. However, most studies have focused on knowledge acquisition, with limited attention to how Socratic agents foster\nhigher-order cognitive processes such as reflection and metacognitive awareness. Moreover, while Socratic dialogue was designed\nfor one-to-one interactions, adapting it to group settings is essential for ecological validity. Although group dynamics may pose\nchallenges like unequal participation, they also facilitate collaborative reflection and critical thinking (Fiock, 2020). Our design\nmitigated these risks by incorporating structured worksheets and turn-taking scaffolds to ensure active participation in small groups,\nthus supporting personalized and collaborative learning.\nAgainst this backdrop, the present study introduced a Socratic Intelligent Conversational Agent (S-ICA) that integrated Socratic\nquestioning strategies with LLM technologies to support inquiry-based learning in university classrooms. To rigorously evaluate its\neffectiveness, we contrasted the S-ICA with a carefully defined non-Socratic ICA (nS-ICA). Both agents operated under identical\nconditions in terms of tasks, time allocation, and interface; the only intended pedagogical difference was that the S-ICA employed\nSocratic questioning while the nS-ICA provided direct answers and clarifications without reflective prompts. We emphasize that the\nnS-ICA refers specifically to the control condition in this study and is not intended to be generalized to all non-Socratic agents. This\ndesign isolates the contribution of Socratic questioning while minimizing potential confounding.\nThe study assesses both cognitive and affective outcomes. At the cognitive level, we investigate whether the S-ICA enhances\nacademic performance and reflective thinking. At the affective level, we explore how the S-ICA influences students’ motivation and\nlearning perceptions—critical yet underexplored dimensions in AI in education. Epistemic Network Analysis (ENA) was employed\nto model the co-occurrence patterns of cognitive categories in dialogue, a widely recognized method in educational research\nfor examining learning processes and knowledge construction. By analyzing connections among reflective categories such as\nunderstanding, reflection, and critical reflection, ENA provides insights into how students’ cognitive engagement evolves during\ncollaborative tasks. In this study, we apply Epistemic Network Analysis (ENA) to reveal distinct cognitive patterns and the underlying\nmechanisms through which ICAs integrating Socratic prompting promote deeper reflective engagement, shedding light on how\nthese cognitive pathways differ between the experimental and control groups. These outcomes are critical to understanding how\npersonalized and adaptive learning support in GenAI-powered agents can promote sustained engagement and deeper learning. To\nthis end, the following research questions were addressed:\n1. Does the S-ICA approach improve learners’ academic performance compared to the nS-ICA?\n2. Does the S-ICA approach foster learners’ reflective thinking compared to the nS-ICA?\n3. Does the S-ICA approach enhance learners’ learning motivation compared to the nS-ICA?\n4. How do students perceive their learning experience when interacting with the two types of ICAs?\n2. Literature review\n2.1. Reflective thinking\nReflective thinking is defined as the capacity to actively and consciously analyze, evaluate, and integrate one’s experiences,\nknowledge, and cognitive processes (Boyd & Fales, 1983; Paul, 2013). From different theoretical perspectives, reflective thinking\nhas been conceptualized as comprising multiple levels and dimensions. For example, Mezirow (1998) and Kember (2000) categorized\nreflective thinking into four progressive stages: habitual action, understanding, reflection, and critical reflection. Among these four\nstages, the first two emphasize the construction of meaning from experience by learners and are considered non-reflective behaviors,\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n2\n\nL. Xi et al.\nwhereas the latter two involve the reevaluation of actions or ideas in response to problems or situations and are regarded as reflective\nbehaviors. Furthermore, Mezirow (1997) defined two hierarchical levels of reflective action, in which the higher and more essential\nlevel—critical reflection—serves as the foundation for deep learning and the assessment of success by learners.\nReflective thinking has been widely recognized in higher education as a key psychological mechanism that promotes deep\nlearning, self-directed learning, and complex problem solving (Ghanizadeh, 2017). Learners who possess reflective thinking skills are\nable to regulate their own learning and recognize the gap between what they know and what they need to know. Reflection plays a\nvital role in the learning process, helping learners improve academic achievement (Menekse, 2020), improve metacognitive abilities\n(Nobutoshi, 2023), and develop cross-context transfer capabilities. According to research on higher-order thinking, educational\napproaches that foster reflective thinking can increase student engagement and participation in flipped learning designs (Chen\net al., 2019).\nAmong current instructional media, GenAI technologies are regarded as having new potential to support reflective learning due\nto their semantic interactivity and language expression capabilities. Albert and Li (2023) noted that AI instructional agents such\nas ChatGPT can provide immediate feedback, guide questions, and suggestions for the organization of ideas in contexts where\nteacher–student interaction is limited, thus promoting reflective behaviors. However, current research on AI-supported reflection\nhas primarily focused on technological feasibility and user acceptance, with limited exploration of the underlying mechanisms by\nwhich such tools contribute to the development of higher-order thinking.\n2.2. Learning outcomes of university students\nAcademic performance and learning motivation are critical indicators for evaluating students’ learning outcomes and their\nsustained engagement. Academic performance reflects the depth of knowledge acquisition of students, the proficiency of their\nskills and the level of their problem-solving abilities (Alexandre & Enslin, 2017). Learning motivation directly influences the\nability of learners to self-regulation, their investment in learning activities, and persistence over time (Schunk & Zimmerman,\n2012). In university learning environments, particularly, students’ ability to engage in self-directed learning and maintain long-term\nmotivation is essential for academic success.\nHowever, with the widespread adoption of GenAI in education, significant changes have emerged in academic performance\nand learning motivation. On the one hand, GenAI tools offer immediate feedback, personalized learning pathways, and adaptive\nresources, which have been shown to improve academic performance and motivation (Derakhshan, 2025; Gao et al., 2024). For\nexample, ICAs have helped students clarify conceptual misunderstandings in real time, fostering deeper comprehension and mastery\nof knowledge (Zhai & Wibowo, 2023). On the other hand, excessive reliance on AI-generated answers can lead to overdependence,\ndiminishing critical thinking, exploration, and potentially undermining intrinsic motivation (Han, 2026).\nMoreover, understanding students’ learning perceptions—their attitudes and beliefs about GenAI tools, is crucial to evaluating\nthe effectiveness of these technologies. These perceptions are strongly influenced by the degree of personalization and adaptivity\noffered by the learning tool. Studies have shown that students’ perceptions of AI’s role significantly affect their engagement and\nmotivation. Positive perceptions, in which students view AI as a supportive learning tool, increase motivation and foster engagement\n(Liu et al., 2024). In contrast, students who perceive AI as a crutch may feel less confident in their abilities, reducing motivation\nand engagement in the learning process. Therefore, further exploration of learning perceptions and motivation is essential, as these\nfactors influence the sustainability of AI-supported learning and its impact on higher-order thinking skills (Yu, 2023).\n2.3. Socratic questioning strategy\nSocratic questioning is a pedagogical philosophy and instructional strategy centered on inquiry, emphasizing cognitive stim-\nulation and intellectual challenge. It encourages learners to actively construct knowledge and engage in self-reflection through\na process of progressive questioning (Hu, 2023). At its core, it is a dialogic method of cognitive training (Clark & Egan, 2015),\ndesigned to provoke learners to question, examine, and ultimately reconstruct their existing beliefs. The key characteristics of\nSocratic questioning include open inquiry, progressive exploration, and the educator’s role as a facilitator of thought, elements\nthat make it particularly effective for fostering higher-order thinking (Dalim et al., 2022).\nEmpirical studies have shown the efficacy of Socratic questioning in improving students’ reflective awareness and critical thinking\nskills. For example, Hsu et al. (2022) found that structured Socratic reflection prompts significantly enhanced elementary school\nstudents’ critical thinking skills. In an experimental study with secondary school students, Yang and Chung (2009) reported notable\nimprovements in critical reasoning and self-expression after sustained Socratic dialogue sessions. Beyond the sciences, this method\nhas gained traction in fields such as English writing instruction, teacher education, and medical ethics, where it facilitates deeper\nreflection on beliefs, judgments, and actions within authentic learning contexts (Dinkins & Cangelosi, 2019; Ho et al., 2023).\nThe pedagogical value of Socratic questioning has also been amplified through its integration with digital technologies. For\nexample, Chang, Hung, and Yang (2024) developed an online writing platform incorporating Socratic prompts, which significantly\nincreased students’ frequency of reflection and depth of written output. In a controlled experiment, Chang, Hung, and Yang (2024)\ndemonstrated that an AI-based dialogue system employing Socratic questioning outperformed traditional feedback mechanisms\nin supporting students’ reflective writing. Despite its promise, the practical implementation of Socratic questioning presents\nchallenges. Teachers must have strong questioning skills and classroom management skills to facilitate deep and flexible thinking.\nSimultaneously, learners may experience cognitive overload, leading to disengagement or shallow responses. As a result, optimizing\nthe pacing of questions, personalizing prompts, and sustaining cognitive engagement through technological mediation have become\ncritical areas for future research on integrating Socratic strategies into contemporary instructional design.\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n3\n\nL. Xi et al.\n2.4. LLM-supported conversational agents\nThe rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) in recent years has garnered significant attention for their application\nin educational contexts (Chang et al., 2024). As a hallmark of GenAI, LLMs possess advanced semantic comprehension and natural\nlanguage generation capabilities, enabling their integration into intelligent educational agents that offer adaptive instructional\nsupport, automated feedback, and personalized learning pathways (Hu et al., 2024). By leveraging the dialogic capabilities of LLMs,\nthese agents can engage in human-like interactions with learners, which are crucial to fostering personalized learning experiences\n(Zheng et al., 2025).\nEmerging research has demonstrated the efficacy of LLM-driven educational agents in tasks such as language writing, problem\nsolving, and critical thinking development (Liebenow et al., 2025; Razafinirina et al., 2024). However, the effectiveness of LLM-\npowered agents in fostering higher-order cognitive skills and strategic learning remains contested. Some studies highlight the\nadvantages of AI-generated feedback in helping learners identify cognitive gaps and misconceptions (Naseer & Khawaja, 2025).\nHowever, concerns have been raised that excessive reliance on such agents can reduce cognitive effort and hinder self-regulation\nengagement, particularly in open tasks, where learners could substitute AI-generated responses for deeper cognitive processing (Zhai\net al., 2024).\nAlthough some evidence suggests that agents driven by LLM can improve cognitive engagement by providing continuous feedback\nand heuristic prompts (Liebenow et al., 2025), the mechanisms through which they influence multidimensional learning outcomes,\nsuch as motivation, self-regulation, and perceptions of learning, remain underexplored. This gap underscores the need for agent\ndesigns that effectively integrate LLM capabilities with dynamic pedagogical principles, balancing guidance, dialogic feedback, and\nreflective stimulation within a multilevel cognitive support framework. Notably, while much of the research on conversational agents\nhas focused on traditional, non-LLM models, these agents often lack the personalized adaptability that LLMs can provide. Non-LLM\nsystems, while valuable for task-specific feedback, are generally less flexible in tailoring interactions to learners’ evolving needs,\nwhich may limit their capacity to foster long-term cognitive and affective engagement.\nIn response, the present study introduces the S-ICA, a hybrid design that incorporates LLM capabilities within the structured\nscaffolding of the Socratic method. This approach aims to enhance not only cognitive outcomes but also affective dimensions such as\nmotivation and learning perceptions, which are critical but often underexplored in current GenAI-enhanced learning environments.\n3. LLM-based S-ICA learning approach\n3.1. Architecture of the S-ICA\nIn this study, we developed an LLM-driven S-ICA to assist students in evaluating and refining their experimental research\nproposals. The system architecture is depicted in Fig. 1. Instructors can provide students with learning resources and materials, such\nas instructional documents on experimental research design, task instructions, question templates, and sample proposals, which are\nintegrated into the agent’s knowledge database for access on demand. Students interact with the S-ICA to evaluate and optimize their\noriginal research proposals. This interaction takes place through a web browser, ensuring compatibility on both desktop and mobile\ndevices, without the need for a dedicated mobile app. They input various components of their proposals, such as research questions,\nhypothesis statements and experimental methods, into the user interface. These inputs are then processed by the back-end system,\nwhere the large language model generates contextually appropriate feedback to guide students in refining their design rationale.\nThe S-ICA employs predefined prompts and dialogue structures, with adaptive questioning based on students’ input. This enables a\npersonalized learning experience, as feedback and questions evolve according to the progress and needs of the learner. In this way,\nthe system adapts to the characteristics of individual students, providing tailored guidance that reflects their current understanding\nand challenges.\nUser Interface. Fig. 2 illustrated the main interaction platform of the system: the Doubao web interface. Built on Doubao, a\nByteDance-affiliated LLM platform, the S-ICA interface embeds Socratic dialogue and questioning strategies at its core. The system\nsupports text input and multi-turn interaction, enabling students to engage in continuous reflection and iterative improvement\nduring the experimental proposal evaluation task. Although the system also supports voice input, all student interactions in this\nstudy were conducted via text input through a web browser. After logging in to Doubao, students submit various components of\ntheir proposals, such as research questions, hypotheses, and experimental methods, via text input. The system, guided by predefined\nprompts and dialogue structures, generates real-time feedback, including counter-questions, clarification requests, and progressive\ninquiries. These adaptive prompts encourage students to reflect on the logical coherence and completeness of their proposals, guiding\nthem through an iterative process of revision and refinement. Additionally, S-ICA tracks and stores the complete dialogue history,\nallowing students to review previous exchanges at any time, which supports ongoing revisions and reflective analysis.\nCore Functional Modules. The S-ICA system was developed with a strong pedagogical orientation, integrating the Socratic\nmethod with LLM technology to construct an intelligent conversational agent with cognitive scaffolding capabilities. It incorporates\nfour core functional modules: input comprehension, dialogue generation, dynamic feedback, and structural memory to support the\nongoing reflection and knowledge construction during the evaluation of experimental research designs.\n(1) input comprehension module: this module processes text input from students and performs semantic parsing, intent recognition,\nand a preliminary assessment of student cognitive level. For example, when a student inputs a research question, the system identifies\nwhether it is descriptive (‘‘What is. . . ?’’) or causal (‘‘Why/How does...?’’) and uses this classification to determine the stage of inquiry.\nSuch classification provides the foundation for the generation of subsequent prompts. The design of this module follows adaptive\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n4\n\nL. Xi et al.\nFig. 1. System architecture of S-ICA.\nFig. 2. Overview of the Doubao Web Interface for S-ICA.\nlearning principles, ensuring that the prompts are matched to the current level of reasoning of the students, thus preventing both\noversimplification and cognitive overload.\n(2) dialogue generation module: this module generates reflective and context-specific questions embedded within a structured\nprompting framework. Drawing on Socratic principles (Paul & Elder, 2008), it employs techniques such as counterquestions,\nclarification, and progressive inquiry to elicit deeper reasoning. For example, if a student proposes ‘‘The impact of gamified teaching\nmodels on primary school students’ academic achievement’’, the system may respond: ‘‘How will you operationalize ‘academic achievement’\nin measurable terms?’’ or ‘‘What mechanism links gamification to learning outcomes?’’. By advancing learners from broad ideas to more\nrigorous formulations, this module scaffolds critical reflection and methodological precision.\n(3) dynamic feedback module: this module adapts the depth and specificity of system responses based on the complexity and\nprecision of the student’s input. A novice level statement such as ‘‘The variable is classroom participation’’ triggers concrete guidance\n(e.g., ‘‘How will participation be measured or observed in your study?’’). In contrast, when a student provides a more elaborate proposal,\nthe system escalates the investigation by prompting consideration of alternative explanations or methodological limitations. This\nensures differentiated scaffolding that matches the developmental stages of the students, fostering both engagement and higher-order\nthinking.\n(4) structural memory module: this module maintains dialogue history and semantic anchors across interactions. For example, if\na student initially defines ‘‘academic achievement’’ as test performance, the system recalls this definition in subsequent turns and\nprompts: ‘‘Does your method of data collection remain consistent with your earlier definition of achievement?’’. By referencing prior input,\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n5\n\nL. Xi et al.\nthe system maintains contextual coherence and promotes iterative refinement. This design supports reflective practice by ensuring\ncontinuity and alignment throughout the inquiry process.\nPrompt Engineering Design. The prompting mechanism operationalizes the dialogue generation and dynamic feedback modules\nthrough a workflow-based design. Each workflow node corresponds to a component of the research design task (e.g., research\nquestions, hypotheses, variables, methods, etc.). When learner input contains specific trigger keywords (e.g., ‘‘hypothesis’’, ‘‘sample\nsize’’, ‘‘data collection’’), the system automatically activates the corresponding workflow and retrieves a tailored prompt template.\nFor example, in the hypothesis formulation stage, an input containing causal terms such as ‘‘effect’’ or ‘‘impact’’ triggers prompts\nlike: ‘‘What mechanism links your independent and dependent variables?’’. Similarly, in the methods stage, references to ‘‘sample size’’\nactivate prompts such as: ‘‘How will you justify the adequacy of your chosen sample for detecting significant effects?’’. These templates\nare pre-configured but dynamically adapted in real time to align with the student’s context and previous dialogue history.\nThe design was iteratively refined through pilot testing. Students interacted with prototype versions of the system and provided\nfeedback on prompt clarity, difficulty, and relevance. Their feedback led to two key revisions: (1) broadening the knowledge\nbase of the system and integrating a controlled web search interface to expand the scope of responses, and (2) reorganizing the\nprompt library into a matrix structure. The tiered structure progresses from clarification to justification (e.g., ‘‘Why is gamification\nan appropriate intervention for this population?’’ ), and finally to refinement (e.g., ‘‘How will you control for differences in prior\nknowledge?’’).\nIn addition to workflow rules, each prompt template encodes explicit skills and constraints. For example, skills include guiding\nthe student to articulate their reasoning, initiating clarification before advancing the dialogue, and encouraging reflection rather\nthan passive acceptance. Constraints ensure pedagogical integrity: all prompts must follow the ‘‘Thinking question:’’ format, and\nthe system is prohibited from directly providing answers to students. These specifications guarantee that the interaction remains\ninquiry-driven, aligns with Socratic principles, and fosters adaptive learning through structured, yet open-ended questioning.\n3.2. S-ICA learning approach\n3.2.1. Application context\nThe instructional practice in this study was designed as an extracurricular self-directed learning activity that extended the formal\ncurriculum on research design theory and methodology. After completing foundational coursework, second-year undergraduates\nmajoring in Educational Technology engaged in a six-week task to collaboratively construct a comprehensive experimental research\nproposal. The task was structured into weekly stages, each targeting a specific subtask: (1) clarifying the research question, (2)\nformulating hypotheses, (3) identifying variables, (4) designing implementation procedures and (5) evaluating the overall design.\nThe students were organized into teams of five to six, each member responsible for contributing to the group proposal. To ensure\nequity between conditions, all students followed identical task instructions, used the same learning materials, and accessed the\nagents through a common institutional portal and browser-based interface.\nAgent conditions and interaction characteristics. To evaluate the impact of ICAs on academic performance, reflective thinking and\nlearning motivation, students were randomly assigned to one of two conditions: an experimental group (EG) and a control group\n(CG). Both groups received identical task requirements, time allocations, materials, and user interface. The sole intended pedagogical\ndifference concern the agent’s feedback strategy.\n• S-ICA (EG). The S-ICA implemented a Socratic questioning strategy: it prioritized clarification, justification, and refine-\nment through reciprocal prompts rather than supplying immediate answers. Typical turns included brief counter-questions\n(e.g., ‘What assumptions underlie this hypothesis’, ‘How would you operationalize this variable in measurable terms’) that\npressed students to articulate reasoning, examine coherence, and iteratively improve design decisions.\n• nS-ICA (CG). The nS-ICA provided direct, task-aligned feedback and procedural clarifications without reflective or recursive\nprompts. Its turns focused on definitional guidance, checklists, and examples relevant to the submitted content (e.g. ‘This\nvariable can be measured via standardized test scores’ ‘A feasible data collection procedure is a classroom survey with informed\nconsent’), without follow-up probing, metacognitive cues, or dialogic escalation. In other words, the nS-ICA could provide\npractical cognitive, behavioral, or procedural guidance, but it deliberately did not engage in Socratic inquiry.\nModel platforms and parity controls. The two agents were deployed with different LLM backends (Doubao for the S-ICA and\nERNIE for the nS-ICA), due to institutional access constraints and token-based interface compatibility, rather than deliberate\npedagogical differentiation. Despite this back-end divergence, both agents were delivered through an identical COZE-based web\nplatform interface, ensuring uniformity in access mode, operational procedures, and user experience. Parity controls were also\napplied by aligning the task design, learning materials, and usage protocols between conditions. Consequently, the only intended\nexperimental manipulation concerns the feedback strategy, thus strengthening the attribution of any observed group differences to\nthe presence or absence of Socratic questioning, rather than to disparities in interface or system access.\nPrior to the main study, the S-ICA was pilot-tested in two stages. First, five master’s and doctoral-level students in Educational\nTechnology reviewed the system to evaluate the clarity and consistency of Socratic prompts, leading to iterative refinements of\nprompt wording and sequencing. Second, one undergraduate team engaged in a pre-class trial session, during which real-time\nfeedback was collected and used to further optimize the system’s usability and alignment with classroom dynamics. This multi-stage\npilot process ensured that the implemented version of the S-ICA was both pedagogically valid and technically robust.\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n6\n\nL. Xi et al.\nFig. 3. Inquiry-based learning framework Using S-ICA.\nFig. 4. Complete Task 1 of the orientation phase.\n3.2.2. Learning activities\nPedaste et al. (2015) propose an inquiry-based learning (IBL) framework comprising five general phases: Orientation, Concep-\ntualization, Investigation, Conclusion, and Discussion. This cycle provides learners with a structured and scaffolded approach to\ninquiry activities and has been shown to support the development of complex inquiry-driven learning processes, as well as inform\nthe design of instructional guidance that optimizes learning efficiency. Following the IBL framework, students begin the inquiry\nprocess with Orientation, during which they not only familiarize themselves with the topic to be explored but also gain a deeper\nunderstanding of the problem that needs to be addressed. In this study, a scaffolded inquiry-based learning model was adopted\nfor the self-practice phase. The experimental group used the S-ICA to facilitate each inquiry stage, enabling students to iteratively\nrefine and revise their experimental proposals. Given that the students did not have enough time to conduct actual experiments,\ncollect data, and perform data analysis, the original ‘‘Conclusion and Data Interpretation’’ phase was omitted. Instead, the student\nteams completed only the design of their experimental research proposals. The learning model, grounded in the IBL framework and\nstandard research design steps, is illustrated in Fig. 3.\n(1) Orientation phase During orientation, the students’ primary task was to define their experimental research topic. Initially,\nthey identified an educational phenomenon or behavioral issue of interest and tentatively delineated the scope and population of\ntheir experiment. The students then progressively narrowed down a broad problem into a specific and well-defined research topic.\nThis focusing process helped establish a clear starting point for research. The detailed workflow for this phase is shown in Fig. 4.\n(2) Conceptualization phase In conceptualization, the students clarified their research question, constructed testable hypotheses,\nand identified key variables for their chosen topic. This stage began with an in-depth analysis of the research objective, gradually\ntranslating it into operational research questions. Based on this foundation, students were expected to propose hypotheses supported\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n7\n\nL. Xi et al.\nFig. 5. Complete Task 2 of the conceptualization phase.\nFig. 6. Complete Task 3 of the investigation phase.\nby relevant literature and explicitly define independent, dependent, and control variables. Thus, conceptualization provided the\ntheoretical and logical foundations for the subsequent design and implementation of research. The specific steps for this phase are\nshown in Fig. 5.\n(3) Investigation phase\nDuring investigation, the students developed a concrete experimental design based on their previously articulated research\nquestions and hypotheses. They specified procedural details, including experimental protocols and workflows, with an emphasis\non operational feasibility. Detailed sub-steps for this phase appear in Fig. 6.\n(4) Discussion phase\nIn the discussion phase, students engaged in a deep dialogue with the S-ICA to evaluate and refine their experimental research\nproposals. Through iterative questions about their chosen topic, variable operationalization, hypothesis validity, and experimental\nprocedures, the agent guided the students to uncover logical gaps and identify opportunities for improvement. This stage prioritized\nthe cultivation of critical and reflective thinking, encouraging students to self-correct and enhance their proposals based on agent\nprompts. The key distinction between the EG and CG during this phase was the type of conversational tool used: the EG interacted\nwith an agent that embedded Socratic questioning strategies to foster deeper reflection, whereas the CG used a generic conversational\nagent without specialized research-design guidance. Fig. 7 highlights this contrast in agent functionality and pedagogical support.\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n8\n\nL. Xi et al.\nFig. 7. Using S-ICA approach to complete task 4 of the discussion phase and some dialogue.\n4. Method\n4.1. Research background and participants\nThis study was implemented during the fall semester of 2024 in a university of teachers in central China. Participants were\nsecond-year undergraduates majoring in Educational Technology who were enrolled in the required course Research Methods in\nEducational Technology. The course lasted 16 weeks and consisted of weekly lecture sessions and practical training sessions. The\ninstruction covered a variety of topics, including research design, literature review, survey and experimental methodologies, and\ndata analysis, ensuring that the students had a sufficient foundation to participate in the experimental task designed for this study. A\ntotal of 94 students participated. Forty-eight students (28 female; mean age = 19.00 ± 0.22 years) were assigned to the experimental\ngroup, the remaining 46 students (25 female; mean age = 19.00 ± 0.15 years) formed the control group. In both conditions, the\nagents were accessed through the same institutional portal and browser interface to minimize extraneous differences; the intended\npedagogical contrast only concerned the feedback strategy, i.e., Socratic questioning versus direct clarifications.\nAll participants were randomly assigned to 16 subgroups of five to six students each. Within these subgroups, students\ncollaborated to design and refine an experimental research proposal over a six-week intervention period. The use of group-based\ntutoring reflected authentic classroom practices in the course and enabled manageable implementation across a relatively large\ncohort. However, group work necessarily involves the potential interdependence of data between students within the same subgroup.\nTo address this issue, statistical analyzes explicitly incorporated group membership as a clustering factor and employed models that\naccount for nested data structures, thereby mitigating risks of inflated significance due to non-independence.\nThe intervention period lasted six weeks, during which the students iteratively developed their research proposals in weekly\nstages. Although relatively brief in duration, the design was intentionally aligned with the temporal constraints of the semester\nschedule and the scope of the course project. As such, the findings are interpreted as evidence of short-term impacts within a\ndefined academic context, and the limitations of long-term transferability and generalizability are acknowledged in the discussion.\nThroughout the study, both groups were instructed by the same professor, who had over 20 years of teaching experience, and\nfollowed an identical syllabus to ensure the equivalence of the instructional context. All participants had prior experience using\nGenAI tools. Before starting the study, ethical approval was obtained from the university’s Institutional Review Board.\n4.2. Experimental procedure\nFig. 8 illustrates the overall experimental procedure. Oriented to theoretical instruction and proposal design for experimental\nresearch methods, we implemented a quasi-experimental intervention lasting six weeks. Each week consisted of two 45 min theory\nsessions and two 45 min practical sessions. The intervention was divided into three phases: a pre-test phase, an intervention phase\nusing ICAs, and a post-test phase.\nPhase 1 (Pretest): All 94 students completed the pre-test measures, which included questionnaires that assessed reflective\nthinking and learning motivation, as well as a knowledge pre-test on experimental research methods (30 min). Independent samples\nt-tests confirmed that, before the test, there were no significant differences between the experimental and control groups in reflective\nthinking (t = 0.45, 𝑝 = 0.65), learning motivation (t = 0.30, 𝑝 = 0.76), or knowledge test scores (t = 0.26, 𝑝 = 0.795 > 0.01).\nPhase 2 (Intervention): Both groups participated in weekly theoretical lectures and self-practice sessions. During theory lectures\n(90 min per week), students in both groups attended face-to-face instruction in a smart classroom, where they were taught the\nfundamental principles, core concepts, and typical methodologies of experimental research. In each self-practice session, the students\nwere required to use the assigned ICAs to evaluate and progressively refine an experimental research design proposal.\nBefore starting the task, all participants received a 15 min training session that provided unified instruction on system operation,\ntask template structure, and the protocol to initiate interaction. Specifically, every student was instructed to start by entering the\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n9\n\nL. Xi et al.\nFig. 8. The experimental procedure.\nstandardized initiating prompt: I plan to conduct an experimental study. The topic is.... This ensured that any subsequent differences\nin interaction were arose solely from the agents’ response mechanisms rather than variations in task initiation or user familiarity.\nBoth agents were accessed through the same coze-based web interface to ensure parity of user experience, but they were\ninstantiated in different back-end models. The S-ICA (experimental group), deployed in Doubao, embedded a structured prompt-\nengineering framework that parsed learner input and generated Socratic-style cognitively scaffolded follow-up questions to stimulate\nreflection and reasoning. In contrast, nS-ICA (control group), instantiated in ERNIE but delivered through the identical interface,\nprovided direct and surface-level responses without recursive probing or structured pedagogical scaffolding. Thus, the only\nexperimental manipulation was related to the feedback strategy, isolating the effect of Socratic questioning on learning outcomes.\nPhase 3 (Posttest): At the end of the intervention, all participants completed a post-test (30 min) comprising the same reflective\nthinking and motivation for the learning questionnaires, as well as a knowledge test, if applicable. In addition, each subgroup’s\nexperimental research proposal was coded and rated according to a standardized rubric. We also collected dialogue logs between\nlearners and their respective agents and conducted semi-structured interviews with selected students to gain deeper insight into\ntheir learning experiences.\n4.3. Measurement instruments\nThe instruments used in this study comprised pre- and post-test knowledge assessments, a learning motivation questionnaire,\nand a reflective thinking questionnaire. In addition to these standardized measures, all dialogue data were derived exclusively from\ntext-based interactions, thereby eliminating variability due to input modality, and were subsequently coded for cognitive network\nanalysis. Semi-structured interviews were also conducted to provide qualitative insights into participants’ learning experiences.\nFinally, the academic performance of the students was evaluated through the quality of their completed experimental research\nproposals.\n4.3.1. Academic performance\nWithin the inquiry-based learning process, a student’s mastery of conceptual knowledge reflects both the depth of their\nunderstanding and the development of research competencies. Consequently, conceptual knowledge scores were chosen as a\nkey indicator of student academic performance. The pretest and posttest instruments were designed around the core content of\nexperimental research methods in educational technology, covering essential concepts such as the logic of experimental design,\nvariable types and operational definitions, random assignment procedures, validity and reliability issues in experimental research,\nand basic statistical analysis techniques. Both tests were co-developed by two experts with extensive experience in educational\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n10\n\nL. Xi et al.\ntechnology research and teaching. They were then reviewed for content validity by one full professor and one associate professor;\ntheir evaluation confirmed that the tests demonstrated strong validity.\nIn addition, the quality of the experimental research proposal of each group served as an indicator of academic performance and\nresearch capability; it was therefore considered a critical student outcome. To evaluate the quality of the proposal, this study adapted\nthe research proposal scoring rubric developed by Zhang et al. (2019), which has been widely used in the instruction in educational\ntechnology and research methods to assess students’ design skills and the quality of academic writing. In line with the context of the\ncourse, the rubric was modified to encompass six core dimensions (see Appendix 7): (1) Research topic, (2) Research question, (3)\nTheoretical rationale, (4) Research content, (5) Research methodology and (6) Research procedures. Each dimension was rated using\na five-level scale ranging from 2 to 10 points, with higher scores indicating better performance. Specifically, ratings were assigned in\nincrements of two points (2 = poor, 4 = needs improvement, 6 = satisfactory, 8 = good and 10 = excellent). Higher scores indicate\nhigher quality. The total possible score for each proposal was 60 points. The rubric was reviewed by the course instructor and an\nadditional expert in educational technology, both of whom confirmed its appropriateness for evaluating the quality of students’\nresearch designs. To ensure objectivity, two teaching assistants independently scored each proposal. Inter-rater reliability, assessed\nby Cohen’s kappa (𝜅 = 0.82), indicated substantial agreement between raters (Cohen, 1988; Landis & Koch, 1977).\n4.3.2. Reflective thinking\nTo assess the reflective thinking ability of the students, we employed a scale adapted from Kember et al. (1999), which measures\nfour dimensions: Habitual Action, Understanding, Reflection, and Critical Reflection. Each dimension comprises four elements, for a\ntotal of 16 elements. To suit the context and population of this study, the wording of the items was revised as needed. The validity of\nthe content was reviewed by two experts in educational technology, who confirmed the suitability of the scale to evaluate reflective\nthinking in the context of experimental research design. For example, one element reads: ‘When I encounter a solution that appears\ncorrect, I reflect on my original viewpoint’. Each item uses a five-point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree, 5 = strongly agree).\nIn this study, the Cronbach’s 𝛼 values for the four subscales, Habitual Action, Understanding, Reflection, and Critical Reflection,\nwere 0.82, 0.88, 0.86, and 0.89, respectively. The overall Cronbach’s 𝛼 for the reflective thinking scale was 0.86, indicating strong\ninternal consistency.\n4.3.3. Learning motivation\nTo measure students learning motivation, we use the instrument developed by Chen et al. (2015), which is a revision of the\nMotivated Strategies for Learning Questionnaire (MSLQ; Pintrich et al., 1993). The scale consists of six items on two subscales:\nIntrinsic Motivation (three items; e.g., ‘‘I enjoy learning challenging knowledge and materials because it allows me to discover new\nthings’’) and Extrinsic Motivation (three items; e.g., ‘‘Achieving good grades in experimental research methods is a major source\nof satisfaction for me’’). All items were rated on a five-point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree, 5 = strongly agree), with higher\nscores reflecting stronger motivation. In the present study, the scale demonstrated acceptable internal consistency, with Cronbach’s\n𝛼 = 0 .81. The validity of the content was reviewed by two domain experts, who confirmed its suitability for use in the context of\nthe instruction of experimental research methods.\n4.3.4. Interview questions\nIn addition, a semi-structured interview protocol was developed to capture students’ experiences and perceptions of using the\nintelligent conversational agent. The interview questions were adapted from Hwang et al. (2009) and included five specific questions.\nThe specific interview outline is provided below. Sixteen students — eight from the experimental group (labeled S1–S8) and eight\nfrom the control group (labeled C1–C8) — were randomly selected for individual interviews lasting approximately 20 min each.\nAll interviews were recorded and transcribed verbatim. Two experienced researchers then performed qualitative content analysis.\nIn instances of coding disagreement, the researchers reviewed and discussed the coding until reaching consensus.\n(1) In what ways does the S-ICA/nS-ICA learning approach differ from other learning methods you have used before?\n(2) Overall, what are the main advantages of the S-ICA/nS-ICA learning approach?\n(3) What are the greatest benefits you personally gained from using the S-ICA/nS-ICA approach? What did you learn most from\nit? Please be specific.\n(4) What aspects of the S-ICA/nS-ICA learning approach do you think need improvement? Please specify.\n(5) Would you recommend the S-ICA/nS-ICA learning approach to your classmates or friends? Why or why not?\n4.4. Data collection and analysis\nAll quantitative analyses were conducted using a combination of Jamovi 2.3.24 and R 4.3.2 (with the lme4, car, and coin pack-\nages). Descriptive statistics were first computed for all outcome measures. Normality was examined using the Kolmogorov–Smirnov\nand Shapiro–Wilk tests, and homogeneity of variance was assessed with Levene’s test.\nFor the conceptual knowledge test, repeated measures were analyzed using a two-level linear mixed effects model (LMM), with\nstudents (Level 1) nested within collaborative groups (Level 2). Time (pre vs. post) and Condition (S-ICA vs. nS-ICA) were modeled as\nfixed effects and pretest scores were included as covariates where appropriate. Welch’s 𝑡 test was used as a robustness check against\nvariance heterogeneity, and ANCOVA models with HC3 robust standard errors were estimated for supplementary confirmation of\ngroup differences. For the research proposal quality, where each group submitted a single collective product (𝑛 = 8 per condition),\nmultiple robust analyses were employed due to the small sample size: Welch’s 𝑡 test, Mann–Whitney U test, and permutation tests\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n11\n\nL. Xi et al.\nTable 1\nComparison of the existing ship path planning methods.\nCode category Abbreviation Definition Example\nHabitual Action HA Routine statements about the task or\ncontext that focus solely on\ncompletion without deeper reflection.\n‘‘I followed the instructor’s steps\nexactly as required and did not think\nfurther’’.\n\nUnderstanding U Restating, explaining, or paraphrasing\na concept or information, indicating\ninitial grasp of the content.\n‘‘According to the literature, an\nexperimental design must include\nrandom assignment and a control\ngroup to ensure fairness’’.\n\nReflection R Questioning or re-examining a\nprevious viewpoint, hypothesis, or\nmethod to explore its rationale and\nlimitations.\n‘‘I’m wondering why we selected this\nparticular variable—does it truly\nrelate to the research question?’’\n\nCritical Reflection CR Evaluating underlying assumptions,\npremises, or contextual factors, or\nproposing alternative interpretations,\nreflecting higher-order critical\nthinking.\n‘‘If we don’t adequately control for\nconfounding variables, the results\ncould be influenced by external\nfactors—does this hypothesis remain\nrigorous?’’\n\nwith 10,000 iterations. Standardized effect sizes (Cohen’s 𝑑, Hedges’ 𝑔, and common-language effect size) were reported with 95%\nconfidence intervals.\nFor the reflective thinking questionnaire, ANCOVA was used to compare post-test scores across conditions while controlling for\npre-test scores. Subscale analyses were performed for Habitual Action, Understanding, Reflection, and Critical Reflection. Because\nthese were self-reported measures, the results were interpreted as the perceived levels of reflective engagement of the participants.\nFor the learning motivation questionnaire, ANCOVA was also applied with pretest motivation as a covariate.\nTo capture deeper cognitive processes, all dialogue transcripts from student-agent interactions were coded for reflective thinking\ncategories (see Table 1) (Habitual Action, Understanding, Reflection, and Critical Reflection) following the framework of Kember\net al. (2000). Two trained raters independently coded all dialogues. The interrater reliability reached 𝜅 = 0 .82 and ICC = 0.85,\nindicating strong agreement. Discrepancies were discussed and consensus was reached after three rounds of deliberation.\nThe coded data were used for Epistemic Network Analysis (ENA) to model the co-occurrence patterns of reflective categories.\nENA is a method for visualizing and analyzing relationships between categories in dialogue. The analysis was performed using\nthe online ENA tool (https://app.epistemicnetwork.org) to construct and visualize epistemic networks. The data were organized\ninto a matrix with dialogues (or group interactions) as rows and reflective thinking categories as columns. This matrix was input\ninto ENA for network construction. ENA modeled the co-occurrence patterns by calculating the frequency of category pairings,\nvisualizing the networks as graphs where nodes represented categories and edges represented co-occurrences. Group-level contrasts\nwere tested by comparing ENA dimensions (e.g., centrality, clustering, and density) using independent-sample t-tests to identify\nsignificant differences in network structures between the experimental (S-ICA) and control (nS-ICA) groups. Key parameters included\na sliding window of 5 utterances, Z-score normalization, PCA for optimal separation of categories, and a threshold of 0.1 to filter\nweak connections, ensuring focus on significant co-occurrences.\nFinally, semi-structured interview data were analyzed using thematic analysis in ATLAS.ti 25. Two researchers independently\nperformed open coding, axial coding, and theme refinement, following the procedures of Zhang and Creswell (2013). Disagreements\nwere resolved through discussion until full agreement was reached, ensuring the reliability and transparency of the qualitative\nfindings.\n5. Results\n5.1. Results on RQ1\n5.1.1. Conceptual knowledge assessment\nTable 2 reports the descriptive statistics for the conceptual knowledge test. Both groups demonstrated substantial pre-to-post\ngains. The S-ICA group (EG, 𝑛 = 48 ) improved from 𝑀 = 68 .05 (SD=1.19) to 𝑀 = 86 .44 (SD=1.04), while the nS-ICA group (CG,\n𝑛 = 46 ) rose from 𝑀 = 67 .60 (SD=1.68) to 𝑀 = 85 .17 (SD=1.90). To assess group differences, we fitted a two-level linear mixed\nmodel with students (Level 1) nested within groups (Level 2), including pre-test scores as a covariate. The results showed a robust\nmain effect of Time ( ̂𝛽 = 17 .568, 𝑆𝐸 = 0 .307, 𝑧 = 57 .23, 𝑝 < 0.001), confirming significant overall improvement. The main effect of\nCondition was not significant ( ̂𝛽 = 0.450, 𝑆𝐸 = 0.304, 𝑧 = 1.48, 𝑝 = 0.138). The Time × Condition interaction was marginal ( ̂𝛽 = 0.821,\n𝑆𝐸 = 0 .430, 𝑧 = 1 .91, 𝑝 = 0 .056, 95% CI [–0.03, 1.67]), suggesting that the S-ICA group achieved a slightly larger gain. The effect\nsize was small to moderate (Hedges’ 𝑔 = 0.35).\nAs robustness checks, we compared gain scores with Welch’s 𝑡 test, which again showed no significant differences (𝑡(70.31) = 1 .70,\n𝑝 = 0.093), and conducted an ANCOVA with post-test as the outcome, pretest as covariate, and Condition as fixed factor, using HC3\nrobust errors. The ANCOVA yielded a significant Condition effect (̂ 𝛾= 1.366, 𝑆𝐸 = 0.329, 𝑡 = 4.15, 𝑝 < 0.001), reflecting an adjusted\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n12\n\nL. Xi et al.\nTable 2\nDescriptive and inferential statistics for the conceptual knowledge test.\nGroup/Test N Pre-test M (SD) Post-test M (SD) Estimate SE t/z p-value\nEG (S-ICA) 48 68.05 (1.19) 86.44 (1.04) – – – –\nCG (nS-ICA) 46 67.60 (1.68) 85.17 (1.90) – – – –\nLMM: Time (main effect) – – – 17.568 0.307 57.23 < 0.001\nLMM: Condition (main effect) – – – 0.450 0.304 1.48 0.138\nLMM: Time × Condition – – – 0.821 0.430 1.91 0.056\nANCOVA: Condition – – – 1.366 0.329 4.15 < 0.001\nTable 3\nResearch proposal quality: descriptive statistics and between-group effect size (team-level, 𝑛 = 8 per condition).\nMeasure S-ICA (EG) nS-ICA (CG) Notes\n𝑁 (teams) 8 8 One proposal per team\nMean (proposal score) 48.09 40.43 Higher is better\nSD 4.65 3.90 –\nBetween-group effect (S-ICA minus nS-ICA)\nMean difference 7.66 Unadjusted difference in means\nHedges’ 𝑔 1.69 Small-sample corrected Cohen’s 𝑑\n95% CI for 𝑔 [0.57, 2.80] Normal-approximation CI\nCLES 0.88 Probability S-ICA > nS-ICA\nmean difference of about one point (S-ICA: 86.49; nS-ICA: 85.12). Given the sensitivity of this test to variance heterogeneity and\nits disregard for within-student dependence, this finding is interpreted as supplementary to the mixed-model results.\nTaken together, these results indicate that both groups made substantial gains in conceptual knowledge. The S-ICA condition\nshowed at most a modest, marginal advantage over the nS-ICA condition, suggesting that Socratic scaffolding may provide\nincremental benefits, although the effect remains small and warrants careful interpretation.\n5.1.2. Experimental research proposal quality\nThe quality of the experimental research proposals was evaluated at the group level, since each team of five to six students\ncollaboratively submitted a single final design. In total, sixteen proposals were collected (𝑛EG = 8 , 𝑛CG = 8 ). Each proposal\nwas independently scored by two experienced raters in educational technology, and interrater reliability was substantial (Cohen’s\n𝜅 = 0.713 for EG and 0.670 for CG; Landis & Koch, 1977).\nDescriptive statistics indicated that the experimental groups (S-ICA) obtained higher scores (𝑀 = 48 .09, 𝑆𝐷 = 4 .65) than the\ncontrol groups (nS-ICA; 𝑀 = 40.43, 𝑆𝐷 = 3.90), yielding an average difference of 7.66 points (see Table 3). Given the limited number\nof independent observations, statistical inference was performed using robust procedures. The Mann–Whitney U test revealed a\nsignificant difference (𝑈 = 60.0, exact 𝑝 = 0.002). A nonparametric Mann–Whitney test converged on the same conclusion (𝑈 = 60.0,\nexact 𝑝 = 0.002), and a permutation test with 10,000 random reallocations of group labels further confirmed the robustness of this\neffect (𝑝 = 0.001).\nThe standardized mean difference was large: Cohen’s 𝑑 = 1 .79 (Hedges’ 𝑔 = 1 .69, 95% CI [0.57, 2.80]). The corresponding\ncommon language effect size (CLES) was approximately 0.88, indicating an 88% probability that a randomly selected S-ICA team\nwould achieve a higher proposal score than a randomly selected nS-ICA team.\nTaken together, these convergent analyses demonstrate that the proposals produced by students interacting with the S-ICA were\nof substantially higher quality than those developed with the nS-ICA, despite the small number of team-level observations. This\nfinding suggests that embedding Socratic questioning into LLM-driven agents can enhance the rigor and coherence of students’\ncollaborative research design outputs, even under authentic classroom constraints. However, the small sample size in this study\n(n=8 per group) limits statistical power and may reduce generalizability of the findings. While the observed effects are statistically\nsignificant, caution is needed when interpreting these results. Future research with larger and more diverse samples is necessary to\nconfirm the robustness and generalizability of the findings.\n5.2. Results on RQ2\n5.2.1. Reflective thinking assessment\nTo address RQ2, we compared EG and CG in post-test reflective thinking scores. The Shapiro–Wilk tests confirmed that all four\nreflective thinking subscales were normally distributed in both EG (𝑛 = 48) and CG (𝑛 = 46; all 𝑝 > 0.05). Levene’s tests indicated no\nsignificant variance inequalities across subscales (max 𝐹 = 1.017, 𝑝 = 0.318), and tests of homogeneity of regression slopes showed\nno significant interactions (max 𝐹 = 1.108, 𝑝 = 0.297), thereby justifying the use of ANCOVA.\nAs shown in Table 4, after controlling for pretest scores, the EG’s overall reflective-thinking score (unadjusted 𝑀 = 4.35; adjusted\n𝑀 = 4 .359) was significantly higher than the CG’s (unadjusted 𝑀 = 3 .80; adjusted 𝑀 = 3 .819; 𝐹 = 9 .218, 𝑝 = 0 .003, 𝜂2 = 0 .114).\nSubscale analyses revealed modest group differences in Habitual Action (𝐹 = 5 .194, 𝑝 = 0 .025, 𝜂2 = 0 .056) and Understanding\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n13\n\nL. Xi et al.\nTable 4\nANACOVA analysis of scores in reflective thinking.\nVariable Group N M SD Adjusted mean SE F 𝜂 2\nOverall Reflective Thinking Experimental 48 4.35 0.37 4.359 0.091 9.218 0.114\nControl 46 3.80 0.42 3.819 0.089\nHabitual Action Experimental 48 3.95 0.40 3.954 0.092 5.194 0.056\nControl 46 3.75 0.45 3.769 0.091\nUnderstanding Experimental 48 4.20 0.36 4.198 0.089 7.003 0.072\nControl 46 3.95 0.39 3.949 0.088\nReflection Experimental 48 4.40 0.38 4.409 0.087 11.438 0.124\nControl 46 3.70 0.43 3.695 0.090\nCritical Reflection Experimental 48 4.25 0.35 4.238 0.086 13.712 0.137\nControl 46 3.69 0.36 3.698 0.079\n(𝐹 = 7 .003, 𝑝 = 0 .010, 𝜂2 = 0 .072). More pronounced differences emerged in the higher-order dimensions: Reflection (𝐹 = 11 .438,\n𝑝 = .001, 𝜂2 = 0.124) and Critical Reflection (𝐹 = 13.712, 𝑝 < 0.001, 𝜂2 = 0.137).\nBecause these outcomes were based on self-reported questionnaires, the results should be interpreted as reflecting participants’\nperceived levels of reflective engagement rather than objectively observed performance. Overall, students in the S-ICA condition\nreported higher reflective thinking, particularly in the advanced dimensions of Reflection and Critical Reflection, suggesting that\nthe use of Socratic questioning within the ICAs may support deeper self-perceived cognitive engagement.\n5.2.2. Reflective thinking process performance\nFig. 9 presents the ENA spatial distributions for the EG and CG as they interacted with their respective ICAs, illustrating\ndifferences in the reflective thinking structures exhibited during dialogue. For ease of comparison, each EG student is represented\nby a red dot and each CG student by a blue dot, corresponding to their individual cognitive-network projection in ENA space.\nThe centroid of the EG’s average cognitive network is indicated by a solid red square, while the CG’s centroid is shown as a solid\nblue square. These two centroids are clearly separated in space; the dashed rectangles surrounding each square denote the 95%\nconfidence interval for each group, further underscoring group differences in cognitive strategy and reflective language use. A\ntwo-sample t-test confirmed this divergence. Along the ENA 𝑋-axis (MR1), the EG’s mean network score (M = 0.36, SD = 0.25, N\n= 48) was significantly higher than the CG’s mean (M = –0.39, SD = 0.30, N = 46), t (92) = 13.08, p < 0.001, Cohen’s d = 2.75.\nThis large effect size indicates that the EG significantly outperformed the CG in terms of cognitive co-occurrence structure. Along\nthe ENA 𝑌 -axis (SVD2), a two-sample t-test assuming unequal variances showed no statistically significant difference between the\nEG (M = 0.00, SD = 0.37, N = 48) and the CG (M = 0.00, SD = 0.24, N = 46), t (81.29) = 0.00, 𝑝 = 1.00, Cohen’s d = 0.00.\nTo more effectively explain the cognitive differences between the groups in the ENA network structure and identify how the\nEG and CG differed in their reflective thinking pathways when using different ICAs, we conducted a differential network analysis.\nBy subtracting the CG’s co-occurrence network from the EG’s (EG minus CG), we generated a contrast network where red edges\ndenote node-pair connections stronger in the EG’s network, while blue edges indicate stronger connections in the CG’s network. The\nthickness of the edges corresponds to the magnitude of the difference and co-occurrence frequency.\nAs shown in Fig. 10(a), the ENA network analysis reveals distinct differences in the cognitive pathways activated by the S-ICA\nand nS-ICA groups. In the EG, red edges represent stronger connections between key reflective thinking categories, indicating a more\nrobust and integrated cognitive structure. As detailed in Table 5, we further examined and compared the connectivity patterns and\nstrengths for each node pair across both groups’ average network graphs. Fig. 10(b) illustrates that, for most node pairs, the EG\nexhibited higher mean connection strength than the CG, highlighting greater co-occurrence density and cognitive integration during\nreflective dialogue. Specifically, the EG showed tighter interconnections among multiple levels of reflective thinking—particularly\nbetween Understanding and Reflection (U–R, 0.53), Reflection and Critical Reflection (R–CR, 0.43), and Understanding and Critical\nReflection (U–CR, 0.32). These connections suggest that EG students, supported by the S-ICA, more frequently activated mid- to\nhigher-order reflective cognitive pathways.\nIn contrast, the CG displayed comparatively stronger connections in low-order pathways, particularly between Habitual Action\nand Understanding (HA–U, 0.65) and between Habitual Action and Reflection (HA–R, 0.35), as shown in Fig. 10(c). These findings\nreveal that, while the CG activated more basic cognitive processes, the S-ICA group was able to develop more sophisticated reflective\nthinking pathways, reinforcing the effectiveness of Socratic prompting in fostering higher-order cognitive engagement.\nThese findings support the hypothesis that Socratic prompting enhances cognitive engagement by encouraging students to explore\nand critically reflect on their understanding. The ENA analysis offers a robust framework for identifying and interpreting the\ncognitive pathways activated by the S-ICA, revealing the underlying cognitive mechanisms that facilitate more sophisticated levels\nof reflective thinking.\n5.3. Results on RQ3\nAn analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) was conducted on posttest learning motivation scores, using pretest motivation as a\ncovariate. Prior to running ANCOVA, Levene’s test for homogeneity of variances revealed no significant difference between the\ngroups (Levene’s F = 0.728, 𝑝 = 0 .396 > 0.05), satisfying the equal-variance assumption. The test for homogeneity of regression\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n14\n\nL. Xi et al.\nFig. 9. Mean of the plotted points for the EG and CG.\nFig. 10. ENA networks of the EG and CG. (a) The comparison networks of the EG and CG, (b) The mean network of the EG, (c) The mean\nnetwork of the CG.\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n15\n\nL. Xi et al.\nTable 5\nConnection coefficients of ENA networks.\nConnection Experimental group (S-ICA approach) Control group (nS-ICA approach)\nHA-U 0.11 0.65\nHA-R 0.13 0.35\nHA-CR 0.08 0.11\nU-R 0.53 0.29\nU-CR 0.32 0.08\nR-CR 0.43 0.07\nTable 6\nANCOVA results for learning motivation scores.\nGroup N M SD Adjusted M SE F 𝑝-value 𝜂2\nExperimental (S-ICA) 48 4.01 0.42 3.924 0.096 1.749 0.190 0.041\nControl (nS-ICA) 46 3.77 0.49 3.818 0.093\nFig. 11. The control group interview results.\nslopes also indicated no significant interaction between the covariate and group (F = 0.603, 𝑝 = 0 .440 > 0.05), validating the use\nof ANCOVA.\nAs reported in Table 6, the ANCOVA results showed no statistically significant difference in adjusted mean motivation scores\nbetween the EG (Madj = 3.924, SE = 0.096) and the CG (Madj = 3.818, SE = 0.093; F = 1.749, 𝑝 = 0 .190 > 0.05). In other words,\nparticipants in the S-ICA condition did not report higher levels of learning motivation compared with those in the nS-ICA condition.\nThis suggests that while the S-ICA may enhance cognitive outcomes such as knowledge and reflective thinking, its influence on\nlearners’ motivational perceptions appears limited within the scope of this study.\n5.4. Results on RQ4\nWe conducted content analysis on the transcribed interview data using ATLAS.ti 25 for inductive qualitative analysis. First,\nopen coding was employed to group codes with similar meanings into themes and subthemes. After excluding irrelevant codes,\nthe EG interviews yielded five themes and eleven subthemes, while the CG interviews yielded four themes and seven subthemes.\nWe used ATLAS.ti 25 network feature to visualize axial coding, linking coded themes (blue nodes) with their respective subthemes\n(orange nodes). Fig. 11 and 12 depict the interview results for both groups. Finally, we compared the two groups’ reported learning\nexperiences and perceptions, distilling the following five overarching themes.\n5.4.1. Stimulate deep thinking\nDuring discipline-specific coursework, using the S-ICA to support learning more effectively stimulates students’ deep thinking.\nIts persistent, probing questions and counter-questions not only encourage students to generate additional ideas in response to the\ndialogue’s trajectory but also progressively prompt them to explore and expand their thinking patterns. For example, one participant\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n16\n\nL. Xi et al.\nFig. 12. The experimental group interview results.\n(S120506) reported that the agent’s questions guided him to re-examine his experimental design step by step; as the agent’s inquiries\ndeepened, the critical issues in his design became increasingly evident, enabling him to make targeted corrections. Another student\n(S120510) noted that using the S-ICA ‘‘fostered a desire to engage in dialogue and to answer questions’’, which in turn broadened\nhis line of thinking and sparked creative insights. A third participant commented, ‘‘The agent’s continuous questioning gradually\nbroke my habitual thinking patterns; when I faced complex problems, I began to analyze them from multiple angles, truly upgrading\nmy cognitive processes’’.\n5.4.2. Promote effective learning\nIn terms of promoting effective learning, a comparison of interview data and coding frequencies between the two groups revealed\nthat only students who used the S-ICA experienced genuinely effective learning. For instance, S120403 stated that ‘‘digging into a\nconcept by asking ‘why’ at every step’’ helped him develop a deeper understanding of that concept. S120402 similarly reported\nthat ‘‘interactive dialogue enabled me to internalize the material more profoundly’’, facilitating knowledge acquisition. Conversely,\nin the control group (using the nS-ICA), some students acknowledged that the agent significantly expedited information access\nand improved their ability to retrieve resources; however, they also remarked that such superficial learning ‘‘did not truly occur’’\n(C120405). Another CG student (C120501) observed that ‘‘using a generic conversational agent resulted in surface-level learning\nwithout generating more creative solutions, rendering the process effectively ineffective’’.\n5.4.3. Raise doubts and reflections\nThroughout the learning process with the S-ICA, experimental-group students were more inclined to generate questions and\nengage in reflective thinking. The agent’s Socratic counter-questions enabled students to continuously revisit and re-evaluate their\ninitial ideas, prompting them to scrutinize the underlying rationale and intentions. One student (S120503) remarked, ‘‘As I interacted\nwith the agent, it kept questioning each element of my experimental design. In responding to these queries, I began to doubt\nmy original logic and thought carefully about how I had planned my study’’. Another student (S120508) noted, ‘‘While I was\nclarifying my experimental reasoning with the S-ICA, I was constantly reflecting on the logic and shortcomings of my thought\nprocess. This caused me to question and delve deeply into the conceptual relationships behind each idea, greatly expanding my\ncognitive horizons’’.\n5.4.4. Enhance knowledge acquisition and learning\nCompared with those using the nS-ICA, students in the experimental group demonstrated stronger knowledge internalization\nand cognitive construction. Most S-ICA users reported that they could better master the target knowledge and enhance their\nunderstanding of the content. For example, one student reported, ‘‘During my learning, I continually unearthed and verified\nadditional experimental factors. By actively searching for information and discussing with teammates, I built a more comprehensive,\nsystematic theoretical framework’’ (S120507). In contrast, a control-group student (C120406) stated, ‘‘The nS-ICA’s guidance was\nsimplistic and mechanistic; although it offered slightly more detailed direction, I ended up completing the task without actually\nlearning anything’’. Another CG participant noted, ‘‘In our subgroup, some members simply copied the agent’s generated answers,\nwhich further hindered genuine knowledge mastery’’ (C120405).\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n17\n\nL. Xi et al.\n5.4.5. Prevent cognitive dependence\nDuring the coding of interview data, students in both groups frequently mentioned ‘‘cognitive dependence’’. EG students felt that\nusing the S-ICA effectively mitigated the cognitive dependence and degradation associated with general large-language-model tools\n(frequency of occurrence among EG interviews: 18). For example, S120403 reported, ‘‘With a generic agent, it’s easy to fall into\na dependence on the tool and gradually lose independent thinking ability. But using the S-ICA immerses me in a highly authentic\ndialogue—so I cannot simply obtain the final answer, and my cognitive skills are exercised instead’’. Overall, nearly every student\nin the experimental group remarked that ‘‘the system prevented me from relying on ready-made answers’’. CG participants similarly\nmentioned cognitive dependence (frequency in CG interviews: 15), observing that reliance on a generic conversational agent stifled\ninnovation and independent thought.\n6. Discussion\n6.1. Academic performance\nThe findings indicate a differentiated impact of the S-ICA across outcome domains. At the individual level, both groups achieved\nsubstantial gains in conceptual knowledge, but the advantage of the S-ICA over the nS-ICA was modest and only marginally\ndetectable. This pattern is consistent with prior research showing that GenAI-based tools can enhance learner achievement, yet\ntheir effects on conventional knowledge tests are typically small to moderate (Hwang & Chen, 2023). Such tests tend to privilege the\ncorrectness of final answers rather than the justificatory and regulatory processes that Socratic dialogue is designed to cultivate (Lin\net al., 2023). As a result, the improvements in epistemic regulation and reasoning depth elicited by the S-ICA may not have been\nfully captured by this form of assessment.\nTaken together, the findings indicate that the educational benefits of Socratic scaffolding manifest differently across outcome\nlevels. While conceptual knowledge tests revealed only modest incremental gains for the S-ICA, the group-authored proposals\ndemonstrated a substantial advantage, underscoring the value of dialogic scaffolding in tasks that demand extended reasoning,\nintegration of perspectives, and collaborative knowledge construction. Pedagogically, this points to a productive sequencing strategy:\nemploying S-ICA to foster individual reflection and epistemic accountability prior to group collaboration, so that collective work\ncan build on stronger, more critically reasoned contributions. More broadly, the results illustrate how GenAI-driven conversational\nagents, when designed with adaptive questioning strategies, can personalize the level of cognitive challenge and support learners in\nways that accumulate into higher-quality collective outcomes Hu & Shao, 2025. This dual role—providing individualized scaffolding\nwhile enhancing group performance—resonates with established traditions in scaffolding and collaborative learning (Molenaar et al.,\n2014) and highlights a promising direction for leveraging GenAI to advance both personalized and collaborative dimensions of\nacademic performance. Although this study provides valuable insights into the impact of Socratic questioning in LLM-driven agents,\nthe small sample size necessitates caution in drawing broad conclusions. Future studies with larger sample sizes are needed to\nvalidate these results.\n6.2. Reflective thinking\nResults indicated that students supported by the S-ICA reported significantly higher levels of reflective thinking than those in the\ncontrol group, with the most pronounced differences emerging in the advanced dimensions of Reflection and Critical Reflection. This\nsuggests that embedding Socratic dialogue strategies into an ICA can foster deeper forms of self-reported reflective engagement. Such\nan interpretation is consistent with prior studies showing that sustained questioning techniques stimulate learners’ critical reasoning\nand self-reflective capacities (Hu, 2023). Similarly, Rospigliosi (2023) demonstrated that LLM-based agents such as ChatGPT\ncan promote iterative cycles of ‘‘question–answer–reflection’’, thereby supporting students’ awareness of their own thinking. The\ntheoretical underpinnings of Socratic pedagogy also reinforce these findings: as Edmunds (2006) emphasized, the Socratic method\npositions the teacher—or in this case, the agent—as a facilitator who provokes cognitive conflict and challenges prior assumptions.\nThrough this process, learners are encouraged not only to articulate what they already know but also to engage in higher-order\nconceptual inquiry and metacognitive regulation. Other scholars have similarly highlighted how dialogic approaches promote\nconsensus-building and cognitive engagement through dialectical reasoning, social negotiation, and self-correction (Darmawansah\net al., 2024).\nBeyond theoretical support, our ENA analyses provided empirical evidence for these patterns. The experimental group exhib-\nited stronger network connections among key pathways—notably Understanding–Reflection, Reflection–Critical Reflection, and\nUnderstanding–Critical Reflection—compared with the control group. These findings suggest that the S-ICA more effectively\nactivated mid- to higher-order reflective pathways. This observation accords with (Moon et al., 2000)’s model of reflective learning,\nwhich characterizes reflection as an iterative and nonlinear process of reconstructing meaning. As Seeskin (1987) noted, Socratic\ndialogue ‘‘thaws frozen thought’’, encouraging learners to pause, reconsider, and deepen their reflection. Accordingly, the present\nstudy contributes to calls for integrating Socratic questioning and other philosophical frameworks into AI-mediated instruction to\ncultivate reflective thinking.\nFurther examination of the ENA network differences reveals the underlying cognitive mechanisms at play. In the control\ncondition, where students interacted with a nS-ICA that provided direct feedback without any prompting for reflection, the\nnetwork exhibited fewer interconnections and weaker reflective pathways. This observation underscores the critical role of Socratic\nquestioning in stimulating deeper cognitive engagement. The S-ICA, by contrast, encouraged a more complex, recursive interaction\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n18\n\nL. Xi et al.\nbetween students’ initial understandings, reflective thought, and critical evaluation. The iterative process of query–response–revision\nobserved in the experimental group represents the core pedagogical mechanism that enhanced reflective thinking. This process\nmirrors concept of reflective practice, where students actively engage in self-regulation and self-correction to refine their reasoning\n(Schön, 1995).\nThese results suggest that the observed improvements in reflective thinking and cognitive integration were not merely the product\nof LLM technology but arose from the integration of Socratic scaffolding with LLM-based conversational agents. The S-ICA does not\nsimply serve as a passive content-delivery tool but actively shapes the learning process by fostering higher-order thinking. This\ninteraction between LLM technology and Socratic questioning offers a compelling model for AI-mediated instruction that supports\ncritical thinking and reflective learning. Besides, the ability of Socratic prompting to activate mid- to higher-order thinking pathways\nis pivotal in deepening students’ understanding and critical thinking skills, which are essential for academic success in complex\nlearning environments.\n6.3. Learning motivation\nThe present study found no statistically significant difference in reported learning motivation between students using the S-ICA\nand those using the nS-ICA. This contrasts with previous research suggesting that intelligent conversational agents and GenAI tools\ncan, under certain conditions, enhance motivation and related affective outcomes such as self-efficacy and learning attitudes (Essel\net al., 2022). In our context, however, the motivational impact of the S-ICA appeared limited, despite its positive effects on cognitive\noutcomes.\nOne plausible explanation lies in Self-Determination Theory, which emphasizes that sustained intrinsic motivation requires the\nfulfillment of autonomy, competence, and relatedness needs (Deci & Ryan, 2012). While the S-ICA’s progressive questioning may\nhave promoted cognitive engagement, it may not have adequately supported students’ sense of autonomy or personal relevance.\nIn other words, dialogic interaction with an agent, in the absence of stronger alignment with learners’ interests or values, may be\ninsufficient to trigger meaningful increases in motivation (Vansteenkiste et al., 2004). This limitation may have been amplified by\nthe nature of the course context—‘‘educational research methods’’—which many students perceive as a technical requirement rather\nthan an intrinsically appealing subject domain Hsieh et al. (2012).\nTaken together, these results suggest a differentiated impact of the S-ICA: while effective in enhancing conceptual knowledge and\nthe quality of collaborative outputs, its influence on students’ motivational perceptions remained negligible. This finding highlights\nthe importance of designing GenAI-supported learning environments that combine cognitive scaffolding with targeted motivational\nsupports, for example, by incorporating greater personalization, adaptive goal-setting, or affective feedback mechanisms. Future\nwork should explore whether longer interventions or stronger personalization features enable S-ICAs to foster not only cognitive\ndevelopment but also more robust motivational engagement.\n6.4. Learning perceptions\nTo complement the quantitative findings, semi-structured interviews were conducted to capture students’ perceptions of their\nlearning experiences. Participants in the experimental group emphasized that the S-ICA, through sustained questioning, refutation,\nand heuristic prompts, encouraged deeper engagement during proposal evaluation. They reported that the system facilitated\ninternalization of research concepts, clarification of variable relationships, and more active participation in knowledge construction.\nIn contrast, students in the control group described the nS-ICA as useful for task completion but noted tendencies toward\n‘‘information dependence’’ and ‘‘cognitive passivity’’. Several admitted to directly adopting AI-generated responses without critically\nengaging with the underlying research logic, which was reflected in weaker rigor and feasibility of their proposals. These accounts\nalign with prior work suggesting that while AI can expedite access to information, effective learning requires structured guidance\nto transform information into deeper understanding (Lin, 2019).\nMoreover, interviews revealed potential affective and motivational benefits of the S-ICA. Many students noted that, although\ncontinuous questioning initially imposed cognitive load, it ultimately ‘‘forced me to think carefully’’, thereby heightening their\nengagement with the task (Belland et al., 2013). This ‘‘cognitive challenge + guidance’’ model aligns with (Delen et al., 2014)\ndescription of immersive learning environments, where strong interactivity and guided scaffolding alleviate feelings of isolation and\nboost motivation. While the quantitative results did not show a significant overall increase in learning motivation, the qualitative\naccounts revealed that some students nevertheless experienced heightened interest, persistence, and exploratory willingness when\ninteracting with the S-ICA. This divergence highlights the distinction between measurable motivational outcomes and students’\nsubjective experiences. It also underscores the need, when optimizing intelligent conversational agents, to balance cognitive\nrigor with user-friendly interactions, thereby enhancing system acceptability and sustainable use. Future research should further\ninvestigate how adaptive learning mechanisms—such as tailoring questioning frequency, depth, and feedback to individual learner\nprofiles—can translate these localized perceptions of increased engagement into sustained and generalizable motivational gains.\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n19\n\nL. Xi et al.\n6.5. Research implications\nThe findings of this study suggest that the integration of LLM-based intelligent conversational agents with Socratic questioning\nstrategies holds potential for enriching higher education learning processes, though the benefits appear differentiated across outcome\ndomains. At the individual level, conceptual knowledge gains were substantial across both conditions, with only a modest and at\nbest marginal advantage for the S-ICA. At the collective level, however, teams supported by the S-ICA produced significantly higher-\nquality research proposals, indicating that dialogic scaffolding may be particularly effective for open-ended, collaborative tasks\nrequiring justification and integration of ideas. In contrast, no statistically significant improvements were observed in students’ self-\nreported learning motivation, suggesting that the cognitive benefits of Socratic prompting did not automatically extend to affective\ndimensions.\nTheoretically, these findings contribute to ongoing debates on how GenAI can be meaningfully combined with established\npedagogical traditions. By empirically examining the role of Socratic strategies in an LLM-mediated environment, this study\nillustrates how dialogic scaffolding can strengthen epistemic processes such as justification and reflection, which may subsequently\naccumulate into more coherent collective outputs. At the same time, the limited motivational impact observed underscores the need\nto more explicitly align AI-supported learning designs with students’ autonomy, competence, and relatedness needs, as emphasized\nin motivational theory. These nuanced outcomes highlight that GenAI’s educational value is not uniform but depends on the specific\nlearning processes targeted and the assessment methods employed.\nPractically, the results yield implications for integrating AI-driven agents into university instruction. Replication of this approach\nrequires not only technical implementation of prompt-engineering strategies but also contextual embedding in authentic coursework,\nensuring alignment with curricular goals and student needs. Rather than being constrained by the study’s scope—a six-week\nintervention and discipline-specific task—the findings point to promising avenues for extending such designs to broader contexts.\nFuture implementations should investigate how stronger personalization, adaptive feedback, and longer exposure periods affect both\ncognitive and motivational outcomes, as well as whether the observed team-level benefits transfer to other forms of academic work.\nIn particular, adaptive learning systems could play a pivotal role in tailoring feedback to learners’ prior knowledge, interests, and\nprogress trajectories, thereby enhancing both performance and motivation. More broadly, the study underscores the importance of\ndesigning GenAI-supported environments that balance immediate cognitive scaffolding with mechanisms for sustaining motivation\nand promoting transferability across domains.\nOverall, the present study highlights both the promise and the boundaries of embedding Socratic strategies into LLM-based\nagents. While the approach demonstrated clear potential to enhance cognitive and collaborative outcomes, its effectiveness will\ndepend on careful contextualization, alignment with motivational principles, and sustained exposure. Future work should therefore\nposition such integrations not as universally transformative solutions, but as targeted pedagogical innovations whose value lies in\ntheir capacity to enrich personalized and adaptive learning when thoughtfully implemented.\n7. Limitations and future directions\nSeveral limitations should be acknowledged. First, the sample was primarily composed of students majoring in educational\ntechnology, and the gender distribution across participants was uneven. This sampling context may limit the generalizability of\nthe findings to learners from other disciplines and backgrounds. Future research should therefore aim to recruit more diverse and\nrepresentative samples to enhance external validity.\nSecond, the instructional tasks implemented in this study predominantly focused on research design-oriented activities. Although\nthis context aligns well with the development of reflective thinking and higher-order reasoning, it remains uncertain whether\ncomparable benefits would be observed in other domains of learning, such as discipline-specific content knowledge, professional\nskills, or affective outcomes. Future studies should therefore extend the application of S-ICAs to a wider range of instructional\nsettings to examine transferability and ecological validity.\nThird, the relatively short duration of the intervention limited the ability to examine the long-term effects of the AI-supported\nSocratic dialogue agent on learners’ reflective practices and sustained behavioral change. Follow-up studies should adopt longitudinal\ndesigns and repeated measures to capture whether the observed gains in reasoning and proposal quality persist, attenuate, or\ncompound over time.\nFourth, the limited number of group-level submissions (eight proposals per condition) constrained statistical power and reduced\nthe precision of effect estimates. While robust analytic techniques were employed, future research should increase the number of\ngroups to better account for group-level variance and enhance the reliability of inferences.\nIn addition, ethical considerations surrounding LLM-based learning processes were not comprehensively addressed in this\nresearch. Issues such as data privacy, content originality, and intellectual property rights pose potential challenges to the responsible\nuse of GenAI-generated content in educational contexts. Accordingly, future work should not only investigate how learners and\neducators perceive these ethical risks, but also develop practical guidelines and institutional policies to ensure that GenAI tools are\nadopted responsibly, transparently, and in ways that safeguard academic integrity.\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n20\n\nL. Xi et al.\nTable 7\nEvaluation rubric for experimental research proposals.\nCriteria Excellent(Score = 10) Good (Score = 8) Satisfactory(Score =\n6)\nNeeds\nImprovement(Score\n= 4)\nPoor(Score = 2)\nResearch\nTopic\nThe title is very concise, clear,\nand standard; accurately\nreflects the experimental scope\nand significance.\nThe title is concise,\nclear, and\nappropriate; reflects\nthe experimental\nscope and\nsignificance.\nThe title is\nmoderately clear;\nreflects part of the\nexperimental scope.\nThe title lacks\nclarity or\ncompleteness; scope\nis vague.\nThe title is vague or\noverly complex;\ndoes not reflect the\nscope.\n\nResearch\nQuestion\nClearly and precisely defines\nthe experimental research\nquestion; variables and\nhypotheses are explicitly\nstated.\nPrecisely defines the\nresearch question;\nmain variables and\nhypotheses are\nidentified.\nThe research\nquestion is generally\nclear; variables\npartially identified.\nThe research\nquestion lacks\nclarity; variables or\nhypotheses are\ninsufficiently stated.\nThe research\nquestion is vague;\nlacks variables and\nhypotheses.\n\nTheoretical\nRationale\nThe literature comprehensively\nreviews key concepts and\nvariables; strongly supports\nthe experimental design.\nThe literature\nsufficiently reviews\nrelevant concepts\nand variables;\nsupports the design.\nThe literature\npartially reviews\nrelevant concepts;\nlimited support for\nthe design.\nThe literature is\ninsufficient or lacks\nfocus on key\nvariables.\nThe literature is\noutdated or\nirrelevant; no\nconceptual basis\nprovided.\n\nResearch\nContent\nExperimental variables and\ndesign are fully specified;\nselection is highly scientific\nand appropriate.\nVariables and\ndesign are clearly\nspecified; selection\nis appropriate.\nVariables and\ndesign are generally\nspecified; selection\nis partially\nappropriate.\nVariables and\ndesign are unclear\nor incomplete;\nselection is\nquestionable.\nVariables and\ndesign are vague or\nunsuitable.\n\nResearch\nMethodology\nMethods are rigorously\nselected and described; clearly\naligned with experimental\ngoals; appropriate model is\ndetailed.\nMethods are\nappropriate and\nwell described; the\nmodel is\nappropriate.\nMethods are\ngenerally\nappropriate; the\nmodel is described\nbriefly.\nMethods are\ninsufficiently\ndescribed or only\npartially\nappropriate; the\nmodel lacks clarity.\nMethods are vague\nor inappropriate;\nthe model is\nmissing.\n\nResearch\nProcedures\nProcedures are comprehensive,\ndetailed, and feasible; clear\nimplementation plan and\npreparation steps are\nprovided.\nProcedures are clear\nand feasible;\nimplementation\nsteps are adequate.\nProcedures are\nmoderately clear;\nsome steps are\noutlined.\nProcedures lack\ndetail or clarity;\nfeasibility is limited.\nProcedures are\nvague or not\nimplementable.\n\n8. Conclusion\nOverall, the primary contribution of the present study lies in the design and empirical validation of the S-ICA that inte-\ngrates Socratic dialogue strategies with LLM technology to enhance students’ research design skills, reflective thinking, and\nperceptions of learning tools within a discipline-specific course. Results indicate that the S-ICA confers significant advantages in\nguiding students through higher-order cognitive tasks, stimulating deep reflection, and improving the scientific rigor of research\nproposals—particularly along the dimensions of Reflection and Critical Reflection.\nA distinctive feature of the S-ICA is its capacity to provide personalized and adaptive scaffolding: by semantically parsing\nlearner input and tailoring subsequent questions to individual reasoning stages, the system was able to align instructional support\nwith students’ needs, strengths, and levels of preparedness. This adaptive responsiveness ensured that feedback was neither overly\nsimplistic nor excessively demanding, thereby sustaining engagement and promoting productive struggle. In this sense, the study\ncontributes to the growing body of work on AI-supported personalized learning by demonstrating how dialogic scaffolding can adapt\nin real time to heterogeneous learners.\nIn the context of the rapid advances in GenAI, a central challenge for educational research is how to leverage LLM-based\nconversational systems to enhance learning without fostering cognitive dependency or encouraging unethical academic practices.\nThis study offers a model for integrating classical instructional strategies with cutting-edge AI technologies, providing empirical\nevidence of their potential efficacy in educational settings. The Epistemic Network Analysis (ENA) results demonstrated that Socratic\nprompting effectively activated more advanced reflective pathways, indicating that such AI-driven systems can play a crucial role in\nhigher education by fostering critical thinking and reflective engagement. Furthermore, the findings suggest that adaptive Socratic\nprompting could strike a balance between two essential goals in higher education: supporting personalized learning paths for\nindividual students while simultaneously promoting collective knowledge construction in collaborative tasks.\nThis approach addresses concerns about the potential risks of AI-generated content in education (Abbas et al., 2024; Ng et al.,\n2024) by demonstrating how structured, question-driven dialogue can mitigate those risks while promoting deeper learning. Future\nresearch should extend this line of inquiry by examining how adaptive Socratic ICAs can be scaled to diverse subject areas, longer\ninterventions, and varied learner populations, thereby advancing the integration of personalized and ethical AI practices in higher\neducation.\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n21\n\nL. Xi et al.\nCRediT authorship contribution statement\nLinjin Xi: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Visualization, Software, Methodology, Investigation, Data curation,\nConceptualization. Yi Zhang: Supervision, Resources, Project administration, Methodology, Funding acquisition, Data curation,\nConceptualization. Qiyun Wang: Writing – review & editing, Supervision, Project administration, Methodology, Conceptualization.\nEthic statement\nThe research involved human participants and followed the procedures of the Education Faculty’s Ethics Committee. Informed\nconsent was obtained for all participants.\nFunding\nThe research was granted by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 72274076).\nDeclaration of competing interest\nThe authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared\nto influence the work reported in this paper.\nAcknowledgments\nWe sincerely thank all the research assistants and participants who have contributed to this study.\nAppendix\nSee Table 7.\nData availability\nData will be made available on request.\nReferences\nAbbas, M., Jam, F. A., & Khan, T. I. (2024). Is it harmful or helpful? Examining the causes and consequences of generative AI usage among university students.\nInternational Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education, 21(1), 10.\nAlbert, D., & Li, T. (2023). Insights from teaching with AI: how ChatGPT can enhance experiential learning and assist instructors. Available At SSRN 4516801.\nAlexandre, J., & Enslin, C. (2017). The relationship between personalized instruction, academic achievement, knowledge application, and problem-solving skills..\nNational Teacher Education Journal, 10(1).\nAskell-Williams, H., Murray-Harvey, R., & Lawson, M. J. (2007). Teacher education students’ reflections on how problem-based learning has changed their mental\nmodels about teaching and learning. The Teacher Educator, 42(4), 237–263.\nBarrot, J. S. (2023). Using ChatGPT for second language writing: Pitfalls and potentials. Assessing Writing, 57, Article 100745.\nBauer, N. J. (1991). Dewey and schon: An analysis of reflective thinking.. ERIC.\nBelland, B. R., Kim, C., & Hannafin, M. J. (2013). A framework for designing scaffolds that improve motivation and cognition. Educational Psychologist, 48(4),\n243–270.\nBorchers, C., Fleischer, H., Schanze, S., Scheiter, K., & Aleven, V. (2025). High scaffolding of an unfamiliar strategy improves conceptual learning but reduces\nenjoyment compared to low scaffolding and strategy freedom. Computers & Education, 236, Article 105364.\nBoyd, E. M., & Fales, A. W. (1983). Reflective learning: Key to learning from experience. Journal of Humanistic Psychology, 23(2), 99–117.\nChang, J.-L., Hung, H.-T., & Yang, Y.-T. C. (2024). Effects of an annotation-supported socratic questioning approach on students’ argumentative writing performance\nand critical thinking skills in flipped language classrooms. Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 40(1), 37–48.\nChang, Y., Wang, X., Wang, J., Wu, Y., Yang, L., Zhu, K., Chen, H., Yi, X., Wang, C., Wang, Y., et al. (2024). A survey on evaluation of large language models.\nACM Transactions on Intelligent Systems and Technology, 15(3), 1–45.\nChen, M.-R. A., Hwang, G.-J., & Chang, Y.-Y. (2019). A reflective thinking-promoting approach to enhancing graduate students’ flipped learning engagement,\nparticipation behaviors, reflective thinking and project learning outcomes. British Journal of Educational Technology, 50(5), 2288–2307.\nChen, C.-H., Wang, K.-C., & Lin, Y.-H. (2015). The comparison of solitary and collaborative modes of game-based learning on students’ science learning and\nmotivation. Journal of Educational Technology & Society, 18(2), 237–248.\nChu, S. K., Chan, C. K., & Tiwari, A. F. (2012). Using blogs to support learning during internship. Computers & Education, 58(3), 989–1000.\nClark, G. I., & Egan, S. J. (2015). The socratic method in cognitive behavioural therapy: a narrative review. Cognitive Therapy and Research, 39, 863–879.\nCloude, E., Carpenter, D., Dever, D., Azevedo, R., & Lester, J. (2021). Game-based learning analytics for supporting adolescents’ reflection. Journal of Learning\nAnalytics, 8(2).\nCohen, J. (1988). Statistical power analysis for the behavioral sciences (2nd ed.). Hillsdale, NJ: Routledge.\nCui, R., & Teo, P. (2023). Thinking through talk: Using dialogue to develop students’ critical thinking. Teaching and Teacher Education, 125, Article 104068.\nDalim, S. F., Ishak, A. S., & Hamzah, L. M. (2022). Promoting students’ critical thinking through socratic method: Views and challenges. Asian Journal of University\nEducation (AJUE), 18(4), 1034–1047.\nDarmawansah, D., Hwang, G.-J., & Lin, C.-J. (2024). Integrating dialectical constructivist scaffolding-based argumentation mapping to support students’ dialectical\nthinking, oral and dialogical argumentation complexity. Educational Technology Research and Development, 1–29.\nDarvishi, A., Khosravi, H., Sadiq, S., Gašević, D., & Siemens, G. (2024). Impact of AI assistance on student agency. Computers & Education, 210, Article 104967.\nComputers & Education 241 (2026) 105494\n22\n\nL. Xi et al.\nDeci, E. L., & Ryan, R. M. (2012). Self-determination theory. vol. 1, In Handbook of theories of social psychology (20), (pp. 416–436). Sage Publications Ltd.\nDelen, E., Liew, J., & Willson, V. (2014). Effects of interactivity and instructional scaffolding on learning: Self-regulation in online video-based environments.\nComputers & Education, 78, 312–320.\nDerakhshan, A. (2025). EFL students’ perceptions about the role of generative artificial intelligence (GAI)-mediated instruction in their emotional engagement\nand goal orientation: A motivational climate theory (MCT) perspective in focus. Learning and Motivation, 90, Article 102114.\nDewey, J. (1922). An analysis of reflective thought. The Journal of Philosophy, 29–38.\nDinkins, C. S., & Cangelosi, P. R. (2019). Putting socrates back in socratic method: Theory-based debriefing in the nursing classroom. Nursing Philosophy, 20(2),\nArticle e12240.\nEdmunds, L. (2006). What was socrates called? The Classical Quarterly, 56(2), 414–425.\nEssel, H. B., Vlachopoulos, D., Tachie-Menson, A., Johnson, E. E., & Baah, P. K. (2022). The impact of a virtual teaching assistant (chatbot) on students’ learning\nin ghanaian higher education. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education, 19(1), 