er: Transcending dichotomies and transforming pedagogy. In C. K. K. Chan & N. Rao (Eds.), _Revisiting the Chinese learner_ (pp. 169–210). Springer. \n\nChan, C. K. K. (2011). Bridging research and practice: Implementing and sustaining knowledge building in Hong Kong classrooms. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _6_ , 147–186. \n\nChan, C. K. K., Tong, Y., & van Aalst, J. (2019). Progressive dialogue in computer-supported collaborative knowl- \n\nedge Building. In N. Mercer, R. Wegerif, & L. Major (Eds.), _The Routledge International Handbook of Research on Dialogic Education_ (pp. 776–799). Routledge. \n\nChen, B. (2017). Fostering scientific understanding and epistemic beliefs through judgments of promisingness. _Educational Technology Research & Development_ , _65_ , 255–277. \n\nChen, B., & Hong, H.-Y. (2016). Schools as knowledge-building organizations: Thirty years of design research. _Educational Psychologist_ , _51_ , 266–288. \n\nDietrichson, J., Bøg, M., Filges, T., & Jørgensen, A. (2017). Academic interventions for elementary and middle school students with low socioeconomic status: A systematic review and meta-analysis. _Review of Educational Research_ , _87_ , 243–282. \n\nD\'Mello, S. K., & Graesser, A. C. (2012). Dynamics of affective states during complex learning. _Learning and Instruction_ , _22_ , 145–157. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.learninstruc.2011.10.001 \n\nEngle, R. A. (2012). The productive disciplinary engagement framework: Origins, key concepts and developments. In D. Y. Dai (Ed.), _Design research on learning and thinking in educational settings: Enhancing intellectual growth and functioning_ (pp. 161–200). Routledge. \n\nEngle, R. A., & Conant, F. C. (2002). Guiding principles for fostering productive disciplinary engagement: Explaining an emergent argument in a community of learners\' classroom. _Cognition and Instruction_ , _20_ (4), 399–483. Fredricks, J. A., Blumenfeld, P. C., & Paris, A. H. (2004). School engagement: Potential of the concept, state of the evidence. _Review of Educational Research_ , _74_ (1), 59–109. \n\nGomoll, A. S., Hmelo-Silver, C. E., Tolar, E., Šabanović, S., & Francisco, M. (2017). Moving apart and coming together: Discourse, engagement, and deep learning. _Educational Technology & Society_ , _20_ (4), 219–232. \n\nHan, S., Capraro, R., & Capraro, M. M. (2015). How science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) project-based learning (PBL) affects high, middle, and low achievers differently: The impact of student factors on achievement. _International Journal of Science and Mathematics Education_ , _13_ , 1089–1113. \n\nJärvelä, S., Kirschner, P. A., Panadero, E., Malmberg, J., Phielix, C., Jaspers, J., Koivuniemi, M., & Järvenoja, H. (2015). Enhancing socially shared regulation in collaborative learning groups: Designing for CSCL regulation tools. _Educational Technology Research and Development_ , _63_ , 125–142. \n\nJordan, M. E., Zuiker, S., Wakefield, W., & DeLaRosa, M. (2021). Real work with real consequences: Enlisting community energy engineering as an approach to envisioning engineering in context. _Journal of Pre-College Engineering Education Research_ , _11_ (1), 13. \n\nLamon, M., Secules, T., Petrosino, A. J., Hackett, R., Bransford, J. D., & Goldman, S. R. (1996). Schools for thought: Overview of the project and lessons learned from one of the sites. In L. Schauble & R. Glaser (Eds.), _Innovations in learning: New environments for education_ (pp. 243–288). Lawrence Erlbaum Associates, Inc. \n\nLee, E. Y., Chan, C. K., & van Aalst, J. (2006). Students assessing their own collaborative knowledge building. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _1_ , 277–307. \n\nLei, C., & Chan, C. K. K. (2018). Developing metadiscourse through reflective assessment in knowledge building environments. _Computers & Education_ , _126_ , 153–169. \n\n- Lobato, J. (2008). Research methods for alternative approaches to transfer: Implications for design experiments. In A. E. Kelly, R. A. Lesh, & J. Y. Baek (Eds.), _Handbook of design research methods in education: Innovations in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics learning and teaching_ (pp. 167–194). Routledge. \n\n- Pekrun, R., Vogl, E., Muis, K. R., & Sinatra, G. M. (2017). Measuring emotions during epistemic activities: The epistemically-related emotion scales. _Cognition and Emotion_ , _31_ (6), 1268–1276. https://doi.org/10.1111/ cdev.12704 \n\n- Raes, A., Schellens, T., & De Wever, B. (2014). Web-based collaborative inquiry to bridge gaps in secondary science education. _Journal of the Learning Sciences_ , _23_ , 316–347. \n\n- Scardamalia, M. (2002). Collective cognitive responsibility for the advancement of knowledge. In B. Smith (Ed.), _Liberal education in a knowledge society_ (pp. 67–98). Open Court. \n\nFOSTERING LOW-ACHIEVING STUDENTS’ PRODUCTIVE DISCIPLINARY ENGAGEMENT \n\n**1529** \n\n- Scardamalia, M., & Bereiter, C. (2006). Knowledge building: Theory, pedagogy, and technology. In R. K. Sawyer (Ed.), _The Cambridge handbook of the learning sciences_ (pp. 97–116). Cambridge University Press. \n\nScardamalia, M., & Bereiter, C. (2014). Knowledge building and knowledge creation: Theory, pedagogy, and tech- \n\nnology. In R. K. Sawyer (Ed.), _The Cambridge handbook of the learning sciences_ (2nd ed., pp. 397–417). Cambridge University Press. \n\nShaffer, D. W. (2017). _Quantitative ethnography_ . Cathcart Press. \n\nShen, P. D., Lee, T. H., & Tsai, C. W. (2007). Applying Web-enabled problem-based learning and self-regulated learn- \n\n- ing to enhance computing skills of Taiwan\'s vocational students: A quasi-experimental study of a short-term module. _Electronic Journal of e-Learning_ , _5_ (2), 147–156. \n\nSinha, S., Rogat, T. K., Adams-Wiggins, K. R., & Hmelo-Silver, C. E. (2015). Collaborative group engagement in a computer-supported inquiry learning environment. _International Journal of Computer Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _10_ (3), 273–307. \n\nSnell, J., & Lefstein, A. (2018). “Low ability,” participation, and identity in dialogic pedagogy. _American Educational Research Journal_ , _55_ , 40–78. \n\nStahl, G. (2006). _Group cognition: Computer support for building collaborative knowledge_ . MIT Press. \n\nTao, D., & Zhang, J. (2021). Agency to transform: How did a grade 5 community co-configure dynamic knowledge building practices in a yearlong science inquiry? _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _16_ , 1–32. \n\nVan Aalst, J. (2009). Distinguishing knowledge sharing, construction, and creation discourses. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _4_ , 259–288. \n\nVan Aalst, J., & Chan, C. K. K. (2007). Student-directed assessment of knowledge building using electronic portfolios. _The Journal of the Learning Sciences_ , _16_ , 175–220. \n\nWhite, B., & Frederiksen, J. (1998). Inquiry, modeling, and metacognition: Making science accessible to all students. _Cognition and Instruction_ , _16_ , 3–118. \n\nWhite, B., & Frederiksen, J. (2005). A theoretical framework and approach for fostering metacognitive development. _Educational Psychologist_ , _40_ (4), 211–223. \n\nYang, Y. (2019). Reflective assessment for epistemic agency of academically low-achieving students. _Journal of Computer Assisted Learning_ , _35_ , 459–475. \n\n- Yang, Y., Chen, Q., Yu, Y., Feng, X., & van Aalst, J. (2020). Collective reflective assessment for shared epistemic \n\nagency by undergraduates in knowledge building. _British Journal of Educational Technology_ , _51_ , 1136–1154. Yang, Y., van Aalst, J., & Chan, C. K. K. (2020). Dynamics of reflective assessment and knowledge building for academically low-achieving students. _American Educational Research Journal_ , _57_ (3), 1241–1289. \n\n- Yang, Y., van Aalst, J., Chan, C. K. K., & Tian, W. (2016). Reflective assessment in knowledge building by students with low academic achievement. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _11_ , 281–311. \n\nYang, Y., Zhu, G., Sun, D., & Chan, C. K. K. (2022). Collaborative analytics-supported reflective assessment for scaffolding pre-service teachers’ collaborative inquiry and knowledge building. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _17_ , 249–292. \n\n- Yin, H., Lee, J. C.-K., & Wang, W. (2014). Dilemmas of leading national curriculum reform in a global era: A Chinese perspective. _Educational Management Administration & Leadership_ , _42_ (2), 293–311. \n\n- Zhang, J., Scardamalia, M., Lamon, M., Messina, R., & Reeve, R. (2007). Socio-cognitive dynamics of knowledge \n\n- building in the work of 9-and 10-year-olds. _Educational Technology Research and Development_ , _55_ , 117–145. \n\n- Zhang, J., Tao, D., Chen, M. H., Sun, Y., Judson, D., & Naqvi, S. (2018). Co-organizing the collective journey of inquiry with idea thread mapper. _Journal of the Learning Sciences_ , _27_ , 390–430. \n\n- Zohar, A., & Dori, Y. J. (2003). Higher order thinking skills and low-achieving students: Are they mutually exclusive? _Journal of the Learning Sciences_ , _12_ , 145–181. \n\n### **SUPPORTING INFORMATION** \n\nAdditional supporting information can be found online in the Supporting Information section at the end of this article. \n\n**How to cite this article:** Yang, Y., Yuan, K., Feng, X., Li, X.,& van Aalst, J. (2022). Fostering low-achieving students\' productive disciplinary engagement through knowledge-building inquiry and reflective assessment. _British Journal of Educational Technology_ , _53_ , 1511–1529. https://doi.org/10.1111/bjet.13267 \n\n', NULL, '2026-09-11 01:05:31', '2026-09-11 01:05:45', '2022 - Yang - Fostering low‐achieving students  productive disciplinary engagement through knowledge-building inquiry and reflective assessment.pdf', 'project_1021/raw/2022 - Yang - Fostering low‐achieving students  productive disciplinary engagement through knowledge-building inquiry and reflective assessment.pdf', 'application/pdf', 5678679, '9cd24710eda7a7c086ef45f0352075c02ef4ca3c3c6d0143c618f599dde13980', '9cd24710eda7a7c086ef45f0352075c02ef4ca3c3c6d0143c618f599dde13980', 'project_1021/parsed/2022 - Yang - Fostering low‐achieving students  productive disciplinary engagement through knowledge-building inquiry and reflective assessment.md', 'ready', NULL);
INSERT INTO `research_resource` VALUES (25, 3, 1023, 'UPLOAD', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'TEXT_EXTRACTED', 'Intern. J. Comput.-Support. Collab. Learn (2024) 19:231–272 https://doi.org/10.1007/s11412-024-09421-8 \n\n# **Reflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for scaffolding knowledge building competencies among undergraduate students** \n\n\n\n**Yuqin Yang**<sup>**1**</sup> **· Carol K. K. Chan**<sup>**2**</sup> **· Gaoxia Zhu**<sup>**3**</sup> **· Yuyao Tong**<sup>**2**</sup> **· Daner Sun**<sup>**4**</sup> \n\nReceived: 14 August 2023 / Accepted: 26 March 2024 / Published online: 13 May 2024 © International Society of the Learning Sciences, Inc. 2024 \n\n### **Abstract** \n\nKnowledge building (KB) competencies are crucial for undergraduates’ creative knowledge work and academic success. While there is substantial research on KB discourse, <u>there are limited efforts in examining how KB competencies in the conceptual, metacog-</u> nitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic dimensions are demonstrated in KB discourse and how the competencies can be scaffolded. Previous studies suggest the effectiveness of reflective assessment on sustainable and productive KB discourse. <u>This study developed a framework for analyzing KB competencies using KB discourse moves. It also examined</u> whether a KB design augmented by reflective assessment enriched by analytic tools and artifacts could foster undergraduates’ KB competencies, and if so, how. This KB design involves principle-based pedagogy with the participants engaging in collaborative inquiry and discussion on Knowledge Forum, and reflective assessment using (a) super synthesis notes, (b) KB interaction rubrics, and (c) learning analytics visualization tools. <u>Qualitative tracking and lag sequential pattern analysis of Knowledge Forum’s discourse revealed</u> that implementing reflective assessment supported by analytics and artifacts could help the undergraduate students develop KB competencies manifested in discourse with evidence of conceptual advance, epistemic engagement, metacognition, and productive socio-emotional interactions with a collective focus. The thematic analysis illustrated the dynamics through which the design enriched by standards and visualizations helped the undergraduates develop KB competencies: leveraging synthesis super notes to promote conceptual and metacognitive advancement and epistemic engagement; employing KB interaction rubrics to cultivate metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic competencies; and harnessing KBDeX visualizations to promote metacognitive and conceptual advancement and to facilitate epistemic engagement. The implications of scaffolding students’ epistemic agency, metacognition, productive collaborative inquiry, and developing KB competencies in a technology-supported metacognitive learning environment are discussed. \n\n**Keywords** Knowledge Building competencies · Knowledge Building discourse · Reflective assessment · Learning analytics · Undergraduate \n\nExtended author information available on the last page of the article \n\nVol.:(0123456789)1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n232 \n\n## **Introduction** \n\nCultivating students’ competencies to collaboratively inquire, explore learning, and participate in productive discourse using digital technologies is recognized as a valuable educational goal and may affect students’ longer-term development (Collins, 2017; Snell & Lefstein, 2018; Wise & Schwarz, 2017; Yang et al., 2020a). It is increasingly recognized that students need to be supported in developing core competencies to navigate in the complex, volatile, and uncertain world and prepare for the future (National Research Council, NRC, 2012; OECD, 2023). While there are many advocates and research on critical competencies such as 21st-century competencies (Chalkiadaki, 2018; Voogt & Roblin, 2012), digital competence (Pettersson, 2018), relatively less attention has been given to knowledgebuilding/creation competencies, which are critical for generating new ideas and advancing collective knowledge in the post-COVID, post-truth era. <u>There is no specific systematic framework for knowledge-building/creation competencies</u> that would encompass conceptual, metacognitive, epistemic, and socio-emotional dimensions. Empirical research on how to help students develop these knowledge-building/creation competencies connecting discourse moves is even rarer. \n\nComputer-supported collaborative inquiry holds the potential to foster the growth of students’ knowledge-building/creation competencies (Stahl, 2015; Tchounikine, 2019; Wise & Schwarz, 2017). This approach combines computer-supported collaborative learning (CSCL) with scientific inquiry that aims to help students work on authentic inquiry like real scientists (Bell et al., 2010) and in communities of scientists (Scardamalia & Bereiter, 2014) for progressive dialogue and continued advances. As a notable computersupported collaborative inquiry model, the Knowledge Building (KB) framework has the capacity to empower students to engage in authentic knowledge-creation cultures and practices and foster the development of advanced competencies (e.g., Chen & Hong, 2016; Zhang et al., 2022). KB, supported by Knowledge Forum, is designed to drive communal knowledge forward through discourse that centers on the progressive improvement of ideas (Scardamalia & Bereiter, 2014). To engage in KB culture and practices for creative work, students need to work on conceptual advances, and proactively generate and share questions and explanations (Hmelo-Silver & Barrows, 2008). They also need to engage in epistemic endeavors of the sustained pursuit of inquiry, tackle complex knowledge problems, and jointly build theories (Yang et al., 2022). Moreover, they must engage in reflective metadiscourse to assess and regulate their KB discourse, pinpointing areas of understanding gaps and steering inquiry in fruitful directions (Andrews-Todd & Forsyth, 2020; Sun et al., 2020; Van Uum et al., 2016; Yang et al., 2022; Yang, Feng, et al., 2023). This entails active interaction with peers within the community, both socially and intellectually. By drawing on social–affective–emotional dispositions, learners can effectively participate, contribute, and foster inclusive collaboration (Hong et al., 2019; Yang et al., 2020b; Yang et al., 2022). These are all considered as students’ knowledge-building/creation competencies. \n\n<u>While</u> the importance of developing core and 21st-century competencies has been widely recognized (NRC, 2012; OECD, 2023), challenges exist in examining and nurturing competencies (Voogt & Roblin, 2012). We argue that CSCL/KB environments and discourse moves can provide useful contexts for promoting and examining these competencies in practice. In CSCL research, students are provided rich environments to collaborate for shared problem solving and KB; simultaneously, their discourse interactions and discourse moves can provide evidence and outcomes of developing competencies. For example, when \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n233 \n\nstudents are engaged in discourse moves of question generation and explanation in collaborative inquiry, they are deploying and developing cognitive–conceptual competencies; discourse moves of progressive explanations for deepening inquiry and rise-above coordinating diverse views suggest students’ epistemic disposition for tackling complex knowledge problems; when discourse moves show they are actively encouraging peers to join with positive reaction to problems, they demonstrate socio-emotional dispositions and competence. We propose linking key cognitive, metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic competencies with discourse moves and making CSCL contexts more relevant to addressing major educational issues such as developing core and 21st-century competencies. \n\n<u>There is a need to consider a more comprehensive framework of KB competencies connecting to discourse in interactions as evidence and outcomes. Previous KB and CSCL</u> studies have shown the centrality of examining interaction and discourse moves (see Jeong et al., 2014 for a review); coding schemes can provide important information about collaboration (Chen et al., 2019). In particular, the KB approach emphasizes how discourse is in essence about KB evidence (Bereiter & Scardamalia, 2016). KB/creation competencies go beyond acquisition or co-constructing understanding of existing knowledge (van Aalst, 2009) and involve collectively generating new ideas and advancing knowledge. Yet many students often lack the awareness and competencies to engage in creative work. Therefore, it is important to characterize these competencies and devise suitable scaffolding strategies and complementary tools to nurture such competencies based on prior work (Resendes et al., 2015; Tao & Zhang, 2018; Yang et al., 2020b; Yang et al., 2020c). Reflective assessment significantly focuses on learners’ active participation in utilizing criteria or learning standards for productive reflection and regulation within their inquiry process (White & Fredericksen, 1998). It can enable students to learn through spiraling cycles (Scardamalia, 2002; White & Fredericksen, 1998; Yang et al., 2020b). Previous studies have illuminated the scaffolding impact of reflective assessment on students’ scientific reasoning (White & Fredericksen, 1998), as well as its application in KB discourse guided by KB principles and portfolios. These efforts have been instrumental in fostering the development of higher-order skills and enhancing domain understanding, as evidenced by the works of the Yang and colleagues (2006, 2007, 2016, 2022) and related work (e.g., Toth et al., 2002). A key idea of reflective assessment is that students need to examine their current work in relation to expected standards, models, and artifacts for identifying gaps and areas of improvement and plans (Borge et al., 2018; White & Fredericksen, 1998). With the recent emergence of learning analytics, reflective assessment can be further enriched using analytics rubrics and visualizations of student-generated data. For example, in the KB classroom, students wrote on the Knowledge Forum to collaboratively generate and pursue inquiry questions. They also engaged in reflecting and reviewing their discourse supported by portfolios (Lee et al., 2006; van Aalst & Chan, 2007) and using analytics to visualize Knowledge Forum discourse (Yang et al., 2016, 2020c) as students metacognitively identified what they have accomplished, what gaps exist, and what further work is needed for deepening inquiry. We have conducted some studies on KB emphasizing discourse and analytics-supported reflective assessment and found the positive impacts of analytics-supported reflective assessment on productive KB discourse and knowledge advancement (Yang et al., 2020b; Yang et al., 2020c; Yang et al., 2022). Specifically, we have shown how reflective assessment supported low achievers developing collaborative, metacognitive, and epistemic competencies for KB productive inquiry (Yang et al., 2020a). lective \n\n<u>Therefore, the present study designed a KB environment augmented by reflective assessment mediated by artifacts and analytics tools to help the undergraduates develop these KB competencies. We aim to develop a framework of KB competencies in relation</u> \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n234 \n\nto discourse to assist in cultivating undergraduates’ KB competencies. More specifically, the study sought to examine the design and dynamics of reflective assessment for engaging undergraduates in developing their KB competencies. <u>We used the super synthesis notes contributed by previous cohorts of undergraduates as exemplars, KB interaction rubrics, and KBDeX to support undergraduates’ reflective assessment. KBDeX, a tool tailored</u> for social network analysis, was meticulously crafted to intricately examine conversations occurring within the context of KB discourse (Oshima et al., 2012). \n\n## **Conceptual framework and literature review** \n\n### **KB community model** \n\nThe KB model stands as a principle-driven collaborative inquiry approach, highlighting learners’ active engagement in advancing collective knowledge and fostering innovative intellectual efforts through purposeful dialogue (Scardamalia & Bereiter, 2014). In KB classrooms, students collaboratively shape problems, refine concepts, and evolve theories through progressive discourse, mirroring the processes employed by scientists within their scientific community to push the boundaries of shared knowledge. Therefore, the cornerstone of the KB approach lies in productive discourse, wherein learners collectively drive the evolution of community knowledge (Scardamalia & Bereiter, 2014). Scardamalia and <u>Bereiter (2014) assert that the success of KB hinges upon the success of KB discourse itself. Consequently, a pivotal objective within a KB environment is to empower learners</u> with shared responsibility for nurturing KB discourse. Productive KB discourse, as emphasized by Yang (2019), revolves around propelling collective inquiry forward, fostering elevated forms of inquiry, and engaging in metadiscourse by vigilantly monitoring progress and reflection. \n\nKnowledge Forum was designed as a collaborative discourse space to support ideacentered discourse, in which learners can develop discourse from a KB perspective with the aid of its various technological functions (e.g., building on others’ ideas, linking ideas, revising ideas, and using rise-above ideas) and scaffolds (e.g., “My opinion,” “I need to understand,” and “My theory”). Furthermore, Scardamalia (2002) developed 12 principles that consisted of a system to characterize KB and scaffold teachers and students to evolve into productive KB discourse. These KB principles, namely community knowledge, improvable ideas, epistemic agency, reflective and transformative assessment, and rise above, are particularly conducive to undergraduates’ productive KB discourse. \n\nNumerous explanations of KB, including its fundamental principles and recent research discoveries, can be found within educational literature focused on learning sciences (see review, Chen & Hong, 2016; Scardamalia, 2002; Scardamalia & Bereiter, 2014). Applying KB within classroom environments has yielded favorable outcomes regarding KB discourse, as evidenced by the positive results documented in KB studies (e.g., Hod & Katz, 2020; Tao & Zhang, 2021; Yang et al., 2016; Yang, 2019). KB involves learners working with multiple discussions in different working spaces (called views) on the Knowledge Forum. This process surpasses mere ideation and problem solving, extending to require active involvement in the co-design of their collective inquiry and metadiscourse. Codesign, in the context of our study, refers to a collaborative process where both the teacher and students jointly design the inquiry experience. Traditionally, the teacher assumes the primary role in establishing instructional conditions. However, in our approach, students \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n235 \n\nare elevated to the role of co-designers, actively participating in shaping their own learning experiences. This methodology fosters a dynamic learning environment where the teacher and students engage in inquiry simultaneously, creating a more integrated and interactive educational experience. In metadiscourse, students monitor and reflect on the community’s idea of sustained knowledge advancement, identify new inquiries for continued discussion, and synthesize the community’s ideas for the higher-level conceptualization of knowledge and development of theories (Chan & van Aalst, 2018; Yang et al., 2020c). In this manner, learners construct a holistic comprehension of the organizational framework underpinning their inquiries. This approach prevents the emergence of fragmented and brief deliberations that lack conceptual advancement and impede the process of generating knowledge. Thus, learners need to be provided with the appropriate tools and scaffoldings to help them engage in metadiscourse with collective agency. Collective agency in the context of our study refers to the collaborative endeavor where students work together, taking shared responsibility for their KB inquires and knowledge building. \n\n### **KB competencies and discourse moves** \n\nSince the last two decades, developing students 21st-century competencies has been widely recognized to help them participate in the global knowledge economy, thrive in diverse societies, and to use new technologies effectively for facing change and uncertainty (NRC, 2012; OECD, 2023). <u>Competencies can be defined as a set of knowledge, skills, affects, and dispositions that students can use and apply in different contexts (Ewell, 2005; Kulikowich & Alexander, 2003; NRC, 2012) that enable individuals to perform tasks, solve problems, and achieve goals effectively. While there have now been many key competencies</u> lists (e.g., 21st-century competencies and digital competence, Chalkiadaki, 2018; Pettersson, 2018; Voogt & Roblin, 2012) emphasizing critical thinking, creativity, collaboration, problem-solving, and digital skills (see Voogt & Roblin, 2012 for an analysis of competencies lists), little attention has focused on knowledge building/creation competencies emphasizing generation and advances of new knowledge in community contexts. There are also issues that competencies are often examined as individual-based competency/skills rather than collective-based competency, which are important for innovation in the current knowledge era. Competencies are often considered as sets of skills to be taught rather than emergent from students’ experience in innovative environments. <u>Recent work on Learning Compass 2030 (OECD) advocated core competencies highlighting student agency and transformative competencies that strike similar chords with KB’s emphasis on collective agency and transformation.</u> \n\nHelping students develop KB competencies is crucial for them to become generative learners, inquirers, and knowledge builders to meet current complex challenges. KB com- <u>petencies refer to students’ capacities, knowledge, skills, and dispositions needed to engage in productive knowledge building/creation and practices. Competencies are complex and</u> there are common challenges in nurturing and assessing core competencies for transformative learning (Voogt & Rublin, 2012). We propose that CSCL environments can provide rich opportunities to nurture competencies and students’ collaborative discourse moves can provide pointers, evidence, and outcomes. Competences and discourse moves are reciprocally related and the deployment of productive collaborative discourse moves can promote competencies. Analyzing students’ discourse is a major research endeavor in CSCL and a common way to understand students’ knowledge, skills, and affect in CSCL (Jeong et al., 2014) and collaboration (Chen et al., 2019). In our previous research (Yang et al., 2022), \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n236 \n\nwe have started examining how discourse moves reflect different kinds of competences and we are refining the approach for identifying more comprehensive dimensions reflecting knowledge building. In this study, we attempt to provide a more systematic account linking KB competencies with discourse moves as outcomes and evidence. Using discourse markers to illustrate and to support the development of pragmatic competence has been demonstrated in language studies (House, 2013)—participants’ use of discourse markers provides evidence and _reinforces_ their pragmatic competencies. Accordingly, this study relies on KB discourse to examine their demonstrated KB competencies. \n\nIn this study, we focus on KB emphasizing generation and advances of knowledge to expand on the general descriptions of creative, critical, collaborative, and innovative skills commonly described in 21st-century competencies. In developing the KB competency framework, we draw from core competencies and CSCL coding schemes enriched with KB theoretical constructs. We focused on frameworks emphasizing competencies for deep learning as they are more aligned with learning sciences, and we adapted from the NRC <u>framework (Hilton & Pellegrino, 2012) encompassing the (a) cognitive, (b) intrapersonal (metacognitive), and (c) interpersonal (social-communicative) areas. We also adapted from the analysis of CSCL coding schemes as lenses on collaborative learning (Chen et al., 2019) that identifies three key areas of (a) cognitive domains (including metacognition), (b) social domains (group management, encouraging talk, affective), and (c) integrated domains (building ideas, evaluation). In developing the framework, we added an epistemic</u> dimension to cognitive, metacognitive, and social-emotional areas emphasized in knowledge building, highlighting epistemic commitment in the sustained pursuit of knowledge and enriched socio-emotional dimension needed in collaborative knowledge building. While KB research studies have been conducted to help students develop KB discourse (e.g., Chen, 2017; Chen et al., 2015; Yang et al., 2020b; Yang et al., 2022; Yuan et al., 2022), disparate coding systems/schemes have been employed to depict quality discourse and epistemic development (e.g., Resendes et al., 2015; Yang, Feng, et al., 2023; Yang et al., 2024). In this study, by reviewing competencies and these existing coding systems/ schemes and being informed by the KB theory, we proposed a framework of KB competencies encompassing four areas: conceptual (cognitive), metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic dimensions with related discourse moves as evidence. All four dimensions <u>have a collective focus emphasizing collaborative inquiry for knowledge advances.</u> \n\n<u>The conceptual (cognitive) dimension emphasizes the cognitive processes for conceptual understanding (e.g., see CSCL coding scheme comparison from Chen et al., 2019) and KB coding schemes including asking fact-based and explanation-based questions, generating claims and arguments, co-constructing explanations, improving ideas, and making summaries</u> (e.g., Resendes et al., 2015; Tao & Zhang, 2018). Previous coding schemes in knowledge building, for example, Tao and Zhang (2018) also included applied questioning (i.e., factual question, explanatory question, idea-initiating wonderment, and idea deepening/elaborating question) and explanations (i.e., intuitive explanation, alternative explanation, refined explanation) to code students’ online discourse for analyzing their contributions to the collective discourse. Asking thought-provoking questions (i.e., factual questions, design questions, and explanatory questions) and improving supporting explanations and seeking alternatives are also included in the Ways of Contributing to Explanation-Seeking Discourse framework (Chuy et al., 2011). lection, moni- \n\n<u>Metacognition and metadiscourse competencies in CSCL, involving reflection, monitoring, review, and shared regulation (Järvelä et al., 2016) are critical for supporting and directing sustained knowledge-related collaborative inquiry (Borge et al., 2018; Damşa,</u> 2014; De Backer et al., 2021; Dorfsman, 2018). KB metacognition and metadiscourse \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n237 \n\ncompetencies reflect students’ joint endeavor to reflect on and assess the current state of communal knowledge and progress within the context of the community (Zhu & Lin, 2023). In this context, students’ discourse becomes the objects of discussion, evaluation, and refinement (Resendes et al., 2015). Specifically, students engage in metacognition and metadiscourse through reflecting, inquiring about the trajectory of discourse, tracking the progress of collective ideas, identifying gaps, and charting new directions collectively for knowledge advancement. fective–emotional competencies \n\n<u>The socio-emotional interactions focus on social–affective–emotional competencies needed to support collaborative KB. CSCL and KB increasingly emphasize the impor-</u> tance of affective and emotional competencies (Järvenoja & Järvelä, 2009; Näykki et al., 2017). <u>This dimension includes students’ competencies to engage in positive socioemotional interactions that benefit productive group functioning such as active partici-</u> pation, encouraging participation/inclusion, and fostering community cohesion as well as their dispositions/attitudes to positively address potential negative socio-emotional interactions such as discouraging marginalization and approaching mistakes positively (Rogat & Linnenbrink-Garcia, 2011; Rogat & Adams-Wiggins, 2015). \n\n<u>The epistemic dimension focuses on how students work with knowledge with epistemic commitments continually pursuing deepening inquiry building and revising their theories. Students conduct collective inquiry intentionally, taking charge in working on</u> unresolved problems. Epistemic competencies are connected to epistemic agency, a key principle of KB that involves students taking collective cognitive responsibility, identifying promising areas of inquiry, using knowledge sources constructively for evidence, engaging in problem-centered inquiry, negotiating the fit between their model with scientific model, creating and revising joint theory toward theory building, and working toward rise above to achieve parsimonious explanation and theories (Scardamalia, 2002; Yang, Feng, et al., 2023). Previous coding schemes in knowledge building have examined how students continue to pursue for deepening and sustained inquiry, and working with diverse views creating rise-above conceptualization (Chuy et al., 2011). These kinds of engagement suggest students’ different epistemic stances and goals in viewing and tackling knowledge. While both epistemic agency and collective agency are integral to the KB process, they differ in their focus. Collective agency emphasizes the social and collaborative aspects of learning, underscoring the importance of group dynamics and emotional intelligence in the learning process. In contrast, epistemic agency focuses more on the cognitive responsibilities of students in the knowledge-building process, particularly how they engage with and contribute to the collective understanding and advancement of knowledge within their learning community. \n\nTo summarize, KB competencies can be seen as the basis and underlying framework that supports productive discourse and creative knowledge work. KB competencies encompass different dimensions including the cognitive–conceptual, metacognitive, <u>socio-emotional, and epistemic knowledge–skills–dispositions for students to engage in</u> meaningful and productive discourse. These competencies are not mutually exclusive and often overlap across dimensions. We consider discourse moves as observable manifestations of KB competencies—discourse moves reflect how individuals apply their competencies in practice as they engage in specific communicative actions to contribute to collective KB efforts. While there may be connections between the two, KB competencies provide a comprehensive and integrated framework for KB, where discourse moves to offer a more specific lens for examining the communicative actions within the KB process. \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n238 \n\nProductive discourse is an important medium for undergraduates to develop these KB competencies. Competencies and discourse moves are reciprocally related as competencies <u>make it possible for students to engage in productive discourse moves, and engagement in multiple rich discourse moves support further development of competencies. Research</u> on discourse, a key research theme in CSCL and the education community, has examined how productive learning can be developed through productive discourse with the affordances of technologies (Chen et al., 2015; Zhang et al., 2020). Nevertheless, numerous students, including those enrolled in undergraduate programs, encounter challenges when it comes to effectively engaging in productive discourse for developing KB competencies. Therefore, additional scaffoldings and tools are needed to help them engage in productive discourse and develop KB competencies regarding the four dimensions described above. \n\n### **Reflective assessment** \n\nReflective assessment is a scaffolding strategy in which the learners assume responsibility for reflecting on and regulating their inquiries and learning against precise evidence/ criteria of their inquiries and learning, and figure out methods to regulate their inquiries and learning for continuous advancement (Yang et al., 2016, 2020a). Within reflective assessment, metacognitive processes such as planning, monitoring, reflection, and regulation propel learners to manifest their metacognitive acumen and proficiencies. This, in turn, guides their endeavors in maintaining the momentum of their inquiries and learning. Active engagement with these metacognitive processes stands as a pivotal factor in fostering meaningful discourse within computer-supported collaborative inquiries (Borge et al., 2018; De Backer et al., 2021; Yang, 2019; Yang et al., 2016, 2020a). In the KB community model, reflection assumes a collaborative nature, permitting learners to scaffold each other’s enhancement of metacognitive awareness and skills through emulation. Importantly, not every learner needs to have high-level metacognitive awareness and skills to participate actively. \n\nPrevious research on portfolios-supported reflective assessment in KB classrooms has demonstrated that such reflective assessment can effectively help learners improve domain understanding and KB discourse (Chan & van Aalst, 2018; Lee et al., 2006; van Aalst & Chan, 2007) and metadiscourse (Lei & Chan, 2018). Nonetheless, we posit that reflective assessment shares epistemological similarities with other domain-specific inquiries, as it is a problem-solving process informed by exact data and evidence of the inquiry process and outcomes. Our prior research has examined reflective assessment using analytics (e.g., KBDeX, Promising Idea tool). These studies are designed to collectively elevate metacognitive awareness among undergraduates and encourage the utilization and cultivation of their metacognitive skills by a learning analytics design (Yang et al., 2020b, 2022; Yang, Feng, et al., 2023). The undergraduates used these analytics tools that provided them with evidence on collective KB (Yang et al., 2020b, 2022). These investigations spotlight the favorable impacts of employing analytics-supported reflective assessment in helping undergraduates engage in productive KB and develop higher-order competencies such as inquiry, knowledge creation, and agency. \n\nThis study builds upon the existing body of research and introduces a reflective assessment process focusing on developing undergraduates’ KB competencies and collective advances. While there is supportive evidence from previous reflective assessment studies in KB classrooms that aid undergraduates in effective KB (Yang et al., 2020b, 2022; Yang, Feng, et al., 2023), there is a need to investigate further what makes reflective assessment \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n239 \n\neffective, (supported by analytics) and to track the process and dynamics through which reflective assessment fostering undergraduates’ KB competencies. \n\nResearchers have proposed that reflection and assessment are effective when students are provided with models, examples, and standards (e.g., White & Fredericksen, 1998; Borge et al., 2018). We extended our earlier work and enriched reflective assessment using (a) super synthesis notes, (b) KB interaction rubrics, (c) visualization and data extracted from undergraduates’ collaborative efforts on Knowledge Forum through KBDeX (Oshima et al., 2012), and (d) accompanying scaffolds and prompt sheets that were designed for productive use of KB socio-collaborative interaction rubrics and KBDeX. The super synthesis notes were created by previous cohorts of students in reflective assessment using KBDeX or KB interaction rubrics. These notes serve as a repository of students’ reflective analyses and action plans for the regulation of their KB inquiries and ideas. Derived from KB principles and informed by the work of van Aalst (2009) and Borge et al. (2018), the KB interaction rubrics offer a concrete framework delineating desired KB interactions and behaviors. These rubrics furnish students with a means to assess and evaluate their KBrelated interactions and behavior. KBDeX, originally developed by Oshima and colleagues (2012) as a tool for analyzing online discourse data to visualize community progress, has predominantly been utilized by researchers. In this study, we have repurposed KBDeX to generate visualization diagrams that offer ongoing feedback to students concerning their Knowledge Forum discussions. Specifically, Knowledge Forum discourse has been input to KBDeX, and a list of conceptual keywords was generated. KBDeX then can generate the visualizations automatically based on the input Knowledge Forum data. \n\n### **The present study** \n\nHelping undergraduates develop KB competencies is crucial for their creative knowledge work and academic success (Yang et al, 2020b, 2022). However, there is a lack of a comprehensive KB competencies framework, and empirical research on how to support students in developing KB competencies is rare. Previous studies (Yang et al., 2020b, 2020c, 2022) have designed analytics-supported reflective assessments to scaffold undergraduates’ productive KB discourse (i.e., observable manifestations of KB competencies) in KB classrooms and suggested the effectiveness of the design. Therefore, the present study aimed to develop a KB competencies framework and to investigate whether and how the design of reflective assessment engaged undergraduates in developing their KB competencies. \n\nInformed by the above-discussed literature, the present study first proposed a specific KB competencies framework that included cognitive-conceptual, metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic dimensions premised on KB theory emphasizing collective knowledge (e.g., Damşa, 2014; Scardamalia & Bereiter, 2014; Sun et al., 2020; Yang et al., 2022; Yang, van Aalst, et al., 2020c). We then proposed a design framework that synergizes KB inquiry design with reflective assessment (Fig. 1). The study is premised on the following KB principles to develop the conceptual and design framework: community knowledge, improvable ideas, epistemic agency, reflective and transformative assessment, and rise above. Guided by these principles, two key design components were included: \n\n> (1) <u>KB inquiry in which the undergraduates engaged in idea-oriented progressive inquiry and worked on Knowledge Forum to collaboratively improve inquiry questions and explanations and advance collective knowledge.</u> \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n240 \n\n\n\n**Fig. 1** A framework of KB design augmented by reflective assessment for undergraduates’ KB competencies \n\n- (2)  Reflective assessment enriched by super synthesis notes, KB interaction rubrics, <u>KBDeX visualizations and data, and accompanying prompt sheets. Reflective assess-</u> ment enriched by super synthesis notes aimed to facilitate the undergraduates collaboratively and reflectively analyzed the conceptual aspects and growth of their inquiry, and co-design their inquiry. The KB interaction rubrics provided students with a concrete model of desired behaviors to evaluate their behavior, identify gaps, and devise strategies to achieve desired behaviors. KBDeX can help students reflect on and synthesize what they have inquired about or not, select the community’s most promising ideas for further inquiry, and regulate and deepen their further inquiry. \n\nA set of tasks and scaffolds were created for this KB design to help the undergraduates develop KB competencies. We mapped these tasks and scaffolds onto KB competencies and tracked how reflective assessment supported undergraduates’ development in KB competencies. Discourse is central, and KB competencies via discourse are assessed using online Knowledge Forum discourse investigating how students develop conceptual, metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic advances. \n\n- 1) How do undergraduates demonstrate their KB competencies in the conceptual, metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic dimensions? (RQ1) \n\n- 2) Do undergraduates’ KB competencies change with the KB design augmented by reflective assessment? If so, how do the undergraduates improve their KB competencies through this design? (RQ2) \n\n## **Methods** \n\n### **Research context and participants** \n\nThe research was carried out within a Scientific Inquiry and Knowledge Creation course at a research-intensive university in central China. The participants were 41 undergraduates (mainly in their third year) from one intact class, from 20 specialties in 8 faculties. The \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n241 \n\nclass experienced a KB environment enriched by reflective assessment. The primary aim of the course was to equip the undergraduates with the fundamental knowledge and essential skills required for effective engagement in inquiry and knowledge creation. The students and course instructor met each workday for 3 hours over 2 weeks for the course. The instructor, possessing 2 years of teaching experience and a doctoral degree in learning sciences, brought a wealth of expertise to the instructional setting. The students were organized into small groups comprising five to seven members for collaborative inquiry. Each of these groups was assigned a specific inquiry topic, such as scientific thinking, argumentation, or knowledge creation. These topics revolved around the collaborative exploration of methods to collectively participate in scientific inquiry and knowledge creation. \n\n### **Design of the KB environment enriched by reflective assessment** \n\nGuided by the five key KB principles, including community knowledge, improvable ideas, epistemic agency, reflective and transformative assessment, and rise-above, we designed a computer-supported knowledge environment. The pedagogical design (Fig. 2) was developed following earlier studies (Lee et al., 2006; Yang, 2019; Yang et al., 2020c, 2022) that <u>include three key components.</u> \n\n**Component 1—Establishing a collaborative and self‑directed culture to foster effective KB (days 1–3)** The instructor orchestrated a sequence of instructional elements aimed at stimulating constructive KB among the undergraduates (as depicted in Fig. 2). As an illustration, to nurture the students’ self-guided learning, an initiative was undertaken wherein they were encouraged to formalize a “learning contract.” This contract encompassed a comprehensive agenda outlining course activities, accompanied by corresponding assessment \n\n\n\n**Fig. 2** The three-component KB design augmented by reflective assessment for undergraduates’ KB competencies \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n242 \n\nweightage. To help the undergraduates develop collaboration skills and motivation and reflect on how to maintain motivation and persistence in scientific inquiry and learning, small groups were required to create a poster collaboratively (Fig. 3a and 3b). To help the undergraduates engage in productive reflective writing each day and further develop metacognition, scaffolding prompts such as “what I have not and have known about the topic, and what I would like to inquire more” were provided. Generally, the instructor in each lesson created whole-class or small-group face-to-face/online discussions to assist the undergraduates in developing skills and habits of questioning—explaining, building on, and sustaining ideas. \n\n**Component 2—Deepening idea‑centered KB discourse on Knowledge Forum (days 2–4)** The undergraduates were asked to <u>work on their inquiries using the Knowledge Forum platform (Fig.</u> 4). Initially, they were asked to contribute high-quality questions and explanations and to analyze the characteristics of good KB socio-emotional interaction patterns (e.g., lengthy discussion threads, responding to others’ ideas). Additionally, \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n(a) An example poster designed by one student group on how to maintain passion and persistence<br>(b) A student group worked collaboratively to make  (c) A student group engaged in reflective assessment<br>a poster  using KBDeX visualizations and data<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n**Fig. 3** Illustrative examples of KB principle-based activities for facilitating the undergraduates’ KB competencies \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n243 \n\n\n\n**Fig. 4** Screenshots of Knowledge Forum view and note \n\nthe student groups were required to establish objectives for their inquiry topics and devise <u>detailed research plans (Fig.</u> 3b). These plans would guide their investigations in subsequent phases. To support the undergraduates’ participation and interaction in productive KB discourse and co-directed inquiry, the instructor frequently used data from the integrated assessment tools of Knowledge Forum to arrange opportunities for joint reflection. \n\n**Component 3—Supporting undergraduates’ co‑design of inquiries, rise‑above and metadiscourse, and fostering productive KB by involving them in reflective assessment (days 5–14)** Figure 5 illustrates our reflective assessment design and scaffolds for developing KB competencies with conceptual, epistemic, socio-emotional, and metacognitive dimensions. \n\nFirst, on day 5, each student group was required to conduct a reflective assessment <u>using super synthesis notes (as shown in Fig.</u> 6a). These notes encapsulate sophisticated \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n244 \n\n\n\n**Fig. 5** Reflective assessment enriched with analytics and artifacts for KB competencies \n\nKB inquiries conducted by previous student cohorts and serve as standards and examples for the students’ reflections. Furthermore, each group was encouraged to revisit and refine <u>their initial research plans. This revision aimed to enhance the depth and quality of their</u> subsequent inquiries. \n\nOn days 6 and 10, they were provided with <u>KBDeX visualizations and data (Fig.</u> 6b and Fig. 6c) to conduct two rounds of reflective assessment. We employed two distinct graphical representations, generated by KBDeX, as our intervention strategy. These representations comprise the <u>degree centrality visualization and the idea-network map. The</u> first visualization pertains to the temporal evolution of cumulative degree centrality, which functions as an evaluative tool for gauging the collective advancement of knowledge (Oshima et al., 2012). A higher cumulative degree centrality value indicates a denser social network. Students can utilize this metric to monitor the progression of their collective knowledge over time. The second graphical representation materializes as a keywords network, often referred to as the idea-network map. This map effectively illustrates the arrangement of ideas, with keywords possessing a greater degree centrality occupying central positions within the network. By employing this visualization, students are empowered to grasp the trajectory of ideas and discern potential gaps that warrant further exploration. Particularly, on day 6 of the study, the undergraduates were guided through a reflective assessment exercise, facilitated by group-level KBDeX visualizations and accompanying data (Fig. 3c). These tools assisted the students in conducting a comprehensive gap analysis, synthesizing information, and attaining an elevated vantage point that transcended the community’s ideas. An overview of the methods used by the instructor to facilitate students’ reflective assessment process through KBDeX was presented in Appendix A. Additionally, to support the students’ development in various dimensions of their KB competencies, they were provided with prompt sheets. These sheets included a variety of cognitive and metacognitive prompts, specifically designed to aid different dimensions of their learning. (refer to Table S1 in the supplementary file). \n\nUpon reaching day 10, a subsequent phase of reflective assessment was implemented using KBDeX. This stage involved a retrospective contemplation encompassing all the \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n245 \n\n**Fig. 6** Artifacts and visualizations used for productive reflective assessment \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n(a) A sample super synthesis notes illustrating collective knowledge building as an exemplar (written by<br>a group of average performance)<br>(b) Growth curve of cumulative degree centrality<br>(c) Idea-network map of ideas/keywords network generated by KBDeX.<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\ndiscussion threads generated by the entire class throughout the semester. Meanwhile, on day 8, the student groups were provided with <u>prompt sheets and scaffolded</u> to conduct \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n246 \n\nreflective assessments using KB interaction rubrics (refer to Table S2 in the supplementary file). This initiative was crafted with the intention of guiding the students in scrutinizing and appraising their KB interactions. These assessments also encompassed the intricate dynamics of idea negotiations and the formulation of strategies aimed at achieving their desired interaction patterns. Additionally, as in Component 2, the data on undergraduates’ participation and interactions were provided in support of undergraduates’ collaborative reflection. \n\n### **Data sources and analysis** \n\n### **Demonstration of undergraduates’ KB competencies: Manifested by knowledge forum discourse moves** \n\nTo examine the KB competencies of undergraduates in the KB environment enriched by reflective assessment (RQ1), we analyzed 1390 Knowledge Forum notes contributed by the <u>41 participating undergraduates. As explained in the ‘Literature review’ section, we argue</u> that the undergraduates’ KB competencies were manifested through their evolving KB discourse. Regarding analyzing these notes, our initial step involved conducting an inquiry thread analysis. <u>Inquiry thread analysis</u> is an analytical technique used to reconstruct original physical strings of notes into different inquiry threads, where an inquiry thread is defined as a sequence of notes addressing a common core issue (Zhang et al., 2007). The <u>1390 KB notes were assigned to 24 inquiry threads. This process was crucial for preproc-</u> essing and categorizing the online discussions into distinct inquiry threads. Such segregation allowed us to delve deeper into the specific areas of inquiry pursued by the undergraduate students. \n\nNext, we used a coding scheme (Table 1) to examine the notes within inquiry threads through content analysis. The scheme was developed and refined based on previous studies that examined the KB discourse characteristics (Tao & Zhang, 2018; Yang, 2019; Yang et al., 2022) and students’ socio-emotional interactions (Rogat et al., 2025; Rogat et al., 2011). The scheme consisted of different categories, including conceptual development, metacognition and metadiscourse, socio-emotional interactions, and epistemic engagement. The creation of the coding framework resulted from an iterative process, combining theoretical and data-driven coding methods. Initially, a basic set of categories was established and continually refined through an interactive process. This led to the identification of a set of categories derived empirically. Two raters (the first author and another researcher with expertise in qualitative discourse analysis within the research team) independently coded the notes in six inquiry threads ( _<u>n</u>_ = 470, 33.81%). They achieved interrater consistency of <u>96% for cognitive–conceptual process, 96% for metacognition and metadiscourse, 94% for socio-emotional interactions, and 93% for epistemic engagement.</u> \n\n### **Undergraduates’ development of KB competencies and how such development was supported by reflective assessment** \n\nWe further examined the effects of reflective assessment on undergraduates’ development of KB competencies by analyzing the changes in KB competencies and how reflective assessment supported such development (RQ2). \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n247 \n\n|**Descriptions of discourse moves**|Asking questions that prompt the definition of terms or<br>concepts or inquiring for factual information about<br>specific subjects.<br>Posing questions that require elaborative, open-ended<br>responses.<br>Share opinions without justifying or elaborating them,<br>indicating shared or different opinions, or understand-<br>ing.<br>Elaborating on terms or phenomena; proposing partial<br>explanations, reasons, relationships, or mechanisms<br>mentioned without elaboration.<br>Providing in-depth explanations of reasons, relationships,<br>or mechanisms.<br>Providing summaries and synthesis of diverse ideas and<br>generating new ideas/theories.<br>Generating statements or questioning understanding and<br>explanations of concepts/ideas to prompt metacogni-<br>tive monitoring, reflecting on and regulation of inquiry<br>process and/or understanding<br>Monitoring and checking inquiry process and/or under-<br>standing, identifying missing knowledge or inconsisten-<br>cies in relation to various aspects of the inquiry process<br>and/or understanding<br>l<br>Reflecting on the inquiry process and ideas constructed,<br>identifying high points or gaps, regulating collaborative<br>efforts to move to a new state of inquiry or to achieve<br>high-level conceptualization|\n|---|---|\n|**Discourse moves**|-<br>Generating fact-seeking questions<br>Generating explanation-seeking questions<br>Generating a simple claim/argument<br>Proposing an elaboration<br>Contributing an explanation<br>Making summaries<br>Creating metacognitive awareness using reflection/<br>monitoring<br>Conducting major review of community ideas<br>Reflecting on and regulating inquiry: Metadiscourse—<br>inquiry into discourse and tracking trajectory of<br>inquiry|\n|**Knowledge Building (KB) competencies**|**Conceptual–cognitive**<br>Competencies in co-constructing and advancing ideas<br>through asking questions; generating claims and argu<br>ments; making elaboration; creating and evaluating<br>alternative explanations; and making summaries<br>**Metacognitive**<br>Competencies in personal and shared metacognition<br>including goal setting, monitoring, reviewing, and<br>regulating own and shared knowledge advances;<br>reflect on the development and progress of joint<br>inquiry identifying what is accomplished, what gaps<br>remain for further advances|\n\n\n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n248 \n\n|**Descriptions of discourse moves**|Demonstrate attentiveness to others in the group by<br>leaning in, acknowledging contributions, and actively<br>sharing ideas.<br>Fostering participation and contributions of all group<br>members to maintain their engagement.|Strengthening the team dynamic by collaborating effec-<br>tively and using inclusive language such as “we” to<br>emphasize collective effort.|Taking actions to include everyone, especially in situa-<br>tions where exclusion is increasing.|Approaching mistakes positively and treating them<br>as opportunities for inquiry and idea improvement,<br>addressing them constructively to enhance understand-<br>ing and theory building.|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|**Knowledge Building (KB) competencies**<br>**Discourse moves**|**Socio-emotional**<br>Competencies in communication and collaboration,<br>social dynamics; maintaining community and social<br>cohesion, inclusivity of members and positive affect in<br>face of failure<br>Active listening/participating<br>Encouraging participation/inclusion|Fostering cohesion|Discouraging marginalization|Approaching mistakes positively|\n\n\n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n249 \n\n|**Descriptions of discourse moves**|Posing problems, identifying inquiry focus and lack of<br>knowledge, and tackling complex problems.<br>Alleviate lack of knowledge and inconsistencies in their<br>knowledge considering validity and using authoritative<br>sources for evidence.<br>Grapple with knowledge conflict and inconsistencies<br>between own and scientific models and explore alterna-<br>tives<br>Building on ideas extending and expanding previous<br>discourse.|Progressively and coherently developing ideas to<br>problematize and to address previous ideas through<br>collaborative efforts.<br>Working toward constructing of theories jointly to explain<br>the fundamental aspects (e.g., the underlying mecha-<br>nisms, basic principles, or causal relationships of the<br>different elements of the phenomenon in question) of<br>the issue under investigation.<br>Deepens ongoing questions and explanation, pushing for<br>clarification and sustaining inquiry.|Summarizing and coordinating collective ideas from<br>previous discussion with reflection/rise above.|Tackling diverse and contradictory viewpoints to synthe-<br>size and to rise above for higher-level conceptualization.|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|**Knowledge Building (KB) competencies**<br>**Discourse moves**|**Epistemic**<br>Competencies in sustained pursuit of knowledge and<br>inquiry; tackling unresolved problem; identifying<br>inquiry focus; using knowledge sources construc-<br>tively; grappling epistemic conflict; engaging in pro-<br>gressive problem solving; and creating and revising<br>joint theory and rise above<br>Creating epistemic inquiry /focus<br>Alleviating lack of knowledge<br>Negotiating a fit and grappling conflict<br>Developing shared progressive understanding|Engaging deepening problem-centered uptake<br>Building revising joint theory<br>Sustaining collective inquiry|Summarizing community ideas|Synthesizing and rising above community ideas|\n\n\n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n250 \n\n**Undergraduates’ development of KB competencies over time** To examine how undergraduates’ KB competencies developed over time, we first compared their discourse moves reflecting different competencies before (Stage 1) and during (Stage 2) the reflective assessment was provided. Our analysis focused primarily on higher-level discourse moves, aiming to specifically capture the development of valued KB competencies. \n\nTo further elucidate the effects of reflective assessment on KB competencies, we investigated the changes in the sequential patterns in two critical dimensions of undergraduates’ KB competencies across different stages: socio-emotional interactions and epistemic engagement. This specific focus was selected due to the significant and specific roles these dimensions play in the KB competency framework. Also, regarding the application of lag sequential analysis (LSA), we found it particularly effective in analyzing the epistemic engagement and socio-emotional dimensions. In contrast, the LSA results were less insightful for the conceptual and metacognitive dimensions. This limitation arises because, in some cases, notes were dual coded with both questions and explanations in the conceptual dimension. Furthermore, a relatively small proportion of notes were coded as metacognitive, which were generally not sequential, further diminishing the relevance of LSA in these dimensions. \n\n**Undergraduates’ development of KB competencies supported by reflective assessment design** To examine how reflective assessment supported undergraduates’ engagement in productive KB, we collected a set of qualitative data sources. We conducted classroom observations by keeping a running record of the events in the classroom, captured what the instructor and undergraduates did through photographs of and field notes on all lessons, and collected ten 1.5 hour classroom videos. We also collected four 1.5 hour videos that <u>recorded the undergraduates’ reflective assessment process.</u> Each audio recording lasted for about 45–60 minutes. We also collected 42 audio recordings of the six student groups’ <u>discussions and reflections, among which 18 recorded reflective assessment sessions, and 6 recorded reflective analyses of synthesis notes from previous cohorts of students. Moreo-</u> ver, we collected students’ artifacts, including 12 group research maps that recorded each group’s research plan, 18 sets of reflective prompt sheets that were designed to scaffold students’ various dimensions of KB competencies during reflective assessment processes, and several reflective synthesis and rise-above notes. The prompt sheets documented the interpretations made by the undergraduates based on KBDeX data, their analysis of the inquiry threads utilizing the KBDeX data, and their strategies outlined for steering future inquiry endeavors. These prompt sheets were distributed to student groups during the two distinct rounds of KBDeX-supported reflective assessment sessions conducted in the classroom and were collected upon the conclusion of each session. Additionally, we conducted informal student interviews during their reflective assessment sessions and collected twelve 10–15 minute interviews. \n\nWe used qualitative investigation; wherein multiple data streams were amalgamated to glean insights into the undergraduates’ engagement in reflective assessment aimed at fostering KB competencies. The classroom observations, video recording of classroom discussion, and audio recording of group discussion captured major classroom events and reflective assessment sessions. Using a narrative approach, we initiated the analysis by perusing through the videos and transcriptions. This initial phase allowed us to develop a holistic understanding of the processes through which the undergraduates engaged with the reflective assessment activities, leveraging the tools and artifacts at their disposal. This was followed by the identification of key episodes that represent the major reflective assessment \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n251 \n\nactivities and key principles of KB such as idea improvement. To understand deeply about students’ reflective processes, we also analyzed other sources (e.g., synthesis super notes, prompt sheets, and interviews). Key themes were identified through the thematic analysis of critical events and episodes and enriched by students’ interviews and perceptions on the usefulness of the activities documented in their synthesis super notes and prompt sheets. \n\n## **Results** \n\n### **Demonstration of undergraduates’ KB competencies (RQ1)** \n\nTable 2 presents the categories and description of cognitive–conceptual processes, epistemic engagement, metacognition and metadiscourse, and socio-emotional interactions with subcategories, which reflected the four dimensions of KB competencies, respectively. Under the conceptual dimension, specifically, the students engaged in more explanation and inquiry-oriented discourse. As an illustration, they contributed more explanatory questions (94) than fact-seeking questions (7) that elicited simple facts. They contributed more explanations (729) and summaries (156) than simple claims (69). This result suggested that the undergraduates deployed cognitive competencies for conceptual advances manifested in their online discourse moves. \n\nTable 2 also revealed that the undergraduates engaged in productive metacognition and metadiscourse that tracked and regulated the community’s progress for sustained growth and deepened the community’s inquiry. They contributed metacognitive questions and statements to stimulate community members to monitor and regulate their inquiry (139), they conducted major reviews of community ideas and inquiry processes (86), and they engaged in productive reflection on and a deepening of the community’s inquiry and ideas (67). \n\nFurthermore, Table 2 shows the undergraduates’ positive socio-emotional interactions. They contributed notably to team cohesion with 614 notes, representing 36.26% of their interactions, indicating a strong focus on creating a supportive and unified group atmosphere. In addition, 34 notes aimed to prevent marginalization, showcasing their commitment to inclusivity. Moreover, their approach to mistakes was constructive and positive, as shown by 88 notes coded as “approaching mistakes positively,” fostering a learning-oriented and resilient environment. These contributions suggested the undergraduates’ competencies and dedication to fostering a harmonious and effective collaborative atmosphere, essential for the community’s overall success and well-being. \n\nAdditionally, Table 2 shows that the undergraduates could engage in productive epistemic engagement. For example, they contributed 286 notes to uptake idea-centered ideas, 65 notes to build joint theory, and 98 notes to sustain collective inquiry. Furthermore, a noteworthy 102 notes were dedicated to the synthesis and rise-above of community ideas. These findings underscore the consistent commitment and competencies exhibited by the undergraduates in facilitating the growth, elevation, and progression of communal knowledge, and the undergraduates had developed a strong community awareness and epistemic commitment to advance knowledge. \n\nIn sum, the above results suggested undergraduates’ KB competencies, manifested through their conceptual development, metacognition and metadiscourse, socio-emotional interactions, and epistemic engagement. \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n252 \n\n**Table 2** Undergraduate students’ KB competencies manifested in discourse moves: Frequency and percentages in conceptual development, metacognition and metadiscourse, socio-emotional interactions, and epistemic engagement \n\n||Level|Abb|_f_|% (_f_/1390)|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|**Conceptual development**|||||\n|Generating fact-seeking questions|L|N/A|**7**|0.50|\n|Generating explanation-seeking questions|H|N/A|94|6.76|\n|Generating a simple claim|L|N/A|69|4.96|\n|Proposing an elaboration|L|N/A|385|27.70|\n|Contributing an explanation|H|N/A|729|52.45|\n|Making summaries|H|N/A|156|11.22|\n|**Metacognition and metadiscourse**|||||\n|Creating metacognitive awareness|L|N/A|139|10.00|\n|Conducting major review of community ideas<br>l|H|N/A|86|6.19|\n|Reflecting on and regulating inquiry|H|N/A|67|4.82|\n|l<br>**Socio-emotional interactions**|||||\n|Active listening/participating|L|ALP|392|28.2|\n|Encouraging participation/ inclusion|L|EPI|233|16.76|\n|Fostering cohesion|H|FOC|614|36.26|\n|Discouraging marginalization|H|DIM|34|2.45|\n|Approaching mistakes positively|H|AMP|88|6.33|\n|**Epistemic engagement**|||||\n|Creating epistemic inquiry/focus|N/A|CEI|35|2.52|\n|Alleviating lack of knowledge<br>i|L|ALK|107|7.70|\n|Negotiating a fit|L|NAF|80|5.76|\n|i<br>Developing shared understanding|L|DSU|585|42.09|\n|Engaging problem-centered uptake|H|EPU|286|20.58|\n|Building joint theory|H|BJT|65|4.68|\n|Sustaining collective inquiry|H|SCIn|98|7.05|\n|Summarizing community ideas|H|SCI|32|2.30|\n|Synthesizing and rising above community ideas|H|SRI|102|7.34|\n\n\n\n_L_ Low, _H_ High, _Abb_ Abbreviation \n\n### **Undergraduates’ development of KB competencies and how the design of reflective assessment supports their development** \n\n### **Changes in KB competencies** \n\n**Changes in KB competencies over time** Table 3 presents the outcomes of the coding analysis of discourse moves that reflected the KB competencies during the two stages. Notably, Table 3 highlights significant disparities in the frequency distribution of discourse moves at a higher level between the two stages, χ<sup>2</sup> [ _df_ = 1, _N_ = 3087] = 63.26, _p_ < 0.001. These results indicate that the undergraduates were engaged more in productive KB discourse in the latter phase. For example, they engaged more in conceptual development, \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n253 \n\n**Table 3** Changes in KB competencies over time: Frequency of conceptual development, metacognition and metadiscourse, socio-emotional interactions, and epistemic engagement \n\n||Level|Stag|e 1|Stag|e 2|Change scores from<br>Stage 1 to Stage|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|||_f_|% (_f_/498)|_f_|% (_f_/892)|<br>2 (%)|\n|**Conceptual development**|||||||\n|Generating fact-seeking questions|L|5|1.00|2|0.22|−0.78|\n|Generating explanation-seeking ques-<br>tions|H|45|9.04|49|5.49|−3.54|\n|Generating a simple claim|L|35|7.03|34|3.81|−3.22|\n|Proposing an elaboration|L|152|30.52|233|26.12|−4.40|\n|Contributing an explanation|H|266|53.41|463|51.91|−1.51|\n|Making summaries|H|17|3.41|139|15.58|12.17|\n|**Metacognition and metadiscourse**|||||||\n|Creating metacognitive awareness|L|49|9.84|90|10.09|0.25|\n|Conducting major review of community<br>ideas|H|15|3.01|71|7.96|4.95|\n|Reflecting on and regulating inquiry|H|2|0.40|65|7.29|6.89|\n|l<br>**Socioemotional interactions**|||||||\n|Active listening/participating|L|131|26.31|261|29.26|2.95|\n|Encouraging participation/ inclusion|L|126|25.30|107|12.00|−13.31|\n|Fostering cohesion|H|190|38.15|424|47.53|9.38|\n|Discouraging marginalization|H|5|1.00|29|3.25|2.25|\n|Approaching mistakes positively|H|11|2.21|77|8.63|6.42|\n|**Epistemic engagement**|||||||\n|Creating epistemic inquiry/focus|N/A|22|4.42|13|1.46|−2.96|\n|Alleviating lack of knowledge|L|37|7.43|70|7.85|0.42|\n|Negotiating a fit|L|29|5.82|51|5.72|−0.11|\n|i<br>Developing shared understanding|L|269|54.02|316|35.43|−18.59|\n|Engaging problem-centered uptake|H|75|15.06|211|23.65|8.59|\n|Building joint theory|H|7|1.41|58|6.50|5.10|\n|Sustaining collective inquiry|H|42|8.43|56|6.28|−2.16|\n|Summarizing community ideas|H|8|1.61|24|2.69|1.08|\n|Synthesizing and rising above com-<br>munity ideas|H|9|1.81|93|10.43|8.62|\n\n\n\nmetadiscourse, and metacognition (e.g., conducting major reviews of community ideas, and reflecting on and regulating their inquiry), socio-emotional interactions (e.g., fostering cohesion, discouraging marginalization, and approaching mistakes positively), and epistemic engagement (e.g., engaging in problem-centered uptake, building joint theory, and synthesizing and rising above community ideas). Collectively, these results substantiate the gradual progression of the undergraduates’ KB competencies manifested in discourse moves, thus illustrating supporting KB competencies with the reflective assessment design. \n\n**Changes in sequential patterns of KB competencies over time** To further understand the impact of reflective assessment on the undergraduates’ KB competencies over time, we \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n254 \n\nfocused on analyzing the temporal evolution and sequential transitions within socio-emotional interactions and epistemic engagement. \n\nFigures 7a and 7b illustrate the significant sequential transitions of socio-emotional interaction codes observed at Stages 1 and 2. Detailed transition matrices and adjusted residuals for these social-emotional interactions across both phases are provided in Tables S3 and S4 of the supplementary file. These figures highlight various productive sequential transition patterns in the socio-emotional interaction codes during the two stages. For instance, a pattern like AMP ≥ AMP ( _z_ -score Stage 1 = 5.79, _z_ -score Stage 2 = 7.48), indicates a consistent and positive engagement in addressing mistakes by the undergraduates. Furthermore, Fig. 7a and 7b reveal distinct sequential transition patterns at different stages. In Stage 1, significant productive sequences included ALP ≥ FOC (2.12) and FOC ≥ AMP (2.16). Conversely, in Stage 2, transitions such as FOC ≥ FOC (4.23) and DIM ≥ DIM (6.47) were more prevalent. These findings suggest a progression in the undergraduates’ engagement with KB activities and peer note reading. In Stage 1, there was a tendency to actively participate in KB activities, fostering cohesion among ideas. In contrast, Stage 2 showed a shift toward discouraging marginalization and further promoting cohesion. This evolution indicates that the undergraduates gradually worked toward maintaining community and social cohesion. Overall, the results suggest a progressive enhancement in \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n(a) Significant sequential transitions of socio- (b) Significant sequential transitions of socio-<br>emotional interactions in Stage 1  emotional interactions in Stage 2<br>(c) Significant sequential transitions of epistemic  (d) Significant sequential transitions of epistemic<br>engagement in Stage 1  engagement in Stage 2<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n**Fig. 7** The comparisons of significant sequential transitions of socio-emotional interactions and epistemic engagement between two Stage 1 and Stage 2. Note: see the abbreviations of the codes in Table 2 \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n255 \n\nthe socio-emotional dimension of KB competencies, facilitated by the implementation of reflective assessment strategies. \n\nFigure 7c and 7d show the significant sequential transitions of epistemic engagement codes in Stage 1 and Stage 2. The transition matrix and adjusted residuals of students’ epistemic engagement in the two stages can be found in Table S5 and S6 in the supplementary file. Figure 7c and d show that there were several productive sequential transition patterns of epistemic engagement codes in the two stages, such as DSU ≥ EPU ( _z_ -score Stage 1 = 2.37, _z_ -score Stage 2 =2.11), SRI ≥ SRI ( _z_ -score Stage 1 = 7.16, _z_ -score Stage 2 = 4.62), and SRI ≥ SCI ( _z_ -score Stage 1 = 4.96, _z_ -score Stage 2 = 5.07). This suggests that the undergraduates engaged in continuous summarization, synthesis, and rise above of collective ideas. Figure 6 also shows that the undergraduates in the earlier and later stages exhibited different sequential transition patterns. For example, in Stage 1, the undergraduates showed some significant productive sequences, such as SCI ≥ CIA (7.01), CIA ≥ SCIn (2.46), SRI ≥ BJT (2.49), and BJT ≥ SRI (2.49). In Stage 2, they manifested productive transitions such as CIA ≥ DSU (3.22), and EPU − BJT (2.11). These results indicate that the undergraduates tended to sustain their progressive KB discourse and deepen their KB inquiries in the earlier stage and tended to develop joint theories and advance collective knowledge in the later stage. The results suggest that the undergraduates progressively improved the epistemic dimension of KB competencies with the scaffoldings of reflective assessment. \n\n### **Undergraduates’ development in KB competencies through reflective assessment** \n\n<u>The qualitative investigation of how undergraduates’ development of KB competencies is</u> supported by artifact- and analytics-enriched reflective assessment design revealed three themes. \n\n### **Theme 1—Using synthesis super notes for conceptual and metacognitive growth and epistemic engagement** \n\nAfter contributing notes on the Knowledge Forum, each group was provided with synthesis notes for effective reflective assessment to foster the conceptual, metacognitive, and epistemic dimensions of the undergraduates’ KB competencies. These super notes were created by previous student groups that provided standards and examples of collective KB. They were asked to analyze the notes and identify the most promising ideas and directions contained therein, reflect on the conceptual growth of their own inquiries, and co-design further inquiries by refining their initial research plan. \n\nWe focused on a group dedicated to exploring critical thinking to exemplify this process. Initially, this group was identified as the least performing in the class, both in the quality of their research plan map (refer to Fig. 8a) and the overall quality and quantity of their contributed notes. Despite these initial challenges, the group demonstrated a significant improvement in their conceptual and metacognitive competencies through active engagement in discussion, analysis, and reflection, facilitated using super synthesis notes. The students’ discussions around organizing and refining their research plan based on the super synthesis notes illustrated this theme, as evident in the following excerpt (a portion of a face-to-face group discussion): \n\nS15: …I think the synthesis note from JH’s group is good in the organization (see \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n256 \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n(a) concept map research plan before reflective assessment using super synthesis notes<br>(b) concept map of research plan after reflective assessment using super synthesis notes<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n**Fig. 8** Comparison of concept maps of research plan before and after analysis and reflection of super synthesis notes \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n257 \n\ntheir concept maps). See, their discussion has two main strands—the core concepts and theories, and theories and practices on how to develop critical thinking (CT). S23: Sure, our map is kind of messy…no logic between these different parts…See, our present discussion is also messy and superficial…It is too bad. S15: …I think we can also design our further research plan in this way. We can also organize our further discussions revolving around the two strands... \n\nS18: Good idea! But I think our further discussion should focus more on how to develop CT. We have discussed very little about this…However, JH’s group did not discuss “the how” well; they just inquired more about practices that develop CT… S8: On “the how,” we can borrow some ideas from CW’s group, such as the developmental phases and mechanisms for developing CT… \n\nS25: Great idea! Sure, we can integrate these interesting ideas into our revised research plan. Also, the promising ones, such as applications in other fields and methods for developing CT (pointing to their own concept map of research plan), I think we can keep… (They then elaborate on why they keep them.) S32: I think we also need to extend what they have achieved and what we have discussed…For example, the subset skills such as evaluation… \n\nIn this example, we found that synthesis notes in reflective assessments played a pivotal role in deepening students’ conceptual understanding. This was evident in how students engaged with the content of the notes, as exemplified by student S15’s observation on the organizational quality of another group’s research plan. Further, student S23’s identification of the disjointed nature of their group’s initial map, contrasted with the structured display seen in JH’s concept maps, reflects an evolving conceptual comprehension and a desire to develop more effective knowledge organization strategies. Such interactions indicated a shift from surface-level engagement to a more profound, theory-driven approach to KB. \n\nThese data also revealed that the student group demonstrated significant growth in metacognitive competencies, as seen in their reflections on the organization and depth of their research plan. The students’ identification of the messiness of their initial approach and their subsequent efforts to co-design a more structured inquiry pathway illustrated their metacognitive competencies. The dialogue involving students S15, S18, and S25 illustrated metacognitive engagement. They not only identified weaknesses in their initial plan but also actively sought to incorporate more structured and comprehensive approaches, as inspired by their engagement with the synthesis notes. The process showcased their development of metacognitive competencies. The informal post-session student interviews further confirmed the impacts of super synthesis notes on the development of students’ metacognitive competencies. For instance, S15 highlighted the role of these notes in sparking self-analysis and co-regulation of collaborative activities: “These super notes seem to provide a model for thinking…they urge us to analyze and reflect on our own discussion.” <u>Furthermore, the initiative to revise their research plan based on the insights gained from other groups’ synthesis notes indicated the group’s epistemic commitment and developing epistemic competencies. As Fig. 8b shows, the revised plan was a substantial</u> improvement on the original (Fig. 8a). It covered avenues for further inquiry, interesting ideas (process, dynamics, models), promising future directions (e.g., applications in different fields), and extensions of group discussions (e.g., components and skills included in CT). These results suggested the group’s willingness to pursue deeper inquiry and their engagement in epistemic processes, such as problem-centered inquiry and negotiating their models with established scientific theories. Their discussion about developing CT and \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n258 \n\nexploring different methodologies points to a deliberate effort to align their inquiry with broader epistemic goals. \n\nPost-session interviews provided further evidence of the positive impact of reflective assessment on students’ KB competencies. Student S15’s remarks (see above) about the synthesis notes providing a model for thinking, and S23’s comments (“they are really helpful…they spark our thinking to co-design our inquiry journey…”) on their role in sparking co-design of inquiry underscored the effectiveness of reflective assessment in fostering both conceptual understanding and metacognitive growth. \n\n### **Theme 2: Using KB interaction rubrics to foster metacognitive, socio‑emotional, and epistemic competencies** \n\nAfter the student groups revised their research plan, they were encouraged to contribute high-quality notes and engage in productive socio-emotional interactions. Subsequently, the student groups engaged in reflective assessment using KB interaction rubrics with prompts. The prompts were expected to support the epistemic, metacognitive, and socioemotional dimensions of KB competencies. \n\nStudent groups were instructed to collaboratively analyze a cluster of their own group’s notes focused on a shared problem. Initially, each group engaged in a joint examination of a selected cluster of notes. This collaborative analysis aimed to identify key characteristics of effective KB interaction patterns and behaviors. Subsequently, they assessed the quality of the selected notes based on the provided KB interaction rubrics. Based on this assessment, the groups formulated action plans to deepen their inquiry and enhance the overall collaborative learning experience. As presented in Table 4, the following excerpt from the Knowledge Creation group’s assessment note illustrates this process. The Knowledge Creation group displayed average performance in their class. \n\nFrom the above excerpts, we found that with the help of KB interaction rubrics and scaffolding prompts, the undergraduates were able to negotiate ideas, assess their sociocollaborative interactions, identify issues in their KB interactions and design strategies, and determine further inquiry directions. \n\nReflective assessment using interaction rubrics enhanced the epistemic dimension of KB competencies. This was evident through how students utilized rubrics to assess and refine their theoretical understandings and explanations. They focused on constructing more refined and advanced theories and explanations, as shown in their use of the Joint Theory Building rubric, which underscored their epistemic competency. They actively engaged in improving their discourse, indicating a deeper engagement with knowledge creation and problem solving. \n\nReflective assessment using interaction rubrics promoted metacognitive competencies by prompting students to self-assess, reflect on their learning process, and identify areas for improvement. The students’ ability to reflect on their discussions’ effectiveness and justify their grading demonstrated advanced self- and group awareness and regulatory skills. They exhibited a heightened level of metacognition by critically evaluating their collaborative efforts and planning for future inquiries, as reflected in the above except. \n\nThe process of using rubrics for reflective assessment inherently supports socio-emotional competencies. The collaborative nature of the assessment, involving idea negotiation and shared responsibility, suggests students’ socio-emotional competencies. The group’s collaborative efforts in assessing and improving their discussions indicate growth in aspects like active listening, fostering cohesion, and approaching mistakes positively. \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n259 \n\n**Table 4** Example prompts and excerpts when groups analyzing their group notes using KB interaction rubrics \n\n|Prompts|Excerpts|\n|---|---|\n|The inquiry topic|Definitions of, the formation process of<br>i|\n|Our discussion focuses on|Definition of Knowledge Creation (KC), the formation process of<br>KC, core components of KC…<br>i|\n|Aspects that we discussed better|1. The definition…<br>2. The characteristics of KC…<br>3. The value and significance of KC…|\n|Issues with our interaction|We have extended our discussion to the characteristics and sig-<br>nificance of KC. However, our discussion is not comprehensive<br>or perfect. The discussion is rather dry and does not show a<br>clear, logical framework… notes were repetitive...|\n|Our interaction pattern|[_Joint Theory Building_] We gave four grades for this note cluster<br>based on the rubrics.<br>Our group made considerable effort to build theories on the<br>inquiry topic, and we have constructed a clearer general expla-<br>nation and theories of KC.<br>Justifications (why we gave four grades): (1) We addressed the<br>inquiries and generated explanations through collaborative<br>efforts… (2) Regarding evidence…Regarding dimension…<br>i  f|\n|What KB inquiries and ideas are critical|We find that differentiating technology innovation from knowl-<br>edge creation is a promising idea. Thus, some discussion will<br>focus on this in the next stage.|\n|…<br>Our plan to deepen the present inquiry|…<br>In the theoretical dimension, we will discuss how KC takes<br>place…<br>In the practical dimension, we will discuss KC in education,<br>including problems and solutions…<br>In the social interaction dimension, we will monitor our group<br>discussion… using the rubrics to assess peer contributions…|\n\n\n\nFurthermore, the students’ immediate written responses to the questions “Is this reflective assessment process using the KB interaction rubrics useful? If so, why?” after finishing the reflective assessment using KB interaction rubric revealed that the process can benefit students greatly. \n\nFor example, S41 wrote, “The assessment and summary of the note clusters, with the help of the KB interaction rubrics and accompanying scaffoldings, can help us summarize our previous discussion, gather the scattered knowledge, and generate new theories…” \n\nS41’s response highlighted the role of KB interaction rubrics in facilitating a comprehensive summary of discussions, the gathering of disparate knowledge, and the generation of new theories. This process exemplified students’ conceptual competencies. \n\nMoreover, S32 wrote, “The KB interaction rubrics seem to provide a model of idea interaction behaviors…analysis using it can inform us about our problems and stimulate us to figure out methods to gain the highest scores.” They also mentioned that “this process can really help us improve our understanding [of conceptual growth] by finding connections between different pieces of knowledge and integrating them into new knowledge.” \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n260 \n\nS32’s comments about using the KB interaction rubrics as a model for understanding and addressing issues within their collaborative process implied their epistemic competencies. The use of rubrics to identify problems and strategies for better outcomes indicated students’ epistemic awareness. Also, they demonstrated socio-emotional competence, and the social dynamics indicated how they used the rubrics in the social interaction dimension to monitor group discussion. Also, the reference to “ideal interaction behaviors” and the emphasis on collaborative analysis for problem solving by S32 revealed the deployment of socio-emotional competencies. The interaction rubrics encouraged students to reflect not only on their cognitive contributions but also on their socio-emotional behaviors and interactions. \n\n<u>These findings suggest that reflective assessment with KB interaction rubrics and scaffolding prompts in Knowledge Forum scaffolded undergraduates to demonstrate conceptual, epistemic, and socio-emotional competencies. Summarizing discussions, integrating knowledge, and critically analyzing collaborative behaviors contribute to a more holistic display of these competencies.</u> \n\n### **Theme 3–Using KBDeX visualizations to foster metacognitive and conceptual growth and epistemic engagement** \n\nAfter reflective assessment using KB interaction rubrics, the student groups were required to conduct another round of reflective assessment using KBDeX visualizations and data of both community-level and group-level and accompanying prompt sheets (Table S1). The following narratives from the Collaboration group (a group with high performance) illustrated the process of prompting the undergraduates to collaboratively synthesize and rise above their collective discourse and ideas using KBDeX. \n\nWe tried to synthesize what we had discussed and identify future directions for generating new theories… With the diagram of ideas [After analysis of the group-level diagram of ideas generated by KBDeX, like the one presented in Fig. 6c], we found that our discussion on collaboration primarily revolved around seven aspects (Fig. 9): the nature and significance of collaboration, how to develop productive groups, factors influencing groups’ productivity… We have discussed the related keywords (e.g., collaboration, team spirit, cooperation, and collectivism), techniques and tools supporting productive collaboration, and factors influencing productive collaboration…our discussion on collaboration seemingly lacks innovative ideas… We have not inquired about theories on group dynamics, collaborative problem-solving… After analysis of the community-level diagram of ideas, we think we can extend our discussion by incorporating ideas such as reflection and regulation, negotiation, critical thinking… can deepen our further inquiry through discussion of theories on maintaining team dynamics, negotiating ideas with critical thinking, and assessment of team performance. \n\nIn the above example, the collaboration group was engaged in analysis and reflection on their inquiry on collaboration, using the group-level and community-level diagrams of ideas (keywords in their Knowledge Forum notes) from KBDeX, with the aim to “synthesize what we had discussed and identify future directions for generating new theories.” This was insightful, as it revealed that the undergraduates recognized \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n261 \n\n\n\n**Fig. 9** A concept map of one group’s analysis of KBDeX idea network \n\nthe importance of synthesizing the community’s ideas and identifying new problems for ongoing discussion. This process was considered essential for developing theories and deepening inquiry, and reflected students’ KB competencies in the conceptual, epistemic, and metacognitive dimensions. \n\nWith the help of the group-level KBDeX diagram, the undergraduates identified the following issues: “Our discussion on collaboration seemingly lacks innovative ideas” and “We have not inquired about theories on group dynamics...” Based on their task analysis, the undergraduates reflected on the quality of and gaps in their inquiry threads, and identified further inquiry directions with the help of a community-level KBDeX diagram, as indicated by the following statement: “We can deepen our further inquiry through discussion of theories on maintaining team dynamics… team performance…” They then generated an action plan to regulate their theory building and inquiry. The undergraduates contributed metadiscourse in which they synthesized the group’s past and present inquiries, tracked problems, and identified promising directions to progressively building theories with KBDeX idea diagrams. \n\nThis process indicated students’ demonstration of metacognitive competencies. By reflecting on the quality of their discussions and planning for future inquiry, the students demonstrated their ability to regulate their learning process and engage in metadiscourse, tracking the progress of their collective ideas and charting new directions for knowledge advancement. This process also reflected their epistemic competencies, showcasing their ability to engage in deeper inquiry and theory building. Their plan to discuss theories on maintaining team dynamics and assessing team performance indicated a productive approach to KB and problem solving. Additionally, this process demonstrated conceptual competencies, where students sought to understand various aspects of collaboration (e.g., the nature and significance of collaboration and \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n262 \n\nfactors influencing group productivity) and identified gaps in their discussions, such as the lack of innovative ideas or inquiries into group dynamics. lective assessment using KBDeX visualizations \n\n<u>Overall, these results suggested that reflective assessment using KBDeX visualizations and accompanying prompts facilitated the undergraduates to deepen their understanding of collaboration (conceptual competency), engage more meaningfully in knowledge creation (epistemic competency), and effectively plan and regulate their future inquiries (metacognitive competency).</u> \n\n## **Discussion and conclusions** \n\n### **Characterization and demonstration of undergraduates’ KB competencies** \n\nHelping undergraduates develop 21st-century competencies is widely advocated (NRC, 2012; Hilton & Pellegrino, 2012; OECD, 2023) to equip them to face the complex and rapidly changing world. Investigation of competencies is aligned with learning sciences and CSCL as designing deep learning is essential for developing 21st-century competence, and the application of competencies, in turn, supports the process of deep learning for knowledge transfer (NRC, 2012; Hilton & Pellegrino, 2012). While there are many lists of 21st-century competencies (Voogt & Roblin, 2012), few specific references have been made to developing KB competencies that refer to knowledge, skills, and dispositions needed for students to generate and advance knowledge and engage in creative knowledge work. KB competencies is an important theme in CSCL and learning-sciences research. The KB research community has made major research progress. Still, a synthesis of KB competence as a framework for what we hope students to develop and how to scaffold and assess such competencies has not been systematically developed and examined (see review, Chen & Hong, 2016). \n\nWith the major recognition in developing core competencies in the education arena (Chalkiadaki, 2018; Gonzalez-Salamanca et al., 2020; NRC, 2012), major challenges exist regarding how to measure and nurture these competencies (Voogt & Rublin, 2012). The CSCL environments supporting students’ collaborative discourse not only nurture students’ core competencies; students can also demonstrate their competencies in different dimensions as they engage in collaborative inquiry. This study has systematically linked key competencies with discourse moves for evidence and outcomes. When students reflect, monitor, and regulate their discourse, they are deploying and developing metacognitive competence. When they encourage peers to participate and persevere when facing challenges, they demonstrate social-emotional competence. Additionally, a sustained pursuit of inquiry to deepen and rise above reflects students’ epistemic commitments. Competencies could be challenging to measure, but discourse moves can provide pointers as manifestations of these developing competencies. This approach and framework may contribute to how CSCL learning environments and discourse can address the problems of fostering and assessing core competencies in the education arena. \n\nThough we have conducted studies to help undergraduates develop KB competencies through the KB design augmented by reflective assessment using learning analytics (Yang et al., 2020b, 2022), we have not yet identified a framework of KB competencies that can help us assess undergraduates’ KB competencies and guide \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n263 \n\npedagogical designs for developing the competencies. This study extends this line of research by proposing a framework of KB competencies and a design framework that synergizes KB inquiry design with reflective assessment to help undergraduates develop these competencies. In this study, we proposed a framework for KB competencies with four dimensions: the conceptual, metacognitive, socio-emotional, and <u>epistemic dimensions. This development of this KB competencies framework aligns</u> with the ongoing discourse in learning sciences, particularly in CSCL discourse interaction, scaffolding, and assessment. The study contributes to CSCL in linking discourse moves, a commonly studied theme in CSCL, to broader educational consideration and learning sciences by proposing the framework for KB competencies and a nuanced understanding of how undergraduates demonstrate their competencies in a KB environment. \n\nThe development of discourse schemes is a major undertaking in CSCL, and this paper seeks to provide a clearer focus on conceptual, metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic dimensions emphasizing the collective aspects. CSCL discourse schemes have generally focused on cognitive, collaborative, metacognitive and social aspects of meaning-making and knowledge construction. This framework contributes to CSCL literature highlighting epistemic competencies central to KB and expands on socio-emotional competencies involving active participation, encouragement, community engagement, inclusivity, and attitude towards failure, much needed for knowledge creation in safe, failure-proof communities for developing students’ sense of well-being. Competencies involve knowledge, skills, values and dispositions, and socio-emotional competencies in CSCL discourse interactions help align CSCL work with core competencies. This study also expands the scope of competencies and discourse moves in CSCL synthesized into a framework. \n\nWe examined undergraduates’ KB competencies manifested by their Knowledge Forum discourse moves in a KB environment augmented by reflective assessment. We differentiate high-level competencies from low-level ones to better understand undergraduates’ KB competencies and their development. Qualitative tracing of the undergraduates’ online KB discourse showed that the undergraduates contributed predominately high-level competencies in the conceptual, metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic dimensions. These results suggested the undergraduates’ development of KB competencies. The findings enriched prior research on scaffolding productive discourse and undergraduates’ learning (Bjuland & Helgevold, 2018; Chan & van Aalst, 2018; Damrow & Sweeney, 2019; Hong et al., 2019) by presenting a KB competencies framework and showing the effectiveness of the reflective assessment-based pedagogical design on the development of undergraduates’ KB competencies. \n\nThe KB design emphasizes both the scaffolding and the transformative function of reflective assessment, establishing learners as active agents (a concept supported by contemporary educational psychology theories) who assume collective cognitive, metacognitive, and epistemic responsibilities for progressive KB discourse. In this study, the integration of reflective assessment within the KB environment serves to overtly underscore these roles within the collaborative KB journey. Notably, the design extends the reach of reflective assessment into the socio-emotional dimension, fostering a scenario where students actively scaffold each other’s participation and group cohesion. This design carries significant implications for researchers and educators who aspire to craft technology-enriched learning settings replete with conceptual, metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic elements to nurture learners’ KB competencies. \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n264 \n\n### **Undergraduates’ development of KB competencies and how the design of reflective assessment supports the development** \n\nComparative analysis of the distributions of frequencies of KB competencies revealed a significant difference in high-level competencies between the latter stage (Stage 2, during reflective assessment using super synthesis notes, KB interaction rubrics, and KBDeX) and the earlier stage (Stage 1, before reflective assessment). Comparison of sequential patterns of socio-emotional interaction and epistemic engagement codes in the two stages further revealed differences in socio-emotional interactions and epistemic engagement that play a crucial role in KB. The results suggest that the undergraduates progressively improved their KB competencies with the scaffoldings of reflective assessment with analytics and artifacts. These results extend the insights gleaned from previous studies that leveraged portfolio assessment as a scaffold for cultivating productive KB discourse (Chan & van Aalst, 2018; Lee et al., 2006; van Aalst & Chan, 2007), as well as initiatives employing prompts rooted in content-related and metacognitive principles to provide structured guidance (Kim & Lim, 2018). Furthermore, they align with the trajectory outlined by studies on reflective assessment bolstered by analytical frameworks (Yang et al., 2020c, 2022). \n\nWhile we have conducted some studies that unveiled the impact of reflective assessment on students’ KB, we have not examined the dynamics of reflective assessment for fostering undergraduates’ KB competencies. Therefore, this study examined the dynamic processes through which reflective assessment fosters the development of KB competencies among undergraduates. Qualitative analysis of a set of the classroom data sources and Knowledge Forum reflective synthesis notes revealed three dynamics through which reflective assessment helps the undergraduates develop KB competencies in conceptual, metacognitive, epistemic, and socio-emotional areas through <u>(1) using synthesis super notes for conceptual and metacognitive growth and epistemic engagement; (2) using KB interaction rubrics to foster metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic competencies; and (3) using KBDeX visualizations to foster metacognitive and conceptual growth and epistemic engagement. These findings collectively</u> underscore the efficacy of conducting reflective assessment, further enriched by including models, rubrics, and visualization aids. This multifaceted approach progressively nurtures and refines students’ KB competencies, including conceptual, metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic dimensions. These dimensions hold paramount significance within the landscape of learning and KB but are often lacking among students. Augmented with reflective assessment design, KB emphasizes students’ agentic roles in inquiring about ideas, constructing shared understanding, tracking the progress of discussions, and gradually improving ideas and building theories. These results expand upon previous studies (e.g., Yang, van Aalst, et al., 2020c; Yang et al., 2022) by demonstrating the dynamics through which reflective assessment fosters undergraduates’ KB competencies. \n\nIn this study, we provided the undergraduates super synthesis notes, KB socio-collaborative interaction rubrics, and KBDeX visualizations of their ongoing discussion data to help them engage in productive reflective assessment. To actively engage in such reflective assessment, the students need opportunities to reflect on and regulate data-informed discourse, thereby fostering idea improvement and theory building and facilitating effective problem-solving (Yang et al., 2016). Prior studies on reflective assessment have primarily focused on portfolio-supported assessment (Lee et al., \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n265 \n\n2006; Lei & Chan, 2018; van Aalst & Chan, 2007). Thus, reflective assessment in this study is extended by introducing super synthesis notes, KB interaction rubrics, and KBDeX accurate inquiry data and evidence that plays a pivotal role in guiding students toward insightful reflection and the regulation of ongoing inquiries, which are vital facets of the productive reflective assessment process. The designed reflective assessment prompt sheets included a metacognitive framework encompassing three integral phases—gap analysis using learning data and evidence, reflection on inquiry process and outcome, and regulation of inquiry in a collective manner, and a set of metacognitive questions that aimed to help undergraduates to engage in each of the three processes in reflective assessment approach. A primary focus has been scaffolding undergraduates to progressively internalize the metacognitive cycle by conducting reflective assessments in several rounds. \n\n### **Implications for educational practices: Supporting students’ KB competencies** \n\nThe study’s findings carry significant implications for nurturing students’ KB competencies. First, cultivating a collaborative and reflective culture is paramount, underpinned by a carefully curated suite of activities grounded in core principles. This strategic cultivation can effectively steer students towards engaging in epistemic and social practices, enabling them to progressively develop essential skills that facilitate productive discourse, such as working with ideas and engaging in collaborative inquiry. Second, the study highlights the effectiveness of reflective assessment designs, especially when supplemented by tools such as super synthesis notes, KB interaction rubrics, and learning analytics. These tools offer models, set standards, and provide visual representations that aid in understanding of KB processes and developing KB competencies. Crucially, they also facilitate metacognitive activities, allowing students to monitor, reflect on, and regulate the progress of their KB. This metadiscourse is a vital component that enhances students’ engagement with the content and the KB process. Third, reflective assessment enhanced by analytics and artifacts substantially benefits students. Yet there is a need for opportunities that encourage students’ collaborative reflection. Such opportunities would enable students to participate in meta-level KB discussions that integrate online KB inquiries with offline reflection. Additionally, providing students with thoughtfully designed prompt sheets to trigger both their conceptual and metacognitive activities is crucial for fostering productive reflective assessment and KB competencies. \n\n### **Limitations and implications for future research** \n\nThe study does have certain limitations. To begin with, while it has provided rich data and empirical substantiation pertaining to the impact of the reflective assessment design in the KB framework on the cultivation of undergraduates’ KB competencies, some aspects warrant consideration. First, competencies can be defined as a set of knowledge, skills, affect, and dispositions that students can use and apply in different contexts (Ewell, 2005; Kulikowich & Alexander, 2003; NRC, 2012). However, this study mainly relied on students’ online discourse to understand their conceptual, metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic competencies, given that discourse analysis is a common way to understand students’ knowledge, skills, and affect in CSCL (Jeong et al., 2014) and discourse is usually more accessible. However, future research should also explore other data sources to \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n266 \n\nexamine students’ KB competencies. For instance, pre- and post-tests can be collected to examine students’ conceptual development and whether it can be improved through technological and pedagogical designs. \n\nSecond, we have reported on the changes over time in students’ use of productive discourse moves to illustrate students’ changing and developing competencies in different dimensions. We acknowledge possible limitations as the use of classroom design-based research involves complex interactions of processes and competencies at different stages of implementation (Collins et al., 2004). We have included qualitative analyses of students’ reflective assessment using different artifacts for illuminating further the possible design effects, but caution will be taken in interpreting the results and future work will continue to use multiple sources of data and evaluation methods to track more carefully the trajectory of changes in different phases. \n\nThird, the examination of the dynamics of reflective assessment in developing undergraduates’ KB competencies was exploratory in nature. Future studies that aim to refine the present pedagogical design and examine the causal attribution of the design of reflective assessment augmented by super synthesis notes, KB interaction rubrics, and learning analytics should extend the design to a wider range of subjects, students, and learning environments. \n\n### **Conclusions** \n\nThis study contributes to the literature by proposing a comprehensive framework of KB competencies that include four dimensions: the conceptual, epistemic, metacognitive, and socio-emotional dimensions with a collective focus, and how these competencies can be advanced using reflective assessment in a computer-supported collaborative environment. We extend the boundaries of reflective assessment using super synthesis notes, KB interaction rubrics, and learning analytics, not just as tools for dissecting online discourse but as mediums that foster collective reflection on discourse progress. Further, our study enriches the literature on the process and dynamics through which reflective assessment facilitates students’ KB competencies in computer-supported collaborative learning communities. We take a reflective assessment approach and elucidate the precise ways in which KB competencies can be systematically honed through a series of dynamics of reflective assessment—using synthesis “super notes” to promote conceptual and metacognitive growth, as well as epistemic engagement; employing KB interaction rubrics to enhance metacognitive, socio-emotional, and epistemic competencies; and leveraging KBDeX visualizations to support metacognitive and conceptual development, alongside fostering epistemic engagement. This study also has implications for teachers in terms of the implementation of this approach, which can help students develop higher-level thinking, collective advancement, and KB competencies. Our design has been implemented in KB communities with a focus on higher education and can be transferred to other learning environments under various grade levels and subjects. \n\n1 3 \n\nReflective assessment using analytics and artifacts for… \n\n267 \n\n## **Appendix A** \n\n\n\n**Fig. 10** The process of reflective assessment using KBDeX. \n\nsee Fig. 10 \n\n1 3 \n\nY. Yang et al. \n\n268 \n\n**Supplementary information** The online version contains supplementary material available at https:// doi. org/ 10. 1007/ s11412- 024- 09421-8. \n\n**Acknowledgments** The authors are indebted to the participating students. \n\n**Funding** National Natural Science Foundation of China, Grant/Award Number: 62107020; Central China Normal University, Grant/Award Numbers: CCNUTEIII 2021-11, CCNUAI&FE2022-03-15; Collaborative Innovation Center for Informatization and Balanced Development of K-12 Education by MOE, Grant/Award Number: xtzd2022-002; Ministry of Education of the People’s Republic of China, Grant/Award Number: 21YJA880078. \n\n**Data availability** These data will be made available to other researchers on a case-by-case basis. \n\n## **References** \n\n- Andrews-Todd, J., & Forsyth, C. M. (2020). Exploring social and cognitive dimensions of collaborative problem solving in an open online simulation-based task. _Computers in Human Behavior, 104,_ Article 105759. https:// doi. org/ 10. 1016/j. chb. 2018. 10. 025 \n\n- Bell, T., Urhahne, D., Schanze, S., & Ploetzner, R. (2010). Collaborative inquiry learning: Models, tools, and challenges. _International Journal of Science Education, 32_ (3), 349–377. https:// doi. org/ 10. 1080/ 09500 69080 25822 41 \n\n- Bereiter, C., & Scardamalia, M. (2016). “Good Moves” in knowledge-creating dialogue. _QWERTY-Interdisciplinary Journal of Technology, Culture and Educ