theory-informed KB discourse<br>to justify instructional design decisions.<br>10. PST groups produce initial instructional designs<br>without GenAI support.<br>11. PST groups engage in delayed GenAI-supported<br>co-design to expand, critique, and refine<br>instructional ideas.<br>n|Strengthen design rationale and<br>connect theory with practice<br>Preserve independent reasoning and<br>avoid premature reliance on AI.<br>Use AI as a reflective design partner<br>rather than an authoritative source<br>al Pre-|Epistemic agency; collective<br>responsibility<br>Epistemic agency<br>Human–AI co-agency;<br>epistemic agency||\n||12. PSTs conduct ASRA-supported reflection on<br>participationpatterns andidea development.<br>ur|Regulate inquiry progress and<br>coordinate collective designefforts<br>|Embedded and transformative<br>assessment; CRII||\n|**Component 3:**<br>**Deepening**<br>**PSTs’ DT**<br>**progressively**<br>**through**<br>**reflective**<br>**assessment and**<br>**GenAI-**<br>**supported co-**<br>**design**|13. PST groups conduct KBDeX-supported<br>reflective assessment of online design discourse.<br>14. PST groups engage in multiple rounds of<br>delayed GenAI-supported co-design<br>15. PST groups present instructional designs and<br>receive integrated feedback from peers,<br>instructor, and GenAI.<br>16. PST groups revise and justify final instructional<br>designs based on feedback and reflection.<br>Jo|Make idea trajectories and gaps visible<br>to support reflective assessment<br>Foster critical comparison between<br>human and AI perspectives.<br>Support cross-group knowledge<br>exchange and evaluative reflection<br>Consolidate learning through<br>evidence-informed revision and<br>reflection.<br>|Embedded and transformative<br>assessment;CRII<br>Human–AI co-agency;<br>epistemic agency<br>Human–AI co-agency;CRII;<br>embedded and transformative<br>assessment<br>Epistemic agency; embedded<br>and transformative assessment|Ideate,<br>Prototype,<br>and Test|\n\n\n\nNote: CRII = Collective responsibility for idea improvement \n\n19 \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n(a). One group’s conceptmap on strategies for effective use of GenAI (translated from Chinese)<br>(b) One group’s inqiry plan (translated from Chinese)<br>(c). One group’s front-end analysis of curriculum standards, learner needs, and pedagogical goals<br>Journal Pre-proof<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n20 \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n(e) One group’s presentation of their instructional<br>(d) One group’s engagement in KBDeX-<br>design<br>supported reflective assessment<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nFigure 6. Examples of principle-based activities for fostering PSTs’ DT through instructional design<br>Journal Pre-proof<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\nFigure 7. The process of Reflective assessment using KBDeX \n\n21 \n\na. An idea-friend map generated by KBDeX. b. Growth curve of the total degree centrality of Notes: The map illustrates how PSTs used ideas generated by KBDeX. Note: The growth keywords during their discussions. Links between curve of total degree centrality from KBDeX keywords show when PSTs connected these ideas reflects the coherence of students’ collaborative in their talk. Keywords closer to the center have discourse. A stable upward trend suggests higher degree centrality, suggesting greater progressive integration of ideas, while strong importance in the discussion. A tightly connected fluctuations indicate fragmented discourse and network reflects coherent and focused discourse, difficulty in forming a cumulative line of inquiry. whereas isolated clusters point to fragmented or <u>less connected exchanges.</u> Figure 8. Example visualizations generated by KBDeX _3.3. Data Sources and Analysis 3.3.1. Understanding PSTs’ DT changes_ To examine the combined effects of GenAI and ASRA within a KB collaborative inquiry environment on PSTs’ DT (RQ1), we collected two complementary data sources: PSTs’ selfreported DT, and multiple versions of group-based instructional design artifacts produced during over time. This combination allowed us to capture both perceived changes in DT and observable changes in design performance. \n\n## _3.3.1.1. PSTs’ self-reported DT competency_ \n\nWe measured PSTs’ self-reported DT using the DT Competency Scale developed and validated by Cai & Yang (2023), which was specifically designed for higher education contexts and grounded in the DDIPT framework. The scale has been previously validated through expert review, exploratory factor analysis, and confirmatory factor analysis, demonstrating satisfactory construct validity and reliability. The scale contains 29 items across five dimensions: 6 for Discovery, 6 for Define, 6 for Ideate, 5 for Prototype, and 5 for Test. Each item was rated on a six-point Likert scale (1 = Never, 6 = Always). To reduce response bias, the 29 items were presented in random order. Higher scores indicate a higher self-reported level of DT. \n\nThe questionnaire was administered online via Wenjuanxing, a widely used and secure survey platform in China (https://www.wjx.cn/vm/reL5LrE.aspx#), at both the beginning and end of the course. The PSTs were given approximately10-15 minutes to complete this questionnaire. Of the 110 participants who initially enrolled in the course, seven were \n\n22 \n\nexcluded from analysis due to incomplete responses or response times below two minutes, which were considered indicative of inattentive responding. The final analytic sample therefore consisted of 103 matched pre–post cases. We assessed internal consistency using Cronbach’s α and examined construct validity using the Kaiser–Meyer–Olkin (KMO) test and Bartlett’s test of sphericity. Exploratory factor analysis in SPSS 27.0 showed a Cronbach’s α of 0.931 for the overall scale. For the five dimensions—Discovery, Define, Ideate, Prototype, and Test—the α values were 0.868, 0.812, 0.830, 0.720, and 0.871, respectively, indicating high reliability. \n\nFor the questionnaire data, the Kolmogorov–Smirnov (K–S) test was first used to examine the normality of the DT scale scores. Following statistical conventions, when the sample size is ≥ 50, the K–S test is preferred for assessing normality. The study sample consisted of 103 PSTs, meeting the large-sample criterion. The results indicated that the data followed a normal distribution (statistic = 0.08, df = 102, p = 0.141 > 0.05). This suggests no statistically significant deviation from the assumption of normality, satisfying the prerequisite for parametric tests. Subsequently, a paired-sample t-test was conducted to compare PSTs’ pre- and post-course self-reported DT data, in order to determine whether their DT competency showed significant changes following the intervention. In addition to statistical significance testing, Cohen’s d for paired samples was calculated to estimate the practical magnitude of change. _3.3.1.2. PTS’ instructional design of lesson plans_ In this study, PSTs’ DT was conceptualized as a dynamic and developmental capability, evolving through repeated cycles of instructional design supported by human collaboration, GenAI co-design, and ASRA. To capture these developmental changes, three rounds of processbased assessments were conducted in Weeks 10 (T1), 12 (T2), and 14 (T3). Instructional design was operationalized through lesson-plan design. Each group (n = 22) developed a coherent instructional unit consisting of three lesson plans, completed sequentially across the semester, with one lesson plan finalized at each time point. This sequencing enabled PSTs to engage repeatedly in the full DT cycle—Discover, Define, Ideate, Prototype, and Test—rather than treating DT as a single, isolated task. For each lesson plan, PSTs followed a consistent two-phase design process that mirrored the intervention design described in Components 2 and 3 (Section 3.2). First, groups independently produced and refined an initial instructional design through peer discussion, Knowledge Forum discourse, and ASRA, without GenAI support. Only after groups perceived diminishing returns from human-only revision was GenAI introduced as a co-design partner, providing alternative ideas, critiques, and enhancement suggestions. Groups then selectively accepted, modified, or rejected AI-generated inputs and documented the rationale for their decisions. As a result, each lesson plan generated two non-independent but theoretically meaningful versions: (a) a humandesigned version and (b) a human–AI co-designed version. Across the three lesson plans, this design yielded six comparable instructional design artifacts per group, allowing us to examine both within-lesson changes (before vs. after GenAI support) and across-lesson developmental trends in DT. Importantly, all PST groups experienced both design conditions as part of the same intervention; therefore, the comparison reflects a within-design developmental contrast rather than a comparison between independent groups. \n\nA scoring rubric was developed based on course learning objectives and an explicit conceptual alignment between the DDIPT DT model (Discover–Define–Ideate–Prototype–Test) and the instructional design process (Appendix C). Specifically, DDIPT informed the DT’ cognitive–epistemic dimensions, while the instructional design process guided the structural and pedagogical quality of instructional design, enabling the rubric to capture DT as enacted in authentic instructional design practice. The rubric included operational definitions for each \n\n23 \n\ndimension, with a total possible score of 100 points. Two authors and an additional researcher independently scored all 132 lesson plan versions, achieving an inter-rater reliability above 92%. Discrepancies were resolved through discussion. \n\nFor analysis, paired-sample t-tests were first conducted to examine within-lesson differences between human-designed and human–AI co-designed versions at each time point. Repeatedmeasures ANOVA was then applied to investigate developmental changes across the three lesson plans, with both time (T1–T3) and design condition (human-designed vs. human–GenAI co-designed) specified as within-subject factors. No between-group factor was included in the model, as the study did not involve a separate control or comparison group. Although lesson plan versions were not independent, repeated-measures ANOVA was appropriate because the research focus was on within-group change over time under systematically varied design conditions, rather than between-group comparison. This analytic specification aligns with the intervention design, in which GenAI was introduced sequentially to the same groups. This approach aligns with the longitudinal, design-based nature of the intervention, where the same groups intentionally engaged in successive design cycles. To reduce the risk of inflated Type I error, effect sizes (partial η² and Cohen’s d) were consistently reported alongside significance tests, and interpretation focused on patterns of change rather than isolated p-values. Statistical significance was set at _p_ < .05. For significant interaction effects, post hoc comparisons with adjusted alpha levels were conducted to identify specific differences. _3.3.2. Understanding the evolutions of PSTs’ DT over time_ The Knowledge Forum notes served as a primary source for tracing how PSTs’ DT evolved during collaborative instructional design inquiry. Across the semester-long online inquiry, 110 PSTs contributed 1,305 Knowledge Forum notes related to instructional design. For each note, we recorded metadata (e.g., author, title, hierarchical level, content) and timestamps, which enabled us to examine temporal relationships among notes and track the progression of PSTs’ DT over time. These discourse data complemented the artifact-based analyses by capturing PSTs’ DT as it unfolded in situ during collaborative inquiry. The analytic unit in this study was the individual note. Although Knowledge Forum discourse is structurally nested (notes within students within groups), analyses focused on the collective inquiry level by aggregating all notes within each phase into a single discourse corpus. Student and group identifiers were therefore not modeled statistically, consistent with learning sciences research that examines community-level knowledge building rather than individual-level inference. **_Preprocessing and content analysis._** We first organized the KF notes into inquiry threads using thematic analysis. An inquiry thread refers to a set of notes addressing the same core problem (Zhang et al., 2007). This step allowed us to trace the progression of ideas within each thread and provided the context for subsequent content analysis. To analyze PSTs’ DT, we applied a coding framework (Appendix D) adapted from prior research (Thuan & Antunes, 2024). The framework comprised five dimensions—Discovery, Define, Ideate, Prototype, and Test—each operationally defined and illustrated with examples. Each Knowledge Forum note was assigned to only one DT dimension based on its dominant epistemic function. Multiple coding for a single note was not permitted. This mutually exclusive coding scheme ensured conceptual clarity and prevented inflation of frequencies across DT categories. \n\nTo ensure the reliability of coding across the full corpus of Knowledge Forum notes, a twostage reliability procedure was implemented. First, two trained researchers (the first and second author) independently coded a stratified random subset of 450 notes (approximately 35% of the total corpus) that were evenly sampled across inquiry phases and groups. Inter-rater reliability, measured by Cohen’s kappa, was 0.863, indicating high agreement. Discrepancies were discussed until consensus was reached, and the coding manual was refined accordingly. Second, \n\n24 \n\nto guard against coder drift, periodic reliability checks were conducted at two additional points during coding of the remaining notes, with kappa values consistently exceeding 0.80. Following these calibration steps, the second author coded the remaining notes using the stabilized coding scheme. This procedure ensured that reliability was maintained across all 1,305 coded Knowledge Forum notes. \n\n**_Examining evolution in DT._** To explore changes in PSTs’ DT over time, we divided the entire online discussion period into three equal stages (Phase 1 to Phase 3) and compared the distribution of coded notes across these stages. A _chi-square test_ was used to examine whether the frequency of each DT dimension differed significantly across stages. Frequency analysis provided a descriptive overview of how coded DT dimensions shifted across phases. As analyses were conducted at the aggregated note level, chi-square results were interpreted as indicators of collective discourse trends rather than independent participant-level effects. To control Type I error across multiple comparisons, Bonferroni-adjusted alpha levels were applied. Next, we conducted a lag sequential analysis (LSA) on the coded data from Phases 1–3 to further investigate how DT dimension transitioned over time. LSA (Sackett, 1978) detects statistically significant sequential patterns by analyzing the order and time intervals between behaviors. Using RStudio, we calculated two indicators: (a) Behavioral transition frequency matrices, which quantified the likelihood of moving from one DT behavior to another; and (b) Yule’s Q coefficients, which measured the strength and direction of association between behaviors (ranging from –1 to +1). Positive values indicated forward transitions, negative values indicated inverse transitions, and absolute values of 0.20, 0.43, and 0.60 were interpreted as small, medium, and large effects, respectively. Frequency analysis described the distribution of coded DT dimensions, whereas lag sequential analysis traced temporal transitions between coded categories, revealing developmental patterns in collective inquiry. Given the large number of possible transitions, Holm–Bonferroni adjustments were applied to control Type I error. Consistent with design-based research, LSA results were interpreted as exploratory indicators of developmental trajectories rather than probabilistic estimates based on independent observations. Only transitions with |Q| ≥ 0.20 were retained. For example, a Q value of 0.65 indicated a strong positive transition from the starting to the ending behavior, whereas a Q value of 0.30 reflected a weaker association. These results were visualized in transition diagrams, providing a clear representation of how PSTs’ DT interaction patterns evolved across the three stages. \n\n## **4. Results** \n\n- _4.1. The Combined Effects of GenAI and ASRA to Support PSTs’ DT (RQ1)_ \n\n- _4.1.1. Changes in PSTs’ DT measured by the DT scale_ \n\nTable 2 presents the means (M) and standard deviations (SD) of PSTs’ DT before and after the combined use of GenAI and ASRA, respectively, and the paired-sample _t_ -test results. Table 1 shows a statistically significant improvement of PSTs’ DT from pre-test (M = 5.15, SD = 0.60) to post-test (M = 5.25, SD = 0.58), _t_ (102) = 2.151, _p_ = .034, with a small effect size (Cohen’s _d_ = 0.21). This indicates that the observed change was modest in magnitude and should be interpreted as gradual developmental movement rather than a large shift in self-perceived competency. \n\nAt the subscale level, statistically significant gains were observed in the Discover and Define dimensions. Students demonstrated increased capacity to explore problems and define them with clarity and depth. The Discover dimension improved from M = 5.25 (SD = 0.46) to M = 5.37 (SD = 0.63), _t_ (102) = 2.258, _p_ = .026, while the Define dimension rose from M = 5.10 (SD = 0.64) to M = 5.25 (SD = 0.57), _t_ (102) = 2.386, _p_ = .019. The Prototype dimension showed a \n\n25 \n\nmarginal increase (p = .052), whereas Ideate and Test did not reach statistical significance. From a learning sciences perspective, the small effect size alongside selective subscale gains suggests that changes in self-perceived DT may lag behind observable performance development. \n\nOverall, these results indicate incremental growth in PSTs’ early-stage DT processes, particularly problem exploration and definition, while later phases such as ideation and testing may require longer-term scaffolding or additional design cycles to become salient in self-report measures. \n\nTable 2. Changes in PSTs’ self-reported design thinking (DT) across the intervention (n = 103 <u>matched cases)</u> \n\n||No. of|Pre|-test|Post|-test|t|df||\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n||Items|M|SD|M|SD|||p|\n|Overall|29|5.15|0.60|5.25|0.58|2.151|102|0.034|\n|Discover|6<br>|5.25<br>|0.46<br>|5.37<br>|0.63<br>|2.258<br>|102|0.026|\n|Define|6<br>|5.10<br>|0.64<br>|5.25<br>|0.57<br>|2.386<br>|102|0.019|\n|Ideate|6<br>|5.13<br>|0.68<br>|5.21<br>|0.71<br>|1.371<br>|102|0.174|\n|Prototype|6<br>|5.03<br>|0.68<br>|5.15<br>|0.68<br>|1.965<br>|102|0.052|\n|Test|5<br>|5.24<br>|0.67<br>|5.27<br>|0.61<br>|0.547<br>|102|0.586|\n\n\n\nDiscover 6 5.25 0.46 5.37 0.63 2.258 102 0.026 Define 6 5.10 0.64 5.25 0.57 2.386 102 0.019 Ideate 6 5.13 0.68 5.21 0.71 1.371 102 0.174 Prototype 6 5.03 0.68 5.15 0.68 1.965 102 0.052 Test 5 5.24 0.67 5.27 0.61 0.547 102 0.586 Note. Seven participants were excluded due to incomplete responses or response times below two minutes, resulting in 103 valid paired observations. Paired-sample t-tests examined withinparticipant change over time. _4.1.2 Developmental changes in PSTs’ DT reflected in instructional design artifacts_ All PST groups progressed through a human-designed phase followed by a delayed human– GenAI co-designed phase as part of the same design sequence (Section 3.2). The analyses therefore capture within-design developmental changes across phases, rather than comparisons between independent groups; the two conditions represent successive stages of the same groups’ instructional design work. Figure 9 presents the PSTs’ performance trends across the three time periods of lesson plan design (T1, T2, and T3). In the human–GenAI co-designed condition, mean scores increased steadily from 14.6 at T1 to 18.2 at T3. By contrast, in the human-designed condition, mean scores rose more slowly, from 7.9 to 9.2 over the same period. Because both design conditions were experienced by the same PST groups as part of a sequential design process, these trends reflect within-design developmental contrasts rather than differences between independent groups. This descriptive pattern suggests that the human–GenAI co-designed condition demonstrated stronger growth in instructional design performance, consequently, their DT competencies across the semester. \n\nA repeated measures ANOVA examined the effects of time, design condition (human–GenAI co-designed vs human-designed), and their interaction. Consistent with the intervention design, both time and design condition were treated as within-subject factors, as all PST groups completed both design phases. As shown in Table 3, significant main effects of time, design condition, and time–design condition interaction were observed for multiple dimensions of DT. Across the semester, time effects were significant for Define, Ideate, Prototype, and Test, but not for Discover. For instance, in Define, scores increased from 2.13 at T1 to 2.50 at T2, and to 2.59 at T3, _F_ (2,42) = 24.24, _p_ < .001, _η²_ = .71. Similar upward trends were found in Ideate ( _F_ = 39.30, _p_ < .001, _η²_ = .80), Prototype ( _F_ = 19.74, _p_ < .001, _η²_ = .66), and Test ( _F_ = 9.01, _p_ < .01, _η²_ = .47). These results indicate sustained improvement over time, with the largest gains occurring between T1 and T2, followed by a period of consolidation. \n\n26 \n\nSignificant main effects of design condition (human–GenAI co-designed vs human-designed) were also significant for all dimensions and total scores, with instructional designs produced during the human–GenAI co-designed phase receiving higher rubric scores than those developed during the human-designed phase. These effects should be interpreted as contrasts between two phases of the same developmental design process rather than comparisons between separate participant groups. At T1, the human–GenAI co-designed condition’s total score (14.6) was nearly double that of the human-designed condition (7.9), and this gap persisted through T3 (18.2 vs. 9.2). Design condition effects were particularly large for Define ( _F_ = 51.00, _η_ ² = .71), Ideate ( _F_ = 92.43, _η_ ² = .82), Prototype ( _F_ = 77.70, _η_ ² = .79), and Test ( _F_ = 71.40, _η_ ² = .77), all at p < .001. \n\nFurther, significant time-by-design condition interaction effects were observed in Define, Ideate, and Prototype. Within the human–GenAI co-designed phase, scores improved significantly across all three time points (Fs > 16.0, ps < .001), whereas improvements during the human-designed phase were smaller and more gradual. For example, in the Define dimension, scores increased from 2.82 to 3.41 and then to 3.60 across time during the human– GenAI co-designed phase, _F_ (2,42) = 22.50, _p_ < .001, _η²_ = .69. Similar patterns were seen in Ideate and Prototype, where the human–GenAI co-designed phase demonstrated stronger growth trajectories. For instance, in Ideate, scores increased from 3.1 at T1 to 4.4 at T3 during the human–GenAI co-designed phase, whereas scores in the human-designed phase showed a more modest increase from 1.8 to 2.4. Overall, these findings suggest that integrating GenAI within a KB collaborative inquiry environment was associated with stronger observable development in PSTs’ DT-related instructional design performance. Rather than indicating a causal comparison between groups, the results illustrate how the introduction of GenAI as a later design phase coincided with accelerated growth in problem definition, idea generation, and prototyping within the same learning trajectory. The observed time-by-design condition interactions therefore suggest that GenAI functioned as a developmental catalyst embedded within an iterative design process. Figure 9. Developmental trends in PSTs’ instructional design performance across sequential design phases Note. Scores are presented for two design phases experienced by the same PST groups: a human-designed phase and a human–GenAI co-designed phase. These conditions do not represent independent participant groups but two successive stages within the same design process. Error bars indicate standard deviations. \n\n27 \n\nTable 3. Repeated-measures ANOVA results for PSTs’ instructional design performance across time and design phases \n\n||T1||T|2|T|3|Main effect|of Time|Main effect ofdes|ign condition|Interactio|n effect|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n||H-D<br>condition|H-GenAI<br>condition|H-D<br>condition|H-GenAI<br>condition|H-D<br>condition|H-GenAI<br>condition|_F_|𝜂<sup>2</sup>|_F_|𝜂<sup>2</sup>|_F_|𝜂<sup>2</sup>|\n||（n=22）|（n=22）|（n=22）|（n=22）|（n=22）|（n=22）|||||||\n|**Total**|7.9±2.4|14.6±3.6|8.5±2.3|16.6±4.0|9.2±2.4|18.2±3.9|36.97***|0.64|144.59***|0.87|79.70***|0.89|\n|DC|1.5±0.8|2.7±1.3|1.5±0.8|2.8±1.3<br>|1.5±0.8<br>|2.9±1.6<br>|0.55<br>|0.03<br>f|19.13***|0.48|0.55|0.03|\n|DF|1.5±0.7|2.8±1.1|1.6±0.7|3.4±1.1<br>|1.6±0.7<br>|3.6±1.1<br>|20.40***<br>|0.50<br>|51.00***|0.71|7.48**|0.26|\n|ID|1.8±0.7|3.1±0.8|2.2±0.7|3.6±0.7<br>|2.4±0.8<br>|4.4±0.7<br>|39.30***<br>ro|0.80<br>|92.43***|0.82|9.80***|0.32|\n|PT|1.8±0.9|3.1±0.9|2.0±0.8|3.6±1.1<br>|2.1±0.9<br>|3.9±0.8<br>|18.14***<br>-p|0.46<br>|77.70***|0.79|3.35*|0.14|\n|TE|1.3±0.5|2.9±1.1|1.4±0.6|3.3±1.0<br>|1.6±0.5<br>|3.5±0.9<br>r|10.92***<br>|0.34<br>|71.40***|0.77|1.57|0.07|\n|Note:<br>Human<br>same 2<br>(T1–T3<br>instruc|human–Ge<br>= Ideate; P<br>*** p＜0<br>–GenAI c<br>2 PST gro<br>) and des<br>tional desi|nAI co-des<br>T = Prototy<br>.001, ** p<br>o-designed<br>ups as part o<br>ign phase (<br>gn performa|igned condi<br>pe; TE = Te<br>＜0.01, * p<br>condition (H<br>f the interv<br>human-desi<br>nce.|tion = H-Gen<br>st.<br>＜0.05.<br>-GenAI) an<br>ention. They<br>gned vs hum<br>|AI conditio<br>d human-de<br>are not inde<br>an–GenAI<br>Journ|n; human-d<br>signed cond<br>pendent exp<br>co-designe<br>al P|esigned con<br>ition (H-D)<br>erimental g<br>d) as withi<br>|dition =<br>refer to<br>roups. T<br>n-subject<br>|H-D condition; D<br>two sequential d<br>he repeated-meas<br>factors to exam|C = Discove<br>esign phases<br>ures ANOVA<br>ine develop|r; DF = Defin<br>completed b<br>treated both<br>mental chang|e; ID<br>y the<br>time<br>es in|\n\n\n\n28 \n\n## _4.2. Evolutions in PSTs’ DT reflected in Knowledge Forum notes_ \n\n## _4.2.1. Evolutions reflected in frequencies_ \n\nA total of 1,305 Knowledge Forum notes written by PSTs across three phases were analyzed to examine how their DT evolved during the intervention, which integrated GenAI support and ASRA in a KB collaborative inquiry environment. Table 4 presents the frequency and proportion of each DT dimension, and Figure 10 illustrate the evolutions of PSTs’ DT across the three phases. Because Knowledge Forum discourse is nested, frequency analyses were conducted at an aggregated phase level to capture collective developmental trends. Chi-square results therefore indicate distributional shifts in community discourse rather than independent causal comparisons. Bonferroni-adjusted alpha levels were applied across DT dimensions to control for multiple testing. \n\nAs shown in Table 3, the PSTs engaged most frequently in higher-level DT dimensions, with _Ideate_ accounting for the largest proportion (28.07%), followed by _Prototype_ (13.15%) and _Test_ (11.23%). This distribution suggests that PSTs were actively involved in generating, developing, and evaluating instructional ideas throughout the course, rather than remaining at early problem-exploration stages. A closer phase-by-phase analysis revealed distinct developmental shifts (see Figure 10). Phase 1 was dominated by early-stage activities, with _Ideate_ (36.21%), _Define_ (22.07%), and _Discover_ (11.95%) as the most frequent behaviors. Phase 2 marked a clear transition toward implementation and evaluation. _Prototype_ (16.07%) and _Test_ (14.94%) showed significant increases compared to Phase 1 (χ² = 38.763, p < .001 for _Prototype_ ; χ² = 98.751, p < .001 for _Test_ ), while _Discover_ and _Define_ dropped sharply (χ² = 49.793, p < .001; χ² = 142.827, p < .001). _Ideate_ also declined significantly (χ² = 41.288, p < .001), suggesting a shift of cognitive resources from idea generation to implementing and evaluating solutions. Phase 3 further strengthened this focus on refinement and validation. _Test_ rose to 34.69% and _Prototype_ to 28.98%, both significantly higher than in Phase 2 (χ² = 44.725, p < .001; χ² = 19.628, p < .001). Notably, _Define_ rebounded to 6.94% (χ² = 16.884, p < .001), indicating that PSTs revisited and clarified problem definitions while refining their designs. In contrast, _Discover_ remained low, and _Ideate_ continued to decline slightly without statistical significance. Overall, from Phase 1 to Phase 3, lower-level behaviors ( _Discover_ , _Define_ ) declined substantially, while higher-level behaviors—especially _Test_ —increased markedly (from 1.61% in Phase 1 to 34.69% in Phase 3, _χ²_ = 259.820, p < .001). The small resurgence of _Define_ in Phase 3 reflects an iterative process, in which problem framing was refined alongside solution development. Overall, these results indicate that the integrated use of GenAI-support and ASRA within the collaborative inquiry environment fostered productive shifts in PSTs’ DT. PSTs progressively moved from idea generation toward iterative testing and refinement, while also revisiting and refining problem definitions as their instructional designs matured. This pattern aligns with the cyclical and adaptive nature of expert design practice, where problem framing and solution development co-evolve rather than proceed in a strictly linear sequence. \n\nTable 4. The frequencies and percentage of different dimensions of PSTs’ DT in different phases \n\n|||Phas|e 1|Phas|e 2|Phas|e 3|Tot|al|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n||Level|f(n=680)|%|f(n=397)|%|f(n=228)|%|f(n=1305)|%|\n|DC|L|104|11.95|17|2.42|10|4.08|131|7.21|\n|DF|L|192|22.07|12|1.71|17|6.94|221|12.16|\n|ID|H|315|36.21|150|21.34|45|18.37|510|28.07|\n|PT|H|55|6.32|113|16.07|71|28.98|239|13.15|\n|TE|H|14|1.61|105|14.94|85|34.69|204|11.23|\n\n\n\nNote: DC = Discover; DF = Define; ID = Ideate; PT = Prototype; TE = Test. \n\n29 \n\nFigure 10. The evolutions of PSTs’ DT across phases _4.2.2. Evolutions reflected in transition patterns_ We conducted lag sequential analysis (LSA) with Yule’s Q to examine how PSTs’ DT dimensions transitioned. LSA allowed us to identify statistically meaningful sequential relationships among DT dimensions, thereby revealing how PSTs moved between design processes during collaborative inquiry. Consistent with the analytic framing, LSA treated coded notes as sequential events within an aggregated discourse corpus rather than independent observations. Holm–Bonferroni adjustments were applied to control for multiple transition tests. Following design-based research conventions, LSA findings are interpreted as exploratory indicators of developmental trajectories rather than probabilistic estimates based on independent data. Table 5 and Table 6 present the frequency matrix of and Yule’s Q coefficients for dimension transition of PSTs’ DT during the entire time period of innervation, respectively. Table 7 and Table 8 present the frequency matrix of and Yule’s Q coefficients for dimension transition of PSTs’ DT for Phases 1, 2, and 3, respectively. Figure 11 visualizes the DT dimension transitions of the whole intervention time period (Figure 11a) and in three different phases (Figure 11b11d). In Figure 9, node size represents the frequency of each DT dimension, arrow direction indicates the transition path, arrow thickness represents transition probability, and Yule’s Q values (−1 to +1) indicate the strength of association between transitions. \n\nTable 5, Table 6 and Figure 11 show that 25 statistically valid transition sequences were identified across the entire inquiry process. The most frequent was _Ideate → Ideate_ (ID→ID, n = 279), followed by _Define → Ideate_ (DF→ID, n = 117), highlighting a strong focus on idea generation. Two large-effect self-loops— _Discover → Discover_ (DC→DC, Q = 0.83) and _Test → Test_ (TE→TE, Q = 0.80)—indicated sustained engagement within these stages. Four medium-effect sequences—ID→ID (Q = 0.50), _Prototype → Prototype_ (PT→PT, Q = 0.50), PT→TE (Q = 0.50), and TE→PT (Q = 0.49)—showed active iteration between prototyping and testing. Smaller positive transitions such as DC→DF (Q = 0.38) and DF→ID (Q = 0.33) reflected early-stage movement from early-stage movement from problem exploration toward solution generation, though with weaker connections. \n\nTable 7 and 8, and Figure 11 shows different transition patterns of DT components in three different phases. In Phase 1, high-frequency sequences included ID→ID, DF→ID, ID→DF, and DF→DF, indicating concentrated activity within and between definition and ideation. Large \n\n30 \n\neffects were observed for DC→DC (Q = 0.81), PT→PT (Q = 0.95), PT→TE (Q = 0.63), and TE→TE (Q = 0.99), reflecting deep focus within individual stages and an emerging prototype– test loop. Medium and small effects (e.g., TE→PT, Q = 0.49; DF→ID, Q = 0.22) suggested initial but limited cross-stage integration. \n\nIn Phase 2, the top sequences were ID→ID, TE→TE, and ID→PT. Large effects included DC→DF (Q = 1.00), TE→TE (Q = 0.74), DC→DC (Q = 0.70), and ID→ID (Q = 0.67), indicating stronger procedural flow from discovery to definition and sustained focus in idea generation and testing. DF→DF (Q = 0.51) showed continued refinement of problem framing. Cross-stage links between prototyping and testing (PT→TE, Q = 0.24; TE→PT, Q = 0.22) remained relatively weak. \n\nIn Phase 3, TE→TE, PT→TE, TE→PT, and PT→PT were most frequent. Large effects were found for DF→DF (Q = 0.93), DC→DC (Q = 0.92), and DF→ID (Q = 0.62), showing strong stability in early-stage processes and strengthened links from problem definition to ideation. Medium effects such as ID→ID (Q = 0.50) and PT→TE (Q = 0.46) highlighted iterative refinement between prototyping and testing. Small effects (e.g., ID→PT, Q = 0.29; TE→TE, Q = 0.36) indicated ongoing integration of ideation with prototyping and validation. Across the three phases, three obvious developmental trajectories emerged. First, connections from lower-level to higher-level DT processes strengthened substantially over time, with Define → Ideate increasing from a small effect in Phase 1 (Q = 0.22) to a large effect in Phase 3 (Q = 0.62), indicating a more deliberate shift from problem framing to solution generation. Second, cycles among higher-level processes became more frequent and stronger, particularly Ideate → Prototype and Prototype → Test, reflecting more mature and iterative design practices. Third, while strong self-loops like Discover → Discover and Test → Test persisted, cross-stage integration improved, suggesting that the integrated use of GenAI and ASRA supported both sustained engagement within specific design stages and more fluid movement across stages over time. Table 5. The frequency matrix of dimension transition of PSTs’ DT during the entire time period of innervation DC DF ID PT TE \n\nAcross the three phases, three obvious developmental trajectories emerged. First, connections from lower-level to higher-level DT processes strengthened substantially over time, with Define → Ideate increasing from a small effect in Phase 1 (Q = 0.22) to a large effect in Phase 3 (Q = 0.62), indicating a more deliberate shift from problem framing to solution generation. Second, cycles among higher-level processes became more frequent and stronger, particularly Ideate → Prototype and Prototype → Test, reflecting more mature and iterative design practices. Third, while strong self-loops like Discover → Discover and Test → Test persisted, cross-stage integration improved, suggesting that the integrated use of GenAI and ASRA supported both sustained engagement within specific design stages and more fluid movement across stages over time. \n\n||DC<br>o|DF<br>|ID<br>|PT<br>|TE|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|DC|56<br>|38<br>|25<br>|5<br>|7|\n|DF|29<br>|63<br>|117<br>|5<br>|6|\n|ID|32|101|279|76|21|\n|PT|6|11|68|82|72|\n|TE|8|6|20|71|99|\n\n\n\nNote: DC = Discover; DF = Define; ID = Ideate; PT = Prototype; TE = Test. \n\nTable 6. Yule’s Q coefficients for dimension transition of DT in the whole time \n\n||DC|DF|ID|PT|TE|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|DC|0.83|0.38|-0.50|-0.73|-0.57|\n|DF|0.19|0.41|0.33|-0.84|-0.78|\n|ID|-0.36|0.17|0.50|-0.19|-0.75|\n|PT|-0.68|-0.67|-0.28|0.50|0.50|\n|TE|-0.51|-0.78|-0.76|0.49|0.80|\n\n\n\nNote: DC = Discover; DF = Define; ID = Ideate; PT = Prototype; TE = Test. \n\n31 \n\nTable 7. The frequency matrix of dimension transition of PSTs’ DT for three phases \n\n|Phase 1|DC|DF|ID|PT|TE|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|DC|51|29|22|2|0|\n|DF|24|61|103|1|1|\n|ID|28|94|174|17|1|\n|PT|1|6|12|32|4|\n|TE|0|0|3|3|9|\n|Phase 2|DC|DF|ID|PT|TE|\n|DC|3|11|3|0|0|\n|DF|8|1|2|0|1|\n|ID<br>|4<br>|0<br>|91<br>|47<br>|7|\n|PT<br>|2<br>|0<br>|45<br>|28<br>|38|\n|TE<br>|0<br>|0<br>|9<br>|37<br>|59|\n|Phase 3<br>|DC<br>|DF<br>|ID<br>|PT<br>|TE|\n|DC<br>|4<br>|0<br>|2<br>|1<br>|3|\n|DF<br>|0<br>|9<br>|8<br>|0<br>|0|\n|ID<br>|1<br>|6<br>|16<br>|19<br>|3|\n|PT<br>|2<br>|1<br>|9<br>-|21<br>|38|\n|TE<br> <br>|3<br> <br>|1<br> <br>|9<br> <br>re|30<br> <br>|41|\n\n\n\n1 \n\nNote: DC = Discover; DF = Define; ID = Ideate; PT = Prototype; TE = Test. \n\nTable 8. Yule’s Q coefficients for dimension transition of DT for three phases \n\n|8. Yule’s Q c<br>|oefficients for<br>|dimension tran<br>al|sition of DT for<br>|three phases<br>||\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Phase 1<br>|DC<br>|DF<br>|ID<br>|PT<br>|TE|\n|DC<br>|0.81<br>|0.00<br>|-0.59<br>|-0.68<br>|-1.00|\n|DF<br>|-0.15<br>|0.14<br>|0.22<br>|-0.92<br>|-0.70|\n|ID<br>|-0.46<br>|0.09<br>|0.33<br>|-0.34<br>|-0.85|\n|PT<br>|-0.83<br>|-0.55<br>|-0.54<br>|0.95<br>|0.63|\n|TE<br>|-1.00<br>|-1.00<br>|-0.56<br>|0.49<br>|0.99|\n|Phase 2<br>|DC<br>|DF<br>|ID<br>|PT<br>|TE|\n|DC|0.70|1.00|-0.49|-1.00|-1.00|\n|DF|0.98|0.51|-0.52|-1.00|-0.61|\n|ID|-0.34|-1.00|0.67|0.13|-0.86|\n|PT|-0.51|-1.00|0.06*|-0.12|0.24|\n|TE|-1.00|-1.00|-0.82|0.22|0.74|\n|Phase 3|DC|DF|ID|PT|TE|\n|DC|0.92|-1.00|0.02|-0.62|-0.17|\n|DF|-1.00|0.93|0.62|-1.00|-1.00|\n|ID|-0.39|0.41|0.50|0.29|-0.84|\n|PT|-0.30|-0.78|-0.33|-0.06|0.46|\n|TE|-0.16|-0.83|-0.46|0.16|0.36|\n\n\n\nNote: DC = Discover; DF = Define; ID = Ideate; PT = Prototype; TE = Test. \n\n32 \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n(a) Entire   (b) Phase 1<br>(c) Phase 2  (d) Phase 3<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n(c) Phase 2 (d) Phase 3 Figure 11. Visualization of DT dimension transitions: (a) overall, (b) Phase 1, (c) Phase 2, (d) Phase 3. Note: DC = Discover; DF = Define; ID = Ideate; PT = Prototype; TE = Test. **5. Discussion and Conclusion** _5.1. The Combined Effects of GenAI and ASRA on PSTs’ DT_ Results from both the scale and instructional design of lesson-design assessments indicate that integrating GenAI and ASRA into a KB collaborative inquiry environment selectively supported PSTs’ DT development rather than uniformly improving all DT phases. Overall scores improved significantly, especially in discovery and definition, suggesting that the design helped PSTs clarify problems and frame design goals. However, the pre–post change in selfreported DT was modest in magnitude, indicating gradual developmental movement rather than a large shift in perceived competence. By contrast, improvements in Ideate and Test were smaller and, in self-reported measures, not statistically significant, despite these phases being central to DT. \n\nThis pattern warrants careful interpretation. Importantly, the relatively small effect size observed in self-reported DT aligns with the study’s design-based and process-oriented orientation, where developmental change is expected to emerge incrementally across iterative design cycles rather than through abrupt shifts. Because the analytic sample consisted of 103 matched cases after excluding seven incomplete or inattentive responses, the results reflect stable within-participant change rather than large cohort-level differences. First, ideation and testing impose higher cognitive and epistemic demands, requiring learners to generate multiple alternatives, evaluate trade-offs, and revise solutions iteratively. Prior studies show that novice designers often experience cognitive overload in these phases and may underestimate their progress when design work becomes more complex and uncertain (Ohly et al., 2017). Second, in this study, GenAI use was intentionally delayed until PSTs had exhausted human-only \n\n33 \n\nrevision. This design may have shifted learners’ focus from perceived fluency to critical judgment, which could explain smaller gains on self-report measures but stronger improvements on performance-based outcomes. \n\nEvidence from instructional design artifacts and discourse trajectories provides a complementary perspective. Repeated-measures analyses showed clearer developmental gains in enacted design practices than in self-reported perceptions, suggesting that PSTs’ design capabilities may have evolved earlier than their self-evaluations. From a design-based research perspective, such divergence between perceived and enacted development is theoretically meaningful, as it indicates that pedagogical change may first manifest in practice before becoming visible in learners’ self-assessment. This triangulation of multiple data sources therefore strengthens the interpretation of DT growth beyond reliance on a single measure. In particular, the human–GenAI co-designed phase demonstrated accelerated growth between early and middle design cycles, suggesting that GenAI functioned as a catalyst for idea elaboration and refinement once foundational problem framing was established. These results underscore that the role of GenAI was not simply additive but developmental, supporting iterative refinement within an ongoing inquiry trajectory. The results align with and extend existing research. First, studies on GenAI in teacher education show benefits for brainstorming, drafting, and feedback (An et al., 2025; Nyaaba & Zhai, 2024), yet often report uneven gains across design phases (e.g., stronger idea fluency than systematic testing). Our results replicate these uneven patterns while offering a process-level explanation: ideation and testing benefited most when GenAI was embedded within reflective, analytics-informed inquiry rather than used as an immediate solution generator. Second, compared with prior KB studies that rely primarily on human facilitation (e.g., Lin et al., 2020), our results show that combining GenAI prompts with analytics-supported reflection reduces unproductive cycling and increases higher-order loops across phases, especially in prototyping and testing. This design operationalizes human–AI co-agency by maintaining learner ownership of decisions while using GenAI for generative support and analytics for regulatory guidance. _5.2. PSTs’ DT Evolution Paths with the Combined use of GenAI and ASRA_ This study traced how PSTs’ DT evolved across three phases of collaborative inquiry. Content analysis of 1,305 Knowledge Forum notes revealed a clear developmental shift from early-stage problem framing and ideation toward later-stage prototyping and testing. While ideation was most frequent in Phase 1, Phase 2 and 3 were marked by steady increases in prototyping and testing, accompanied by a small rebound in problem definition—suggesting that learners revisited and refined problem framing as their solutions matured. Transition analysis further revealed that early phases were characterized by persistence within single stages and fragmented links between stages. Over time, however, transitions between definition and ideation became stronger, and iterative loops between prototyping and testing grew more interconnected. Together, these patterns indicate that PSTs gradually moved from stage-bound engagement toward more integrated and iterative design activity, a trajectory that resembles key characteristics of expert-like design practice reported in prior DT research. Importantly, these interpretations reflect the study’s analytic framing. Because Knowledge Forum discourse was examined at an aggregated phase level, frequency shifts and transition patterns are interpreted as indicators of collective inquiry development rather than independent participantlevel effects. Consistent with design-based research, the analyses aim to trace trajectories of practice evolution within an authentic learning context rather than establish causal comparisons across isolated units. \n\nThese results enrich prior research on KB and DT in teacher education. Earlier studies have shown that PSTs often remain at descriptive or early stages of inquiry without sustained \n\n34 \n\nscaffolding (Hong et al., 2025). The study’s results align with these patterns in Phase 1, while further illustrating how the combined use of GenAI and ASRA may help reorganize discourse trajectories toward iterative refinement and cross-phase integration over time. This processoriented evidence complements existing work on GenAI in education, which have emphasized its potential for positive learning outcomes such as idea generation and social-emotional learning (Henriksen et al., 2025; Verma et al., 2023), but offered limited empirical evidence of how GenAI shapes the temporal structure of DT processes. process trajectories of DT over time. By examining both frequency distributions and transition patterns, this study demonstrates that the added value of GenAI and ASRA lies not only in increasing activity levels within individual DT phases, but also in strengthening connections across phases and supporting sustained iteration. \n\nRather than probabilistic claims about independent behaviors, transition statistics are interpreted as descriptive signals of evolving discourse organization within collaborative inquiry, consistent with learning sciences approaches that emphasize process trajectories and epistemic development over isolated counts. In this sense, the integrated design contributes to KB research by providing empirical evidence that a GenAI-supported and analytics-informed KB environment can foster more coherent and reflective design trajectories in PSTs. While this study does not examine large-scale implementation or cross-context transfer, its practical contribution lies in transferable pedagogical logics rather than immediate generalizability. The core design principles including cultivating a collaborative design culture, strategically delaying GenAI use, and embedding analytics-supported reflection within KB inquiry are not tied to a specific subject area, technology platform, or class size. Future work should examine how these principles function across institutions, cohorts, and teacher education contexts, and how they can be adapted for scaled or blended implementations. _5.3. Implications for Educational Practices for Supporting PSTs’ DT_ This study’s results offer actionable design implications for supporting PSTs’ DT in AI-rich teacher education contexts. First, a collaborative design culture should be established before intensive GenAI use, with explicit norms for idea improvement, critique, and shared responsibility. Such a foundation enables PSTs to engage with DT as a collective knowledgebuilding activity rather than as individual task completion. Second, GenAI should be introduced strategically rather than continuously. Delayed or conditional access encourages PSTs to engage in independent reasoning and peer-based refinement before turning to AI support. When combined with requirements to justify the adoption or rejection of AI suggestions, this approach positions GenAI as a resource for reflection and comparison, rather than an authoritative solution provider. Third, structured reflective prompts and analytics-based visualizations are essential for supporting sustained DT. These tools help PSTs monitor collective progress, identify gaps in design reasoning, and coordinate inquiry across phases, making complex design processes visible and manageable. Finally, instructional design artifacts should be treated as evolving objects, with planned cycles of revision, reflection, and justification. This practice aligns learning activities with the iterative and non-linear nature of authentic design work and supports DT as a developmental process. Overall, these implications highlight that the educational value of GenAI lies not in automation, but in principled pedagogical integration that foregrounds reflection, judgment, and collaborative inquiry. \n\n## _5.4. Limitations and Future Directions_ \n\nThis study has several limitations that also point to directions for future work. First, the participating PSTs were relatively homogeneous, consisting mainly of female PSTs from an \n\n35 \n\nEnglish education program at a teacher education university. While this reflects the typical composition of the program, it limits the generalizability of the findings to other subject domains, institutional settings, or more gender-balanced cohorts. Future research should examine whether similar DT trajectories emerge in PST programs with different disciplinary focuses and demographic profiles. Second, the intervention was implemented by an instructor with extensive prior experience in KB and ASRA, which may introduce instructor expectancy effects. Although this expertise supported faithful implementation, future studies could involve multiple instructors or use more standardized facilitation protocols to better separate instructional design effects from instructor-related influences. Third, the instructional sequence required PSTs to first produce a human-only instructional design before engaging in GenAIsupported co-design, which introduces a potential order or practice effect. This sequencing was intentionally designed to foreground epistemic agency and reduce premature reliance on AI; however, it limits causal claims about the independent contribution of GenAI. Future work could adopt counterbalanced or parallel-group designs to more rigorously isolate the effects of GenAI at different points in the design process. Finally, although the study theorizes scalability at the level of reusable pedagogical principles—such as delayed GenAI use, structured reflective prompts, and discourse analytics—it does not empirically test large-scale or crosscontext implementation. Future design-based and quasi-experimental research is needed to examine how these principles function with larger cohorts, different instructors, and varied institutional conditions, as well as whether DT development transfers to later teaching practice. _5.5. Conclusions_ This study examined whether and how PSTs’ DT can be supported through the integration of GenAI and ASRA within a KB collaborative inquiry environment. By conceptualizing DT as a developmental and socially mediated practice rather than a set of isolated skills, we articulated a theory-informed design framework and operationalized it through an integrated pedagogical approach. As clarified earlier in Section 3.2, the term “GenAI-RKBCoD” is used only as a concise label for this integrated design, ensuring terminological continuity rather than introducing a new model at the conclusion stage. Across complementary data sources, the results consistently show that PSTs’ DT developed over time, with clear gains in problem discovery and definition and increasingly integrated engagement across ideation, prototyping, and testing. The results further indicate that GenAI contributed most effectively when positioned as a reflective design partner rather than an immediate solution generator. When GenAI use was deliberately delayed and embedded within collaborative inquiry, it supported idea expansion and refinement without undermining PSTs’ epistemic agency. At the same time, ASRA helped PSTs monitor collective progress, identify gaps in design reasoning, and sustain productive iteration. Together, these findings highlight the importance of aligning GenAI use with pedagogical structures that foreground reflection, judgment, and collective responsibility. \n\nThis study provides a process-oriented account of how DT can be cultivated through principled integration of Gen AI and reflective assessment supported by analytics. By tracing changes in design performance and inquiry trajectories over time, the study provides empirical evidence that DT development can be supported as a gradual shift from stage-bound participation toward more coherent and iterative design activity. Theoretically, this study extends learning sciences research by articulating how GenAI and ASRA can be productively integrated within a KB environment to support DT development, enriching discussions of human–AI co-agency, collective responsibility for idea improvement, and embedded assessment in knowledge creation. Practically, the study offers a principled and empirically grounded approach for teacher education, suggesting that carefully designed GenAI–analytics– KB integration can support PSTs in developing reflective, adaptive, and collaborative design \n\n36 \n\ncapacities essential for teaching in AI-rich contexts. While further research is needed to examine scalability and transfer, this work provides a foundation for designing AI-augmented learning environments that prioritize deep, process-oriented professional learning. \n\n### \n\n37 \n\n## **Appendix A. Representative Sample of GenAI Prompts and Decisions Based on the DDIPT Model A.1 Table D1. A prompting action guide** \n\nPrompt-type key (used in Table D1–D2): \n\n- A. Idea expansion: Generate multiple candidate options. \n\n- B. Alignment diagnosis: Check and critique alignment among goals, activities, assessment, and time. \n\n- C. Structured prototype: Turn a chosen option into an implementable procedure/script. \n\n- D. Differentiation & scaffolds: Design supports for mixed levels (basic/challenge), reduce anxiety, prevent simple copying. \n\n- E. Assessment & feedback: Design low-burden formative assessment and feedback loops. \n\n- F. Constraint-based revision: Revise under constraints (time, resources, classroom management) and stress-test against scenarios. \n\n|**DDIPT stage**|**Design objective in this stage**<br>**P**<br>|**rompt type(s)**<br>|**Recommended prompt template (concise)**<br>r|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Discover|Identify key problems in the current lesson-plan<br>segment and clarify why it needs revision.<br>|B + F<br>Pre-|Template: “Based on the lesson-plan segment/context below, identify three<br>major issues (ranked by severity) and explain how each may hinder goal<br>attainment. Then propose two revision directions (a minor tweak and a<br>structural change).”<br>p|\n|Define|Translate needs into measurable learning goals<br>and explicit constraints so the model does not<br>generate generic ideas.<br>urn|B<br>al|Template: “Our learning goal is … (measurable). Constraints: …<br>(time/learner profile/resources/classroom management). Please (1) refine the<br>goal into 2–3 success criteria, and (2) specify activity requirements that match<br>these criteria (e.g., each student must …).”<br>|\n|Ideate|Generate alternative activities without rewriting<br>the whole lesson; compare trade-offs under<br>constraints.<br>Jo|A + F<br>|Template: “Propose three alternative activities (do not rewrite the entire<br>lesson). For each option, specify: (1) teacher moves, (2) student talk/skill<br>target, (3) risks and mitigations, and (4) which learner profiles it fits<br>best/least. Keep total time ≤ … minutes and avoid extra platforms.”<br>|\n|Prototype|Convert<br>the<br>selected<br>option<br>into<br>an<br>implementable classroom procedure and add<br>scaffolds for mixed proficiency.|C + D|Template: “Select the best option and write it as a ready-to-paste lesson-plan<br>step: a minute-by-minute timeline, teacher instructions, student actions, target<br>language, and materials/board plan. Provide a basic and a challenge scaffold,<br>and explain how to prevent students from simply reading aloud.”|\n|Test|Evaluate whether the activity produces evidence<br>of learning, and iterate based on formative<br>assessment.|E + B|Template: “Design a low-burden formative assessment for this step: what to<br>observe and when, a simple 3-level rubric, and how to close the feedback loop<br>in ≤2 minutes. Then test the design using two counterfactual classroom<br>scenarios and provide a revised version.”|\n\n\n\n**Prompt type(s)** B + F structural change).” B A + F \n\n38 \n\n## **A.2 Table D2. Representative multi-turn interaction sample (one design change)** \n\nThis section focuses on one specific revision point in Group 7’s lesson plan (Listening and Talking): Step 2 Pre-listening. It documents ten turns of interaction around the same design change, showing how students iteratively refined requirements, added constraints, and aligned the activity with listening targets and evidence of learning. **Original lesson-plan excerpt (before revision): Step 2 Pre-listening (6 minutes)** \n\nMain approach: The teacher shows pictures of “Adventure Camp” and “International Youth Camp” and asks students to discuss and predict: \n\n- Can you name these camps? \n\n- What kind of camp are they? \n\n- Who will be there? \n\n|What<br>What<br>**Round**|will they do?<br>will they learn i<br>**DDIPT stage**|n these camps?<br>**Prompt type(s)**|**Student prompt (excerpt)**<br>|**Key model output (excerpt)**<br>proof|**Decision and lesson-plan**<br>**revision**|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|1|Discover|B + A|Please optimize this picture-based lead-in<br>+ prediction activity so it is more<br>engaging and motivates students.<br>nal Pr|Suggests multiple “fun lead-in” ideas (e.g.,<br>guessing game, role-play, scenario simulation,<br>picture-story chain, question cards, mind map,<br>group competition).<br>e-|Rewrite. Some ideas require extra<br>platforms (e.g., Kahoot), which<br>violates<br>resource<br>constraints.<br>Keep<br>“pictures<br>+<br>prediction<br>questions”<br>and<br>reserve<br>role-<br>play/question-card elements for<br>later refinement.|\n|2|Define →<br>Ideate|A|Design one pre-listening activity that<br>trains students’ prediction skill for this<br>listening-and-speaking lesson.<br>Jour|Proposes a “Future camp design challenge” with<br>roles, cards, mapping, presentation, and feedback.<br>|Reject. Too many steps/materials;<br>not feasible for a 6-minute pre-<br>listening slot. Shift to a single<br>activity with high-density output.|\n|3|Define|B + F|Design only one activity, keep it within 5<br>minutes, and make it closely tied to the<br>listening content. Please be specific.<br>|Provides a 5-minute flow: quick picture viewing<br>→ flash questions → individual writing → group<br>sharing → whole-class prediction → teacher<br>transition to listening.<br>|Rewrite. Timing is feasible, but<br>“individual writing” may reduce<br>oral production. Keep the fast<br>“predict-then-verify”<br>rhythm;<br>require each student to say at least<br>one future-tense prediction.|\n|4|Discover →<br>Define|B|I still worry about: (1) students may write<br>words instead of speaking English; (2)<br>predictions may not align with listening<br>focus (what they are going to do at camp;<br>future<br>expressions).<br>Diagnose<br>and<br>propose targeted fixes; label which fix<br>addresses which issue.|Recommends:<br>replace<br>writing<br>with<br>oral<br>prediction + peer follow-up questions; require<br>future structures (be going to / will); reduce the<br>five questions to two focused ones; add a<br>Prediction–Evidence chart to connect to during-<br>listening verification.|Adopt. Refine questions; add a<br>language-form constraint (future<br>tense);<br>add<br>the<br>Prediction–<br>Evidence chart and explicitly link<br>it to during-listening tasks.|\n|5|Ideate|A + F|Without<br>increasing<br>time<br>(total<br>≤6|Offers three options: (A) info-gap clue cards|Adopt (A as core; integrate talk|\n\n\n\n- What will they do? \n\n- What will they learn in these camps? \n\n39 \n\n|||minutes) and without extra platforms,<br>propose three alternative pre-listening<br>prediction versions. For each: teacher<br>moves, student talk goals (≥1 suggestion<br>+<br>≥1<br>follow-up<br>question),<br>and<br>risks/mitigations.|(students<br>hold<br>different<br>clues<br>and<br>must<br>ask/answer); (B) “two titles, one true” with<br>evidence; (C) prediction sentence chain, plus<br>classroom-management tips.|norms from C). Final direction:<br>use info-gap clue cards and make<br>“suggest + ask follow-up” an<br>explicit participation norm.|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|6<br>Prototype|C|I choose option A (info-gap clue cards).<br>Write<br>a<br>ready-to-paste<br>6-minute<br>procedure: minute-by-minute; teacher<br>instructions; student actions and target<br>language<br>(future<br>tense,<br>suggestions,<br>follow-up questions); materials limited to<br>pictures + paper cards.<br>|Outputs a structured script (e.g., 0:00–1:00<br>picture activation; 1:00–3:00 pair exchange using<br>future predictions; 3:00–5:00 group consolidation<br>+ prepare one follow-up; 5:00–6:00 rapid sharing<br>+ board key predictions), plus teacher talk and<br>sentence supports.<br>of|A