T and generative models in science teacher education. _Journal of Science Teacher Education, 34_ (8), 793–798. https://doi.org/10.1080/1046560X.2023.2263251 \n\n- White, B. Y., & Frederiksen, J. R. (1998). Inquiry, modeling, and metacognition: Making science accessible to all students. _Cognition and instruction_ , _16_ (1), 3-118. https://doi.org/10.1207/s1532690xci1601_2 \n\n- Wise, A. F., Knight, S., & Shum, S. B. (2021). Collaborative learning analytics. In Cress, U., Rosé, C., Wise, A.F., Oshima, J. (eds), _International handbook of computer-supported collaborative learning_ (pp. 425-443). Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-03065291-3_23 \n\n- Wise, A. F., & Schwarz, B. B. (2017). Visions of CSCL: Eight provocations for the future of the field. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _12_ (4), 423-467. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11412-017-9267-5 \n\n- Yüksel, A. O. (2025). Design-based STEM activities in teacher education and its effect on pre-service science teachers’ design thinking skills. _Journal of Science Education and Technology_ , 1–15. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10956-025-10215-2 \n\n- Zhang, J., Scardamalia, M., Lamon, M., Messina, R., & Reeve, R. (2007). Socio-cognitive dynamics of knowledge building in the work of 9- and 10-year-olds. _Educational Technology Research and Development, 55_ (2), 117–145. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11423-006-9019-0 \n\n- Verma, G., Campbell, T., Melville, W., & Park, B. Y. (2023). Navigating opportunities and challenges of artificial intelligence: ChatGPT and generative models in science teacher education. _Journal of Science Teacher Education, 34_ (8), 793–798. https://doi.org/10.1080/1046560X.2023.2263251 \n\n- Wise, A. F., & Schwarz, B. B. (2017). Visions of CSCL: Eight provocations for the future of the field. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _12_ (4), 423-467. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11412-017-9267-5 \n\n- Zhang, J., Tao, D., Chen, M. H., Sun, Y., Judson, D., & Naqvi, S. (2018). Co-organizing the collective journey of inquiry with idea thread mapper. _Journal of the Learning Sciences, 27_ (3), 390–430. https://doi.org/10.1080/10508406.2018.1444992 \n\n- Zhang, J., Yuan, G., & Bogouslavsky, M. (2020). Give student ideas a larger stage: Support cross-community interaction for knowledge building. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning, 15(4)_ , 389–410. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11412-020-09332-4 \n\n52 \n\n## Highlights \n\n- Conceptualizes design thinking (DT) as a socially mediated, developmental practice \n\n- Proposes a theory-informed model linking KB, GenAI, and reflective assessment \n\n- Operationalizes design thinking through a principle-based, replicable pedagogy \n\n- Integrates delayed GenAI use with analytics-supported reflective assessment \n\n- Reveals shifts from stage-bound to iterative design practices in PST learning \n\n- Advances theorization of human–AI co-agency in collaborative inquiry contexts \n\n', NULL, '2026-09-12 16:17:48', '2026-09-12 16:18:29', '2026-C&E-Yang-Fostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI.pdf', 'project_1026/raw/2026-C&E-Yang-Fostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI.pdf', 'application/pdf', 4058595, '4134d6da05d2d357cf6aaf2d4bbfaaff27a62062c8310b913f92a3d99dedc96b', '4134d6da05d2d357cf6aaf2d4bbfaaff27a62062c8310b913f92a3d99dedc96b', 'project_1026/parsed/2026-C&E-Yang-Fostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI.md', 'ready', NULL);
INSERT INTO `research_resource` VALUES (31, 3, 1026, 'UPLOAD', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'TEXT_EXTRACTED', 'Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399 \n\n\n\nContents lists available at ScienceDirect \n\n# Computers & Education \n\njournal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/compedu \n\n\n\nThe balancing act between AI and authenticity in assessment: A case study of secondary school students’ use of GenAI in reflective writing \n\n\n\n## Cecilia Ka Yuk Chan<sup>a,*</sup> , Katherine K.W. Lee<sup>b</sup> \n\na _Faculty of Education/Teaching and Learning Innovation Centre, University of Hong Kong, Pokfulam, Hong Kong_ b _Teaching and Learning Innovation Centre, University of Hong Kong, Pokfulam, Hong Kong_ \n\nA R T I C L E I N F O A B S T R A C T _Keywords:_ This study examines how secondary school students balance the use of Generative AI (GenAI) Generative AI tools while maintaining authenticity in reflective writing. Reflective writing, a cognitively Reflective writing demanding task, can benefit from AI’s ability to improve efficiency and organisation. Using a case Secondary school studentsCase study study approach, data were collected from the reflective writing pieces of five secondary school students, as well as the accompanying GenAI chat logs and individual interviews following a AI guilt Ethical AI use three-week summer internship programme. Thematic analysis found that while GenAI supports technical quality and reduces cognitive load, it also poses challenges regarding authenticity, depth of reflection, and students’ over-reliance on AI. Students reported experiencing AI guilt, particularly regarding perceived laziness, fear of judgment, and concerns over self-efficacy. The findings highlight a tension between producing polished writing and preserving personal voice. Educators must guide students to use AI ethically, ensuring that it supports rather than undermines authentic reflection. \n\n### **1. Introduction** \n\nReflective writing is a widely-used pedagogical tool in both secondary and higher education, notably within experiential learning opportunities including internships. Engaging in the reflective process encourages students to critically evaluate their experiences and promotes deeper understanding, self-regulated learning, critical thinking skills, lifelong learning, and personal growth (Boud et al., 2013; Fullana et al., 2016; Rogers, 2001). It also facilitates their making of connections between classroom learning with their real-life experiences, making learning more meaningful and relevant (Ash & Clayton, 2009; Higgins, 2011). \n\nAt the same time, reflective writing can be challenging for students due to its demanding nature, requiring complex cognitive processes and substantial time investment (Chan & Lee, 2021). Consequently, students may struggle with the various demands of reflective writing, which can lead to superficial engagement. Not only does this hinder the depth of critical and meaningful reflection, but variations in engagement and motivation will also affect the quality of students’ reflective process. \n\nWith the rising prevalence of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) and its increasing integration into educational practices, students now have access to a multitude of GenAI tools that can assist them with the various aspects of writing, including with \n\n- Corresponding author. Faculty of Education/Teaching and Learning Innovation Centre (TALIC, University of Hong Kong), Room CPD-1.81, \n\n- Centennial Campus, Pokfulam Road, Hong Kong. \n\n_E-mail addresses:_ ckchan09@hku.hk (C.K.Y. Chan), kathkw@connect.hku.hk (K.K.W. Lee). \n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.compedu.2025.105399 \n\nReceived 22 September 2024; Received in revised form 6 June 2025; Accepted 9 July 2025 Available online 10 July 2025 0360-1315/© 2025 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY license ( http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ ). \n\n_Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399_ \n\n_C.K.Y. Chan and K.K.W. Lee                                                                                                                                                                                       Computers & Education 238_ \n\nreflection. These tools can offer support in idea generation, grammar correction, and even full-text generation, potentially easing the burden of reflective tasks. However, the use of GenAI in academic writing, particularly in reflective practices, raises important questions about its impact on student learning, motivation, and the authenticity of the reflective process. \n\nAt the secondary school level, reflective writing is particularly challenging due to two reasons. First, teachers tend to focus on content coverage and passive learning (Denton, 2011), depriving students of the opportunities to reflect. Second, reflective writing requires complex reasoning, which is usually developed in late adolescence and adulthood (King & Kitchener, 2004). AI poses similar challenges to secondary school students. AI mastery is developmental, meaning advanced concepts and skills are often too difficult for junior secondary school students (Ng et al., 2024). Lee et al.’s (2021, pp. 191–197) study found that GenAI activities are in general cognitively demanding for middle secondary school students. Nevertheless, it is important to promote reflective writing and GenAI competencies in secondary schools to lay the foundation for more advanced learning at the postsecondary level. In view of these, the current study focused on secondary school students’ use of GenAI in reflective writing. \n\nThis study sought to answer the question: “How does GenAI support or hinder the development of deeper reflection in secondary school students’ reflective writing?” by exploring the ways in which secondary school students used and interacted with GenAI tools during a reflective writing task following a three-week internship programme. It looked at both the process of how students engaged with AI as well as the outcomes in terms of the depth and quality of their reflection. By investigating these processes during the task, focusing on students’ rationales and decisions to use or not to use GenAI, this paper looks at the implications of AI-assisted reflection on student learning. The findings will be particularly relevant in light of existing literature on the challenges that students face in reflective practices, as well as the increasing integration of GenAI into education. \n\n### **2. Literature review** \n\nIn their systematic literature review on the challenges of reflection in education, Chan and Lee (2021) propose a framework outlining student-learning, teacher-pedagogical, institutional, and sociocultural factors that can impede efforts to promote student reflection. Central to this framework is the student-learning level, which describes the challenges that students face in developing their capacity for meaningful reflection. Focused on the reflective practice of individual students, this study explored their perceptions and experiences of AI-assisted reflective writing at the student-learning level. In this section, common challenges students face in reflective writing and the impact of GenAI on reflective writing will be discussed. \n\n### _2.1. Challenges for student engagement in reflective writing_ \n\n### _2.1.1. Cognitive demands and processes_ \n\nAs mentioned, engaging in reflection requires complex cognitive processes that may be time-consuming and cognitively demanding. Studies have shown that time is a critical factor influencing student motivation and engagement in reflective activities (Ahmed, 2020; Fakazli and Gonen, 2017¨ ; Mahlanze & Sibiya, 2017; O’Connell & Dyment, 2011), where the need for substantial time commitment often leads to students’ perceptions that reflective tasks are burdensome, resulting in reduced engagement levels and potentially shallow reflections (Jindal-Snape & Holmes, 2009; Power, 2012). As Chan and Lee (2021) stress in their reflection literacy framework, time constraint, lack of motivation, as well as challenges in comprehending the significance and purpose of reflection are barriers to student engagement with reflection tasks. \n\nThis is further compounded by the cognitive demands of reflection. Students may struggle to elaborate their responses, especially if they are accustomed to providing brief answers (Ahmed, 2020). Students may also prioritise quantity over quality in their writing (McGarr & Moody, 2010), particularly when looking to satisfy formal assessment requirements and when their reflections will be graded. When students are unable to meet reflection-based goals, they may feel even less inclined to engage deeply with the reflective process, viewing it as additional workload rather than a meaningful learning activity (Mortari, 2012; O’Reilly & Milner, 2015), resulting in a performative reflection (Ryan, 2013) \n\nAt the same time, poor quality and “mechanical” reflective pieces (Power, 2012) may be a result of students’ struggles to understand the purpose and objectives of reflection, particularly when they lack training, prior exposure, and knowledge of how to structure their reflections effectively (O’Connell & Dyment, 2011). Ryan (2012, 2013) highlights issues such as lack of focus, neglecting supporting evidence, and failure to connect experiences to beliefs and values, all of which can impede students’ ability to reconstruct their learning, hindering the integration of new knowledge and leading to superficial reflection. \n\n### _2.1.2. Contextual and environmental influences_ \n\nApart from the cognitive demands of reflective writing, contextual and environmental factors such as academic cultures and pedagogical practices can also pose challenges to reflective writing activities among students. It should be noted that the abundance of literature on reflective writing is predominantly within higher education settings, possibly due to the increased autonomy afforded to students in this less formal environment, as Tight (2024) points out. Its flexibility and emphasis on self-directed learning may offer students more opportunities for extracurricular involvement, independent exploration, and critical thinking, creating an environment conducive to reflective practices. \n\nIn contrast, secondary schools often adhere to stricter and more rigid curricula, which may limit opportunities for personalised reflection and autonomous learning experiences; consequently, secondary school students may vary in their exposure to and understanding of reflective practices, as well as in their metacognitive development. There is some literature that looks at the context of secondary schools, many of which further emphasise the cognitive demands and processes discussed above. \n\n2 \n\n_Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399_ \n\n_C.K.Y. Chan and K.K.W. Lee                                                                                                                                                                                       Computers & Education 238_ \n\nFor example, Ramlal and Augustin (2020) highlight the inherent challenges that adolescents and young adults face when engaging in reflective practices which require a sophisticated interplay of cognitive and emotional processing, as well as a certain level of linguistic ability. Although high school students (grades 9 to 12) are expected to possess metacognitive skills (Schneider, 2008), studies on this demographic have found that students still express uncertainty regarding how reflections should be constructed and organised (Ramlal & Augustin, 2020; Van Velzen, 2017). Greene (2011) also observed surface-level reflections in 9th graders due to unfamiliarity with reflective processes, leading to simplistic statements like “I enjoyed/hated the activity”; this superficiality reveals motivational and psychological barriers hindering deeper introspection. \n\nEffective reflection necessitates the ability to derive meaning from experiences, which is further complicated by secondary school students’ varying levels of metacognitive knowledge – the process of thinking, which underscores the contemplative essence of reflective thinking (Jaleel & Premachandran, 2016; Moon, 2001). \n\n### _2.1.3. Ethical concerns and emotional impact of reflective practice_ \n\nAnother factor influencing student engagement in reflective writing is the ethical and emotional impact of reflective practice. Reflective writing often involves the expression of personal thoughts and emotions, which can be uncomfortable for students. The requirement to share reflections with teachers or peers, coupled with the assessment of such personal content, can lead to feelings of vulnerability, anxiety, and reluctance to engage fully in the reflective process (Fernandez-Pe´ na et al., 2016˜ ; O’Connell & Dyment, 2011). \n\nStudents’ discomfort may be further compounded by ethical concerns about privacy and the potential for reflections to be judged or scrutinised by others (Chan & Lee, 2021). As Tight (2024) writes, the act of reflection is usually a private and personal process, though in education, it may involve students sharing their thoughts with others such as their peers and teachers; requiring reflection to be mandatory, shared, and possibly evaluated by a teacher or supervisor can jeopardise the individual’s privacy, exposing them to scrutiny and evaluation. The discomfort, which may also be due to the intrusive nature of reflection and the potential need to confront painful or unpleasant memories, can further result in self-censorship, fabrication of reflections, or once again, superficial engagement with the task (Fakazli & Gonen, 2017¨ ; Olson et al., 2016). \n\n### _2.2. Potential impact of GenAI on reflective writing and learning_ \n\nWith GenAI being increasingly integrated in teaching and learning, its potential for enhancing the teaching of writing has gained traction in recent higher education research (e.g., Ironsi & Ironsi, 2025; Kim et al., 2025). However, there are relatively few studies on the impact of GenAI on reflective writing especially at the secondary school level. The following sections focus on the potential impact of GenAI on reflective writing and learning among Generation Z, exploring Generation Z learners’ perceptions of GenAI, the potential benefits and applications of GenAI, as well as the concerns and challenges associated with its use in teaching and learning. \n\n### _2.2.1. Gen Z perception of GenAI_ \n\nGeneration Z (Gen Z), encompassing individuals born between 1995 and 2012, is characterised by their immersion and exposure to digital technology and social media from a young age (Chan & Lee, 2023). This generation, comprising present-day high school and undergraduate students, typically favour hybrid learning methods integrating technology and multimedia, alongside a desire for personalised learning environments (Seemiller & Grace, 2016, 2017). Gen Z learners also show preferences for tech-driven educational experiences with real-world relevance, and showcase self-sufficiency in utilising technology for information retrieval and problem-solving (Genota, 2018). \n\nAt the same time, students of this demographic also tend to expect immediate feedback (Chan & Lee, 2023), with research suggesting that they may also exhibit shortcomings in critical thinking and information literacy skills (Bashri & Rafeeq, 2020; Marr, 2022; Seibert, 2020). While they may be quicker and more adept at adopting GenAI compared to older generations (Chan & Colloton, 2024), the extent to which they engage with and employ AI critically, while continuing to hone their own skills and foster personal growth, remains an open question. Another challenge is the rise of AI Guilt, defined by Chan (2024) as the moral discomfort that arises from using AI in tasks traditionally performed by humans. AI guilt can manifest in three main dimensions: perceived laziness and authenticity, fear of judgment, and identity and self-efficacy concerns (Chan, 2025a,b). \n\n### _2.2.2. Potential benefits and applications_ \n\nThe incorporation of generative AI tools, which are now widely accessible to the public, into reflective writing presents a number of potential avenues for enhancing students’ reflective practices. This include alleviating the aforementioned cognitive demands of reflective writing, where AI can assist students with brainstorming, organising, and proofreading. At the same time, AI can also be used to more explicitly guide students throughout the reflective process, such as by offering topics, structure, and cues to help students explore new perspectives and analyse their experiences more deeply. \n\nLiterature has frequently underscored the importance of prompting and feedback in facilitating effective reflection. For example, Van Velzen (2017) suggests that instructor prompts and feedback are crucial in guiding reflective writing, particularly for students who are hesitant to engage in reflection. Similarly, Chen et al. (2009) emphasise the value of prompting in online reflection tasks, while Berthold et al. (2007) found that students who received cognitive or metacognitive prompts while journaling experienced improved learning outcomes. Al-Rawahi and Al-Balushi (2015) further support this notion, indicating that writing alone may not yield significant learning benefits unless coupled with metacognitive prompts that encourage students to reflect on their knowledge and learning processes. \n\n3 \n\n_Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399_ \n\n_C.K.Y. Chan and K.K.W. Lee                                                                                                                                                                                       Computers & Education 238_ \n\nGenerative AI (GenAI) tools, such as ChatGPT and Bing, have emerged as innovative supports for enhancing students’ reflective capabilities. Vasconcelos and dos Santos (2023) describe these tools as instruments that can stimulate critical thinking, creativity, and problem-solving skills in educational contexts. Although research on GenAI’s impact on students’ writing remains limited – largely due to its relatively new release to the wider public – existing studies highlight its various potentials for students’ writing. \n\nFor instance, AI-powered tools can be used to facilitate various aspects of academic writing, including grammar checking, vocabulary suggestions, and sentence structuring, thereby improving clarity and coherence (Alharbi, 2023; Dale & Viethen, 2021; Marzuki et al., 2023). Recent studies have found that ChatGPT was beneficial in assisting students with generating initial ideas and structuring research proposals, though students had to refine and elaborate on the content themselves (Farrokhnia et al., 2024; Rospigliosi, 2023). Additionally, Gayed and colleagues (2022) found that AI tools had a positive impact on students’ writing proficiency and self-efficacy through the provision of timely feedback and support in improving their writing skills. \n\nIn terms of applications to reflective writing, some studies have explored the use of software such as AcaWriter for reflective writing, which utilises natural language processing techniques to analyse reflective language, in order to offer formative feedback. Research has shown that AcaWriter’s feedback can enhance students’ self-reflection, peer-reflection, and self-improvement, while also being user-friendly and fostering confidence (Lucas et al., 2021). \n\n### _2.2.3. Compounded concerns and challenges_ \n\nOn the other hand, the use of AI simultaneously raises concerns regarding the authenticity and originality of student reflections. As Chan and Lee (2021) remark, emerging modes of reflection, made possible by technological advancements, have introduced a range of new challenges and further emphasise pre-existing ethical issues related to personal disclosure and privacy. \n\nThe potential for students to over-rely on technology could detract from the personal and introspective nature of reflective practice (Cheng & Chan, 2019; Roche & Coote, 2008). Results from an experiment by Darvishi and colleagues (2024) found that students tended to rely on AI assistance rather than learn from it; additionally, those relying too heavily on AI may also do so at the expense of practising and developing their own critical thinking and writing skills – both skills necessary for effective reflection – further hampering their ability to express themselves and make sense of their own experiences. In the context of reflective writing, over-reliance on GenAI can defeat the purpose of the task, which is to develop critical and reflective thinking. When students use GenAI to generate a piece of reflective writing without integrating their own insights and without actively scrutinising GenAI’s outputs, they adopt the role of passive learners, which in turn reduce their engagement in the learning process. Such a phenomenon may result in students treating reflective writing as merely a task to be completed without paying attention to the quality of their work rather than viewing it as an opportunity to develop metacognitive and critical thinking. \n\nSimilarly, Chan (2025a,b) highlights the issue of “AI-giarism,” questioning whether utilising AI outputs without proper citation constitutes a form of plagiarism, especially given that generative AI tools are trained on existing data, obscuring the origins of their outputs. This is echoed by Fyfe (2022), who raises questions about the ownership and representation of ideas generated by AI, noting the difficulty in identifying the viewpoints expressed by AI outputs. \n\nThe complexity of learning, which encompasses emotional factors such as motivation and context, further complicates the integration of AI in reflective practices. AI’s role in reflective writing intersects with issues of both student motivation and engagement. For instance, students may turn to AI to alleviate the perceived burden of reflective tasks, but this could lead to a more mechanical approach to reflection, where the focus shifts from deep learning to mere task completion. There are also practical implications of integrating AI in the classroom. \n\n### **3. Methodology** \n\n### _3.1. Research design_ \n\nThis research adopted a case study approach to explore how GenAI tools influence students’ engagement and motivation towards reflective writing, as well as the depth and quality of the writing itself. The case study method was chosen to provide an in-depth understanding of the phenomenon within a real-life context, focusing on five secondary school students who participated in a three-week summer internship programme related to GenAI in education. \n\nData was collected through three primary sources: students’ written reflections completed at the end of their internship, their GenAI chat logs containing their prompts and interactions with the AI tools they used to support their reflection task, and follow-up individual interviews (see section 3.3). These three data sources were triangulated to ensure a comprehensive analysis of how students used GenAI in their reflective writing and how it impacted their engagement, motivation, and reflective depth. The data was then subjected to thematic analysis, which was conducted collaboratively by two researchers to ensure rigor and consistency. \n\n### _3.2. Participants_ \n\nThe participants in this study were five secondary school students from an English-medium local secondary school in Hong Kong, aged between 14 and 17. These students participated in an internship programme that introduced them to AI tools and their applications in education. As part of the programme, students were required to submit reflective writing pieces at the end of their internship, use AI tools for research and writing tasks, and engage in follow-up interviews discussing their experiences with GenAI. \n\n4 \n\n_Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399_ \n\n_C.K.Y. Chan and K.K.W. Lee                                                                                                                                                                                       Computers & Education 238_ \n\n### _3.3. Data collection_ \n\nThe data was collected from three key sources. \n\n1. **Reflective Writing Logs** : Each student submitted a 500-word reflective writing piece as part of the internship programme. These reflections detailed the students’ learning experiences, the challenges they faced, and their accomplishments. The students were encouraged to use AI tools during the writing process, and their reflections also included insights into their use of GenAI for research, structuring, and text generation. \n\n2. **GenAI Chat Logs** : Students were required to submit their chat logs with the AI tools they used to assist their reflective writing process via POE. These logs provided insight into how students interacted with AI, including the prompts and types of questions they asked, how they utilised AI-generated content, and whether they relied on AI for surface-level tasks or deeper reflective insights. \n\n3. **Individual Interviews** : Follow-up individual interviews were conducted with each participant to explore their perceptions of using AI for reflective writing. The interviews focused on how the students felt AI tools influenced their motivation, engagement, and reflective depth, as well as any challenges or ethical concerns they encountered during the process. \n\n### _3.4. Data analysis_ \n\nTo analyse the collected data, thematic analysis was employed to identify patterns and themes within the data related to the paper’s research question. This approach was chosen because it is well-suited for exploring complex phenomena including student engagement and reflective writing. \n\nAn inductive thematic analysis was conducted following Braun and Clarke’s (2006) six-phase process: familiarising with data, generating initial codes, searching for themes, reviewing themes, defining and naming themes, and producing the report. The reflective writing logs, GenAI chat logs, and transcribed individual interviews were analysed separately. To ensure reliability, the process was undertaken by the authors independently. For each dataset, the data were read several times to obtain a thorough understanding of the ideas and issues contained in them. Since the aim of thematic analysis is to describe surface-level and latent ideas in the data, the frequency of words or phrases is often not the focus of the analysis (Guest et al., 2012). Hence, an idea that appeared once \n\n**Table 1** \n\nSecondary school students’ experiences with the use of GenAI in reflective writing. \n\n|Aspect|Theme|Description|Code|Sample Quotes|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|**Cognitive and**<br>**affective**<br>**engagement**|**AI as a tool for**<br>**enhancing efficiency**<br>**and reducing cognitive**<br>**load**<br>**i**|AI made the reflective writing process more<br>efficient by helping students quickly<br>organise their thoughts and overcome<br>writer’s block. This kept them engaged and<br>motivated to complete the task.|Efficiency, writer’s<br>block, time-saving|-_“I had no idea where to start, so I_<br>_used AI to give me an outline. It_<br>_helped me get things moving.” _–Heidi<br>-_“It made it faster because I didn’t _<br>_have to overthink. I got an outline,_<br>_and I just built from there.” _–Sarah|\n||**Balancing efficiency**<br>**and authenticity**|Students were careful to balance their use of<br>AI, ensuring that while they benefited from<br>its efficiency, the final reflection retained<br>their personal touch and authenticity.|Personalization,<br>selective AI use|<br>-_“I don’t want it to sound like AI_<br>_wrote the whole thing. I want it to_<br>_sound like me, so I only use AI for_<br>_grammar and structure.” _–Ruth<br>-_\"I use AI to fix my grammar, but the_<br>_actual reflection part is still mine.\"_–<br>Sarah|\n||**AI guilt and its**<br>**emotional dimensions**|Students who relied heavily on AI expressed<br>feelings of guilt and concern over becoming<br>too dependent on it, fearing it would<br>diminish their writing skills and self-<br>efficacy.|Dependency, loss of<br>self-efficacy<br>l|-_“I feel like AI has spoiled me because_<br>_now I don’t know if I can write_<br>_without it.” _–Heidi<br>-_“AI makes it easier, but sometimes I_<br>_wonder if I’m relying on it too much_<br>_and not learning how to write on my_<br>_own.” _–Orla<br>_l_|\n|**Metacognitive**<br>**engagement**|**Lack of depth and**<br>**quality of reflection**|Students found that while AI could help with<br>technical fixes, the content it generated<br>often lacked depth and did not align with the<br>introspective nature of reflective writing.|Shallow reflection,<br>lack of insight|-_“AI can write a reflection, but it’s _<br>_not really deep. I have to change a lot_<br>_to make it more personal and_<br>_insightful.” _–Kevin<br>-_“The AI-generated content is just too_<br>_shallow. It’s okay for grammar, but_<br>_not for deep reflection.” _–Ruth|\n||**Uniformity and lack of**<br>**originality**|Some students expressed concerns that if<br>everyone relied too much on AI, their<br>reflections would all start to sound similar,<br>reducing the individuality of each student’s<br>writing.|Similarity,<br>originality concerns|_l_<br>-_“If everyone is using AI, the_<br>_reflections might all start sounding the_<br>_same. I want my reflection to stand_<br>_out as my own.” _–Ruth<br>-_“I worry that too many students are_<br>_using AI, and it’s making everyone’s _<br>_writing sound too similar.” _–Sarah|\n\n\n\n5 \n\n_Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399_ \n\n_C.K.Y. Chan and K.K.W. Lee                                                                                                                                                                                       Computers & Education 238_ \n\nwas treated as important as those that were repeated in the data. Initial codes were assigned to segments of data describing students’ experiences with the use of GenAI in their reflective writing. Codes that were related were then organised into themes before they were examined against the coded data segments to determine whether the themes were suitable to explain the issues and topics raised in the segments. \n\nAfter the analysis of each dataset was completed, the codes and themes were compared across datasets and merged to highlight prominent issues concerning students’ use of AI in reflective writing that were observed across the datasets. Following this, the authors met to compare and discuss their analyses. Differences were resolved through discussion and negotiation. The process of independent coding followed by intercoder comparison of coding was undertaken to mitigate biases arising from individual researchers’ interpretations. The codes and themes were revised if necessary, and finally a description was constructed for each theme to define what it covers. The five themes identified, which are subsumed under two aspects (i.e., cognitive and affective engagement, metacognitive engagement), describe the participants’ experiences with GenAI in reflective writing including their perceptions of its benefits and challenges. The themes, their descriptions, as well as the codes and sample quotes that describe each theme are presented in Table 1. \n\n### _3.5. Ethical considerations_ \n\nEthical approval was obtained prior to data collection, and informed consent was received from both students and their parents. The students were informed that their reflections, GenAI logs, and interview transcripts would be used for research purposes, and confidentiality was maintained throughout the study. Pseudonyms were used to protect the identity of all participants, and all data was securely stored and anonymised during analysis. \n\n### **4. Findings** \n\n### _4.1. Case studies: how did each student use GenAI for their reflective writing?_ \n\nUnderstanding how each student utilised GenAI (used interchangeably with AI in the below) in their reflection task is crucial for identifying the ways in which it supported and hindered the reflective writing process. Based on the data collection, Orla and Heidi heavily relied on GenAI for their reflective writing, with Orla using GenAI to generate full drafts and Heidi using it extensively for outlining, brainstorming and paraphrasing. Sarah and Ruth used GenAI more selectively, mainly for outlining, grammar correction, and paraphrasing, whereas Kevin used it minimally for only grammar checking and format suggestions. Below is a summary of how each student used GenAI for their reflective writing. \n\n### _4.1.1. Student Orla_ \n\nOrla used AI extensively, directly inputting guiding questions from the reflection task as prompts, letting AI generate initial drafted sections, and later editing and paraphrasing the content to make it more personal. She also used AI to improve the structure and grammar of any writing or edits she made on her own, adjusting its suggested adjectives or verbs to better suit her preferences. \n\n### _4.1.1.1. Major highlights._ \n\n1. **Heavy Reliance on AI** : Orla relied on AI for generating a substantial part of her reflection. However, she did not simply submit what the AI produced – instead, she went through it “ _paragraph by paragraph_ ” to paraphrase and refine the AI’s output. \n\n2. **Balancing Personalization and AI Use** : Despite using AI for most of her content, Orla still believed in adding her own voice to the writing. She did not want the reflection to “ _sound like it is all AI_ ” and made efforts to tweak it, “ _[adding] in [her] own experiences to make sure the reflection has a personal touch_ ” and “ _to make it [her] own._ ” \n\n3. **Concerns About Over-reliance** : Orla admitted feeling guilty about relying too much on AI, acknowledging that while it made the writing process easier, it may also be hindering her own development as a writer. She stated, “ _AI definitely helps me structure and improve my writing, but sometimes I feel like I rely on it too much. It made me a bit lazy, to be honest._ ” \n\n### _4.1.2. Student Heidi_ \n\nHeidi used AI to help generate an outline for her reflective writing and adjust the tone of her writing, especially in sections where she struggled to articulate her thoughts clearly. In particular, AI also helped Heidi overcome her writer’s block, aiding her writing process to be faster and smoother. \n\n### _4.1.2.1. Major highlights._ \n\n1. **AI as a Writing Assistant** : Heidi found AI extremely useful for helping her break through her writer’s block and for generating ideas regarding how she could structure her reflection. She appreciated the speed and ease that AI provided, especially when she was feeling stuck with what and how to write her reflection. \n\n6 \n\n_Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399_ \n\n_C.K.Y. Chan and K.K.W. Lee                                                                                                                                                                                       Computers & Education 238_ \n\n2. **Concerns About AI Dependency** : Despite using AI extensively, Heidi was worried that it was “ _spoil[ing]_ ” her and making it hard for her to write without it. She expressed concerns about losing her ability to write unaided, saying that “ _now, I feel like I can’t write without it. It is something I’m worried about, especially for exams when I won’t have AI._ ” \n\n3. **Balancing Professionalism and Personal Voice** : Heidi wanted her reflections to sound polished, formal, and professional, but she also feared that using AI too much might strip her writing of its “ _personal element_ ”, finding this to be “ _a tough balance._ ” \n\n### _4.1.3. Student Sarah_ \n\nIn contrast to Orla and Heidi, Sarah primarily used GenAI to help her generate an outline for her reflection. She did not want AI to fully generate the content, feeling that reflective writing should be personal. Instead, she used AI to help her organise her thoughts, as well as for grammar checking and reformatting sentences that she felt were too complicated or hard to follow. \n\n### _4.1.3.1. Major highlights._ \n\n1. **AI as a Convenience Tool** : Sarah appreciated how AI could quickly provide an outline to help her get started with the task, especially as she was initially unsure of how to structure her reflection. However, she was cautious not to let AI do too much, explaining that she would only use it for certain tasks, “ _like generating an outline or fixing my grammar, but I don’t let it write the whole thing. I want it to be personal._ ” \n\n2. **Authenticity Concerns** : Sarah was very aware of the importance of ensuring her reflective writing was still personal. She did not trust AI to generate unique and authentic content, worrying that this would make her piece generic and stating that, “ _I feel like if someone else puts the same prompt in, they might get something really similar. I want my reflection to be my own._ ” \n\n3. **Convenience and Avoiding Fear of Judgment** : Sarah also utilised AI to answer questions she felt would inconvenience her teachers, such as if they were unavailable at the time. She also felt that AI could explain things well to her, helping her avoid feeling “ _embarrassed_ ” if she asked her teacher a “ _dumb_ ” question. \n\n### _4.1.4. Student Ruth_ \n\nRuth primarily used AI to paraphrase her writing and improve her grammar and vocabulary. She preferred to write the main content herself, but used AI to polish and refine it. \n\n### _4.1.4.1. Major highlights._ \n\n1. **Preference for Personal Writing** : Ruth valued the personal aspect of reflective writing and was careful not to let AI take over. She used AI only for technical improvements, but “ _<u>[did not] let it write the content</u>_ ”. She wanted to ensure her reflections remained personal and unique, stating that she “ _like[d] writing my reflections myself because it is about my thoughts._ ” \n\n2. **Concerns About Uniformity** : Ruth worried that if too many students used AI for reflective writing, there would be a loss of individuality and uniqueness in the pieces. She wanted her writing to stand out as her own, stating that “ _If everyone uses AI for their reflections, they’ll all sound the same. I want mine to reflect my own experiences._ ” \n\n3. **Selective AI Use for Authenticity** : Ruth was careful to use AI selectively, ensuring that while AI improved the technical aspects of her writing, the reflection itself remained authentic. She was “ _always making sure that it is still [her] own reflection. It has to feel personal._ ” \n\n### _4.1.5. Student Kevin_ \n\nIn contrast to his peers, Kevin used AI sparingly, mainly for checking grammar and asking it for formatting suggestions. Though he did attempt to generate content with AI, he felt that the output was too superficial and did not reflect the depth he wanted in his reflections. \n\n### _4.1.5.1. Highlights._ \n\n1. **Scepticism About AI’s Depth** : Kevin was critical of AI’s ability to generate meaningful content for the reflective writing task. He felt that the content AI produced was often “ _just shallow_ ” and not aligned with the personal nature of reflective writing. He commented that “ _it is like [AI] doesn’t understand me, and the writing feels like it is missing depth._ ” \n\n2. **Focus on Authentic Reflection** : Kevin preferred to write his reflections himself because he believed that it should come from personal insight and experience, which AI “ _cannot capture_ ”. He was concerned that using AI would take away the authenticity of his reflections, admonishing its output for being “ _just made-up fiction, presented confidently as facts … I’d rather write it myself._ ” \n\n7 \n\n_Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399_ \n\n_C.K.Y. Chan and K.K.W. Lee                                                                                                                                                                                       Computers & Education 238_ \n\n3. **Lack of Trust in AI** : Kevin’s overall limited use of AI stemmed from his belief that AI could not be trusted to generate accurate or meaningful content, especially for reflections. He used it for technical and grammatical fixes, but “ _that is it_ ”. \n\n### _4.2. Major key themes_ \n\nWhile students varied in how much they used and relied on AI, there were several common themes that emerged across all of their experiences, including their notable concerns regarding its impact on the reflective writing process. \n\nFindings from the thematic analysis reveal a detailed understanding of how secondary school students use and approach AI in their reflective writing, highlighting both benefits and challenges for maintaining authenticity and producing deep reflection. The varied ways in which students engage with AI reveal important insights into their approach to reflective writing, their motivations for using AI, and the tensions they experience when balancing the technical assistance AI provides with their personal voice. \n\n### _4.2.1. AI as a tool for efficiency_ \n\nMost of the students in this case study used AI to facilitate faster task completion and enhance the efficiency of the reflective writing process, particularly when they struggled to get started or organise their thoughts. They already recognised that AI could significantly enhance task efficiency, deeming it “ _lenient_ ” that they were permitted to utilise it; for this task, AI helped them overcome writer’s block and structure their reflections, in turn enhancing their motivation to continue engaging with the task. \n\nHeidi, for example, described how AI helped her when she didn’t know where to begin: “ _When I get writer’s block, I ask AI for an outline or for help with paraphrasing. It really helps keep the momentum going._ ” This demonstrates how AI can support students in the initial stages of writing, providing structure and breaking down the overwhelming task of reflection into manageable steps. \n\nSimilarly, Sarah appreciated the speed and simplicity AI provided, particularly she was uncertain the structure of a reflective writing should be: “ _It made it faster because I didn’t have to overthink. I got an outline, and I just built from there._ ” The efficiency that AI provides, as well as the direction and starting point it sets for otherwise challenging or unfamiliar tasks, can be a motivational factor for students to use AI for their reflective writing. \n\n### _4.2.2. Balancing AI use with authenticity_ \n\nDespite the technical support AI offers, students were highly conscious of maintaining authenticity in their reflective writing. Many expressed concerns about using or relying on AI too heavily, fearing that it might compromise the personal and introspective nature of reflection. \n\nSarah, for instance, noted her hesitancy to rely on AI for more than technical fixes: “ _I don’t want it to sound like AI wrote the whole thing. I want it to sound like me, so I only use AI for grammar and structure._ ” Similarly, Ruth was careful to use AI selectively, ensuring that while AI helped with grammar and structure, the content and personal voice remained her own. As she explained, “ _I like writing my reflections myself because it is about my thoughts. AI is helpful … but I don’t let it write the content._ ” \n\nRuth’s concern about maintaining control over the personal elements of her writing reflects an understanding of the purpose of reflective writing – that it should be deeply personal and not merely a refined, yet generic piece of work. These concerns about authenticity emerged as common theme across the experiences of all five students of this case study; while AI helped them improve the clarity and structure of their writing, students worried or disliked that it would make their reflections feel impersonal. \n\nMoreover, this tension – between using AI to refine and polish writing and ensuring that the personal voice, authenticity, and integrity of reflections remain intact – presents challenges in and of itself. Orla articulated this struggle well: “ _I want it to sound better, but I don’t want to lose my personal voice. That is really important in reflection._ ” Heidi shared similar concerns, noting, “ _AI makes the writing sound more professional, but I don’t want it to sound too polished. It still has to feel like my reflection._ ” \n\nThus, though students recognise that reflective writing requires both technical proficiency and a deep connection to personal experiences, there is a fine line to navigate. This is a delicate balancing act that students face – as they strive for their writing to meet academic standards, they also grapple with the fear that excessive use and reliance on AI could diminish or strip away the personal insights crucial to reflective writing. \n\n### _4.2.3. AI guilt and its dependency_ \n\nFor students who relied more heavily on AI, particularly Orla and Heidi, there was a growing sense of dependency and guilt. Both students recognised the convenience and efficiency AI provided but expressed concerns about losing their ability to write unaided. \n\nAs shown in 4.1.1, Orla, who used AI to generate full drafts of her reflections before personalising them, admitted: “ _… sometimes I feel like I rely on it too much. It made me a bit lazy, to be honest._ ” This guilt associated with using AI may stem from the recognition that using AI to make the writing process easier has simultaneously diminished her confidence in her own skills. Heidi echoed this concern, highlighting the internal conflict students face when they become too dependent on AI – while they appreciate its benefits, they worry about the long-term impact on their self-efficacy and ability to engage in reflective writing independently. \n\n### _4.2.4. Lack of depth in AI-generated content_ \n\nA crucial insight from students’ reflective writings and chat logs is while AI was effective for improving the technical aspects of writing (grammar, structure, etc.), it often failed to provide the depth and introspection necessary for meaningful reflection. Several students, including Kevin, found that AI-generated content tended to be superficial, requiring significant editing to align with the \n\n8 \n\n_Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399_ \n\n_C.K.Y. Chan and K.K.W. Lee                                                                                                                                                                                       Computers & Education 238_ \n\npersonal nature, experiences, and insights involved in reflective writing. \n\nKevin was particularly critical of AI’s ability to generate meaningful reflections, stating, “ _AI can write a reflection, but it is not really deep. I have to change a lot to make it more personal and insightful._ ” This highlights a key limitation of AI – while it can help organise thoughts and improve surface-level quality, it cannot replace the personal reflection and critical thinking required for deeper engagement. \n\nRuth echoed these concerns, explaining that she found “ _AI-generated content … just too shallow. It is okay for grammar, but not for deep reflection._ ” This suggests that while students valued AI’s technical support, they do in fact recognise its limitations for producing the deeper, meaningful insights. These issues raised by the participants on AI-generated content appear to demonstrate the limited role of GenAI in promoting students’ metacognition in reflective writing. \n\n### _4.2.5. Concerns About Uniformity and lack of uniqueness_ \n\nAnother significant concern for students was that if too many relied on AI, their reflections would eventually become uniform and homogenised, losing individuality and uniqueness. This fear was particularly strong for students like Ruth and Sarah, who both expressed concerns that the widespread use of AI would “ _make everyone’s writing sound too similar_ ”, diminishing the diversity of reflective writing and blurring the distinction between individual experiences and reflections. \n\n### **5. Discussion** \n\nThe cross-case analysis reveals students’ experiences with the use of GenAI in terms of their interaction with the technology in reflective writing and how the interaction affected their reflective writing. Specifically, the findings demonstrate that their interaction with GenAI is an embodied experience that impacts their cognitive, affective, and metacognitive engagement (see Table 1). The notion of cognition as embodied activities concerns learning as being grounded in one’s interactions with the physical world and environment (Shapiro & Stolz, 2019). In a technology-enhanced context, students’ interaction with AI tools in the learning process is a form of embodied experience (Chang et al., 2025), which in this study involved students’ use of GenAI in reflective writing. In addition, there were also key tensions between the support that AI offers and its potential to undermine critical reflection. These dynamics will be explored in the following sections. \n\n### _5.1. Cognitive and affective engagement_ \n\n### _5.1.1. AI as a tool for enhancing efficiency and reducing cognitive load_ \n\nOne of the most immediate benefits of AI that emerged from the study was its ability to enhance efficiency and reduce cognitive load in the writing process. Many students, including Sarah and Heidi, appreciated how AI helped them overcome writer’s block, provided structure, and allowed them to focus more on the reflection content itself. This mirrors findings in the literature that highlight the cognitive demands of reflective writing (Moon, 2001); as Moon (2001) notes, reflection requires substantial mental effort, and students often struggle with the organisational and structural aspects of reflective tasks. By handling these technical challenges, AI allows students to direct their cognitive resources toward engaging with their experiences. \n\nMoreover, Heidi in particular noted that AI helped her overcome her writer’s block by providing outlines and paraphrasing support, which allowed her to “ _keep the momentum going._ ” This aligns with Chan and Lee’s (2021) discussion on the challenges of achieving reflection literacy, demonstrating how AI tools can alleviate some of the cognitive barriers to reflection such as by organising thoughts and managing time, which are often cited as obstacles to meaningful reflection (Mahlanze & Sibiya, 2017). GenAI tools such as ChatGPT reduce a complex task to easy steps, provide concise information to help students focus on the most critical aspects of the task, and allow students to check their answers, thus easing intrinsic, extraneous, and germane cognitive load (Patac & Patac, 2025). \n\nHowever, while AI’s ability to improve efficiency and reduce cognitive load is clear, there are concerns about how this may affect the depth of reflection. Students such as Kevin and Ruth noted that while AI helped with surface-level improvements, it often produced content that lacked depth, requiring them to make significant adjustments as found across their reflective writings, chat log history, and interviews. Kevin also remarked, _“the core element of a reflection is based on a person’s own experiences and attitude and feelings. So basically, even if ChatGPT can write an entire reflection for you, it is not going to be really accurate.”_ This echoes Darvishi and colleagues’ (2024) study, where it was found that students who rely too heavily on AI tools may complete tasks more efficiently, but wind up engaging less deeply with the content. \n\n### _5.1.2. Balancing efficiency and authenticity_ \n\nA major theme that emerged from this case study was the balancing act students faced as they navigated between using AI to produce polished, professional writing and ensuring their reflections remained authentic and personal. Most students, including Ruth and Sarah, expressed concerns about using AI too extensively, fearing that it would compromise the personal voice that is central to reflective writing. \n\nThis balancing act reflects Tight’s (2024) critique of reflective practices in educational settings, where students may be driven to produce work that meets academic standards but risks losing the authenticity and introspection necessary for deep reflection. It also demonstrates the paradoxical relationship between AI and reflective writing highlighted in the literature (Chan & Lee, 2021); while AI can alleviate the cognitive load associated with writing, helping students organise their thoughts and reduce stress, it also risks reducing reflection to a mechanical task. \n\nMoreover, while the literature on reflective writing consistently emphasises the importance of authenticity in reflection, where \n\n9 \n\n_Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399_ \n\n_C.K.Y. Chan and K.K.W. Lee_ \n\nstudents critically engage with their personal experiences to derive meaningful insights (Fullana et al., 2016; Moon, 2001), AI, with its capacity to refine and enhance language, can sometimes push students toward performative reflection (Ryan, 2012) where the focus shifts to meeting external expectations rather than internal reflection. Power (2012) and Ryan (2013) both noted that students often struggle to balance formal requirements with authentic self-expression in reflective writing, a challenge compounded by AI’s ability to refine surface-level features of writing while neglecting deeper engagement. \n\n### _5.1.3. AI guilt and its emotional dimensions_ \n\nThe concept of AI guilt (Chan, 2024) plays a central role in understanding how students engage with AI in reflective writing. These dimensions, discussed below, highlight the emotional and cognitive tensions that shape students’ interactions with AI tools. \n\n1. **Perceived Laziness and Authenticity** : Some students, such as Orla and Heidi, expressed concerns about relying too heavily on AI as it made them feel lazy or less capable of writing independently – a classical feeling of imposter syndrome (Chan, 2024). Orla’s acknowledgement that, _“AI … made [her] a bit lazy”_ reflects concerns raised by Chan (2025a,b) and Fyfe (2022) about over-reliance on AI leading to a diminished capacity for critical thinking and reflective engagement. While AI can ease the burden of writing, it may also result in surface-level engagement when students become more focused on task completion rather than meaningful reflection (Brookfield, 2009). \n\n2. **Fear of Judgment** : Many students used AI as a tool to avoid judgment from teachers and peers, refining their writing to ensure it met academic standards. Sarah shared, _“I don’t want my teacher to think my writing is bad, so I use AI to make it sound clearer.”_ This is similar to findings from Fernandez-Pe´ na and colleagues (2016)˜ , who observed that students often feel vulnerable when their reflective writing is subject to assessment, leading to more formulaic or mechanical engagement with the reflective process. \n\n3. **Identity and Self-Efficacy Concerns** : One of the factors that influences AI guilt is cognitive dissonance (Festinger, 1957). Several students expressed discomfort when their actions conflicted with their beliefs and personal effort, especially when AI appeared to be a more capable writer. As Heidi admitted, “ _ChatGPT has spoiled me because now I don’t know if I can write without it,_ ” reflecting the broader concerns in the literature about students’ dependency on AI tools and the potential for these tools to undermine their confidence in their writing abilities (Seo et al., 2021). Roche and Coote (2008) similarly warned that over-reliance on technology can detract from students’ development of critical reflection and independent writing skills. \n\nThese findings show that students’ guilt and concerns are their emotional responses to engaging with the use of GenAI in reflective writing. As Barrett and Lindquist (2008) argue, people’s emotions are deeply intertwined with how the world is experienced and physical events. In this study, the physical experiences of having much of the task completed by GenAI caused the participants to think that they were “lazy” and perceiving that work produced by GenAI was better than theirs made them feel uncomfortable. These emotions are also associated and interlinked with other emotions such as guilt, vulnerability, and apprehension over the loss of personal voice explained in Section 5.1.2. \n\n### _5.2. Metacognitive engagement_ \n\n### _5.2.1. Lack of depth and quality of reflection_ \n\nA key issue that emerged from the findings was that while AI improved the technical quality of reflective writing, it often led to superficial reflections, which in turns stagnate metacognitive skills. Reflection is closely related to metacognition with reflective writing often believed to be an effective tool to promote students’ metacognitive development (Cohn & Stewart, 2016; Zarestky et al., 2022). Gibson et al. (2016) define weak reflection as a lack of metacognitive activities consisting of self-regulation, knowledge of metacognitive strategies, and critical evaluation of experiences. Kevin’s observation that AI-generated content lacked depth reflects a broader challenge in using AI for reflective tasks where AI tools, while effective in improving grammar and structure, are limited in their ability to produce critical insights and personal engagement, which are indicators of metacognitive abilities and putative metacognitive regulation. In other words, the use of GenAI may diminish the potential of reflective writing to encourage students’ metacognition, that is, the ability to learn about learning. \n\nStudies on reflective practices emphasise the importance of critical thinking and personal insight, particularly in higher-order reflection (Brookfield, 2009; Moon, 2001). However, AI’s tendency to focus on surface-level improvements often results in reflections that, like the result of many of the issues presented above, meet technical standards but lack the depth of critical analysis. This finding echoes Mair’s (2012) concern that reflective practice in educational settings tends to emphasise what has been learned instead of how learning occurs and prioritise writing styles over metacognition. \n\n### _5.2.2. Uniformity and lack of originality_ \n\nAnother concern that emerged from the findings was the risk of uniformity and lack of originality in reflections when too many students rely on AI tools. This fear reflects broader concerns in the literature about homogenisation of thoughts and outputs when AI is used widely (Chan & Colloton, 2024). While AI can assist with technical aspects, its use may lead to reflections that lack the uniqueness and depth (as discussed in Section 5.2.1) that are essential to meaningful reflection. The absence of sufficient support such as metacognitive prompts in GenAI environments may undermine students’ engagement in self-regulated learning (Xu et al., 2025), contributing to a phenomenon Fan et al. (2025) term as “metacognitive laziness” as students delegate complex cognitive tasks to the technology. Secondary school students are particularly susceptible to these risks due to the educational and developmental challenges associated with reflective writing and AI use. Without teacher guidance and proper training, the integration of GenAI in reflective \n\n10 \n\n_Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399_ \n\n_C.K.Y. Chan and K.K.W. Lee                                                                                                                                                                                       Computers & Education 238_ \n\nwriting may deter rather than promote their higher order thinking. \n\nIt is interesting that these secondary school students appear to understand how to engage in reflective writing, despite literature suggesting that **effective reflection** often requires a higher level of metacognitive knowledge – an advanced ability to derive meaning from experiences through deep thinking. This process, as described by Jaleel and Premachandran (2016) and Moon (2001), involves a contemplative approach that many secondary students may not fully develop due to the complexity of reflection. Yet the students of this study, such as Sarah and Ruth, were highly conscious of the need to keep their reflective writing critical, personal, and authentic. This shows an understanding that reflective writing is not merely about summarising experiences, but involves the infusion of one’s own thoughts and insights. \n\n### **6. Conclusion** \n\nThe findings of this paper reveal the complex interplay between the benefits and challenges of using AI in reflective writing. While AI supports students by improving efficiency, reducing cognitive load, and helping them overcome writer’s block, it also introduces significant challenges related to authenticity, depth of reflection, and metacognitive activities, which are the fundamental elements of reflective writing. In authentic reflection, the writer critically engages with their experiences, reflecting deeply on their learning process, acknowledging challenges, and exploring their own emotional and cognitive responses. The focus is on personal insight and growth, rather than simply meeting the expectations of the reader or teacher. \n\nWith the integration of AI into the process of reflective writing however, the tension between polished writing and authenticity highlights the delicate balance students must maintain to ensure that their reflections remain personal and meaningful, even as they use AI to meet academic standards. The concept of AI guilt, with its dimensions of laziness, fear of judgment, and identity concerns, also emerges as a critical factor shaping students’ engagement with AI, reflecting the emotional and cognitive complexities involved in using AI for reflective tasks. \n\n### _6.1. Limitations_ \n\nOne key limitation of this study is its small sample size, focusing on only five secondary school students. While the case study approach allowed for in-depth exploration of their reflective writing processes and experiences, the findings may not be fully generalisable to a broader population of students. Future research could explore a larger and more diverse sample of students, potentially across different educational settings, to further investigate the impact of GenAI on reflective writing. Another limitation is that the study was conducted during a three-week summer internship programme, limiting its ability to assess the long-term effects of AI use on reflective writing. Students may change and adapt their use of AI over time, and this study does not fully capture how their ongoing interactions with AI may impact reflective practices in the future. Finally, as with most qualitative interpretative research, this study was susceptible to researcher bias despite intercoder comparison of analyses. The process of coding and interpretation of data might have been influenced by the researchers’ academic backgrounds and experiences. \n\n### _6.2. Implications_ \n\nThe findings from this study highlight important implications for how educators and institutions should approach the integration of GenAI tools in reflective writing tasks. These insights provide a foundation for future educational practices and research, emphasising the need for a balanced approach to AI use in academic writing. \n\n**Holistic Approach to AI Policy** : The findings of this study suggest that student learning in an AI-enhanced environment is an embodied experience involving the cognitive, affective, and metacognitive dimensions as they interact with AI technologies in a physical world. Hence, an effective educational policy that aims to promote genuine learning and AI literacy should emphasise a holistic educational experience integrating the physical, cognitive, affective, and metacognitive aspects. In the same vein, educators’ role needs to evolve from that of teachers and instructors to include multiple roles which are broader and perhaps informal at times, for example, facilitators, coaches, mentors, and learning partners. \n\n**Guiding Ethical AI Use in Reflection** : One of the primary implications is the need for structured guidance on how to ethically and effectively integrate AI into reflective writing tasks. Students reported experiencing AI guilt due to concerns about authenticity, perceived laziness, and over-reliance on AI; this suggests that educators must not only encourage AI use for technical improvement but also emphasise that reflective writing should remain a deeply personal process. Teachers can offer frameworks that help students identify when and how to use AI for tasks like grammar correction or brainstorming without compromising the personal introspection necessary for genuine reflection. \n\n**Balance between AI Assistance and Independent Reflection** : Another key implication is the importance of helping students strike a balance between using AI as a tool to support their writing while still engaging in authentic reflection. Educators should create assignments or prompts that encourage deeper reflection beyond surface-level improvements, ensuring students focus on selfawareness and critical thinking rather than simply completing a task. Rubrics can also be adjusted to reward depth, originality, and personal insight in reflective writing, incentivising students to move beyond performative reflection. \n\n**Fostering Digital Literacy and Critical Engagement with AI** : The findings of this paper further suggest a need to develop students’ AI literacy alongside their reflection literacy. Students must learn to critically engage with AI tools, understanding both their benefits and limitations. For instance, while AI can enhance structure and clarity, it often lacks the ability to generate the meaningful, critical insights needed to achieve deep reflection. Educators should ensure that students understand what AI is currently capable of \n\n11 \n\n_Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399_ \n\n_C.K.Y. Chan and K.K.W. Lee                                                                                                                                                                                       Computers & Education 238_ \n\nand its ethical implications, promoting a more critical, reflective use of AI that enables students to make informed decisions about when and how to use these tools. At the secondary school level, it is also important for teachers to provide guidance and metacognitive support to help students optimise the use of AI for task completion without compromising its potential for fostering higher order thinking. \n\n### _6.3. Future research directions_ \n\nA key area for future research is to examine the long-term impacts of AI on students’ reflection literacy and reflective skills. This study focused on short-term effects, employing a case study approach to explore how five students used AI for a specific task within a summer internship. Hence, the findings of this study are preliminary insights into how GenAI impacts student engagement in reflective writing. Future studies could involve a larger sample of students to explore how their engagement with reflective writing evolves when they use AI over longer periods of time. Research can also seek to assess whether this prolonged use of AI in reflective writing will lead to diminished critical thinking and metacognitive skills, increased dependency on AI, or shifts in how students approach personal introspection. A quantitative approach using large-scale surveys can be adopted to examine the impact of GenAI on these aspects. \n\nFuture research should also examine how the use of AI in reflective writing varies across different educational contexts. This study focused on secondary school students; however, it would be valuable to compare their experiences with those of university students or adult learners. Understanding how different groups balance the use of AI with maintaining authenticity could provide insights into how educational institutions can tailor AI use to different levels of student maturity, learning objectives, or discipline-specific requirements. Furthermore, to validate and build on the case study findings, large-scale research involving a bigger student population from different geological locations can provide insights into how students from different backgrounds interact with AI technologies and how the interactions impact various aspects of their engagement in learning. \n\n### **CRediT authorship contribution statement** \n\n**Cecilia Ka Yuk Chan:** Writing – original draft, Validation, Software, Project administration, Investigation, Formal analysis, Conceptualization, Writing – review & editing, Visualization, Supervision, Resources, Methodology, Funding acquisition, Data curation. **Katherine K.W. Lee:** Data curation, Writing – review & editing. \n\n### **Declaration of generative AI and AI-assisted technologies in the writing process** \n\nDuring the preparation of this work the author(s) used ChatGPT 4.0 in order to check the grammar of the writing and improve the writing structure of this paper. After using this tool, the authors reviewed and edited the content as needed and take full responsibility for the content of the published article. \n\n### **Funding** \n\nThis research did not receive any specific grant from funding agencies in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. \n\n### **Declaration of competing interest** \n\nThe authors declare that they have no conflict of interest. This research was funded by RGC GRF 17616525 and also UGC FITE. \n\n### **Acknowledgements** \n\nThe authors wish to thank the students of this study for their participation. \n\n### **Data availability** \n\nData will be made available on request. \n\n### **References** \n\n- Ahmed, A. M. (2020). From reluctance to addiction: The impact of reflective journals on Qatari undergraduate students’ learning. _Reflective Practice, 21_ (2), 251–270. https://doi.org/10.1080/14623943.2020.1735328 \n\nAl-Rawahi, N. M., & Al-Balushi, S. M. (2015). The effect of reflective science journal writing on students’ self-regulated learning strategies. _International Journal of Environmental & Science Education, 10_ (3), 367–379. https://doi.org/10.12973/ijese.2015.250a Alharbi, W. (2023). AI in the foreign language classroom: A pedagogical overview of automated writing assistance tools. _Education Research International, 2023_ (1), Article 4253331. https://doi.org/10.1155/2023/4253331 \n\n- Ash, S. L., & Clayton, P. H. (2009). Generating, deepening, and documenting learning: The power of critical reflection in applied learning. _Journal of Applied Learning in Higher Education, 1_ , 25–48. https://doi.org/10.57186/jalhe_2009_v1a2p25-48 \n\nBarrett, L. F., & Lindquist, K. A. (2008). The embodiment of emotion. In G. R. Semin, & E. R. Smith (Eds.), _Embodied grounding: Social, cognitive, affective, and neuroscientific approaches_ (pp. 237–262). Cambridge University Press. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511805837.011. \n\n12 \n\n_Computers & Education 238 (2025) 105399_ \n\n_C.K.Y. Chan and K.K.W. Lee                                                                                                                                