TPACK in blended teacher training on technology-enhanced learning. _International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education_ , _14_ , 34. https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-017-0072-z \n\n- Razzouk, R., & Shute, V. (2012). What is design thinking and why is it important? _Review of Educational Researc_ h _, 82_ (3), 330–348. https://doi.org/10.3102/0034654312457429 \n\n- Rodríguez-Chirino, P., Gutiérrez-Braojos, C., Martínez-Gámez, M., & Rodríguez-Domínguez, C. (2025). Technologies for reflective assessment in knowledge building communities: A systematic review. _Information_ , _16_ (9), 762. https://doi.org/10.3390/info16090762 \n\n- Sackett, G. P. (Ed.). (1978). _Observing behavior: Data collection and analysis methods_ (Vol. 2). University Park Press. \n\n- Scardamalia, M. (2002). Collective cognitive responsibility for the advancement of knowledge. _Liberal Education in a Knowledge Society, 97_ , 67–98. \n\n- Oshima, J., Oshima, R., & Matsuzawa, Y. (2012). Knowledge Building Discourse Explorer: a social network analysis application for knowledge building discourse. _Educational technology research and development, 60_ , 903-921. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11423012-9265-2 \n\n- Razzouk, R., & Shute, V. (2012). What is design thinking and why is it important? _Review of Educational Researc_ h _, 82_ (3), 330–348. https://doi.org/10.3102/0034654312457429 \n\n- Rodríguez-Chirino, P., Gutiérrez-Braojos, C., Martínez-Gámez, M., & Rodríguez-Domínguez, C. (2025). Technologies for reflective assessment in knowledge building communities: A systematic review. _Information_ , _16_ (9), 762. https://doi.org/10.3390/info16090762 \n\n- Scardamalia, M., & Bereiter, C. (2014). Knowledge building and knowledge creation: Theory, pedagogy, and technology. In R. K. Sawyer (Ed.), _The Cambridge handbook of the learning sciences_ (2nd ed., pp. 397–417). Cambridge University Press. \n\n- Scardamalia, M., & Bereiter, C. (2022). Knowledge building and Knowledge creation. In R. K. Sawyer (Ed.), _The Cambridge Handbook of the Learning Sciences_ (pp. 385–405). Cambridge University Press. \n\n- Shively, K., & Palilonis, J. (2018). Curriculum development: Preservice teachers’ perceptions of design thinking for understanding digital literacy as a curricular framework. _Journal of Education_ , _198_ (3), 202-214. https://doi.org/10.1177/0022057418811128 \n\n- Siemens, G., & Gasevic, D. (2012). Guest editorial-learning and knowledge analytics. _Journal of Educational Technology & Society, 15_ (3), 1-2. \n\n- Tao, D., & Zhang, J. (2021). Agency to transform: how did a grade 5 community co-configure dynamic knowledge building practices in a yearlong science inquiry? _International_ \n\n51 \n\n_Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , 16, 403-404. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11412-021-09353-7 \n\n- Thuan, N. H., & Antunes, P. (2024). A conceptual model for educating design thinking dispositions. _International Journal of Technology and Design Education_ , _34_ (5), 1879– 1902. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10798-024-09881-x \n\n- Tong, Y., Yang, C., & Chen, G. (2024). A visual learning analytics approach for knowledge building: Impact on students\' epistemic understanding of discourse, productive inquiry and domain knowledge. _British Journal of Educational Technology_ , _55_ (3), 992-1019. https://doi.org/10.1111/bjet.13409 \n\n- Tseng, J. J., Cheng, Y. S., & Yeh, H. N. (2019). How pre-service English teachers enact TPACK in the context of web-conferencing teaching: A design thinking a pproach. _Computers & Education_ , _128_ , 171-182. <u>https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compedu. 2018.09.022</u> \n\n- Van Aalst, J., & Chan, C. K. K. (2007). Student-directed assessment of knowledge building using electronic portfolios. _The Journal of the Learning Sciences, 16_ , 175–220. https://doi.org/10.1080/10508400701193697 \n\n- Verma, G., Campbell, T., Melville, W., & Park, B. Y. (2023). Navigating opportunities and challenges of artificial intelligence: ChatGPT and generative models in science teacher education. _Journal of Science Teacher Education, 34_ (8), 793–798. https://doi.org/10.1080/1046560X.2023.2263251 \n\n- White, B. Y., & Frederiksen, J. R. (1998). Inquiry, modeling, and metacognition: Making science accessible to all students. _Cognition and instruction_ , _16_ (1), 3-118. https://doi.org/10.1207/s1532690xci1601_2 \n\n- Wise, A. F., Knight, S., & Shum, S. B. (2021). Collaborative learning analytics. In Cress, U., Rosé, C., Wise, A.F., Oshima, J. (eds), _International handbook of computer-supported collaborative learning_ (pp. 425-443). Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-03065291-3_23 \n\n- Wise, A. F., & Schwarz, B. B. (2017). Visions of CSCL: Eight provocations for the future of the field. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _12_ (4), 423-467. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11412-017-9267-5 \n\n- Yüksel, A. O. (2025). Design-based STEM activities in teacher education and its effect on pre-service science teachers’ design thinking skills. _Journal of Science Education and Technology_ , 1–15. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10956-025-10215-2 \n\n- Zhang, J., Scardamalia, M., Lamon, M., Messina, R., & Reeve, R. (2007). Socio-cognitive dynamics of knowledge building in the work of 9- and 10-year-olds. _Educational Technology Research and Development, 55_ (2), 117–145. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11423-006-9019-0 \n\n- Verma, G., Campbell, T., Melville, W., & Park, B. Y. (2023). Navigating opportunities and challenges of artificial intelligence: ChatGPT and generative models in science teacher education. _Journal of Science Teacher Education, 34_ (8), 793–798. https://doi.org/10.1080/1046560X.2023.2263251 \n\n- Wise, A. F., & Schwarz, B. B. (2017). Visions of CSCL: Eight provocations for the future of the field. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _12_ (4), 423-467. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11412-017-9267-5 \n\n- Zhang, J., Tao, D., Chen, M. H., Sun, Y., Judson, D., & Naqvi, S. (2018). Co-organizing the collective journey of inquiry with idea thread mapper. _Journal of the Learning Sciences, 27_ (3), 390–430. https://doi.org/10.1080/10508406.2018.1444992 \n\n- Zhang, J., Yuan, G., & Bogouslavsky, M. (2020). Give student ideas a larger stage: Support cross-community interaction for knowledge building. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning, 15(4)_ , 389–410. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11412-020-09332-4 \n\n52 \n\n## Highlights \n\n- Conceptualizes design thinking (DT) as a socially mediated, developmental practice \n\n- Proposes a theory-informed model linking KB, GenAI, and reflective assessment \n\n- Operationalizes design thinking through a principle-based, replicable pedagogy \n\n- Integrates delayed GenAI use with analytics-supported reflective assessment \n\n- Reveals shifts from stage-bound to iterative design practices in PST learning \n\n- Advances theorization of human–AI co-agency in collaborative inquiry contexts \n\n', NULL, '2026-09-12 17:24:27', '2026-09-12 17:24:59', '2026-C&E-Yang-Fostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI.pdf', 'project_1028/raw/2026-C&E-Yang-Fostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI.pdf', 'application/pdf', 4058595, '4134d6da05d2d357cf6aaf2d4bbfaaff27a62062c8310b913f92a3d99dedc96b', '4134d6da05d2d357cf6aaf2d4bbfaaff27a62062c8310b913f92a3d99dedc96b', 'project_1028/parsed/2026-C&E-Yang-Fostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI.md', 'ready', NULL);
INSERT INTO `research_resource` VALUES (34, 3, 1029, 'UPLOAD', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'TEXT_EXTRACTED', '# Journal Pre-proof \n\nFostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI and Analytics-Supported Reflective Assessment in a Collaborative Inquiry Environment \n\nYuqin Yang, Linbaiyu Chen, Daner Sun, Wangda Zhu, Shiyan Pang \n\n\n\nPII: S0360-1315(26)00040-0 DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compedu.2026.105602 Reference: CAE 105602 \n\nTo appear in: _Computers & Education_ \n\nReceived Date: 26 August 2025 Revised Date: 20 February 2026 Accepted Date: 22 February 2026 \n\nPlease cite this article as: Yang Y., Chen L., Sun D., Zhu W. & Pang S., Fostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI and Analytics-Supported Reflective Assessment in a Collaborative Inquiry Environment, _Computers & Education_ , https://doi.org/10.1016/ j.compedu.2026.105602. \n\nThis is a PDF of an article that has undergone enhancements after acceptance, such as the addition of a cover page and metadata, and formatting for readability. This version will undergo additional copyediting, typesetting and review before it is published in its final form. As such, this version is no longer the Accepted Manuscript, but it is not yet the definitive Version of Record; we are providing this early version to give early visibility of the article. Please note that Elsevier’s sharing policy for the Published Journal Article applies to this version, see: https://www.elsevier.com/about/policies-and- <u>standards/sharing#4-published-journal-article. Please also note that, during the production process,</u> errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. \n\n© 2026 Published by Elsevier Ltd. \n\n## **Fostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI and Analytics-Supported Reflective Assessment in a Collaborative Inquiry Environment** \n\nYuqin Yang & Linbaiyu Chen Central China Normal University, China \n\nDaner Sun \n\nThe Education University of Hong Kong \n\nWangda Zhu \n\nThe Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong \n\nShiyan Pang * Central China Normal University, China _Yuqin Yang_ , Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education, Central China Normal University, Wuhan, Hubei, China E-mail: yangyuqin@ccnu.edu.cn _Linbaiyu Chen_ , Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education, Central China Normal University, Wuhan, Hubei, China E-mail: baiyu_cl_apply@163.com _Daner Sun_ , Department of Mathematics and Information Technology, The Education University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong S.A.R. E-mail: dsun@eduhk.hk dsun@eduhk.hk Wangda Zhu, School of Design The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong S.A.R. E-mail: wangda.zhu@polyu.edu.hk \n\n_Linbaiyu Chen_ , Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education, Central China Normal University, Wuhan, Hubei, China \n\n_Daner Sun_ , Department of Mathematics and Information Technology, The Education University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong S.A.R. E-mail: dsun@eduhk.hk dsun@eduhk.hk \n\n_Shiyan Pang_ , Associate Professor* \n\nFaculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education, Central China Normal University, Hubei, China \n\nEmail: <u>pangsy@ccnu.edu.cn</u> \n\n## *** Corresponding author** \n\n_Shiyan Pang_ , Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education, Central China Normal University, NO.152 Luoyu Road, Wuhan, Hubei, P. R. China, 430079. E-mail: <u>pangsy@ccnu.edu.cn</u> \n\n## **Note to the Editor** \n\nThe corresponding author is currently on maternity leave following the birth of a baby and may have limited access to email during this period. To ensure timely communication and avoid any delays in the submission- and review-related process, \n\nplease contact the first author first for all manuscript-related correspondence. \n\n_Primary contact: Yuqin Yang, [yangyuqin@ccnu.edu.cn]._ \n\nThe first author is authorized to handle all submission- and review-related matters for this manuscript on behalf of the author team. \n\n## **Declaration** \n\n**Availability of data and materials:** These data will be made available to other researchers on a case-by-case basis. \n\n**Acknowledgments:** The authors are indebted to the participating students. **Funding:** This study is supported by national-level funding to the first author, and removed for blinded review. \n\n**Authors’ biographies** _Yuqin Yang_ is a full professor of the learning sciences at the Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education in Central China Normal University. Her research interests include pedagogy and assessment of knowledge building, data science education, learning analytics, and collaborative learning. Her publications have appeared in international journals such as Computers & Education, Internet and Higher Education, American Educational Research Journal, International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning, British Journal of Educational Technology, Teaching and Teacher Education, International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education, and Journal of Computer Assisted Learning. She also serves as guest editors and board members for some journals, such as British Journal of Educational Technology, Learning and Instruction. She also services as associate editors for journals such as International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning, Learning: Research and Practices, and board members for several journals such as Learning & Instruction, Learning and Individual Differences. _Linbaiyu Chen_ is a research assistant at the Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education in Central China Normal University. Her research interests include design thinking, GenAI-augmented learning, dialogic pedagogy, knowledge-building pedagogy and learning analytics. _Daner Sun_ is associate professor at the Department of Mathematics and Information Technology, The Education University of Hong Kong. Her research interests are ICT-supported science learning, seamless learning and teacher education. Her publications have appeared in international journals such as Computers & Education, British Journal of Educational Technology, International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning, Journal of Research in Science Teaching, and Education Technology and Society. She also services as associate editors for several journals such as Education and Information Technologies, and Educational Technology and Society \n\nWangda Zhu is an Assistant Professor of Interaction Design at The Hong Kong Polytechnic University. His research focuses on creative learning system design and development, with particular interests in design education, learning analytics, and AIpowered educational technologies. His publications have appeared in leading journals \n\nsuch as Computers & Education, British Journal of Educational Technology, and Internet and Higher Education, as well as in major conferences including LAK, ASEE, and AIED, where his work has received multiple best paper nominations. \n\n_Shiyan Pang_ is an associate professor at the Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education at Central China Normal University. Her research interests include, application of artificial intelligence in education, data mining, classroom data analysis. Her publications have appeared in international journals such as ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing, Journal of Computing in Higher Education and International Journal of Remote Sensing. \n\n## **Fostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI and Analytics-Supported Reflective Assessment in a Collaborative Inquiry Environment** \n\nFostering preservice teachers’ (PSTs) design thinking (DT) is essential for addressing complex instructional challenges, yet remains difficult to achieve in teacher education. Although generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) and analytics-supported reflective assessment (ASRA) show promise for supporting DT, how they can be pedagogically integrated within collaborative inquiry remains relatively underexplored. The study aimed to explore the effects of the combined use of GenAI and ASRA within a collaborative inquiry Knowledge Building (KB) environment on PSTs’ DT, and how their DT competencies evolves with this integrative design. Participants were 110 PSTs enrolled in a semester-long instructional design course. We proposed a conceptual framework integrating collaborative inquiry, strategically delayed GenAI use, and ASRA, and operationalized it through a pedagogical model that positions GenAI as a cognitive and reflective design partner rather than a solution provider. Multiple data sources were analyzed, including a DT competency scale, successive instructional design artifacts, and 1,305 online discourse notes. Results showed significant gains in problem discovery and definition, along with a developmental shift from stage-bound engagement toward more integrated and iterative practices, particularly in prototyping and testing. GenAI was most effective when introduced after human-only design efforts, while reflective analytics supported monitoring of collective progress and sustained iteration. This study contributes to learning sciences by theorizing how GenAI and ASRA can be integrated within a KB environment to support DT, and offers an empirically grounded approach for preparing PSTs for AI-rich teaching contexts. _Keywords:_ Teaching/learning strategies; pedagogical issues; improving classroom teaching; cooperative/collaborative learning; personalized learning \n\n1 \n\n## **1. Introduction** \n\nDesign thinking (DT) is increasingly viewed as a key competence of higher-order thinking, enabling learners to tackle complex, real-world problems through creative and iterative approaches (Bereiter & Scardamalia 2022; Calavia et al., 2023; Hong et al., 2025; Lee & Kim, 2025). DT is commonly defined as an iterative, human-centered process that involves addressing ill-structured problems through cycles of problem framing, ideation, prototyping, testing, and reflection (Buchanan, 1992; Razzouk & Shute, 2012). Recent studies in teacher education show that DT-oriented activities help PSTs frame authentic pedagogical problems, make principled instructional decisions, and design more adaptive learning environments (Calavia et al., 2023; Hong et al., 2025; Lee & Kim, 2025). Beyond technical skill development, DT fosters dispositions such as resilience, tolerance and metacognitive awareness for ambiguity—qualities crucial for navigating complex, ill-structured problems (Koh et al. 2015a; Thuan & Antunes, 2024). It also promotes creative, innovative, and critical thinking— competencies central to 21st-century education (Koh et al., 2015b; Lin et al., 2020). Without well-developed DT, PSTs often struggle to design meaningful, learner-centered lessons aligned with contemporary educational demands (Avsec & Jagiełło-Kowalczyk, 2021; Baran & AlZoubi, 2024; Yüksel, 2025). Consequently, helping PSTs develop DT competencies is crucially important. However, developing PSTs’ DT remains challenging. Many PSTs rely heavily on existing templates or online resources, lacking the ability to independently design instruction (Ananda et al., 2023; Baran & AlZoubi, 2024). Even in design-oriented coursework, PSTs often struggle to sustain iterative cycles of framing, ideation, prototyping, and testing, particularly when design work requires coordination, justification, and revision over time (Calavia et al., 2023; Lee & Kim, 2025). Prior research indicates that teacher education often emphasizes final lesson products over process-oriented idea improvement and reflection (Ohly et al., 2017; Panke & Harth, 2019). In addition, PSTs frequently lack tools that make design processes visible and support assessment of idea connections and reasoning gaps during collaboration (RodríguezChirino et al., 2025). These challenges are closely tied to the nature of instructional design, a core professional practice through which PSTs are expected to enact DT. Instructional design involves analyzing learner needs, aligning goals with instructional strategies, designing assessments, iterating instructional prototypes, and evaluating learning outcomes (Branch & Kopcha, 2013; Merrill, 2002). Effective instructional design therefore requires PSTs to justify design decisions, integrate theory, and revise instruction based on evidence, rather than follow fixed templates. In this sense, instructional design provides an authentic context for DT development, while DT represents the mode of thinking that enables PSTs to engage productively in such design work. \n\nThe rapid growth of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) offers new opportunities to support instructional design, but also raises pedagogical concerns. Studies show that GenAI can facilitate idea generation, provide alternative design options, and support early lesson drafting in teacher education (Moorhouse & Kohnke, 2024; Moorhouse et al., 2024). At the same time, concerns persist regarding accuracy, over-reliance, and teacher agency (Ko et al., 2025; Liu et al., 2025). Research on GenAI-supported knowledge building further suggests that GenAI can promote elaboration and synthesis when embedded in collaborative inquiry rather than used as \n\n2 \n\na stand-alone tool (Chen et al., 2023). However, most existing studies focus on PSTs’ attitudes or intentions, leaving underexplored how GenAI can be pedagogically integrated into structured, collaborative DT processes for instructional design. \n\nParallel research on Knowledge Building (KB) and analytics-supported reflective assessment (ASRA) provides promising foundations for addressing this gap. KB is a collaborative inquiry framework in which learners work as a community to advance shared knowledge through sustained idea improvement (Scardamalia & Bereiter, 2014, 2022). Prior studies show that KB environments support progressive discourse and design-oriented inquiry in teacher education (Chen & Hong, 2016; Hong et al., 2019). ASRA complements KB by helping learners monitor, evaluate, and regulate inquiry processes over time (Borge et al., 2018; Rodríguez-Chirino et al., 2025). Reflective assessment refers to a learning-oriented approach in which learners using rubric or data about learning to monitor, evaluate, and adjust their learning strategies in order to improve inquiry and learning process and outcomes (Lei & Chan, 2018; Scardamalia, 2002; White & Frederiksen, 1998), while learning analytics make inquiry processes visible and interpretable (Tao & Zhang, 2021; Tong et al., 2024). Such support is especially important in GenAI-augmented contexts, where idea generation may be accelerated but reflective judgment and principled revision remain demanding. Despite these advances, how GenAI and ASRA can be jointly integrated within KB environments to support PSTs’ DT in instructional design remains insufficiently understood. Building on these lines of research, this study designed a KB collaborative inquiry environment enriched with GenAI and ASRA to support PSTs’ DT through instructional design. The study aimed to examine the effects of the combined use of GenAI and ASRA on PSTs’ DT, and how PSTs’ DT competencies evolved over time within this integrative design. Participants were 110 third-year PSTs enrolled in a compulsory instructional design course at a teacher education university in central China. GenAI tools (e.g., Kimi, DeepSeek, iFlytek Spark, Doubao-Seed) were used as cognitive and reflective partners to support ideation, elaboration, and critique, while ASRA scaffolded monitoring, reflection, and action planning in KB inquiry. Reflective assessment was supported by the Knowledge-Building Discourse Explorer (KBDeX; Oshima et al., 2012), a learning analytics tool that provides social network–based visualizations of learners’ notes, enabling PSTs to see how ideas connect, how contributions develop, and how their work advances collective progress. These visualizations offer actionable feedback that helps learners track their inquiry, identify gaps, and deepen their inquiry (Wise et al., 2021). In this study, KBDeX functioned as a process-oriented scaffold aligned with the goal of fostering PSTs’ DT within a KB environment. \n\n## **2. Conceptual Framework and Literature Review** \n\n## _2.1. Design Thinking (DT), Instructional Design and Preservice Teachers (PSTs)_ \n\nDT has been increasingly recognized as crucial for preparing PSTs to address complex and ill-structured problems of teaching practice (Baran & AlZoub, 2024; Yüksel, 2025). Rather than focusing on fixed procedures or predefined solutions, DT emphasizes iterative cycles of problem framing, ideation, prototyping, testing and reflection, which align closely with the nature of instructional decision-making in classrooms (Buchanan, 1992; Razzouk & Shute, \n\n3 \n\n2012). Among existing formulations, the Discover–Define–Ideate–Prototype–Test (DDIPT) model is one of the most widely adopted DT frameworks in educational research, providing a process-oriented structure for addressing ill-structured problems (Kelley & Kelley, 2013). This structure aligns well with instructional design, where PSTs must continuously interpret learner needs, test instructional ideas, and refine designs based on feedback and evidence (Lin et al., 2020; Tseng et al., 2019). In teacher education, DT therefore functions not as a generic innovation skill, but as a form of professional thinking that supports instructional decisions under uncertainty (Tseng et al., 2019). Empirical studies have shown that engaging PSTs in DT can strengthen instructional reasoning, promote adaptive expertise, and support the design of learning experiences that respond to learners’ needs and contextual constraints (Calavia et al., 2023; Lin et al., 2020). \n\nPrevious research studies provide growing evidence of the value of DT for PSTs development. For example, Lin et al. (2020) reported that engaging PSTs in a KB collaborative inquiry environment supported their DT by promoting iterative idea improvement and reflection on instructional decisions. Similarly, Calavia et al. (2023) showed that DT-based interventions helped PSTs address authentic classroom challenges and develop more sustainable instructional designs. Baran and AlZoubi (2024) further found that DT-oriented coursework can reshape PSTs’ mindsets and conceptualizations of teaching and learning, suggesting DT’s potential to support future-oriented teaching competencies. These results suggest that DT is most effective when embedded in sustained, reflective, and design-oriented instructional work, rather than isolated creative activities (Hong et al., 2019). However, prior research also reveals obvious challenges in helping PSTs develop DT. Although the PSTs are often able to complete instructional design tasks, many remain focused on surface-level lesson planning and struggle to justify instructional decisions, connect learning theories to practice, or iteratively improve their instructional designs based on feedback (Baran & AlZoubi, 2024; Calavia et al., 2023). The PSTs also face challenges in generating and synthesizing diverse ideas, reflecting on and regulating collaborative inquiry process and ideas, and coordinating group efforts in collaborative DT activities (Authors, 2024a; Shively & Palilonis, 2018). These results suggest that DT does not automatically emerge from participation in instructional tasks and requires deliberate support for reflection, regulation, and collaboration across design cycles. These challenges point to the central role of instructional design as both a professional competence and an authentic context for developing DT. Instructional design goes beyond the technical production of lesson plans and requires PSTs to analyze learners’ needs, align learning goals with pedagogical strategies, design assessments, and iteratively refine instructional plans based on evidence of learning (Branch & Kopcha, 2013; Merrill, 2002). Instructional design positions PSTs as designers of learning rather than implementers of scripts (Laurillard et al., 2018), and conceptualized as a method for developing teachers’ professional knowledge in technology-supported learning environments (Papanikolaou et al., 2017). As such, instructional design constitutes a complex, ill-structured problem-solving activity that is inherently design-oriented and reflective in nature. \n\nDT provides a process-oriented lens through which instructional design can be meaningfully developed. While instructional design specifies what needs to be designed and evaluated, DT focuses on how PSTs frame instructional problems, generate and test ideas, and reflect on the \n\n4 \n\nconsequences of design decisions. In this sense, DT can be understood as the mode of thinking that enables PSTs to engage productively in instructional design rather than merely following prescribed models or templates. Prior research suggests that without opportunities to engage in DT, PSTs often approach instructional design as a technical task, relying on predefined procedures with limited reflection or adaptation to learners’ needs (Calavia et al., 2023; Baran & AlZoubi, 2024). Therefore, fostering PSTs’ DT within instructional design processes is essential for developing adaptive instructional competence. When PSTs engage in instructional design as an iterative, design-oriented activity, they are more likely to justify pedagogical decisions, integrate learning theories with contextual knowledge, and improve instructional plans based on feedback and reflection. This integration supports the development of professional judgment, creative problem framing, and reflective decision-making—capabilities that are central to effective teaching but are not fully addressed through procedural design training alone. Given these challenges, recent studies have pointed to the need for collaborative inquiry environments that provide appropriate scaffolds and tools to support PSTs’ engagement in iterative DT and the development of instructional design competence (Hong et al., 2025; Lin et al., 2020). When such supports are in place, PSTs are better positioned to reflect on their design processes and the quality of ideas, and make informed decisions about subsequent design actions. Meanwhile, collaborative inquiry scholarship has begun to discuss how GenAI may reshape collaborative knowledge building, calling for designs that preserve learners’ agency and collective responsibility while leveraging GenAI as a resource for idea development (Cress & Kimmerle, 2023). Despite growing interest in technology-supported collaborative inquiry environments, however, empirical work that examines how multiple forms of support, particularly those enriched by GenAI and learning analytics, can be effectively combined to support PSTs’ DT remains limited. Addressing this gap is essential for understanding how PSTs’ DT can be effectively fostered in technology-supported collaborative inquiry contexts. _2.2 Knowledge Building (KB) Collaborative Inquiry Model for DT_ KB is a theory-driven collaborative inquiry model that explains how knowledge advances through sustained collective effort rather than through the transmission of fixed content (Scardamalia, 2002; Scardamalia & Bereiter, 2014, 2022). In KB, learners work as a community to progressively improve ideas over time, taking collective responsibility for advancing shared understanding. From this perspective, KB provides a coherent theoretical foundation for teacher education, where PSTs are expected to frame educational problems, iteratively develop and refine instructional solutions, engage in reflections and regulations, and collaborate productively around complex design challenges. KB is characterized by a coherent set of twelve KB principles that underpin both pedagogical design and empirical research (Scardamalia, 2002). Rather than prescribing fixed procedures, KB principles define norms for high-quality knowledge work while leaving substantive decisions to learners (Scardamalia, 2002; Zhang et al., 2020). This structure supports PSTs in working with ill-defined instructional problems, making informed design choices, and adapting solutions to contextual constraints. Such experiences are central to DT in teaching, where instructional design requires continuous \n\n5 \n\ninterpretation of learner needs, classroom conditions, and emerging evidence. Three KB principles that are particularly relevant for fostering PSTs’ DT are elaborated. \n\n- a) _Epistemic agency_ positions learners as responsible for initiating questions, setting inquiry goals, monitoring progress, and assessing the adequacy of emerging ideas, and monitoring and regulating the inquiry process. For PSTs, this form of agency closely parallels DT processes, in which designers must actively define problems, test alternatives, and revise plans based on feedback. By exercising epistemic agency, PSTs develop strategic reasoning and self-regulation that are essential for sustained instructional design and professional decision-making. \n\nb) _Collective responsibility_ for idea improvement frames knowledge advancement as a shared endeavor, encouraging PSTs to treat diverse perspectives as resources for collaborative design rather than as competing viewpoints. The principle that ideas are improvable embeds iteration at the core of inquiry, directly aligning with DT’s emphasis on prototyping, feedback, and refinement. c) _Embedded and transformative assessment_ supports the reflective dimension of DT. In KB, assessment is integrated into ongoing inquiry and functions as feedback that informs subsequent actions and idea refinement. This continuous assessment encourages PSTs to monitor the coherence and quality of instructional ideas, reflect on design rationales, and adjust strategies over time, thereby strengthening metacognitive awareness and reflective practice. KB collaborative inquiry generally is supported by Knowledge Forum® which is an online platform. Knowledge Forum (Figure 1) provides shared spaces where learners contribute questions, theories, and ideas, co-construct multimedia notes, and build on each other’s thinking through dialogue. Built-in scaffolds (e.g., “My Theory,” “My Reflection”) prompt learners to articulate emerging ideas and engage in theory-building discourse, while integrated analytics tools—such as the Analytic Toolkit, Promising Ideas Tool (Chen, 2017), and KBDeX visualizations (Oshima et al., 2012)—make learning processes visible and support embedded and transformative assessment. These affordances help learners monitor contributions, identify knowledge gaps, and iteratively refine ideas, enabling KB to cultivate higher-order competencies including critical thinking, collaboration, and innovation (Authors, 2024a; Chen, 2017; Oshima et al., 2020; Zhang et al., 2018). Previous research studies in teacher education contexts provide growing evidence that KB collaborative inquiry environments can support PSTs’ DT, particularly by fostering iterative idea development and reflective instructional reasoning. Lin et al. (2020), for example, found that engaging PSTs in KB discourse supported their DT through sustained idea improvement and reflection on instructional rationales. However, their results also suggest that PSTs’ engagement varies considerably, with deeper design outcomes emerging only when learners actively build on peers’ ideas. Hong et al. (2025) extended this line of work through designbased research on lesson design of interdisciplinary science, technology, engineering, arts and mathematics. They found that while sustained idea-developing discourse supported elements of teacher agency, yet discourse often remained superficial without additional scaffolding. \n\nRecent work has begun to explore how emerging technologies may enrich design-oriented KB collaborative inquiry. Hong et al. (2025) find that integrating GenAI into KB activities support more elaborated and synthesis-oriented discourse, indicating the potential of human– \n\n6 \n\nAI co-agency in collaborative design. _Human–AI co-agency_ refers to a distributed form of agency in which learners and AI systems jointly contribute to problem framing and idea development, while learners retain responsibility for assessment, decision-making, and the direction of inquiry (Krakowski, 2025; Järvelä et al., 2025). This form of co-agency is especially important for PSTs’ DT, as instructional design involves ill-structured problems that benefit from exploring multiple alternatives and reflecting on competing ideas. When grounded in KB principles, AI-generated contributions are treated as improvable ideas rather than authoritative solutions, supporting PSTs’ epistemic agency and reflective assessment. \n\nDespite these promising directions, existing research often examines KB, learning analytics, or AI supports in isolation. Empirical studies that systematically integrate KB principles with generative AI and ASRA to foster PSTs’ DT remain limited. Addressing this gap is essential for understanding how multiple forms of pedagogical and technological support can be coherently combined to support design thinking in collaborative inquiry environments. \n\nFigure 1. Knowledge Forum view and notes \n\n7 \n\n## _2.3. GenAI_ \n\nRecent advances in GenAI, including large language models, have prompted growing interest in their potential to support design-oriented learning processes. Rather than positioning GenAI as an inherently beneficial tool, recent research suggests that its educational value depends critically on how it is integrated into learning activities and how learners engage with it (Cress & Kimmerle, 2023). In teacher education contexts, GenAI has been explored as a resource for supporting ideation, explanation, and reflection, particularly in complex tasks such as lesson design and instructional planning (Hong et al., 2025). From a DT perspective, GenAI can function as a cognitive partner that expands the design space by generating alternative ideas, perspectives, or representations that PSTs can evaluate and refine, and providing provisional feedback that prompts PSTs’ further reflection, comparison, and revision of instructional decisions. Previous research suggests that when learners engage GenAI through reflective prompts, iterative design tasks, or comparative questioning, they are more likely to demonstrate higher-order thinking, including critical evaluation and creative synthesis, rather than surfacelevel reproduction of content (An et al., 2025; Liao et al., 2024). In teacher education, such interactions can support PSTs in exploring multiple instructional possibilities and articulating the rationale behind their design decisions, which are central to DT. Meanwhile, research consistently cautions that GenAI does not automatically promote DT. Shallow uses—such as copying AI-generated lesson plans or treating outputs as authoritative answers—may reduce learners’ epistemic engagement and reinforce procedural approaches to instructional design (Cress & Kimmerle, 2023). In contrast, deeper forms of interaction, characterized by co-regulation, questioning, and iterative testing of ideas, are more likely to support design-oriented reasoning and reflective assessment. As illustrated in Figure 2, AI– human interaction in learning can be conceptualized along a continuum, ranging from passive knowledge reception to co-design of ideas and meaning-making. In shallow interactions, learners depend on AI primarily for factual retrieval or surface-level feedback, engaging mainly lower-order cognitive processes. In deeper interactions, learners draw on AI as cognitive partner, actively engaging in problem reframing, prototyping, and evaluation. These deeper forms of engagement are more likely to promote soft knowledge creation and design-driven inquiry. This interaction-based distinction provides a conceptual lens for understanding why GenAI supports DT only under certain conditions. It has been further articulated through perspectives on human–AI co-agency, which emphasize that educational benefits arise when human learners retain responsibility for sense-making, evaluation, and decision-making, while AI contributes generative and analytic resources (Järvelä et al., 2025; Krakowski, 2025). For PSTs, maintaining such co-agency is especially important, as instructional design involves illstructured problems that require judgment, justification, and adaptation rather than the application of fixed solutions. \n\nBuilding on this line of work, the present study conceptualizes GenAI as a co-designer within PSTs’ KB collaborative inquiry rather than as a substitute for human reasoning. When embedded in a KB environment and coupled with ASRA, GenAI is positioned to support PSTs in reframing instructional problems, testing alternative design ideas, and reflecting on the quality of emerging solutions. This integrated design seeks to leverage GenAI’s generative strengths while addressing its limitations through principled pedagogical scaffolding. However, \n\n8 \n\nempirical research that systematically combines GenAI with KB principles and reflective assessment to foster PSTs’ DT remains limited. Addressing this gap is essential for advancing evidence-based designs of AI-supported teacher education environments. \n\nFigure 2 depicts a continuum of AI–human interaction in design-oriented learning, from shallow use for information retrieval or surface feedback to deep co-design involving problem reframing, idea development, and evaluation. The figure highlights that generative AI supports DT only when learners retain epistemic agency and engage in reflective, iterative inquiry. \n\nFigure 2. Levels of AI–human interaction in design-oriented in inquiry \n\n## _2.4. Reflective assessment_ \n\nReflective assessment refers to a learning-oriented approach in which learners monitor, evaluate, and adjust their learning strategies to improve inquiry and learning process and outcomes (Borge et al., 2018; Scardamalia, 2002; White & Frederiksen, 1998). Rather than serving as a summative assessment, reflective assessment supports learners in becoming aware of their progress and actively directing subsequent learning actions. Reflective assessment is commonly characterized as concurrent, embedded, and transformative (Scardamalia, 2002). First, it is concurrent—students reflect while learning is happening. Second, it is embedded— reflection is part of collaborative inquiry, not a separate task. Third, it is transformative— learners use reflection to refine their understanding and make better decisions for their next steps. \n\n9 \n\nIn collaborative inquiry environments such as KB environments, reflective assessment has been shown to play a critical role in supporting idea improvement and regulation of collaborative inquiry (Rodríguez-Chirinoet al., 2025). Prior studies using portfolio-supported reflective assessment suggest that learners who productively reflect on the quality and coherence of their contributions are better able to identify conceptual gaps, synthesize ideas, and sustain collective inquiry over time (Lee et al., 2006; van Aalst & Chan, 2007; Lei & Chan, 2018). These findings suggest that reflective assessment supports not only individual metacognition but also collective responsibility for idea improvement, which is central to design-oriented inquiry. More recent research has extended reflective assessment by integrating learning analytics to make inquiry processes visible and actionable (Tao & Zhang, 2021; Tong et al., 2024). Learning analytics can provide learners with process-oriented information—such as participation patterns, discourse connections, and idea development trajectories—that is otherwise difficult to perceive in collaborative settings (Siemens & Gašević, 2012; Wise & Schwarz, 2017). When coupled with reflective prompts, analytics-supported reflective assessment (ASRA) helps learners interpret these data, recognize strengths and weaknesses in their inquiry, and regulate their collaborative inquiry and learning more productively (Tao & Zhang, 2021). Related evidence from AI-supported learning contexts further shows that autoassessment and feedback systems can effectively promote reflective practice. Studies in physical education report that AI-based assessment systems providing timely, criterionreferenced feedback improved learners’ performance and supported reflection, motivation, and critical evaluation, especially when individualized instructor feedback was limited (Hsia et al., 2024; Hsia et al., 2025). These findings underscore the role of AI-supported assessment as a reflection-promoting mechanism, aligning closely with the aims of ASRA. Empirical studies on PSTs show that ASRA supports PSTs in synthesizing contributions, monitoring progress across iterations, and planning subsequent inquiry actions, leading to improvements in higherorder thinking and knowledge quality (Authors, 2022, 2024a). To operationalize ASRA in KB environments, prior research has used the KnowledgeBuilding Discourse Explorer (KBDeX; Oshima et al., 2012), a learning analytics tool that visualizes discourse structures and idea connections. KBDeX provides social network–based representations of learner-generated notes, enabling learners to trace how ideas evolve, examine interaction patterns, and reflect on their contributions to collective knowledge advancement (Oshima et al., 2012; Oshima et al., 2020). Such visualizations support learners in monitoring inquiry status, identifying gaps in idea development, and making informed decisions about how to deepen and extend their work (Authors, 2024a; Oshima et al., 2020). In KB contexts, KBDeX functions not as an evaluative instrument but as a reflective scaffold aligned with embedded and transformative assessment principles. \n\nFrom the DT perspective, ASRA is particularly important because DT requires iterative cycles of ideation, testing, evaluation, and refinement. Without effective support for reflection, PSTs may focus on completing design tasks without critically examining the rationale, coherence, or effectiveness of their design decisions. ASRA provides a mechanism for PSTs to reflect on their evolving instructional designs, evaluate emerging ideas against design goals, and make evidence-informed revisions across design cycles. This function is especially critical in collaborative design contexts, where progress depends on coordinating ideas, tracking collective development, and aligning individual contributions with shared design goals \n\n10 \n\n(Authors, 2024a). Specifically, by making collaborative design processes and idea trajectories visible, analytics tools such as KBDeX support PSTs in reflecting on how their instructional ideas emerge, connect, and improve across design cycles, thereby strengthening reflective assessment and design-oriented reasoning. \n\nBuilding on this body of research, the present study integrates KBDeX-supported reflective assessment with KB and GenAI to support PSTs’ DT. By embedding reflective assessment within collaborative inquiry and augmenting it with analytics and AI-based feedback, the study aims to help PSTs take ownership of their design processes, engage in principled reflection on instructional decisions, and iteratively improve their designs through cycles of feedback, revision, and co-creation. This integrated approach addresses a key gap in existing research, which has rarely examined how ASRA and GenAI can be ASRA and GenAI can be jointly integrated within KB environments to foster design thinking in teacher education. \n\nintegrated within KB environments to foster design thinking in teacher education. _2.5. The Present study_ While prior research has examined the use of GenAI or learning analytics to support PSTs’ inquiry and learning, there is limited empirical studies that integrate these supports within a collaborative inquiry framework to foster PSTs’ design thinking (DT). Existing work often treats GenAI, learning analytics, or collaborative inquiry as isolated interventions, making it difficult to explain how they are theoretically connected and how they jointly support designoriented learning in teacher education. Building on the literature reviewed above, this study positions DT as the focal learning outcome, with KB serving as the collaborative inquiry context in which DT is enacted and developed. Within this context, GenAI and ASRA are conceptualized as complementary supports rather than independent frameworks. Specifically, GenAI is designed to function as a cognitive and design partner, supporting PSTs in generating alternative instructional ideas, reframing problems, and exploring multiple design possibilities through human–AI co-agency. ASRA, operationalized through KBDeX-based visualizations and structured reflective prompts, supports PSTs in monitoring collective progress, evaluating the quality of emerging ideas, and regulating subsequent design actions across iterative cycles. Together, these elements form an integrated design that supports epistemic agency, reflection, and iterative improvement. Based on this synthesis, we developed a theory-driven conceptual framework (Figure 3) that integrates KB inquiry, GenAI, ASRA, and PSTs’ DT, illustrating how these elements interact to support DT as a dynamic and socially mediated practice rather than a fixed procedural sequence. \n\nBuilding on this conceptual framework, we further developed a design framework (Figure 4) that translates these theoretical relationships into concrete pedagogical and technological supports. This framework integrates several key components: (1) Knowledge Forum’s built-in assessment tools and scaffolds for collaborative and reflective inquiry, (2) GenAI-supported codesign for idea generation, critique, and refinement, (3) analytics-supported visualizations and data, and metacognitive prompts analytics-supported visualizations and metacognitive prompts, particularly KBDeX-based representations of online discourse, to guide reflective assessment. \n\nDrawing on principles of epistemic agency, collective responsibility for idea improvement, human–AI co-agency, and embedded and transformative assessment, the integrated framework supports PSTs in navigating the nonlinear phases of discover, define, ideate, prototype, and test \n\n11 \n\ninvolved in the DDIPT DT model. In this study, DT is conceptualized not merely as a process of solving instructional problems, but as a dynamic, socially mediated practice that evolves through human–AI co-agency and co-design. Guided by this two-level framework of the theory-driven conceptual framework (Figure 3) and the design framework (Figure 4), this study aimed to examine the combined effects of GenAI and ASRA within a KB inquiry environment on PSTs’ DT. Two research questions (RQ) were addressed: \n\n- (1) Do PSTs develop their DT through the combined use of GenAI and ASRA in a KB collaborative inquiry environment? (RQ1) \n\n- (2) How do PSTs’ DT evolve over time through the integrate use of GenAI and ASRA within the KB collaborative inquiry environment? (RQ2) \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nFigure 3. The conceptual framework illustrating the integrated design of KB inquiry, GenAI<br>and ASRA to support PSTs’ DT<br>Journal Pre-proof<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\nFigure 3. The conceptual framework illustrating the integrated design of KB inquiry, GenAI and ASRA to support PSTs’ DT \n\n12 \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nJournal Pre-proof<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\nFigure 4. The conceptual design framework of the KB collaborative inquiry design enriched by GenAI and ASRA for developing PSTs’ DT \n\n13 \n\n## **3. Methods** \n\n## _3.1 Research Context and Participants_ \n\nThis study was conducted at a top-tier teacher education university in central China, whose core institutional mission is to prepare qualified K–12 teachers. The participants were 110 thirdyear PSTs enrolled in a compulsory undergraduate course. All participants were English education majors, a discipline that is highly gender-skewed in the Chinese teacher education context. As a result, 86.4% of the participants were female (n = 95), while 13.6% were male (n = 15), which reflects the typical demographic distribution of this program rather than a selection criterion imposed by the study. The participants were between 20 and 22 years old and had completed foundational coursework in pedagogy and subject content but had limited prior experience with instructional design and no formal classroom teaching experience. Importantly, none of the participants had prior experience with human–AI co-design for instructional design purposes, although they were familiar with common digital tools for learning. The study was situated in a 17-week compulsory course that incorporated both face-to-face sessions and online collaborative inquiry activities. Rather than focusing on the general aims of the course, the present study used this course as an authentic context to implement and examine a design-oriented learning environment that integrated KB, GenAI and ASRA. Compared with previous iterations of the course, the instructional design in this study was substantially enhanced by the systematic integration of GenAI and KBDeX-supported reflective assessment, allowing PSTs to engage more deeply in iterative instructional design and reflection. Students worked in self-selected groups of five (22 groups in total) and were encouraged to engage in cross-group idea sharing through the KB platform. To mitigate potential grade-related bias associated with conducting research in a required course, we adopted the following measures: (1) participation in the study was voluntary and had no impact on course grades; (2) alternative learning activities were made available for students who chose not to participate, ensuring that no academic disadvantage resulted from non-participation; (3) all data used for research purposes were anonymized and analyzed only after course grading was completed. Prior to data collection, all participants signed informed consent forms and were explicitly informed of their right to withdraw from the study at any time without penalty. Ethical approval for the study was obtained from the university’s institutional review board, and all procedures complied with relevant ethical guidelines for educational research. The course was taught by an experienced instructor with nearly ten years of experience designing and facilitating KB collaborative inquiry supported courses and implementing ASRA in teacher education contexts. This instructional expertise ensured the enactment of KB principles and reflective assessment practices, providing a stable pedagogical foundation for examining how GenAI and ASRA jointly supported PSTs’ DT. \n\n## _3.2 KB Design-oriented Collaborative Inquiry Design Augmented by GenAI and ASRA_ \n\nGuided by the four theoretically grounded principles—epistemic agency, collective responsibility for idea improvement, human–AI co-agency and co-design, and embedded and transformative assessment—and informed by the conceptual design framework (Figure 4), we developed an integrated pedagogical design that coordinated KB inquiry, GenAI-supported codesign, and ASRA. To maintain conceptual continuity and avoid introducing new terminology late in the manuscript, we adopt the term GenAI-Reflective Knowledge Building Co-Design (GenAI-RKBCoD) from this section onward as a concise working label for the integrated design rather than a separate theoretical model.The model comprises a set of principle-based activities designed to foster PSTs’ DT through instructional design, with lesson plans treated as \n\n14 \n\nevolving design artifacts rather than final products (see details in Table 1). Table 1 summarizes each activity, its instructional purpose, and the intended DT dimensions. \n\nGrounded in our previous KB design that incorporates ASRA, the present design extends this work by integrating GenAI as a cognitive and design partner within KB inquiry (Authors, 2024a, 2024b). As illustrate in Figure 5, integrated design (hereafter referred to as GenAIRKBCoD for brevity) comprises three interrelated perspectives: (1) a design-oriented collaborative inquiry cycle structured around the DDIPT model (Discover–Define–Ideate– Prototype–Test); (2) GenAI-supported co-design to expand, critique, and refine instructional ideas; and (3) ASRA, including KBDeX-based discourse visualizations and metacognitive scaffolds to guide monitoring and regulation. Throughout the manuscript, this label functions only as shorthand for the coordinated pedagogical approach already introduced, rather than as a newly proposed framework. This study treated the design as a theory-informed intervention whose effectiveness was empirically examined through our research questions and analyses. Importantly, the three “components” shown in Figure 5 do not represent discrete instructional stages. Instead, they reflect overlapping and sustained strands of pedagogical support that were intentionally interwoven across the semester. For example, the overlap between Component 1 (Weeks 1–5) and Component 2 (Weeks 3–10) reflects the continued cultivation of collaborative norms and reflective routines while PSTs entered more intensive instructional design work. **Component 1 (Weeks 1-5): Developing a collaborative and design culture for DT.** The course began by creating a supportive learning environment to foster collaborative norms, inquiry dispositions, and design-oriented reflection on instructional design. A series of activities was used to initiate classroom culture building. PSTs first participated in a class-wide brainstorming activity on productive uses of GenAI in teaching and learning. Then, they collaboratively summarized effective prompting strategies (Figure 6a), and tested these strategies through human–AI interaction on small tasks. Next, PSTs explored course-designated digital tools and documented reusable prompting patterns (e.g., requesting alternatives, constraints, and rationales) that would later support instructional design cycles. Finally, each PST signed a learning contract outlining roles, expectations, and responsibilities, which also served as a social contract for course learning. Following this, PSTs formed small, self-selected groups, identified a shared inquiry topic that related with the themes of their instructional design (e.g., festivals and celebrations, daily life in senior high school, natural disasters, community service), and further collaboratively designed an inquiry plan (Figure 6b). To mitigate potential bias associated with self-selection, three design measures were implemented: (a) explicit reporting of role allocation within each group, (b) planned rotation of discussion leadership across weeks, and (c) structured crossgroup critique sessions. These measures were intended to broaden exposure to diverse ideas and reduce insular collaboration. Supported by GenAI, groups conducted a front-end analysis of curriculum standards, learner needs, and pedagogical goals (Figure 6c), addressing the _discover_ and _define_ stages of DT. \n\n**Component 2 (Weeks 3-10): Promoting DT through iterative inquiry, peer collaboration, and delayed GenAI support.** In this component, students engaged in extended, iterative design tasks supported by face-to-face meetings, online KB discourse (via Knowledge Forum, see Figure 1), strategically delayed GenAI support, and ASRA. In _face-to-face_ collaboration, groups developed and refined instructional units based on their inquiry goals and received feedback from peers and instructors. In _Knowledge Forum discussions_ , groups participated in two coordinated strands: (a) iterative refinement of instructional designs through feedback and revision, and (b) theory-informed discussion of educational technology and pedagogy to justify design decisions. Scaffolds embedded in the Knowledge Forum (e.g., “My Contribution,” “New Problem We Identified,” “My Reflection”) supported structured reflection and deeper discourse. \n\n15 \n\nIn this component, GenAI was intentionally introduced only after each group had developed and refined an initial instructional design draft without AI support. PSTs were first expected to improve their designs through peer discussions, reflection, and revisions until further progress became limited. GenAI was then introduced to offer alternative ideas, critiques, and suggestions for improvement. To enhance procedural transparency and support comparability across groups, all PSTs followed a shared GenAI use protocol. Although several GenAI tools were available (e.g., KIMI, DeepSeek, iFlytek Spark, Doubao-Seed), students were not required to use a single platform; instead, they selected tools based on task demands and personal preferences, reflecting authentic technology use in teacher education contexts. In practice, KIMI and iFlytek Spark were most frequently used across groups. Rather than standardizing a specific GenAI tool, the study standardized the process of GenAI-supported co-design. GenAI was introduced only after human-only revision cycles, consistent with the delayed-use principle described above. \n\nDuring GenAI-supported co-design, PSTs documented (a) prompts, (b) AI outputs, and (c) decision rationales within shared design logs. To reduce risks associated with AI-generated pedagogical advice, students were required to verify suggestions through peer discussion, course readings, and instructor guidance before adoption. Representative examples of prompts, AI responses, and decision records are provided in Appendix A. This practice encouraged critical comparison between human and AI reasoning and positioned GenAI as a reflective design resource rather than an authoritative source. The process primarily supported the _ideate and prototype_ aspects of instructional DT. In parallel, ASRA provided PSTs with regular data on their KB participation and interaction. These data were used to prompt metacognitive reflection, peer feedback, and goal setting at both individual and group levels, enabling PSTs to regulate their instructional design inquiry more effectively. **Component 3 (Weeks 11-17): Deepening PSTs’ DT progressively through KBDeXsupported reflective assessment and GenAI-supported co-design.** PSTs shifted toward evaluating and refining their instructional designs, focusing on the _ideate, prototype, and test_ phases of DT through the combined use of KBDeX-supported reflective assessment and GenAI supported co-design. At this stage, instructional design was treated as a coherent, theoryinformed design of learning experiences, rather than as isolated lesson plans. To facilitate productive Knowledge Forum inquiry and discussions around instructional design, each group was guided in performing KBDeX-supported reflective assessment. Figure 6 depicted the KBDeX-supported reflective assessment process. As illustrated in Figure 7, before each groups’ individual KBDeX-supported reflective assessment, the instructor first demonstrated how to run KBDeX and how to interpret KBDeX’s data and visualizations (Figure 8) in relation to online instructional design discussions. Then, the PSTs groups engaged in the use the KBDeX’s data and visualizations with structured prompt sheets (Appendix B) to productively conduct reflective assessment activity (see Figure. 6d). This process supported the PSTs in examining idea connections, identifying gaps in design reasoning, and synthesizing diverse contributions at the community level, thereby strengthening reflective judgment about their instructional designs. \n\nIn parallel, GenAI was used to support iterative co-design by providing alternative ideas, critiques, and enhancement suggestions for instructional designs. Consistent with the course protocol, PSTs were required to verify AI-generated advice through collaborative discussion before integration into designs. PSTs engaged in multiple rounds of interaction with GenAI, selectively accepting, adapting, or rejecting AI-generated inputs based on their design goals and pedagogical reasoning. Groups were required to articulate which suggestions were taken up and why, and which were rejected, ensuring that GenAI functioned as a design resource rather than a decision-maker. This process enabled PSTs to critically compare human and AI perspectives and progressively improve their instructional designs through informed judgment. \n\n16 \n\nFollowing these cycles of reflection and co-design, each group presented their instructional design to the class and received integrated feedback from peers, the instructor, and GenAI (Figure 6e). Final instructional designs were revised accordingly with PSTs explicitly reflecting on and justifying the design changes they made. This closing cycle of KBDeX-supported reflective assessment and GenAI-supported co-design supported sustained iteration, strengthened PSTs’ DT, and deepened their understanding of how technology can be meaningfully integrated into pedagogy. \n\n### \n\n17 \n\n|owledge Building Co-Design) pedagogic<br> <br>Journal Pre-proof|\n|---|\n\n\n\nFigure 5. GenAI-RKBCoD (GenAI-Reflective Knowledge Building Co-Design) pedagogical model. Note: DDIPD = Discover–Define–Ideate– Prototype–Test <u>Table 1. Overview of the three-component pedagogical design with principle-based activities</u> \n\n|**The three-**<br>**component**<br>**pedagogical**<br>**design **|**Principle-based activities designed for PSTs**|**Purpose of the activities**|**Principles**|**Intended**<br>**DT**<br>**di