than a large shift in perceived competence. By contrast, improvements in Ideate and Test were smaller and, in self-reported measures, not statistically significant, despite these phases being central to DT. \n\nThis pattern warrants careful interpretation. Importantly, the relatively small effect size observed in self-reported DT aligns with the study’s design-based and process-oriented orientation, where developmental change is expected to emerge incrementally across iterative design cycles rather than through abrupt shifts. Because the analytic sample consisted of 103 matched cases after excluding seven incomplete or inattentive responses, the results reflect stable within-participant change rather than large cohort-level differences. First, ideation and testing impose higher cognitive and epistemic demands, requiring learners to generate multiple alternatives, evaluate trade-offs, and revise solutions iteratively. Prior studies show that novice designers often experience cognitive overload in these phases and may underestimate their progress when design work becomes more complex and uncertain (Ohly et al., 2017). Second, in this study, GenAI use was intentionally delayed until PSTs had exhausted human-only \n\n33 \n\nrevision. This design may have shifted learners’ focus from perceived fluency to critical judgment, which could explain smaller gains on self-report measures but stronger improvements on performance-based outcomes. \n\nEvidence from instructional design artifacts and discourse trajectories provides a complementary perspective. Repeated-measures analyses showed clearer developmental gains in enacted design practices than in self-reported perceptions, suggesting that PSTs’ design capabilities may have evolved earlier than their self-evaluations. From a design-based research perspective, such divergence between perceived and enacted development is theoretically meaningful, as it indicates that pedagogical change may first manifest in practice before becoming visible in learners’ self-assessment. This triangulation of multiple data sources therefore strengthens the interpretation of DT growth beyond reliance on a single measure. In particular, the human–GenAI co-designed phase demonstrated accelerated growth between early and middle design cycles, suggesting that GenAI functioned as a catalyst for idea elaboration and refinement once foundational problem framing was established. These results underscore that the role of GenAI was not simply additive but developmental, supporting iterative refinement within an ongoing inquiry trajectory. The results align with and extend existing research. First, studies on GenAI in teacher education show benefits for brainstorming, drafting, and feedback (An et al., 2025; Nyaaba & Zhai, 2024), yet often report uneven gains across design phases (e.g., stronger idea fluency than systematic testing). Our results replicate these uneven patterns while offering a process-level explanation: ideation and testing benefited most when GenAI was embedded within reflective, analytics-informed inquiry rather than used as an immediate solution generator. Second, compared with prior KB studies that rely primarily on human facilitation (e.g., Lin et al., 2020), our results show that combining GenAI prompts with analytics-supported reflection reduces unproductive cycling and increases higher-order loops across phases, especially in prototyping and testing. This design operationalizes human–AI co-agency by maintaining learner ownership of decisions while using GenAI for generative support and analytics for regulatory guidance. _5.2. PSTs’ DT Evolution Paths with the Combined use of GenAI and ASRA_ This study traced how PSTs’ DT evolved across three phases of collaborative inquiry. Content analysis of 1,305 Knowledge Forum notes revealed a clear developmental shift from early-stage problem framing and ideation toward later-stage prototyping and testing. While ideation was most frequent in Phase 1, Phase 2 and 3 were marked by steady increases in prototyping and testing, accompanied by a small rebound in problem definition—suggesting that learners revisited and refined problem framing as their solutions matured. Transition analysis further revealed that early phases were characterized by persistence within single stages and fragmented links between stages. Over time, however, transitions between definition and ideation became stronger, and iterative loops between prototyping and testing grew more interconnected. Together, these patterns indicate that PSTs gradually moved from stage-bound engagement toward more integrated and iterative design activity, a trajectory that resembles key characteristics of expert-like design practice reported in prior DT research. Importantly, these interpretations reflect the study’s analytic framing. Because Knowledge Forum discourse was examined at an aggregated phase level, frequency shifts and transition patterns are interpreted as indicators of collective inquiry development rather than independent participantlevel effects. Consistent with design-based research, the analyses aim to trace trajectories of practice evolution within an authentic learning context rather than establish causal comparisons across isolated units. \n\nThese results enrich prior research on KB and DT in teacher education. Earlier studies have shown that PSTs often remain at descriptive or early stages of inquiry without sustained \n\n34 \n\nscaffolding (Hong et al., 2025). The study’s results align with these patterns in Phase 1, while further illustrating how the combined use of GenAI and ASRA may help reorganize discourse trajectories toward iterative refinement and cross-phase integration over time. This processoriented evidence complements existing work on GenAI in education, which have emphasized its potential for positive learning outcomes such as idea generation and social-emotional learning (Henriksen et al., 2025; Verma et al., 2023), but offered limited empirical evidence of how GenAI shapes the temporal structure of DT processes. process trajectories of DT over time. By examining both frequency distributions and transition patterns, this study demonstrates that the added value of GenAI and ASRA lies not only in increasing activity levels within individual DT phases, but also in strengthening connections across phases and supporting sustained iteration. \n\nRather than probabilistic claims about independent behaviors, transition statistics are interpreted as descriptive signals of evolving discourse organization within collaborative inquiry, consistent with learning sciences approaches that emphasize process trajectories and epistemic development over isolated counts. In this sense, the integrated design contributes to KB research by providing empirical evidence that a GenAI-supported and analytics-informed KB environment can foster more coherent and reflective design trajectories in PSTs. While this study does not examine large-scale implementation or cross-context transfer, its practical contribution lies in transferable pedagogical logics rather than immediate generalizability. The core design principles including cultivating a collaborative design culture, strategically delaying GenAI use, and embedding analytics-supported reflection within KB inquiry are not tied to a specific subject area, technology platform, or class size. Future work should examine how these principles function across institutions, cohorts, and teacher education contexts, and how they can be adapted for scaled or blended implementations. _5.3. Implications for Educational Practices for Supporting PSTs’ DT_ This study’s results offer actionable design implications for supporting PSTs’ DT in AI-rich teacher education contexts. First, a collaborative design culture should be established before intensive GenAI use, with explicit norms for idea improvement, critique, and shared responsibility. Such a foundation enables PSTs to engage with DT as a collective knowledgebuilding activity rather than as individual task completion. Second, GenAI should be introduced strategically rather than continuously. Delayed or conditional access encourages PSTs to engage in independent reasoning and peer-based refinement before turning to AI support. When combined with requirements to justify the adoption or rejection of AI suggestions, this approach positions GenAI as a resource for reflection and comparison, rather than an authoritative solution provider. Third, structured reflective prompts and analytics-based visualizations are essential for supporting sustained DT. These tools help PSTs monitor collective progress, identify gaps in design reasoning, and coordinate inquiry across phases, making complex design processes visible and manageable. Finally, instructional design artifacts should be treated as evolving objects, with planned cycles of revision, reflection, and justification. This practice aligns learning activities with the iterative and non-linear nature of authentic design work and supports DT as a developmental process. Overall, these implications highlight that the educational value of GenAI lies not in automation, but in principled pedagogical integration that foregrounds reflection, judgment, and collaborative inquiry. \n\n## _5.4. Limitations and Future Directions_ \n\nThis study has several limitations that also point to directions for future work. First, the participating PSTs were relatively homogeneous, consisting mainly of female PSTs from an \n\n35 \n\nEnglish education program at a teacher education university. While this reflects the typical composition of the program, it limits the generalizability of the findings to other subject domains, institutional settings, or more gender-balanced cohorts. Future research should examine whether similar DT trajectories emerge in PST programs with different disciplinary focuses and demographic profiles. Second, the intervention was implemented by an instructor with extensive prior experience in KB and ASRA, which may introduce instructor expectancy effects. Although this expertise supported faithful implementation, future studies could involve multiple instructors or use more standardized facilitation protocols to better separate instructional design effects from instructor-related influences. Third, the instructional sequence required PSTs to first produce a human-only instructional design before engaging in GenAIsupported co-design, which introduces a potential order or practice effect. This sequencing was intentionally designed to foreground epistemic agency and reduce premature reliance on AI; however, it limits causal claims about the independent contribution of GenAI. Future work could adopt counterbalanced or parallel-group designs to more rigorously isolate the effects of GenAI at different points in the design process. Finally, although the study theorizes scalability at the level of reusable pedagogical principles—such as delayed GenAI use, structured reflective prompts, and discourse analytics—it does not empirically test large-scale or crosscontext implementation. Future design-based and quasi-experimental research is needed to examine how these principles function with larger cohorts, different instructors, and varied institutional conditions, as well as whether DT development transfers to later teaching practice. _5.5. Conclusions_ This study examined whether and how PSTs’ DT can be supported through the integration of GenAI and ASRA within a KB collaborative inquiry environment. By conceptualizing DT as a developmental and socially mediated practice rather than a set of isolated skills, we articulated a theory-informed design framework and operationalized it through an integrated pedagogical approach. As clarified earlier in Section 3.2, the term “GenAI-RKBCoD” is used only as a concise label for this integrated design, ensuring terminological continuity rather than introducing a new model at the conclusion stage. Across complementary data sources, the results consistently show that PSTs’ DT developed over time, with clear gains in problem discovery and definition and increasingly integrated engagement across ideation, prototyping, and testing. The results further indicate that GenAI contributed most effectively when positioned as a reflective design partner rather than an immediate solution generator. When GenAI use was deliberately delayed and embedded within collaborative inquiry, it supported idea expansion and refinement without undermining PSTs’ epistemic agency. At the same time, ASRA helped PSTs monitor collective progress, identify gaps in design reasoning, and sustain productive iteration. Together, these findings highlight the importance of aligning GenAI use with pedagogical structures that foreground reflection, judgment, and collective responsibility. \n\nThis study provides a process-oriented account of how DT can be cultivated through principled integration of Gen AI and reflective assessment supported by analytics. By tracing changes in design performance and inquiry trajectories over time, the study provides empirical evidence that DT development can be supported as a gradual shift from stage-bound participation toward more coherent and iterative design activity. Theoretically, this study extends learning sciences research by articulating how GenAI and ASRA can be productively integrated within a KB environment to support DT development, enriching discussions of human–AI co-agency, collective responsibility for idea improvement, and embedded assessment in knowledge creation. Practically, the study offers a principled and empirically grounded approach for teacher education, suggesting that carefully designed GenAI–analytics– KB integration can support PSTs in developing reflective, adaptive, and collaborative design \n\n36 \n\ncapacities essential for teaching in AI-rich contexts. While further research is needed to examine scalability and transfer, this work provides a foundation for designing AI-augmented learning environments that prioritize deep, process-oriented professional learning. \n\n### \n\n37 \n\n## **Appendix A. Representative Sample of GenAI Prompts and Decisions Based on the DDIPT Model A.1 Table D1. A prompting action guide** \n\nPrompt-type key (used in Table D1–D2): \n\n- A. Idea expansion: Generate multiple candidate options. \n\n- B. Alignment diagnosis: Check and critique alignment among goals, activities, assessment, and time. \n\n- C. Structured prototype: Turn a chosen option into an implementable procedure/script. \n\n- D. Differentiation & scaffolds: Design supports for mixed levels (basic/challenge), reduce anxiety, prevent simple copying. \n\n- E. Assessment & feedback: Design low-burden formative assessment and feedback loops. \n\n- F. Constraint-based revision: Revise under constraints (time, resources, classroom management) and stress-test against scenarios. \n\n|**DDIPT stage**|**Design objective in this stage**<br>**P**<br>|**rompt type(s)**<br>|**Recommended prompt template (concise)**<br>r|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Discover|Identify key problems in the current lesson-plan<br>segment and clarify why it needs revision.<br>|B + F<br>Pre-|Template: “Based on the lesson-plan segment/context below, identify three<br>major issues (ranked by severity) and explain how each may hinder goal<br>attainment. Then propose two revision directions (a minor tweak and a<br>structural change).”<br>p|\n|Define|Translate needs into measurable learning goals<br>and explicit constraints so the model does not<br>generate generic ideas.<br>urn|B<br>al|Template: “Our learning goal is … (measurable). Constraints: …<br>(time/learner profile/resources/classroom management). Please (1) refine the<br>goal into 2–3 success criteria, and (2) specify activity requirements that match<br>these criteria (e.g., each student must …).”<br>|\n|Ideate|Generate alternative activities without rewriting<br>the whole lesson; compare trade-offs under<br>constraints.<br>Jo|A + F<br>|Template: “Propose three alternative activities (do not rewrite the entire<br>lesson). For each option, specify: (1) teacher moves, (2) student talk/skill<br>target, (3) risks and mitigations, and (4) which learner profiles it fits<br>best/least. Keep total time ≤ … minutes and avoid extra platforms.”<br>|\n|Prototype|Convert<br>the<br>selected<br>option<br>into<br>an<br>implementable classroom procedure and add<br>scaffolds for mixed proficiency.|C + D|Template: “Select the best option and write it as a ready-to-paste lesson-plan<br>step: a minute-by-minute timeline, teacher instructions, student actions, target<br>language, and materials/board plan. Provide a basic and a challenge scaffold,<br>and explain how to prevent students from simply reading aloud.”|\n|Test|Evaluate whether the activity produces evidence<br>of learning, and iterate based on formative<br>assessment.|E + B|Template: “Design a low-burden formative assessment for this step: what to<br>observe and when, a simple 3-level rubric, and how to close the feedback loop<br>in ≤2 minutes. Then test the design using two counterfactual classroom<br>scenarios and provide a revised version.”|\n\n\n\n**Prompt type(s)** B + F structural change).” B A + F \n\n38 \n\n## **A.2 Table D2. Representative multi-turn interaction sample (one design change)** \n\nThis section focuses on one specific revision point in Group 7’s lesson plan (Listening and Talking): Step 2 Pre-listening. It documents ten turns of interaction around the same design change, showing how students iteratively refined requirements, added constraints, and aligned the activity with listening targets and evidence of learning. **Original lesson-plan excerpt (before revision): Step 2 Pre-listening (6 minutes)** \n\nMain approach: The teacher shows pictures of “Adventure Camp” and “International Youth Camp” and asks students to discuss and predict: \n\n- Can you name these camps? \n\n- What kind of camp are they? \n\n- Who will be there? \n\n|What<br>What<br>**Round**|will they do?<br>will they learn i<br>**DDIPT stage**|n these camps?<br>**Prompt type(s)**|**Student prompt (excerpt)**<br>|**Key model output (excerpt)**<br>proof|**Decision and lesson-plan**<br>**revision**|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|1|Discover|B + A|Please optimize this picture-based lead-in<br>+ prediction activity so it is more<br>engaging and motivates students.<br>nal Pr|Suggests multiple “fun lead-in” ideas (e.g.,<br>guessing game, role-play, scenario simulation,<br>picture-story chain, question cards, mind map,<br>group competition).<br>e-|Rewrite. Some ideas require extra<br>platforms (e.g., Kahoot), which<br>violates<br>resource<br>constraints.<br>Keep<br>“pictures<br>+<br>prediction<br>questions”<br>and<br>reserve<br>role-<br>play/question-card elements for<br>later refinement.|\n|2|Define →<br>Ideate|A|Design one pre-listening activity that<br>trains students’ prediction skill for this<br>listening-and-speaking lesson.<br>Jour|Proposes a “Future camp design challenge” with<br>roles, cards, mapping, presentation, and feedback.<br>|Reject. Too many steps/materials;<br>not feasible for a 6-minute pre-<br>listening slot. Shift to a single<br>activity with high-density output.|\n|3|Define|B + F|Design only one activity, keep it within 5<br>minutes, and make it closely tied to the<br>listening content. Please be specific.<br>|Provides a 5-minute flow: quick picture viewing<br>→ flash questions → individual writing → group<br>sharing → whole-class prediction → teacher<br>transition to listening.<br>|Rewrite. Timing is feasible, but<br>“individual writing” may reduce<br>oral production. Keep the fast<br>“predict-then-verify”<br>rhythm;<br>require each student to say at least<br>one future-tense prediction.|\n|4|Discover →<br>Define|B|I still worry about: (1) students may write<br>words instead of speaking English; (2)<br>predictions may not align with listening<br>focus (what they are going to do at camp;<br>future<br>expressions).<br>Diagnose<br>and<br>propose targeted fixes; label which fix<br>addresses which issue.|Recommends:<br>replace<br>writing<br>with<br>oral<br>prediction + peer follow-up questions; require<br>future structures (be going to / will); reduce the<br>five questions to two focused ones; add a<br>Prediction–Evidence chart to connect to during-<br>listening verification.|Adopt. Refine questions; add a<br>language-form constraint (future<br>tense);<br>add<br>the<br>Prediction–<br>Evidence chart and explicitly link<br>it to during-listening tasks.|\n|5|Ideate|A + F|Without<br>increasing<br>time<br>(total<br>≤6|Offers three options: (A) info-gap clue cards|Adopt (A as core; integrate talk|\n\n\n\n- What will they do? \n\n- What will they learn in these camps? \n\n39 \n\n|||minutes) and without extra platforms,<br>propose three alternative pre-listening<br>prediction versions. For each: teacher<br>moves, student talk goals (≥1 suggestion<br>+<br>≥1<br>follow-up<br>question),<br>and<br>risks/mitigations.|(students<br>hold<br>different<br>clues<br>and<br>must<br>ask/answer); (B) “two titles, one true” with<br>evidence; (C) prediction sentence chain, plus<br>classroom-management tips.|norms from C). Final direction:<br>use info-gap clue cards and make<br>“suggest + ask follow-up” an<br>explicit participation norm.|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|6<br>Prototype|C|I choose option A (info-gap clue cards).<br>Write<br>a<br>ready-to-paste<br>6-minute<br>procedure: minute-by-minute; teacher<br>instructions; student actions and target<br>language<br>(future<br>tense,<br>suggestions,<br>follow-up questions); materials limited to<br>pictures + paper cards.<br>|Outputs a structured script (e.g., 0:00–1:00<br>picture activation; 1:00–3:00 pair exchange using<br>future predictions; 3:00–5:00 group consolidation<br>+ prepare one follow-up; 5:00–6:00 rapid sharing<br>+ board key predictions), plus teacher talk and<br>sentence supports.<br>of|A