 are explicit.<br>• Maintains versioning<br>(V1→V2) with traceable edits<br>and rationales.|\n||Carry out<br>relevant<br>teaching<br>practice<br>activities and<br>actively seek<br>feedback.|• No practi<br>external fe<br>reflection.|ce teaching and/or no<br>edback beyond self-<br>|• Some rehearsal/try-<br>out is conducted, but<br>feedback<br>documentation is<br>weak or mostly oral.<br>• Revisions are<br>superficial;<br>feedback–revision<br>linkage is unclear.<br>Journal P|• Some rehearsal is<br>indicated but<br>incomplete;<br>documentation is<br>weak.<br>• Feedback is<br>impressionistic (oral-<br>only) and revisions are<br>mainly cosmetic.<br>re|• Conducts one micro-<br>teaching or peer try-out;<br>obtains 1–2 feedback items.<br>• Records feedback<br>basically; linkage to<br>revisions is partially<br>explained.|• Completes micro-<br>teaching/simulated enactment<br>and actively collects feedback<br>(peers/mentor).<br>• Feedback process is<br>documented with traceable<br>evidence (screenshots,<br>annotated notes, transcripts).<br>• Synthesizes feedback into<br>actionable points and revises<br>accordingly (feedback →<br>revision chain is explicit).|\n\n\n\n46 \n\n|**Dimension**<br>**s**|**Operational**<br>**Description**|**Not Met**|**Needs**<br>**Improvement**|**Partially**<br>**Met**|**Satisfactory**|**Excellent**|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n||Able to obtain<br>teaching<br>design<br>evaluations<br>through<br>various means,<br>integrate<br>feedback to<br>optimize the<br>teaching<br>process, and<br>promote<br>continuous<br>teaching<br>improvement.|<br>• No substantive evaluation<br>beyond self-judgment; no<br>external feedback or<br>improvement evidence.<br>|• Uses at least two<br>evaluation<br>sources/methods, but<br>evidence is mostly<br>summarized and<br>prioritization is<br>limited.<br>• Revisions are made,<br>but iteration is<br>incomplete and<br>before–after<br>comparison is weak.<br>|<br>• Evaluation is single-<br>source/method;<br>evidence is thin<br>(mostly narrative).<br>• Improvement points<br>are stated but weakly<br>enacted; no before–<br>after comparison.<br>re-proof|• Uses at least two sources<br>or methods (e.g., peer +<br>mentor; observation +<br>survey) and makes targeted<br>revisions.<br>• Integration is less<br>systematic (limited<br>prioritization); some<br>evidence is summarized<br>rather than original.|• Uses multiple<br>sources/methods (peers,<br>mentor, AI, and/or learner<br>data) and archives original<br>evidence.<br>• Integrates feedback into a<br>prioritized issue list with<br>decisions, and implements<br>process-level improvements<br>(with revision evidence).<br>• Demonstrates iteration (at<br>least one test–revise–recheck<br>cycle or a clear revalidation|\n|**Test**|||l|||plan).|\n||The overall<br>design is<br>innovative,<br>capable of<br>optimizing the<br>teaching<br>process based<br>on feedback,<br>with iterative<br>improvement<br>and distinctive<br>features.|• No innovation or iteration; no<br>evidence of<br>feedback→revision→refinement<br>.<br>|• Shows some<br>innovation, but<br>justification and<br>expected gains are<br>limited.<br>• Iteration exists but<br>is modest; evidence<br>chain is incomplete<br>or partly superficial.<br>Journa|• Innovation is weak or<br>misaligned; revisions<br>are largely superficial<br>(formatting/graphics).<br>• Rationale–evidence<br>linkage is unclear;<br>iteration quality is hard<br>to judge.<br>|• Includes identifiable<br>innovation and completes<br>one feedback-driven<br>iteration beyond cosmetic<br>edits.<br>• Distinctiveness exists but<br>is modest; impact claims are<br>briefly supported.|<br>• Shows a clear iteration chain<br>with structural changes (e.g.,<br>redesigning materials,<br>rewriting instructions, adding<br>scaffolds) and provides V1/V2<br>evidence.<br>• Innovation is problem-<br>aligned and justified (why the<br>change, expected learning<br>gains).<br>• Produces a reusable signature<br>solution (task + scaffold +<br>assessment toolset).|\n\n\n\n47 \n\nAppendix D. The coding framework for analyzing PSTs’ DT in online Knowledge Forum notes \n\n|Dimensions|Level|Abbreviation|Definition|Examples|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Discover|L<br>|DC<br>|Analyze learner<br>characteristics or<br>discuss<br>instructional<br>constraints<br>Identify<br>instructional<br> <br>|“First, ensure students master a<br>specific tense, voice, or<br>sentence pattern, with goals<br>aligned to their proficiency.<br>Second, choose grammar<br>points that are representative<br>and reflect core rules and<br>uses.”<br>“We should choose at least<br>two instructional modes. A<br>lecture mode is essential, and<br>we could add a simple but<br>f|\n|Define|L<br>|DF<br>|priorities and<br>difficulties; select<br>instructional media<br>and modes<br>Design innovative<br>and effective<br>Pre-p|<br>effective collaborative inquiry<br>mode where students work in<br>small groups to consolidate<br>new knowledge through peer<br>discussion.”<br>“We could introduce the<br>grammar point with an<br>engaging story or vivid video.<br>roo|\n|Ideate|H<br>|ID<br>urna|activities; develop<br>scaffolds or task<br>sheets<br>Produce an<br> <br>l|Small-group discussions can<br>deepen understanding,<br>followed by varied exercises<br>and activities.”<br>“I have two questions: Is this<br>lesson plan based on the 15-<br>minute micro-lesson format?<br>|\n|Prototype|H<br>J|PT<br>o|instructional design<br>prototype and seek<br>feedback<br>Evaluate the design<br>process and<br>|<br>How can we ensure activities<br>run smoothly during micro-<br>teaching given the differences<br>from in-person instruction?”<br>“I suggest improvements: (1)<br>Use more multimedia tools,<br>such as images, audio, and<br>video, with high clarity to<br>|\n|Test|H|TE|<br>outcomes; make<br>iterative<br>improvements|<br>capture students’ attention. (2)<br>Integrate interactive tools, such<br>as Tianyi Classroom, to enable<br>live competitions and motivate<br>students.”|\n\n\n\nNotes: L = Low, H = High \n\n48 