T and generative models in science teacher education. _Journal of Science Teacher Education, 34_ (8), 793–798. https://doi.org/10.1080/1046560X.2023.2263251 \n\n- White, B. Y., & Frederiksen, J. R. (1998). Inquiry, modeling, and metacognition: Making science accessible to all students. _Cognition and instruction_ , _16_ (1), 3-118. https://doi.org/10.1207/s1532690xci1601_2 \n\n- Wise, A. F., Knight, S., & Shum, S. B. (2021). Collaborative learning analytics. In Cress, U., Rosé, C., Wise, A.F., Oshima, J. (eds), _International handbook of computer-supported collaborative learning_ (pp. 425-443). Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-03065291-3_23 \n\n- Wise, A. F., & Schwarz, B. B. (2017). Visions of CSCL: Eight provocations for the future of the field. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _12_ (4), 423-467. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11412-017-9267-5 \n\n- Yüksel, A. O. (2025). Design-based STEM activities in teacher education and its effect on pre-service science teachers’ design thinking skills. _Journal of Science Education and Technology_ , 1–15. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10956-025-10215-2 \n\n- Zhang, J., Scardamalia, M., Lamon, M., Messina, R., & Reeve, R. (2007). Socio-cognitive dynamics of knowledge building in the work of 9- and 10-year-olds. _Educational Technology Research and Development, 55_ (2), 117–145. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11423-006-9019-0 \n\n- Verma, G., Campbell, T., Melville, W., & Park, B. Y. (2023). Navigating opportunities and challenges of artificial intelligence: ChatGPT and generative models in science teacher education. _Journal of Science Teacher Education, 34_ (8), 793–798. https://doi.org/10.1080/1046560X.2023.2263251 \n\n- Wise, A. F., & Schwarz, B. B. (2017). Visions of CSCL: Eight provocations for the future of the field. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning_ , _12_ (4), 423-467. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11412-017-9267-5 \n\n- Zhang, J., Tao, D., Chen, M. H., Sun, Y., Judson, D., & Naqvi, S. (2018). Co-organizing the collective journey of inquiry with idea thread mapper. _Journal of the Learning Sciences, 27_ (3), 390–430. https://doi.org/10.1080/10508406.2018.1444992 \n\n- Zhang, J., Yuan, G., & Bogouslavsky, M. (2020). Give student ideas a larger stage: Support cross-community interaction for knowledge building. _International Journal of Computer-Supported Collaborative Learning, 15(4)_ , 389–410. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11412-020-09332-4 \n\n52 \n\n## Highlights \n\n- Conceptualizes design thinking (DT) as a socially mediated, developmental practice \n\n- Proposes a theory-informed model linking KB, GenAI, and reflective assessment \n\n- Operationalizes design thinking through a principle-based, replicable pedagogy \n\n- Integrates delayed GenAI use with analytics-supported reflective assessment \n\n- Reveals shifts from stage-bound to iterative design practices in PST learning \n\n- Advances theorization of human–AI co-agency in collaborative inquiry contexts \n\n', NULL, '2026-09-12 19:50:23', '2026-09-12 19:50:56', '2026-C&E-Yang-Fostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI.pdf', 'project_1029/raw/2026-C&E-Yang-Fostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI.pdf', 'application/pdf', 4058595, '4134d6da05d2d357cf6aaf2d4bbfaaff27a62062c8310b913f92a3d99dedc96b', '4134d6da05d2d357cf6aaf2d4bbfaaff27a62062c8310b913f92a3d99dedc96b', 'project_1029/parsed/2026-C&E-Yang-Fostering Preservice Teachers’ Design Thinking by Integrating Generative AI.md', 'ready', NULL);
INSERT INTO `research_resource` VALUES (35, 3, 1030, 'UPLOAD', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'TEXT_EXTRACTED', 'Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492 \n\n\n\nContents lists available at ScienceDirect \n\n# Computers & Education \n\njournal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/compedu \n\n\n\nTheorizing AI literacy development using Habermas’ three cognitive knowledge interests from a systematic review: A STEM interdisciplinary perspective \n\n\n\nYuk Mui Elly Heung<sup>a,b</sup> , Hong-biao Yin<sup>a</sup> , Lyn English<sup>c</sup> , Thomas K.F. Chiu<sup>a,d,e,*</sup> \n\n\n\na _Department of Curriculum and Instruction, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR China_ \n\nb _Department of Mathematics and Information Technology, The Education University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR China_ c _Faculty of Creative Industries, Education & Social Justice, School of Education, The Queensland University of Technology, Australia_ d _Centre for Learning Sciences and Technologies, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR China_ e _Centre for University and School Partnership, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR China_ \n\nA R T I C L E I N F O \n\nA B S T R A C T \n\n_Keywords:_ Existing research demonstrates AI’s potential to improve learning outcomes and engagement, AI literacy emphasizing the importance of pedagogical designs that promote AI literacy. This systematic Interdisciplinary learning literature review investigates how different literacies connect to AI literacy and offers pedagogical Habermas’ cognitive knowledge interests suggestions from a science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) learning Data literacy perspective. Using the PRISMA approach, we screened 1603 articles from four databases and Mathematical literacy selected 58 for this review. We theorize AI literacy development using Habermas’ three cognitive knowledge interests: technical, practical, and emancipatory as the primary analytical framework. We examine each interest individually and collectively to structure and connect the progression levels within UNESCO’s AI competency framework for teachers. Our findings highlight the need for an interdisciplinary approach, with various literacies—data, digital, mathematical, algorithmic, scientific, computational, media, language, and civic—being critical for developing AI literacy. We presented a hierarchical structure to describe how the literacies related to AI literacy. Moreover, we suggest age-appropriate, culturally sensitive pedagogical methodologies, with project-based and problem-based learning helpful in K-12 and higher education and game-based learning, which incorporates AI toys and role-play, especially advantageous in early childhood education. Furthermore, we emphasize case-based, reflective, and cultural learning as important strategies for establishing ethical AI citizenship by allowing students to balance sociocultural aspects while developing unbiased and responsible AI-enhanced applications for society and the environment. \n\n## **1. Introduction** \n\nLiterature has suggested how to design artificial intelligence (AI) education across various levels (Heung & Chiu, 2025), including early childhood education (Su & Yang, 2022), K-12 education (Casal-Otero et al., 2023; Chiu et al., 2022), and higher education \n\n* Corresponding author. Room 303, Ho Tim Building, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR China. \n\n_E-mail addresses:_ eymheung@link.cuhk.edu.hk (Y.M.E. Heung), yinhb@cuhk.edu.hk (H.-b. Yin), l.english@qut.edu.au (L. English), tchiu@cuhk. edu.hk (T.K.F. Chiu). \n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.compedu.2025.105492 \n\nReceived 15 May 2025; Received in revised form 3 October 2025; Accepted 30 October 2025 Available online 4 November 2025 \n\n0360-1315/© 2025 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. \n\n_Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\n_Y.M.E. Heung et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computers & Education_ \n\n(Laupichler et al., 2022; Xia et al., 2024). Studies on student AI literacy have become more popular and require collaboration across fields and disciplines (Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024; Long & Magerko, 2020; Yang et al., 2025). AI literacy is related to knowledge and skills for using AI, as well as the capacity to work with AI effectively and responsibly in a specific context (e.g., learning, healthcare practice, or scientific research). This includes grasping foundational concepts (like machine learning, data, algorithms), being able to use AI tools, and thinking critically about AI’s impact on society (Casal-Otero et al., 2023). Particularly, AI literacy is relevant to science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) fields, as STEM disciplines frequently utilize AI in research and innovation, such as AI-informed biomedical analysis in health science education (Hersh et al., 2023), AI-enhanced design optimization in engineering education (Usher & Barak, 2024), and AI algorithm-based mathematical modeling in mathematics education (Kong et al., 2024). \n\nPrevious review studies frequently focused on identifying AI content and concepts, technological abilities, and ethical knowledge that were required for student AI literacy development. For example, Long and Magerko (2020) used a casual review approach (not following the PRISMA protocol) to suggest 17 skills and 15 design considerations for fostering AI literacy. This study is mainly engineering-focused and lacks an educational and theoretical perspective. Rizvi et al. (2023) review identified essential AI ideas, topics, and learning outcomes. It also raises major gender differences and concerns about coding skills. Casal-Otero et al.’s (2023) review focused on implementing AI education in K-12 schools, identifying challenges and issues with the implementation. Liu and Zhong’s (2024) review focuses on analyzing research design related to AI education in K-12. Overall, these review studies have cataloged knowledge, concepts, skills and tools but have not sufficiently clarified the conceptual overlaps and omissions between them. The field of AI education lacks a unified and operational definition of AI literacy. Moreover, most of these studies did not look at the AI literacy development from a theoretical perspective. AI literacy connects to various literacies: data, digital, mathematical, algorithmic, scientific, computational, media, language, and civic literacy (Chiu, 2025, pp. 80–109; Long & Magerko, 2020). \n\nWhile Vygotsky (1967) excels in explaining cognitive development through social interaction, and Freire (2020) provides an essential framework for emancipatory critique of power structures. Habermas (1978) is chosen in this study because his theory provides the most comprehensive procedural and normative framework for analyzing and designing educational interactions that aim to develop citizens capable of engaging in reasoned, respectful, and productive dialogue to solve shared problems. He complements the others by providing the rules of the game for the very dialogue that Vygotsky’s cognitive development and scaffolding as well as Freire’s empowerment and challenging oppression. \n\nThis systematic review study pioneers applying Habermas’s (1978) framework to AI education. Existing AI literacy models often replicate past research, lacking theoretical depth for holistic understanding. By clearly demonstrating how three Habermas domains—technical, practical, and emancipatory—address these gaps, this study establishes a more persuasive, transformative foundation for AI literacy. The findings of this study unveil patterns from selected empirical studies and provide valuable insights for researchers and educators. They could contribute to the development of AI literacy definition. \n\n## **2. Theoretical framework** \n\n## _2.1. Existing AI literacy framework_ \n\nAI literacy encompasses a broad range of skills that include effectively using, assessing, and applying digital tools, resources, and services throughout one’s lifelong learning journey (Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024; Falloon, 2020; Long & Magerko, 2020). AI literacy can be defined as a set of knowledge and skills that enable individuals to critically use, assess, apply, and evaluate AI technologies to communicate and collaborate effectively and healthily in our daily lives (Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024; Long & Magerko, 2020). This literacy encompasses a balanced perspective on the ethical and healthy use of technology, integrating considerations related to privacy and security, legal responsibilities, and the societal impact of digital technologies (Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024; Falloon, 2020). This literacy emphasizes not only understanding AI concepts but also the practical application of AI in various contexts. \n\nThe development of AI literacy is intricately linked with other STEM-related literacies that enable expression, communication, and access to knowledge, forming a complex web of interconnected and interdisciplinary skills (Joseph et al., 2024; Yang et al., 2025). Digital literacy is a foundational prerequisite for AI literacy, enabling individuals to effectively understand and interact with AI technologies (Long & Magerko, 2020). While computational literacy, defined as the ability to use code skills to express ideas (DiSessa, 2018), can enhance one’s understanding of AI, it is not a necessary prerequisite, as most users interact with AI systems without the need to program them (Kong et al., 2024; Long & Magerko, 2020). Scientific literacy is the ability to critically connect scientific knowledge with real-life contexts, enabling informed decision-making and active participation in social change (Valladares, 2021), which is particularly important in applying machine learning practices and the broader scientific context in which AI operates (Yang et al., 2025). Mathematical literacy facilitates students’ logical reasoning, enabling their practical application of technology and fostering their analytical and reasoning skills (Blum & Niss, 1991). The definition of mathematical literacy has evolved and has put more emphasis on the active processes of formulating, employing, and interpreting mathematics in real-world problem-solving (Colwell & Enderson, 2016; De Lange, 2003). As such, developing strong mathematical literacy can empower students to comprehend AI technologies better, critically evaluate their applications, and effectively use AI tools to solve real-world problems. Algorithmic literacy refers to understanding algorithms, critically evaluating them, and possessing appropriate skills to cope with algorithmic systems (Dogruel et al., 2022). Despite the recognition of these interconnected literacies, there remains a significant gap in understanding how to effectively adopt the interdisciplinary approach to AI education curricula (Casal-Otero et al., 2023; Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024). Specifically, research is needed on a pedagogical design that seamlessly combines digital, computational, scientific, mathematical, and other relevant literacies to create a comprehensive AI literacy framework (Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024). Therefore, this study aims to explore the pedagogical design opportunities of integrating these literacies into STEM education, providing educators with \n\n2 \n\n_Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\n_Y.M.E. Heung et al._ \n\ninsights into creating a more engaging and equitable learning environment. \n\nCurrent AI literacy frameworks encompass a variety of components aimed at equipping learners with the knowledge, skills, and attitudes necessary to understand, use, and critically engage with AI technologies. Existing frameworks emphasize not only the content knowledge and technical skills, such as understanding AI concepts, algorithms, and tools (Kong et al., 2024; Papert & Solomon, 1972; Touretzky et al., 2023; Williams et al., 2023), but also the ethical use of AI (Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024; Williams et al., 2023). For example, Touretzky et al. (2023) followed the guidelines based on CSTA Computing Standards (CSTA, 2017) to structure the frameworks (known as the Five Big Ideas under the AI4K12 Initiative) around five components, namely perception, representation and reasoning, learning, natural interaction, and societal impacts. These ideas adopted content approaches to emphasize students’ understanding of how AI processes information and its broader impacts. Chiu, Ahmad, et al. (2024) proposed a framework with five components that foster abilities and confidence in using AI: technology, impact, ethics, collaboration, and self-reflection. This framework began with foundational knowledge of AI, defining it as a machine’s ability to perform tasks akin to human decision-making while covering concepts like big data, machine learning, and cloud computing. Students were taught to understand perception concerning human senses and explore AI applications across various industries. The framework emphasized the impacts of AI on the future of work, social good, and associated risks, fostering awareness of its benefits and challenges. Ethical considerations were integrated by simplifying AI principles such as fairness and accountability. \n\nFurthermore, collaboration was encouraged through hands-on activities that enhanced communication skills with AI technologies. Finally, self-reflection was fostered to build confidence and promote lifelong learning in an AI-driven world, ensuring students continuously evaluate and adapt their understanding of AI. Other scholars categorized AI literacy into multiple dimensions, namely cognitive (technical content knowledge of AI algorithms), affective (motivation, emotional engagement, and self-efficacy for interacting with AI), behavioral (collaboration and problem-solving) (Ng, Xinyu, et al., 2024), and ethical (societal impacts, bias, and other risks associated with the use of AI) (Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024; Kong et al., 2024). \n\nDespite the richness and diversity of these frameworks, a challenge remains: the terminology and conceptual boundaries used across AI literacy models vary widely, which can lead to confusion and hinder coherent curriculum design and assessment. Habermas’s theory of knowledge interests (Ewert, 1991; Habermas, 1978)—technical, practical, and emancipatory—offers a unifying lens as all these AI literacy frameworks standardize and clarify AI literacy components in three major dimensions. By adopting Habermas’s framework, AI literacy discourse can achieve greater clarity and coherence. \n\n## _2.2. Conceptualizing AI literacy through the lens of Habermas’s three human interests of knowledge_ \n\nHabermas’s three human interests of knowledge interests can guide AI literacy development, as these interests provide a philosophical and theoretical framework to understand the relationship between scientific knowledge, learning, and human cognitive interests (Habermas, 1978). He classified the modes of inquiry into three cognitive interests: technical, practical, and emancipatory, centering on work, communication, and emancipation (Habermas, 1978; Bullough et al., 1984). \n\n## _2.2.1. Technical interest in AI literacy_ \n\nHabermas (1978) defines the technical interest as a fundamental human drive to manipulate the external environment. This interest is governed by technical rules and based on empirical investigation, aiming to achieve control over natural objects and events through instrumental action (Habermas, 1978). This interest is closely aligned with positivistic science and empirical-analytic methods, which focus on predicting and controlling natural phenomena through methodical experimentation and observation (Kalle & Heinz, 1985) and are particularly applicable in STEM disciplines. However, as Bullough et al. (1984) suggested, while instrumental rationality proved effective in technical contexts, it often neglected broader social and ethical considerations. This resonates with concerns in AI literacy development that people mainly focus on viewing AI as a tool for some technical action when more emphasis is needed on fostering reflection and communication (Kandlhofer et al., 2016). In considering the curriculum for developing AI literacy, this paper explores how technical interest contributes to the development of AI literacy. \n\n## _2.2.2. Practical interest in AI literacy_ \n\nPractical interest is linked to the social sciences and emphasizes understanding and interpreting social interactions in constituting knowledge (Habermas, 1978). It involves developing through facilitating mutual understanding and cooperation, requiring clarification of conditions for communication and intersubjectivity (Mezirow, 1981). Unlike technical interest, which mainly focuses on controlling the world through scientific research of reality objects (Habermas, 1978), practical interest emphasizes understanding through interpretive research as hermeneutic methods that capture nuanced interactions. Meanings are socially and culturally constructed in the conversation and interaction of human agents, which facilitate the decision-making process about how to behave rationally and morally (Grundy, 1987). This interest is crucial for understanding how AI impacts social interactions and communication. \n\n## _2.2.3. Emancipatory interest in AI literacy_ \n\nEmancipatory interest identified self-reflection, involving insights gained through critical self-awareness, as emancipatory in that at least one can recognize the correct reasons for problems (Habermas, 1978). This interest is distinct from the technical and practical interest in its focus on liberating individuals from the constraints of their socio-historical context through self-reflection and critical analysis (Schubert, 1986). Self-emancipation gains knowledge through reflection, leading to a transformed consciousness or self-determination (Ewert, 1991; Grundy, 1987). The emancipatory interest refers to the ability of an individual to take control of their \n\n3 \n\n_Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\n_Y.M.E. Heung et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computers & Education_ \n\nlives responsibly (Grundy, 1987). Considering AI literacy development from the emancipatory interest perspective implies a critical reflection on AI’s sociopolitical impacts. This paper reviews each cognitive interest for implying the future of AI in STEM education across multiple levels. \n\nTable 1 shows technical, practical, and emancipatory interests that explain AI literacy development. \n\n## _2.3. Mapping AI literacy growth with UNESCO’s progression levels_ \n\nAs part of the global effort to standardize AI literacy, UNESCO released an AI Literacy Framework for Students in 2024 (UNESCO, 2024a). It defines 12 AI literacies for students, organized under four dimensions: (1) a Human-Centered Mindset, (2) Ethics of AI, (3) AI Techniques and Applications, and (4) AI System Design. These dimensions ensure coverage of values, ethics, technical knowledge, and creation/design, respectively—which correspond closely to the emancipatory, ethical, technical, and practical aspects we discussed. For instance, a human-centered mindset and Ethics of AI relate to the emancipatory interest, whereas AI techniques & applications are technical, and AI system design involves practical interest, see Fig. 1. \n\nImportantly, UNESCO introduces three progression levels for each literacy: “Understand”, “Apply”, and “Create”. These levels represent increasing degrees of mastery. First, “Understand” refers to the foundational level where a student can comprehend AI concepts and recognize AI applications. It’s about awareness and basic knowledge—analogous to the technical understanding phase (and partly practical understanding). Second, “Apply” refers to the intermediate level where the student can use AI tools or concepts in context. This is the practical use of AI literacy—doing something meaningful with the knowledge. Lastly, “Create” is the advanced level where the student can design or innovate using AI, effectively co-creating AI solutions or content. This not only requires strong technical skills but also implies a higher-order capability, including creativity and often consideration of ethical design principles. These levels reflect increasing sophistication and proficiency in AI literacy, and importantly, increasing ethical consciousness. UNESCO’s framework explicitly ties the highest level to being a “responsible and creative citizen” in the AI era, with literacies like critical judgment of AI systems and designing inclusive, sustainable AI solutions. This study will map the development of AI literacy with these progression levels. \n\n## **3. The present study and methodology** \n\n## _3.1. Research questions_ \n\nThis systematic literature review is to synthesize the available evidence on how various literacies relate to the development of AI literacy from a STEM learning perspective across different educational contexts, drawing on Habermas’s three human interests. The main goal is to theorize the AI literacy development using Habermas’s human interests: technical, practical, and emancipatory interests, along with three levels: understand, apply, and create (UNESCO, 2024a). \n\n- The main research question (RQ1): How do various literacies contribute to the development of AI literacy in light of the perspective of Habermas’s three human interests? \n\n**Table 1** \n\nAI literacy development explained by Habermas’s three human interests. \n\n|Habermas’s Knowledge Interest|Related Literacies|\n|---|---|\n|Technical Interest|Cognitive dimension: Learning about AI<br>- Supervised learning<br>- Neural networks<br>- Machine learning<br>- Deep learning<br>- AI mechanisms and algorithms|\n|Practical Interest|Affective, behavioral, and metacognitive dimensions: Learning about how to interact with AI<br>- Practical use of AI technologies<br>- Emotional engagement<br>- Collaboration<br>- Confidence<br>- Problem-solving<br>- Comprehending, interpreting and understanding AI<br>- Social interaction<br>- Shared meaning-making|\n|Emancipatory Interest|Ethical and sociocultural dimensions: Learning for life with AI<br>- AI safety<br>- Security<br>- Critical reflection on social, ethical, and political implications<br>- Ethical AI use and responsible behavior<br>- Recognizing biases, privacy, and power imbalances<br>- Ethical AI citizenship|\n\n\n\n4 \n\n_Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\n\n\n**Fig. 1.** Relationships between UNESCO’s four dimensions and Habermas’ three interests. \n\n- The other research question (RQ2): What pedagogical design considerations support AI literacy development in early childhood, K- 12, and higher education? \n\n## _3.2. Article selection process_ \n\nWe used Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews (PRISMA) to identify articles for analysis and present the results. Fig. 2 illustrates the article selection process following the PRISMA approach. The search query [(“STEM” OR “science” OR “technology” OR “engineering” OR “mathematics”) AND (“pedagogical design” OR “pedagog*” OR “instructional” OR “curricul*” OR “teaching strateg*”) AND (“Artificial Intelligence” OR “AI”) AND (“Literac*” OR “competenc*“)] in all fields was used to select articles published from January 2014 to January 2025 from four widely used digital academic databases related to education: Web of Science Core Collection, Scopus, PsycINFO, and ERIC. The search period is appropriate, as most of the research on AI literacy started in 2018. During the search process, the inclusive selection criteria are (a) journal articles, (b) written in English, (c) empirical studies, (d) educational research related to STEM, and (e) published between 2015 and 2025. The exclusive criteria are (a) the intervention is not related to AI, (b) missing key data, and (c) irrelevant to AI literacy development or pedagogical considerations. The initial and revised search was completed on March 21, 2025. Conference proceedings were excluded as they generally hold less weight in educational research \n\n\n\n**Fig. 2.** Article selection process following the PRISMA approach. \n\n5 \n\n_Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\n_Y.M.E. Heung et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computers & Education_ \n\n## compared to peer-reviewed journal publications. \n\n## _3.3. Article identification and analysis approach_ \n\nTo retrieve the articles for screening, we used the search strings: STEM or science or technology or engineering or mathematics or computer AND (“pedagogical design” or “pedagog*” or “instructional” or “curricul*” or “teaching strateg”) AND “Artificial Intelligence” OR “AI” AND Literac* OR competenc*. After the retrieval, screening and inclusion criteria were adopted to select articles for the primary analysis. First, 1604 records were identified through database searching; see Fig. 2. A rater then examined the titles and abstracts of the articles to identify articles related to AI literacy, pedagogical design, and focus on STEM education. The full texts of the remaining 190 articles were then read to exclude further papers that only focus on the application of AI technologies but are irrelevant to the development of AI literacy. Finally, 58 articles were included in the review; see Table 2. Moreover, two raters applied the theoretical framework using Habermas’s cognitive interests shown in Table 1 (coding table) and the 3-level (understand, apply, and create) in section 2.3 to analyze the articles independently. If there were any inconsistent results, they discussed the results and sought the third rater for the final decisions. The inter-rater reliability of Cohen’s kappa (0.9) was calculated based on a subset of 15 papers independently coded by two raters during the preliminary coding stage, indicating a high level of agreement between them. \n\n## **4. Results and discussions** \n\n## _4.1. Descriptive statistics_ \n\nTable 3 presents the descriptive statistics of 58 selected articles. The statistics show that a majority of these studies focused on higher education (n = 26; 45 %) and K-12 education (n = 28; 48 %), with a smaller number addressing early childhood education (n = 4; 7 %). The articles covered a wide range of interdisciplinary degrees within STEM education: interdisciplinary AI literacy programs (n = 30), STEM Citizenship Education (n = 2), and single disciplines: engineering (n = 1), mathematics (n = 1), and technology (n = 9). Additionally, the articles addressed a broad spectrum of science subjects, including environmental science (n = 2), health sciences (n = 9), and natural sciences (n = 4). The selected studies were conducted worldwide, with the highest representation from Asia (n = 38; 66 %), followed by North America (n = 9; 16 %), Europe (n = 6; 10 %), and both Oceania and Africa (n = 2; 3 % each), with South America contributing one study (n = 1; 2 %). \n\n## _4.2. RQ1: how do various literacies contribute to the development of AI literacy?_ \n\nAI is an interdisciplinary field encompassing data, mathematical, scientific, media, and computational literacy (Casal-Otero et al., 2023; Chiu, 2024; Long & Magerko, 2020). To answer this question, we examined articles from three perspectives: technical, practical, and emancipatory interests. Each interest had three levels: understand, apply, and create. \n\n## _4.2.1. Technical interest_ \n\nThis technical interest focuses on content knowledge of AI and concerns the student’s cognitive development of AI knowledge, see Table 4. \n\n- Understand: Students are required to comprehend the nature of AI. They are expected to build basic interdisciplinary foundational knowledge and skills to gradually deepen their understanding of data and algorithms, allowing them to integrate AI knowledge into their actions in society and daily life at the application level (UNESCO, 2024a). Recommended topics for this level cover the history and foundations of AI and machine learning, with a focus on big data, cloud computing, deep learning, neural networks, and unsupervised and supervised learning (Chiu et al., 2022; Zhang et al., 2024; Zhao et al., 2022). Digital, data, and media literacy are all related literacies. This finding is consistent with the Artificial Intelligence for K-12 initiative’s third Big Idea, Learning, which states that computers can learn from data. For example, in order to build a technical and conceptual grasp of the principles of AI solutions, students must be data literate (Ayanwale et al., 2024; Chiu, 2021; Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024; Eguchi et al., 2021; Gronseth et al., 2025; Huang, 2021; Kong et al., 2024; Lin, Wang, et al., 2023; Markus et al., 2024; Mertala et al., 2022; Zhang et al., 2023). Digital literacy is linked to AI literacy when students understand the function of AI (e.g., big data and trained data) in personalized and predictive marketing online (Zhang et al., 2023). Students studied machine learning ideas and principles by executing mathematical operations and training classifiers or models created using machine learning libraries or Google Teachable Machine (Chiu, 2021; Su & Yang, 2024). Students’ ability to prepare data for modeling and evaluating machine learning models is linked to data literacy and mathematical literacy (Hersh et al., 2023; Yang, 2022). \n\n- Apply: Students are expected to apply fundamental AI knowledge to AI processes such as 1) how AI sees through computer vision, face detection and recognition, and image classification; 2) how AI hears and speaks through speech and language processing, textto-speech techniques, and speech-to-text techniques; and 3) how AI reads and writes through natural language processing (Chiu, 2024; Chiu et al., 2022). The contents are consistent with perception in the Five Big Ideas (Touretzky et al., 2023), demonstrating that computers may perceive the external environment using sensors. In the selected articles (Al-Abdullatif & Alsubaie, 2024; Dai et al., 2024; Mertala et al., 2022; Yang et al., 2024), students indicated how numerical sense influences the process of extracting and mapping visual elements in numbers, bridging the gap between mathematical literacy, data literacy, and AI literacy (Dai et al., 2024). Students understood how AI can be integrated with sensor technologies such as cameras to gather information from their \n\n6 \n\n**Table 2** \n\nThe selected 58 articles in this study. \n\n|ID|Reference|Journal|Method|Educational<br>Level|Subject Domain|Pedagogical Design|Habermas’s Cognitiv|e Interests||\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n||||||||Technical Interest|Practical Interest|Emancipatory Interest|\n|1|Chiu (2021)|TechTrends|Qualitative|K-12<br>education|Computer<br>Science|Problem-based<br>learning|History and AI<br>foundations;<br>machine learning;<br>see; hear and speak;|AI reasoning; Human and<br>AI communications|AI for social good; Security<br>and privacy; Bias and<br>fairness; Ethical AI<br>citizenship with a human-<br>centered mindset|\n|2|Williams et al. (2023)|International Journal<br>of Artificial<br>Intelligence in<br>Education|Quantitative|K-12<br>education|Computer<br>Science|Project-based<br>learning|History and AI<br>foundations; See|AI robotics|AI for social good: Ethical<br>AI citizenship with a<br>human-centered mindset