onments.|(Ayanwale et al., 2024;Chiu,<br>2021;Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024;<br>Eguchi et al., 2021;Gronseth<br>et al., 2025;Huang, 2021;<br>Kong et al., 2024;Lin et al.,<br>2021,2023;Mertala et al.,<br>2022;Ng, Xinyu, et al., 2024;<br>Sanusi et al., 2022;Su&Yang,<br>2024;Tenberga&Daniela,<br>2024;Wu et al., 2024;Zhang<br>et al. 2023)|16|\n||Machine<br>learning|Google Teachable<br>Machine and other<br>machine learning tools,<br>collecting data|Data literacy,<br>mathematical<br>literacy|Perform mathematical operations<br>and train classifiers or models<br>using machine learning libraries<br>acquainted with neural networks;<br>Train a machine-learning model<br>to classify different recycling<br>garbage images; Data<br>preparation, exploration,<br>modeling, and evaluation using<br>AI/ML tools.|,<br>(Chiu, 2021;Dai et al., 2024;<br>Hersh et al., 2023;Hsu&<br>Chen, 2024;Huang, 2021;<br>Kong et al., 2023;Mertala<br>et al., 2022;Ng, Xinyu, et al.,<br>2024;Su&Yang, 2024;<br>Tenberga&Daniela, 2024;<br>Vargas et al., 2020;Yang et al.,<br>2024)|12|\n|Apply|See|Computer vision, face<br>detection, face<br>recognition, image<br>classification|Data literacy,<br>mathematical<br>literacy|Explain how the process of<br>extracting and mapping visual<br>features in numbers is affected by<br>a numerical sense.|(Chiu, 2021;Chiu, Chen, et al.,<br>2024;Chiu et al., 2024,2024;<br>Dai et al., 2024;Hsu&Chen,<br>2024;Huang, 2021;Lin,<br>Wang, et al., 2023;Yang et al.,<br>2024)|8|\n||Hear and<br>speak.|speech and language<br>processing, Text-to-<br>speech, speech-to-text|Data literacy,<br>language literacy|Give voice commands to control<br>Pupbo (AI robot). Students<br>learned how to phrase commands<br>appropriately to get the desired<br>responses or actions through<br>iterative interactions.|(Chiu, 2021;Salhab, 2024;<br>Yang et al., 2024)|3|\n||Read and<br>write|Natural language<br>processing|Data literacy,<br>language literacy|Observe how AI processes and<br>predicts words and generates<br>outputs using identical prompts.|(Al-Abdullatif&Alsubaie,<br>2024;Salhab, 2024)|2|\n|||||Understand the processes that<br>make ChatGPT function, such as<br>how it generates answers based<br>on large datasets.|||\n|Create|AI robotics|Examine various AI<br>robots (e.g., Pubo,<br>Clearbot, Qobo, and<br>Coji).<br>Analyze operational<br>mechanisms and<br>specific applications.|Scientific literacy,<br>computational<br>literacy|<br>Storytelling about AI robots<br>playing, chatting, and teaching;<br>Program stories and tasks for AI<br>robots to perform, displaying<br>emojis (emotions), laughing, and<br>dancing.|(Hsu&Chen, 2024;<br>Kewalramani et al., 2021;Su<br>&Yang, 2024;Williams et al.,<br>2023;Yang et al., 2024)|5|\n\n\n\n*Remark: One or more topics and literacies could be identified in one paper. \n\nprocess of word prediction and output production using identical prompts (Al-Abdullatif & Alsubaie, 2024), students gained conceptual knowledge of how AI chatbots, such as ChatGPT, produce answers based on big datasets. Data literacy enables people to strategically grasp how AI gets reliable results from vast datasets. \n\n- Create: Students are expected to investigate and develop various AI applications by working with educational robots as social robots that interact through expressions and movements, autonomous robots that navigate environments, and programmable robots designed for coding and problem-solving while studying their operating principles and specific uses. Through controlling AIenabled robots in a game-based learning environment, where robots perform tasks like displaying emojis, laughing, and dancing, students gain a conceptual understanding of AI, alongside scientific literacy and computational literacy (Hsu & Chen, 2024; Kewalramani et al., 2021; Su & Yang, 2024; Williams et al., 2023). This hands-on approach enhances their ability to apply AI concepts practically and develop critical thinking and technical skills essential for the modern digital world. \n\n## _4.2.2. Practical interest_ \n\nThis practical interest focuses on communication and collaboration with AI. It combines affective, behavioral, and metacognitive \n\n13 \n\n_Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\n_Y.M.E. Heung et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computers & Education_ \n\nelements to learn about how we interact socially with other humans and computers to foster mutual understanding and cooperation (Habermas, 1978; Kong et al., 2024; Laupichler et al., 2022; Long & Magerko, 2020), see Table 5. \n\n- Understand: The findings revealed that the topics students learned are related to AI reasoning, specifically scientific reasoning (i.e., rule-based, skill-based, and knowledge-based), which is consistent with UNESCO’s AI competency framework and the Five Big Ideas that AI agents support reasoning and representation (Chiu, 2024; Touretzky et al., 2023; UNESCO, 2024a). For example, when students used AI-enabled virtual reality simulations in a health science course, they could interpret personalized feedback via self-paced conversational learning (Liaw et al., 2023). Another example is that students might use AI algorithms to examine real-time typhoon data and anticipate future actions, improving disaster preparedness in the Earth science course (Choi, 2025). They could critically evaluate the model’s accuracy and usefulness by comparing the AI-generated predicted pathways to actual data using scientific methodologies. These two examples show how students can use scientific and data literacy to make educated decisions after processing and assessing medical and scientific data powered by AI (Lee et al., 2024). \n\n- Apply: Students are required to turn theoretical knowledge into practical skills by encouraging collaboration, emotional engagement, and confidence while addressing real-world technological and societal concerns (UNESCO, 2024a). The topic at this level is human-machine communications, human-AI interfaces, and human-AI interaction, such as prompt engineering, because this \n\n**Table 5** \n\nContribution of literacies to the practical interest of AI literacy. \n\n|Progression|AI Topics|Descriptions<br>i|Relevant Literacies|Interdisciplinary Examples|Sample Studies|Count|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Understand|AI reasoning|Scientific reasoning<br>(rule-based, skill-<br>based, knowledge-<br>based)|Data literacy,<br>scientific literacy|Apply AI-enabled virtual<br>reality simulation in health<br>science;<br>Interpret personalized<br>feedback, conversational<br>learning, and self-paced<br>exploration;<br>Process and evaluate medical<br>data driven by AI;<br>Analyze real-time typhoon<br>data to predict future<br>movements and compare<br>predicted paths|(Chiu, 2021;Choi, 2025;<br>Connolly et al., 2023;<br>Fazlollahi et al., 2023;<br>Kajiwara&Kawabata, 2024;<br>Lee et al., 2024;Liaw et al.,<br>2023;Salhab, 2024;Yang<br>et al., 2024;Çalıs¸kan et al.,<br>2022)|10|\n|Apply|Human and AI<br>communications|Human and AI<br>interfaces, human<br>and AI interaction,<br>prompt<br>engineering, and<br>large language<br>models<br>i|Data literacy,<br>mathematical<br>literacy, language<br>literacy|.<br>Analyze human-machine<br>conversation (compare the<br>similarities and differences<br>between human intelligence<br>and AI to grasp AI’s unique<br>attributes and processes and<br>appreciate the human-AI<br>relationship).<br>Digital information analysis<br>and synthesis;<br>Critical appraisal of AI<br>literature, clinical decision-<br>making aided by AI, and<br>patient communication<br>regarding AI tools and AI<br>model use in diagnostics;|(Dai et al., 2024;Huang, 2021;<br>Knoth et al., 2024;Markus<br>et al., 2024;Naamati-Schneider<br>&Alt, 2024;Ng, Xinyu, et al.,<br>2024;Sanusi et al., 2022;<br>Simonsmeier et al., 2022;<br>Singla et al., 2024;Swartz<br>et al., 2025;Villarino, 2025;<br>Wu et al., 2024;Yang, 2022)|13|\n|Create|Think and create|Artificial general<br>intelligence,<br>Generative AI|Digital literacy,<br>media literacy|Work with AI to generate<br>creative and innovative<br>artifacts of their identities;<br>Integrative Analysis and<br>multidimensional<br>presentation;<br>Craft AI artifacts.<br>i|(Naamati-Schneider, 2024;<br>Naamati-Schneider&Alt,<br>2024;Ng, Xinyu, et al., 2024;<br>Relmasira et al., 2023;Sanusi<br>et al., 2022;Swartz et al., 2025;<br>Tzirides et al., 2024;Yim,<br>2024;Yue et al., 2025;Zhong<br>&Liu, 2025)|10|\n||Problem-solving<br>with AI Tools|Apply AI<br>knowledge and<br>technologies to<br>solve problems|Digital literacy,<br>scientific literacy,<br>data literacy,<br>mathematical<br>literacy,<br>computational<br>literacy|Make a fishing net to collect<br>trash from the“ocean.”<br>Solve real-world biomedical<br>and healthcare problems by<br>improving clinical decision-<br>making and research.|(Ayanwale et al., 2024;Chiu,<br>Chen, et al., 2024;Connolly<br>et al., 2023;Hersh et al., 2023;<br>Lin et al., 2021;Lin, Wang,<br>et al., 2023;Markus et al.,<br>2024;Salhub, 2024;Singla<br>et al., 2024;Vargas et al., 2020;<br>Wu et al., 2024;Yang, 2022;<br>Yang et al., 2024;Yim, 2024;<br>Yue et al., 2025;Çalıs¸kan et al.,<br>2022)|16|\n\n\n\n*Remark: One or more topics and literacies could be identified in one paper. \n\n14 \n\n_Y.M.E. Heung et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\npractical interest values interaction and communicative action (Mezirow, 1981; Touretzky et al., 2023). As a result, students used data literacy and language literacy to examine similarities and contrasts in talks between humans and AI (Dai et al., 2024; Knoth et al., 2024). After reviewing the responses, they used mathematical literacy to undertake digital statistical analyses and synthesize results, thereby improving human-AI communication techniques (Naamati-Schneider & Alt, 2024). For example, in a medical class, students used data literacy to improve patient communication and optimize diagnostic processes through effective human-AI interaction in clinical decision-making (Connolly et al., 2023; Fazlollahi et al., 2023; Çalıs¸kan et al., 2022). This holistic approach promotes a better knowledge of AI capabilities and human communication nuances (Singla et al., 2024). \n\n- Create: Students are expected to become responsible AI co-creators. Related subjects interest students in AI innovation, encouraging them to create new AI tools using open-source and/or configurable datasets (UNESCO, 2024a). The subjects covered include problem-solving with AI tools and “think and create” (i.e., developing solutions and reasoning). Students in these areas could use AI knowledge and technologies to solve predetermined issues (problem-solving using AI tools) and collaborate with generative AI (“think and create”). These solutions and artifacts should benefit both humans and society, necessitating various literacy skills (UNESCO, 2024a). Not all students are capable of coding as developers; most students engage with but do not construct AI apps in their daily lives. A high emphasis on coding activities may cause learning hurdles for students who lack a solid foundation in mathematical and computational skills (Zhong & Liu, 2025). \n\n**Table 6** \n\nContribution of literacies to emancipatory interest of AI literacy. \n\n|Progression|AI Topics|Descriptions|Relevant<br>Literacies|Interdisciplinary Examples|Sample Studies|Count|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Understand|AI for social<br>good|Understanding how AI<br>addresses real-world<br>issues and implications<br>for future workforce|Algorithmic<br>literacy, digital<br>literacy|Discuss the impacts of AI tools<br>and algorithmic implications<br>Evaluate the strengths and<br>weaknesses of AI tools, fostering<br>an analytical mindset.<br>Case studies to promote social<br>good illustrate transformative<br>effects on the future of work|(Çaliskan et al., 2022;Chiu,<br>2021;Chiu, Chen, et al.,<br>2024;Hersh et al., 2023;<br>Kajiwara&Kawabata, 2024;<br>Mertala et al., 2022;Lin,<br>Wang, et al., 2023;Ng,<br>Xinyu, et al., 2024;<br>Relmasira et al., 2023;<br>Tenberga&Daniela, 2024;<br>Tzirides et al., 2024;<br>Williams et al., 2023;Yang<br>et al., 2024;Yue et al., 2025;<br>Zhang et al., 2023)|15|\n||Security and<br>privacy|Security and privacy|Media literacy,<br>algorithmic<br>literacy|Discuss privacy and data-sharing<br>regulatory issues;<br>Critically evaluate concerns and<br>limitations behind AI<br>technologies through simulation<br>games.<br>Evaluate the findings of medical<br>data analysis|(DiPaola et al., 2024;Eguchi<br>et al., 2021;Hersh et al.,<br>2023;Kajiwara&Kawabata,<br>2024;Singla et al., 2024)||\n||Bias and<br>fairness|human rights,<br>autonomy, and<br>discrimination|Algorithmic<br>literacy,<br>mathematical<br>literacy|<br>Examine the roles of humans and<br>AI from the standpoint of<br>autonomy and responsibility.<br>Scenario-based discussion on<br>fairness and justice;<br>Exhibit biases that stem from<br>blind spots in the training data;<br>Propose bias mitigation<br>strategies|(Chiu, 2021;Chiu, Chen,<br>et al., 2024;DiPaola et al.,<br>2024;Duan&Zhao, 2024;<br>Gronseth et al., 2025;<br>Kajiwara&Kawabata, 2024;<br>Kong et al., 2024;Salhub,<br>2024;Singla et al., 2024;Su<br>&Yang, 2024;Tenberga&<br>Daniela, 2024;Yang et al.,<br>2024;Zhang et al., 2023)|13|\n|Apply|Ethical AI<br>citizenship with<br>a human-<br>centered<br>mindset|Ethical principles and<br>responsible behavior in<br>AI usage.|Algorithmic<br>literacy, media<br>literacy, digital<br>literacy|Make arguments for or against<br>aspects raised in the dilemmas;<br>Evaluate how educated citizens<br>apply AI in everyday life,<br>evaluate their perceptions of and<br>feelings about AI, and advance<br>their AI knowledge and skill|<br>(Ali et al., 2021;Ayanwale<br>et al., 2024;Choi, 2025;<br>DiPaola et al., 2024;Huang,<br>2021;James et al., 2024;<br>Kong et al., 2023;Markus<br>et al., 2024;Ng, Xinyu,<br>et al., 2024;Salhub, 2024;<br>Tenberga&Daniela, 2024;<br>Usher&Barak, 2024;<br>Villarino, 2025;Williams<br>et al., 2023;Yim, 2024)|15|\n|Create|Design of AI<br>systems|Design and deploy AI<br>systems ethically|Civic literacy,<br>digital literacy,<br>scientific literacy|Undergo a bill-making<br>simulation in which students<br>apply their knowledge to design<br>a bill that addresses different<br>stakeholders|<br>(DiPaola et al., 2024;Huang<br>et al., 2024;Yue et al., 2025)|3|\n\n\n\n*Remark: One or more topics and literacies could be identified in one paper. \n\n15 \n\n_Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\n_Y.M.E. Heung et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computers & Education_ \n\nThe other topic, “think and create”, in this create level requires computational literacy, which allows students to solve issues using computational procedures and methods (Wing, 2006; Yim, 2024). For example, students could begin by using digital literacy to efficiently select AI technologies for projects (Salhab, 2024). They worked on collaborative problem-solving solutions using AI technology (Chiu et al., 2022; Moon et al., 2024; Vargas et al., 2020). Students could use data literacy to better evaluate, analyze, and communicate with AI-informed data (Hersh et al., 2023; Singla et al., 2024). They could then use computational and scientific literacy to create innovative solutions to some domain-specific issues, such as supporting sustainable development by inventing an AI-enhanced fishing tool to clean the ocean (Huang, 2021; Wu et al., 2024; Yang et al., 2024). Above all, in “think and create”, students used digital, computational, and scientific literacy to collaborate with generative AI to develop creative and inventive solutions. \n\n## _4.2.3. Emancipatory interest_ \n\nThe emancipatory interest in AI literacy development emphasizes critical engagement with the ethical and sociocultural implications of AI, encouraging learners to critically examine who benefits from, who is disadvantaged by, and what societal values are reinforced or challenged by AI systems (Ewert, 1991; Kong et al., 2024; Long & Magerko, 2020; Ng, Xinyu, et al., 2024; Touretzky et al., 2023). It fosters students’ reflective awareness of their own learning processes and the broader societal impact of AI. Table 6 shows the results. \n\n- Understand: Students should think critically about three topics: AI for social good, security and privacy, and bias and fairness. In the first topic—AI for social good—teachers guided students to critically discuss the impacts of AI tools and systems in subject-domain industries, reflect on algorithmic implications, and evaluate the strengths and weaknesses of AI tools in an analytical mindset (Ayanwale et al., 2024; Hersh et al., 2023; Ng, Xinyu, et al., 2024; Relmasira et al., 2023; Salhub, 2024; Tzirides et al., 2024; Williams et al., 2023; Çalıs¸kan et al., 2022). Through case studies, the teacher engaged students in thinking about how AI can impact the future workforce and their ability to adapt to new opportunities. Students used algorithmic literacy to critically analyze how AI algorithms are used to solve real-world problems (Long & Magerko, 2020; OECD, 2021). The second topic is security and privacy (Borenstein & Howard, 2021; Zhang et al., 2023). In terms of security and privacy, students discussed privacy and data-sharing regulatory issues, critically evaluated concerns and limitations of AI technologies using simulation games, and evaluated medical data analysis findings (DiPaola et al., 2024; Eguchi et al., 2021; Hersh et al., 2023; Singla et al., 2024). Learning tasks include assessing how existing legislation may fail to protect personal information in an age of ubiquitous data collecting, potentially leading to misuse and a loss of public trust (Singla et al., 2024). For example, in a medical data study, students evaluated concerns like data ownership, consent, and the potential for unequal access to healthcare guided by AI, emphasizing how these aspects may erode trust and faith in medical systems (Hersh et al., 2023). The final topic is bias and fairness, which covers human rights, autonomy, and discrimination. Students in the selected articles investigated the roles of humans and AI in terms of autonomy and accountability (DiPaola et al., 2024; Kajiwara & Kawabata, 2024). They took part in scenario-based discussions about fairness and justice, led by their teachers (Kong et al., 2023; Yim, 2024). Students presented methods for minimizing AI biases resulting from blind areas in training data (Singla et al., 2024). The approach described above combines several literacies, such as data literacy and algorithmic literacy, to create a more in-depth understanding of AI’s ethical implications. Students improve their data literacy skills by studying biases and fairness through the lenses of human rights, autonomy, and discrimination as they analyze and understand information on AI systems. Simultaneously, engaging in scenario-based discussions promotes algorithmic literacy by teaching students to examine how algorithms can represent societal biases. This multifaceted learning experience provides students with the critical thinking skills required to detect and address ethical concerns offered by AI, eventually encouraging responsible and informed decision-making in the digital age. \n\n- Apply: Topics promote ethical AI citizenship to ensure that ethical standards and responsible behavior are followed when using AI (Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024). The topics help students build an ethical attitude, allowing them to evaluate moral and ethical difficulties (James et al., 2024; Kim & Kwon, 2023; Ng, Xinyu, et al., 2024; Usher & Barak, 2024; Zhang et al., 2024). Students used media literacy to conceptualize socio-technical systems, deepfakes, and other sources of disinformation in the online media environment before investigating their implications for spreading misinformation and potential policies to mitigate their harm (Ali et al., 2021; Williams et al., 2023). \n\n- Create: Topics aim to educate students to be ethical AI creators or developers, strengthening their intellectual engagement with the social duties expected of AI citizens (UNESCO, 2024a). For example, by creating a legal bill that satisfies the needs of multiple stakeholders, students actively participate in the democratic process, establishing a sense of agency and responsibility. This experience helps them to express their values and advocate for the needs of many communities, raising critical awareness and pushing them to think of inventive solutions to societal problems. Design-based learning fostered students’ views as designers, users, and evaluators, encouraging them to consider the opportunities and challenges of developing AI systems and addressing societal ethical problems (Yue et al., 2025). Furthermore, students constructed art-based artifacts to portray AI as non-human stakeholders, thinking about the environmental implications and ethical obligations of AI development (Yim, 2024). They used scientific literacy to guide their decisions, attempting to strike a balance between societal and environmental concerns (Yang, 2022). \n\n_4.2.4. Summary of literacies for AI literacy using Habermas’s cognitive interests_ \n\nTable 7 shows the summary of the findings. In this summary, we used frequency counts to discuss causal relationships among various literacies. This approach suggests observational associations rather than hierarchical conclusions. The high frequency suggests a stronger observational association between a literacy and broader AI literacy, in term of numbers. Understanding these observational \n\n16 \n\n**Table 7** \n\nResults for various literacies in each interest. \n\n|Topic|Paper Related|Data|Digital|Mathematical|Algorithmic|Scientific|Computational|Media|Language<br>literacy|Civic<br>literacy|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Technical|||||||||||\n|History and AI<br>foundations|Ayanwale et al., 2024;Chiu, 2021;Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024;Eguchi<br>et al., 2021;Gronseth et al., 2025;Huang, 2021;Kong et al., 2024;<br>Lin et al., 2021;Lin, Wang, et al., 2023;Mertala et al., 2022;Ng,<br>Xinyu, et al., 2024;Sanusi et al., 2022;Su&Yang, 2024;Tenberga<br>&Daniela, 2024;Wu et al., 2024;Zhang et al., 2023|16|16|0|0|0|0|0|0|0|\n|Machine learning|<br>Chiu, 2021;Dai et al., 2024;Hsu&Chen, 2024;Huang, 2021;<br>Hersh et al., 2023;Kong et al., 2023;Mertala et al., 2022;Ng,<br>Xinyu, et al., 2024;Su&Yang, 2024;Tenberga&Daniela, 2024;<br>Vargas et al., 2020;Yang et al., 2024|12|0|12|0|0|0|0|0|0|\n|See|<br>Chiu, 2021;Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024;Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024;Hsu&<br>Chen, 2024;Huang, 2021;Dai et al., 2024;Lin, Wang, et al., 2023;<br>Yang et al., 2024|8|0|8|0|0|0|0|0|0|\n|Hear and speak|Chiu, 2021;Salhab, 2024;Yang et al., 2024|3|0|0|0|0|0|0|3|0|\n|Read and write|<br>Al-Abdullatif&Alsubaie, 2024;Huang, 2021|2|0|0|0|0|0|0|2|0|\n|AI robotics|Hsu&Chen, 2024;Kewalramani et al., 2021;Su&Yang, 2024;<br>Williams et al., 2023;Yang et al., 2024|0|0|0|0|5|5|0|0|0|\n|Subtotal<br>Practical||41|16|20|0|5|0|5|5|0|\n|AI reasoning|Çalıs¸kan et al., 2022;Chiu, 2021;Choi, 2025;Connolly et al., 2023;<br>Fazlollahi et al., 2023;Kajiwara&Kawabata, 2024;Lee et al., 2024;<br>|10|0|0|0|10|0|0|0|0|\n||Liaw et al., 2023;Salhab, 2024;Yang et al., 2024)||||||||||\n|Human and AI<br>communications|Dai et al., 2024;Huang, 2021;Knoth et al., 2024;Markus et al.,<br>2024;Naamati-Schneider&Alt, 2024;Ng, Xinyu, et al., 2024;<br>Sanusi et al., 2022;Simonsmeier et al., 2022;Singla et al., 2024;<br>Swartz et al., 2025;Wu et al., 2024;Villarino, 2025;Yang, 2022|13|0|13|0|0|0|0|13|0|\n|Think and create|Naamati-Schneider, 2024;Naamati-Schneider&Alt, 2024;Ng,<br>Xinyu, et al., 2024;Relmasira et al., 2023;Sanusi et al., 2022;<br>Swartz et al., 2025;Tzirides et al., 2024;Yim, 2024;Yue et al.,<br>2025;Zhong&Liu, 2025|0|10|0|0|0|0|10|0|0|\n|Problem-solving with AI<br>Tools|Ayanwale et al., 2024;Çalıs¸kan et al., 2022;Chiu, Chen, et al.,<br>2024;Connolly et al., 2023;Hersh et al., 2023;Lin et al., 2021;Lin,<br>Wang, et al., 2023;Markus et al., 2024;Salhub, 2024;Singla et al.,<br>2024;Wu et al., 2024;Vargas et al., 2020;Yang, 2022;Yang et al.,<br>2024;Yim, 2024;Yue et al., 2025|16|16|16|0|16|16|0|0|0|\n|Subtotal<br>Emancipatory||39|26|29|0|26|10|16|13|0|\n|AI for social good|Çaliskan et al., 2022;Chiu, 2021;Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024;Hersh<br>et al., 2023;Kajiwara&Kawabata, 2024;Mertala et al., 2022;Lin,<br>Wang, et al., 2023;Ng, Xinyu, et al., 2024;Relmasira et al., 2023;<br>Tenberga&Daniela, 2024;Tzirides et al., 2024;Williams et al.,<br>2023;Yang et al., 2024;Yue et al., 2025;Zhang et al., 2023|0|15|0|15|0|0|0|0|0|\n|Security and privacy|<br>DiPaola et al., 2024;Eguchi et al., 2021;Kajiwara&Kawabata,<br>2024;Hersh et al., 2023;Singla et al., 2024|5|0|0|5|0|0|5|0<br>(_continued_|0<br>_on next page_)|\n\n\n\n**Table 7** ( _continued_ ) \n\n|Topic|Paper Related|Data|Digital|Mathematical|Algorithmic|Scientific|Computational|Media|Language<br>literacy|Civic<br>literacy|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Bias and fairness|Chiu, 2021;Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024;DiPaola et al., 2024;Duan&<br>Zhao, 2024;Gronseth et al., 2025;Kajiwara&Kawabata, 2024;<br>Kong et al., 2024;Salhub, 2024;Singla et al., 2024;Su&Yang,<br>2024;Tenberga&Daniela, 2024;Yang et al., 2024;Zhang et al.,<br>2023|0|0|13|13|0|0|0|0|0|\n|Ethical AI citizenship<br>with a human-<br>centered mindset<br>18|Ali et al., 2021;Ayanwale et al., 2024;Choi, 2025;DiPaola et al.,<br>2024;Huang, 2021;James et al., 2024;Kong et al., 2023;Markus<br>et al., 2024;Ng, Xinyu, et al., 2024;Salhub, 2024;Tenberga&<br>Daniela, 2024;Usher&Barak, 2024;Williams et al., 2023;<br>Villarino, 2025;Yim, 2024|0|15|0|15|0|0|15|0|0|\n|Design of AI systems|<br>(DiPaola et al., 2024;Huang et al., 2024;Yue et al., 2025)|0|3|0|0|3|0|0|0|3|\n||Subtotal|5|33|13|48|3|20|0|0|3|\n||total|85|75|62|48|34|21|30|18|3|\n\n\n\n*Remark: One or more topics and literacies could be identified in one paper. \n\n_Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\n_Y.M.E. Heung et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computers & Education_ \n\nrelationships provides researchers, policymakers, and educators with vital ideas for establishing an interdisciplinary AI literacy program and AI education. \n\nOur findings show that data literacy (n = 85), digital literacy (n = 75), and mathematical literacy (n = 62) are the most closely related to AI literacy. This finding is consistent with Chiu’s (2024) study of the cognitive links between various literacies and AI literacy. First, data literacy focused on the relationship between big data and AI, emphasizing the importance of improving data literacy in AI education (Yang et al., 2025). It stresses the technical skills required to evaluate and understand data. It also emphasizes the value of data literacy in real-world applications, which improves decision-making processes. Second, because AI literacy is a subset of digital literacy, it is inextricably tied to digital literacy, which encompasses the ability to successfully use and evaluate digital tools, resources, and services (Falloon, 2020; Long & Magerko, 2020). Digital literacy pervades all aspects of Habermas’ cognitive interests, emphasizing its importance in technical knowledge, efficient communication, and a strong link to critical thinking skills and understanding of digital ethics. Mathematical literacy is also present in all three interests, demonstrating the significance of improving students’ fundamental mathematical knowledge and reasoning skills in order to understand AI. A solid conceptual understanding of mathematical topics such as statistics, algorithms, and probability is essential for analyzing data and making sound judgments based on AI outputs (Chiu, 2024; Kong et al., 2024; Long & Magerko, 2020). This literacy enables students to interact with AI devices while critically analyzing the mathematical ideas that underpin these systems. Algorithmic literacy is defined as being aware of and knowledgeable about algorithm use, being able to critically examine algorithms and exercise agency with trust and faith in algorithms, and appreciating algorithms. Algorithmic literacy in the emancipatory interest (n = 48) shows a strong link to understanding the ethical implications of algorithms. Scientific literacy (n = 34) refers to basic scientific knowledge that is useful for applying scientific procedures to pose, find, or determine answers using AI technology (Park et al., 2023). Media literacy (n = 30) serves both practical and emancipatory purposes, emphasizing its significance in human-to-human and human-to-AI communication. It focuses on skills such as interpreting AI-generated data, detecting biases in discussions and content, and recognizing the dangers of discrimination and misinformation. This highlights the importance of self-reflective mindsets and critical thinking skills with media (Ghani et al., 2025). Language literacy (n = 18) and computational literacy (n = 21) are also important for AI literacy, but to a lesser degree. Language literacy is fundamental to AI literacy, particularly in comprehending how AI analyzes and generates words. Effective communication skills improve the ability to connect with AI systems, especially natural language processing (Tang, 2024). Lin, Zhou, et al. (2023) discovered a positive relationship between computational literacy and AI literacy. Students with higher computational literacy are better able to solve issues using computational steps and methods (Kim & Kwon, 2023; Wing, 2006; Yim, 2024). Finally, civic literacy (n = 3) is the least relevant of the other literacies. The emphasis on critical interaction is consistent with the need for ethical issues in AI. Understanding civic rights and responsibilities can help with talks concerning the societal implications of AI technologies (DiPaola et al., 2024). However, this is not a prerequisite for AI literacy; rather, it supplements AI literacy in evaluating the broader ramifications of AI in society. \n\n## _4.3. RQ2: what pedagogical design considerations support AI literary development?_ \n\nIn this review, we identified five pedagogical approaches for different education levels to ensure that the suggested instructional approaches for developing AI literacy are age-appropriate: project-based learning/problem-based learning, game-based learning, casebased learning, reflective learning, and cultural learning, see Table 8. Project-based or problem-based learning (PBL) is widely used in K-12 and higher education, whereas game-based learning is the preferred strategy in early childhood education. Cultural and casebased learning can be viewed as promising but emergent pedagogical designs requiring further research, rather than a wellestablished approach due to their relatively few frequency counts. \n\n## _4.3.1. Project-based/problem-based learning_ \n\nThe majority of studies (n = 33) found that active learning approaches, such as project-based learning or problem-based learning, improve students’ confidence and readiness to use AI, motivation to learn AI, and AI relevance (Kong et al., 2023, 2024; Vargas et al., 2020). PBL allows students to use a variety of literacies to define problems, devise solutions, and formulate them in a real-world setting, encouraging research, data analysis, effective cooperation, and problem-solving (Huang, 2021; Huang et al., 2024). Students consolidated their learning through collaborative, hands-on tasks that required them to communicate their approaches, reflect on their products, and evaluate their solutions. They not only understood core AI concepts, but they also developed their own AI products to help others (AI for social good). Project-based learning, or problem-based learning, by including ethics into the curriculum, \n\n**Table 8** \n\nPedagogical approaches for different education levels. \n\n||Early Childhood Education|K-12 Education|Higher<br>Education|Total|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Project-Based/Problem-based Learning|2|19|12|33|\n|Game-based Learning<br>l|3|7|6|16|\n|Reflective Learning|0|4|8|12|\n|l<br>Case-based Learning|0|1|7|8|\n|Cultural Learning|1|5|3|9|\n\n\n\n*Remark: One or more pedagogies could be identified in one paper. \n\n19 \n\n_Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\nhelps students improve their AI literacy over time, independent of their programming experience. This learning approach promotes a critical perspective on AI, giving students the ability to thoughtfully reinvent the AI systems they encounter on a daily basis (Kong et al., 2023, 2024). \n\n## _4.3.2. Game-based learning_ \n\nGame-based learning (n = 16) is an accessible way to introduce AI knowledge to beginners with low AI experience, which helps to maintain motivation (Long & Magerko, 2020). Interactive game components encourage students to apply their AI knowledge in a compelling manner, hence lowering AI learning anxiety (Hsu & Chen, 2024). This learning is influenced by experiential learning, which consists of four stages: concrete experience, reflective observation, abstract conceptualization, and active experimentation (Kolb, 1984). In Hsu and Chen’s (2024) study, students began by gaining specific experiences at the start of each class, when the teacher presented concepts or examples linked to computational literacy at the concrete experience stage. They next moved on to the reflective obse rvation phase, in which the teacher directed the students in reflecting on their experiences. They then moved on to the abstract conceptualization stage, where the knowledge topic was conveyed via digital teaching methods such as videos to guide students through associated reflections. Finally, they moved on to the active experimentation stage, which involves providing students with the teaching platform and course tasks so that they can operate and complete them autonomously. According to empirical studies, this strategy promotes positive emotions, motivation, creativity, engagement, and literacy development (Al-Abdullatif & Alsubaie, 2024; Gronseth et al., 2025; Hsu & Chen, 2024; Liaw et al., 2023; Ng, Xinyu, et al., 2024; Wu et al., 2024). In early childhood education, game-based learning is combined with embodied learning, role-playing, and AI robotics toys, allowing young students to safely manipulate AI toys, engage in role-playing activities, and interact with peers under embodied instruction (Henry et al., 2021; Kewalramani et al., 2021; Su & Yang, 2024; Yang, 2022; Yang et al., 2024). This learning approach is consistent with Vygotsky’s Social Development Theory, which emphasizes the importance of social contact in learning and suggests that children learn more efficiently when they engage with their peers. In parallel, Piaget (1978) suggests that children progress through cognitive stages, with play being essential during the concrete operational stage (ages 7–11). Overall, game-based learning offers hands-on experiences that help younger children develop important abilities like problem-solving and critical thinking. \n\n## _4.3.3. Reflective learning_ \n\nReflective learning (n = 12) effectively improves students’ basic concepts of ethical issues but not higher-order thinking of ethical issues (Takahara & Kajiwara, 2013). This learning is integrated into the AI literacy programs for K-12 and higher education, emphasizing self-reflective mindsets and responsible and ethical citizens (Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024; Kong et al., 2024; Naamati-Schneider, 2024; Usher & Barak, 2024). Students complete reflective journals and writing assignments to contemplate their ethical knowledge and awareness of AI (Relmasira et al., 2023). This development of a reflective mindset aligns with the formal operational stage of Piaget’s cognitive theory (Piaget, 1978), which begins around age 11 and continues into adulthood. Individuals at this stage are capable of abstract thinking and hypothetical reasoning, allowing them to engage in discussions regarding AI’s ethical implications, assess complex problems, and investigate AI’s societal impacts. Encouraging students to reflect on their learning processes and the ethical dimensions of AI technologies could improve students’ critical thinking skills and various literacies essential for AI literacy development. \n\n## _4.3.4. Case-based learning_ \n\nFor K-12 and higher education, case-based learning (n = 8) was proven to be effective in enhancing students’ basic concepts of ethical issues but not in improving learners’ higher-order thinking of ethical issues (Takahara & Kajiwara, 2013). Studies suggested group debates and case studies from case-based learning are sophisticated pedagogical approaches to enhancing students’ ethical awareness (Kong et al., 2023; Lin et al., 2021). Students were encouraged to investigate ethical dilemmas found in real-world AI applications (Liaw et al., 2023; Lin, Wang, et al., 2023; Usher & Barak, 2024; Zhang et al., 2024). This approach helps ground abstract ethical concepts, making them more understandable and relevant to students. Recent research shows that case studies on AI applications can greatly enhance both AI literacy and ethical awareness among primary school students and those in higher education (Kong et al., 2023). \n\n## _4.3.5. Cultural learning_ \n\nNine studies have adopted culturally responsive pedagogy as an emancipatory practice to address diversity and inclusivity to strengthen learners’ sense of identity while promoting equity, inclusivity, and appreciation of diverse cultures (Chiu et al., 2022; Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024; Eguchi et al., 2021; Hossain et al., 2025; Li et al., 2021; Li & Manzari, 2025; Swartz et al., 2025; Villarino, 2025; Villegas, 1991). Incorporating a culturally responsive pedagogical approach adopts constructionist learning ideas to ensure cultural relevance, creating meaningful contexts for students from diverse cultural backgrounds (Eguchi et al., 2021). For example, the AI-ethics curriculum in Japan replaces YouTube with Yahoo, as Yahoo is more widely used in Japan for learning how AI algorithms make predictions for personalized needs (Eguchi et al., 2021). On the other hand, MTR’s KKbox chatbot is used as a local example for students to understand AI’s societal impact in Hong Kong (Chiu et al., 2022). Cultural learning considers human values and cultural contexts in AI education, recognizing that data can be biased based on how it is collected or selected. This approach enhances students’ ethical awareness by involving them in reflective, cross-cultural experiences highlighting biases and differences in cultural norms and language nuances (Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024; Sanusi et al., 2022). Forming cross-cultural virtual teams, for example, allows students to collaboratively explore how AI systems can perpetuate biases when trained on non-diverse datasets, as reflected in students’ emphasis on including diverse ages, genders, ethnicities, and cultures in AI training and testing data (Swartz et al., 2025; Zhang et al., 2023). \n\n20 \n\n_Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\n_Y.M.E. Heung et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computers & Education_ \n\nStudents develop a deeper ethical understanding of AI’s consequences by actively reflecting on real-world examples of algorithmic bias, such as facial recognition systems disproportionately misidentifying people of color, and discussing the societal impacts of such biases (Zhang et al., 2023). This culturally grounded pedagogy fosters critical consciousness by empowering students to identify and challenge biases in AI systems and advocate for more equitable technologies while aligning with culturally responsive teaching principles that value students’ backgrounds and create safe, inclusive learning environments for sharing and critically examining diverse perspectives. \n\n\n\n\n\n**Fig. 3.** Ai literacy related to various literacies and its pedagogy. \n\n21 \n\n_Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\n_Y.M.E. Heung et al._ \n\n## **5. Conclusion, limitations, and future directions** \n\nThis systematic literature review synthesizes 58 studies on AI literacy development across various educational contexts. We theorize AI literacy development using Habermas’s cognitive knowledge interests (technical, practical, and emancipatory) (Ewert, 1991; Habermas, 1978; McCarthy, 1978) and categorize the topics into three progression levels: understand, apply, and create (UNESCO, 2024a; 2024b). \n\nOur findings highlight the necessity of an interdisciplinary approach, as various literacies are essential for cultivating AI literacy. Fig. 3 visualizes the findings of RQ1 and RQ2, showing how AI literacy relates to various literacies and suggesting its pedagogy from a STEM interdisciplinary perspective. We used the frequency of each literacy that appeared in the reviewed article to propose a hierarchical structure. We hypothesize that higher frequency is more strongly related to AI literacy development, while lower frequency is less strongly related. This structure describes how the literacies are related to AI literacy. Data, digital, and mathematical literacy emerge as the top three interests closely connected to AI literacy. We acknowledge that this structure is not validated. We suggest future studies should use empirical studies to validate this structure. Our findings reveal that data, digital and mathematical literacies span both technical and practical dimensions, while scientific literacy features prominently in the practical dimension. Meanwhile, algorithmic, digital, and computational literacies predominantly cluster under the emancipatory interest, reflecting the focus on critical evaluation of societal impact of AI algorithms. These patterns highlight common pairings of competencies and pedagogy observed across studies, serving as a guide for curriculum design. \n\nRegarding pedagogical considerations, we propose five age-appropriate, culturally responsive teaching strategies, namely projectbased learning/problem-based learning, game-based learning, case-based learning, reflective learning, and cultural learning. Projectbased and problem-based learning are the most widely used approaches in K-12 and higher education, providing active learning opportunities that allow students to apply technical knowledge and skills in collaborative problem-solving environments (Kong et al., 2023, 2024; Vargas et al., 2020). For example, a secondary-school AI curriculum could use project-based learning to teach data collection and analysis alongside coding basics, thereby solidifying the technical groundwork while simultaneously training students to apply these skills in a problem-solving project context. By sequencing instructional activities from discrete technical exercises to integrative, real-world applications, students are guided toward higher-order cognitive engagement. Upon acquiring a conceptual understanding of AI, learners transition to project development—such as designing a game—where they articulate problems, engineer solutions, and implement them in situated, meaningful environments. For early childhood education, game-based learning is particularly effective, incorporating AI toys and role-play activities that encourage interaction with peers and machines (Kewalramani et al., 2021; Su & Yang, 2024; Yang, 2022; Yang et al., 2024), aligning with Piaget’s theory of cognitive development and Vygotsky’s social learning theories (Piaget, 1978; Vygotsky, 1967). To foster responsible and ethical AI citizenship, we identified case-based, reflective, and cultural learning as effective methods for enhancing students’ ethical awareness. Students can reflect on their learning experiences and consider sociocultural factors, such as age, gender, and race, when developing unbiased algorithms while also acknowledging the challenges posed by disinformation and bias (Ayanwale et al., 2024; Hossain et al., 2025; Salhab, 2024; Swartz et al., 2025; Zhang et al., 2023). For example, when designing a module on machine learning algorithms in K12 for higher education, educators could design case-based learning tasks through group debates and case studies for analyzing a real-world case of algorithmic bias and composing a reflective essay on how technical and practical decisions contributed to ethical implications. This pedagogical approach fosters connections among algorithmic, computational, mathematical and scientific literacies (Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024; Kong et al., 2024; Naamati-Schneider, 2024; Relmasira et al., 2023; Usher & Barak, 2024). It broadens considerations of fairness and societal impact, thereby encompassing the multidimensional nature of AI literacy. To promote cultural relevance and inclusivity, educators can contextualize examples, such as Japan’s Yahoo case or Hong Kong’s localized chatbot (Chiu et al., 2022; Eguchi et al., 2021). By inviting students to incorporate their own cultural perspectives into projects and classroom discourse, it cultivates cultural and civic literacies for developing a human-centered mindset of AI, empowering learners to recognize bias and advocate for equitable AI design (Chiu, Chen, et al., 2024; Sanusi et al., 2022). Collaborative group projects involving cross-cultural virtual teams on AI-related topics can further operationalize this approach, reinforcing the interdependence of practical collaboration and emancipatory awareness (Swartz et al., 2025; Zhang et al., 2023). \n\nThis study has five limitations. First, to maintain the quality of the studies included in our analysis, we focused on published research on AI literacy development from academic journals indexed in major academic databases such as the Web of Science and Scopus. While this approach ensured a high standard of published evidence, it may have excluded valuable regional publications and perspectives from the Global South, which are often underrepresented in these databases. Articles from other databases and grey literature (government reports, theses, dissertations, technical reports, and other unpublished works) may have been overlooked due to search string limitations. Future research could benefit from expanding the inclusion and exclusion criteria to incorporate a broader range of literature, thereby capturing relevant data from more diverse sources. Second, this review focuses on the pedagogical design considerations in STEM-related disciplines. STEM education emphasizes computational thinking, data analysis, and interdisciplinary learning for AI literacy development. As such, our findings primarily reflect literacies and pedagogies central to STEM fields. We acknowledge that some insights may transfer to non-STEM contexts. Therefore, we encourage future researchers to explore AI literacy development in non-STEM disciplines to address their distinctive challenges and affordances, enriching the interdisciplinary understanding of AI education. We also recommend that forthcoming studies examine AI literacy progression longitudinally across educational levels to generate deeper insights into how AI understanding and literacies develop over time and across disciplines. Third, the Western socio-philosophical origins of Habermas’ framework potentially limit its direct cross-cultural applicability in AI literacy. Future research is suggested to explicitly integrate it with established learning theories—linking practical interest to Vygotskian scaffolding and emancipatory interest to critical pedagogy—thus strengthening the theoretical foundation and mitigating cultural \n\n22 \n\n_Computers & Education 242 (2026) 105492_ \n\n_Y.M.E. Heung et al._ \n\nconstraints. Fourth, it is important to note that while frequency counts reveal prevalent themes, they cannot establish a pedagogical hierarchy or confirm that one competency is a causal prerequisite for another. The observational relationships suggested in this study require further empirical validation to determine their actual foundational role in learning pathways. Last, we used a two-independentraters approach to select the articles for analysis. We acknowledge the absence of a formal risk of bias assessment tool such as ROBIS and recommend that future studies could use a tool such as ROBIS to assess the risk of bias. \n\n## **CRediT authorship contribution statement** \n\n**Yuk Mui Elly Heung:** Writing – original draft, Visualization, Validation, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptualization. **Hong-biao Yin:** Writing – review & editing. **Lyn English:** Writing – review & editing. **Thomas K.F. Chiu:** Writing – review & editing, Visualization, Validation, Supervision, Methodology, Funding acquisition, Formal analysis, Conceptualization. \n\n## **Ethics approval and consent to participate** \n\nThis study got ethical clearance from the first author’s university and got the consents from the participants to collect their data. \n\n## **Consent for publication** \n\nThe participant has consented to the submission of the manuscript to journals and conferences. \n\n## **Availabi